window.PREVIEW_MOCKS = window.PREVIEW_MOCKS || {};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["repeats-1"] = {"id": "repeats-1", "title": "Repeats Drill 1", "fullTitle": "Repeats Drill 1: Panwar's list + 3 or more papers", "sub": "75 real past-paper Qs · Amit Panwar's repeated stems first, then every question asked in 3 to 7 papers · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Psychoanalytical theory about human personality was proposed by", "options": {"a": "Carl Jung", "b": "Sigmund Freud", "c": "Skinner", "d": "Alfred Adler"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory of personality was proposed by Sigmund Freud. Freud (1856 to 1939), a Viennese neurologist, developed psychoanalysis from his work with hysterical patients, first with Josef Breuer in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and then in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). He held that much of mental life is unconscious and that behaviour is driven by instincts, chiefly the sexual drive (libido) and later aggression. His topographic model divides the mind into conscious, preconscious and unconscious levels; his structural model (The Ego and the Id, 1923) divides personality into the pleasure-seeking id, the superego or internalised conscience, and the ego, which mediates between them and reality. Anxiety signals danger, and the ego protects itself with defence mechanisms such as repression and projection. Personality develops through the oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital psychosexual stages, and unresolved conflict at a stage can leave a fixation. His methods were free association, dream analysis and the study of slips of the tongue. Example: a man who calls his wife by his mother's name may, in Freudian terms, be revealing an unconscious wish. Option b is right because Freud founded and named psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung's theory is analytical psychology and Alfred Adler's is individual psychology; both are psychodynamic, but only Freud's is psychoanalysis.", "topic": "Freud's psychoanalytic theory of personality", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which theory of personality was developed by Sigmund Freud?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic", "b": "Behaviouristic", "c": "Humanistic", "d": "Psychosocial"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is psychoanalytic. Sigmund Freud founded the psychoanalytic theory of personality, which holds that behaviour is driven largely by unconscious forces, early childhood experiences, and the interplay of three structures: the id (primitive drives operating on the pleasure principle), the ego (the reality-oriented mediator), and the superego (the moral conscience). Freud also proposed psychosexual stages of development and the mechanisms of repression and defence. The distractors are each associated with different founders: behaviourism was developed by John Watson and extended by B.F. Skinner, emphasising observable stimulus-response learning and rejecting the unconscious; humanistic psychology was led by Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, who stressed free will, self-actualisation, and the drive toward growth; and psychosocial theory was created by Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian who expanded Freud's stages across the whole lifespan with eight crises. Knowing that Freud equals psychoanalytic is foundational because his ideas shaped a century of clinical practice, grew out of the talking cure (Anna O.'s name for Josef Breuer's method), and remain the basis for psychodynamic therapy. It also anchors students so they can correctly attribute the many competing personality theories they will encounter.", "topic": "Freud's life and the birth of psychoanalysis", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Insight learning is basically learning by", "options": {"a": "Cognition", "b": "Conditions", "c": "Trial and Error", "d": "Imitation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: insight learning is basically learning by cognition. Insight learning was described by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler, who studied chimpanzees at the anthropoid station on Tenerife between 1913 and 1920 and reported the work in The Mentality of Apes (1917 in German, 1925 in English). A banana was hung out of reach or placed outside the cage. After failed attempts, a chimpanzee named Sultan would pause, seem to survey the situation, and then suddenly join two short sticks into a long one, or stack boxes, to get the fruit. Kohler argued that the animal had mentally restructured the perceptual field, seeing the relationship between the parts and the goal. Insight has typical marks: the solution comes suddenly (the \"aha\" experience), it is performed smoothly once found, it is remembered, and it transfers to similar problems. Everyday example: you struggle with a riddle, stop, and the answer suddenly clicks, after which you can solve others like it. Option a is right because insight depends on thinking and understanding relationships, a cognitive process, rather than on reinforced associations. Exam trap: insight is the opposite of Edward L. Thorndike's gradual trial-and-error learning in the puzzle box, and Kohler's work was a Gestalt challenge to behaviourism.", "topic": "Köhler's insight learning", "fig": "Wolfgang Köhler"}, {"q": "Who is the most valuable contributor in the theory of insight learning?", "options": {"a": "E.L Thorndike.", "b": "E.C Tolman.", "c": "K. Koffka.", "d": "W. Kohler."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) is the main contributor to the theory of insight learning. Kohler, one of the three founders of Gestalt psychology, was stranded at the anthropoid research station on Tenerife from 1913 to 1920 and studied chimpanzees there (The Mentality of Apes, 1917 in German, 1925 in English). His best-known chimpanzee, Sultan, could not reach a banana outside the cage with either of two short sticks. After a pause he fitted one stick into the other and at once raked in the fruit. Other apes stacked boxes to reach fruit hung from the roof. Kohler called this insight: a sudden reorganisation of the perceptual field in which the animal sees how the parts of the problem relate to the goal. Insight learning is abrupt rather than gradual, smooth once found, well remembered and readily transferred to new problems, and the moment of understanding is the 'aha' experience. Example: Arjun stares at a puzzle for ten minutes, then suddenly sees that turning one piece sideways solves it, and next time he repeats the solution at once. Option d is right because Kohler's ape studies are the founding evidence and theory of insight learning. Exam trap: Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941) was Kohler's Gestalt colleague, so the shared school makes c tempting, and Edward Thorndike (1898) is offered because his cats in puzzle boxes are the classic contrast: their learning was gradual trial and error, the opposite of insight.", "topic": "Köhler's insight learning", "fig": "Wolfgang Köhler"}, {"q": "During insight learning there is the sudden realization of the solution to a problem. This is known as the:", "options": {"a": "‘aha’ experience", "b": "Light bulb", "c": "Momentary", "d": "Learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The correct answer is the aha experience. Insight learning refers to the sudden grasp of the solution to a problem without overt trial and error, a concept demonstrated by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler in his experiments with chimpanzees, most famously Sultan. Kohler placed bananas out of reach and provided sticks or stackable boxes; rather than gradually fumbling toward the answer, the chimps would pause, appear to study the situation, and then suddenly assemble the tools or stack the boxes in one smooth, correct sequence. This abrupt restructuring of the perceptual field, in which the elements of a problem are suddenly reorganised into a meaningful whole, is the hallmark of insight. The subjective moment of sudden comprehension is popularly called the aha experience (or sometimes the eureka moment). It contrasts sharply with Thorndike's trial-and-error learning, where solutions emerge gradually through reinforcement of successful responses. Insight learning highlights the cognitive, perceptual reorganisation that Gestalt psychologists emphasised against the purely associationist view, showing that learning can be sudden, all-or-none, and transferable to new but similar problems.", "topic": "Latent learning, insight and learning sets", "fig": "Wolfgang Köhler"}, {"q": "The prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Wechsler", "b": "Binet", "c": "Terman", "d": "Stanford"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by Binet. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction asked for a way to identify children who could not benefit from ordinary schooling and needed special classes. Alfred Binet, working with the physician Theodore Simon, rejected Francis Galton's approach of measuring reaction time and sensory acuity and instead tested higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning. The Binet-Simon scale of 1905 had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, creating the concept of mental age, and a further revision followed in 1911, the year Binet died. Later work built on this prototype: William Stern proposed the intelligence quotient (mental age divided by chronological age), and Lewis M. Terman of Stanford University adapted the test as the Stanford-Binet (1916), multiplying the ratio by 100. Example: on the 1908 scale, a seven-year-old who passed the items most nine-year-olds pass had a mental age of nine. Option b is right because the Binet-Simon scale was the first practical intelligence test and the model for later tests. Exam trap: Terman popularised the IQ in America and Wechsler introduced the deviation IQ, but neither created the first test, and \"Stanford\" is a university, not a person.", "topic": "Binet-Simon scale, first intelligence test", "fig": "Alfred Binet"}, {"q": "Which of the following psychologists is associated with the development of the first intelligence test?", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Piaget", "c": "Binet", "d": "Skinner"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The first practical intelligence test was developed by the French psychologist Alfred Binet, together with his collaborator Theodore Simon. In 1904 the French government commissioned Binet to devise a way to identify schoolchildren who needed special educational assistance, and the resulting Binet-Simon Scale (1905) measured children's performance on tasks of judgment, comprehension, and reasoning. Its 1908 revision grouped the tasks by age and introduced the mental level (later called mental age), the age level at which a child was performing. This scale was the foundation of modern intelligence testing. It was later revised by Lewis Terman at Stanford University into the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, which adopted the intelligence quotient (IQ), a term from William Stern, computed as mental age divided by chronological age times 100. The distractors are unrelated to this achievement: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, Jean Piaget studied stages of cognitive development (though he briefly worked in Binet's laboratory), and B. F. Skinner pioneered operant conditioning. Because Alfred Binet developed the first intelligence test, option c is correct.", "topic": "The Binet scales", "fig": "Alfred Binet"}, {"q": "Which intelligence test includes the Pattern Drawing Test?", "options": {"a": "Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence", "b": "Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS-IV)", "c": "Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices", "d": "Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale (Fifth Edition)"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Pattern Drawing Test is one of the five subtests of Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence. Amit Panwar's Master Guide lists this stem as a repeat across CET and RML papers. C. M. Bhatia assembled the battery in 1955 and standardised it on Indian children, so that intelligence could be measured without reading, writing or any one language. Its five subtests are: Kohs Block Design, after Samuel C. Kohs (1920), where coloured cubes are arranged to copy a printed design; Alexander Pass-Along, after W. P. Alexander (1932), where blocks are slid along a tray, never lifted, to reach a target pattern; Pattern Drawing, where the person copies line figures; Immediate Memory, recalling digits forward and backward, the one task that is not a performance task; and Picture Construction, where cut pieces are put together into a picture. Because it needs no literacy, it is the test of choice for an illiterate child or adult from a rural area: it keeps cultural and educational bias low, while the WISC-IV and the Stanford-Binet carry Western verbal content. Example: a 12-year-old from a village who never went to school cannot be fairly tested on vocabulary, but can copy patterns, build block designs and assemble pictures. Option a is right because Pattern Drawing belongs to Bhatia's battery and to none of the other scales listed. Exam trap: block design appears in both Bhatia's battery (as Kohs blocks) and the Wechsler scales, so a block item cannot tell them apart; Pattern Drawing and Picture Construction can. The Seguin Form Board, the other performance test Indian clinics use, is not part of Bhatia's battery.", "topic": "Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests", "fig": "C. M. Bhatia"}, {"q": "Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing:", "options": {"a": "Personality", "b": "Intelligence", "c": "Attitude", "d": "Aptitude"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing intelligence. C. M. Bhatia (1955) built the Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence for Indian conditions, including people who cannot read. It has five performance subtests: Kohs block design (copying coloured designs with cubes), Alexander's pass-along test (sliding blocks in a tray into a target pattern), pattern drawing, immediate memory for digits, and picture construction (assembling cut pieces of pictures). Because it needs no reading and little language, and was standardised with separate norms for literate and illiterate children aged 11 to 16, it is used with illiterate and less schooled people and minimises the educational bias of verbal tests. The subtest scores are combined into one performance IQ. It stands beside other Indian intelligence tests such as the Binet-Kamat Test (V. V. Kamat, 1930s) and Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (Arthur Malin, 1969). For example, a psychologist assessing Arjun, a 12-year-old from a village with little schooling, chooses Bhatia's Battery over the WISC, which leans on schooling and foreign norms. Option b is right because the battery is a non-verbal performance test of general intelligence. Exam trap: because its subtests look like practical, manipulative tasks, students mistake it for an aptitude test, but it yields one intelligence score, not a profile of specific aptitudes.", "topic": "Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests", "fig": "C. M. Bhatia"}, {"q": "A 10-year-old child from a rural area, with no formal schooling, is brought for an intellectual assessment due to academic struggles. The child is illiterate and has had minimal exposure to formal testing materials. Which of the following tests would be the MOST appropriate to use to minimize cultural and educational bias?", "options": {"a": "Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence", "b": "Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC-IV)", "c": "Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale (5th ed.)", "d": "Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC)"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence, because for an illiterate, unschooled child it minimizes cultural and educational bias by relying on nonverbal performance tasks rather than language or academic knowledge. Understand the principle at stake, which is the distinction between verbal and performance (nonverbal) measures of intelligence and the problem of test bias. Any intelligence test that depends on reading, vocabulary, general information, or arithmetic taught in school will systematically underestimate the ability of a child who has had no formal education and cannot read, not because the child is less intelligent but because the test measures acquired schooling rather than underlying reasoning; this is cultural and educational bias, a threat to validity. Performance tests sidestep this by assessing reasoning through hands-on, nonverbal tasks such as assembling blocks, completing patterns, or manipulating objects, which do not require literacy or culturally specific verbal knowledge. Bhatia's battery, developed by C. M. Bhatia in 1955 specifically for the Indian population, is a set of five performance subtests (including Koh's block design, Alexander's pass-along, pattern drawing, immediate memory, and picture construction) designed to be usable with illiterate and rural children by minimizing verbal and educational demands. Because the vignette emphasizes illiteracy, no schooling, and minimal exposure to formal testing, the appropriate choice is the purely performance-based, Indian-standardized Bhatia's battery. The distractors, WISC-IV, Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916), and even the Indian MISIC, all include verbal components and presuppose schooling, so they carry more bias, making Bhatia's the best answer.", "topic": "Performance tests and Indian scales", "fig": "C. M. Bhatia"}, {"q": "In a 3 × 4 contingency table, using the chi-square test, the degrees of freedom associated with the chi-square will be:", "options": {"a": "12", "b": "7", "c": "9", "d": "6"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 6 degrees of freedom. The chi-square test was introduced by Karl Pearson in 1900 to compare observed frequencies with the frequencies expected if the null hypothesis is true. In a contingency table, the test of independence asks whether two categorical variables are related, for example diagnosis (three categories) and treatment setting (four categories). Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of cell values that are free to vary once the row and column totals are fixed. For a contingency table the formula is df = (r - 1)(c - 1), where r is the number of rows and c the number of columns. Here, (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 × 3 = 6. Intuition: if all row and column totals are known, you can fill only 2 cells in each of the first 3 columns freely; every remaining cell is then fixed by the totals. The df matter because the critical value depends on them: at the .05 level, chi-square needs to exceed 12.59 when df is 6. Option d is right because applying (r - 1)(c - 1) to a 3 × 4 table gives 6. Exam trap: students multiply rows by columns (12, the number of cells), add them (7), or subtract one from only one dimension (9), instead of subtracting one from each dimension before multiplying.", "topic": "Chi-square degrees of freedom", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What would be the degrees of freedom for chi-square based on a 4 x 5 contingency table?", "options": {"a": "5", "b": "9", "c": "12", "d": "20"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "For a chi-square test on a contingency table the degrees of freedom equal (rows minus one) multiplied by (columns minus one), so for a 4 by 5 table the calculation is (4 minus 1) times (5 minus 1), which is 3 times 4, equalling 12, making c correct. To understand why, recall what degrees of freedom mean: they represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints are imposed. In a contingency table the row totals and column totals (the marginal totals) are treated as fixed. Given those fixed marginals, once you know all but the last cell in each row and each column, the remaining cells are determined automatically, because each row and each column must sum to its known marginal. This is why one degree of freedom is subtracted from both the number of rows and the number of columns. The chi-square test of independence itself compares observed frequencies in each cell against the frequencies expected if the two categorical variables were independent, summing the squared differences divided by expected counts. The degrees of freedom then determine which chi-square distribution to compare the statistic against in order to find the critical value and significance. Getting the degrees of freedom right is essential because using the wrong value leads to an incorrect critical threshold and a wrong conclusion about statistical significance.", "topic": "Chi-square: comparing frequencies", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:", "options": {"a": "Interrater consistency", "b": "Internal consistency", "c": "Test-retest reliability", "d": "Construct validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Cronbach's alpha measures internal consistency, how well the items of a test hang together in measuring one thing. Lee Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha. The idea is simple: if all the items of a scale tap the same attribute, a person who scores high on one item should tend to score high on the others. Alpha summarises these inter-item relationships in one number, usually between 0 and 1. It equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the problem that different ways of splitting a test give different answers. It generalises the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several response points, such as Likert items. Only one administration of the test is needed. Values of about .70 and above are usually treated as acceptable for research, and higher values are expected for clinical decisions about individuals. Alpha rises as items are added, so a long test can have a high alpha even if it measures more than one thing; a high alpha does not prove the scale is unidimensional. Example: Arjun builds a 20-item exam stress questionnaire and gives it once to 200 students; an alpha of .86 tells him the items move together. Option b is right because alpha is calculated from the relationships among items within a single sitting, which is the definition of internal consistency reliability. Exam trap: Cronbach's name is also on the classic construct validity paper with Paul Meehl (1955), so students drift to validity, but alpha is a reliability coefficient.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha", "fig": "Lee Cronbach"}, {"q": "Borderline Personality Disorder is most characteristically associated with which primary defense mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Repression", "b": "Splitting", "c": "Sublimation", "d": "Rationalization"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is splitting because it is the primitive defence mechanism most characteristically associated with Borderline Personality Disorder. Splitting, a concept emphasised by object-relations theorists such as Melanie Klein and later Otto Kernberg in his work on borderline organisation, refers to the inability to hold together the good and bad qualities of oneself or others into a single, integrated, realistic whole. Instead, people and situations are experienced in black-and-white, all-good or all-bad terms, and these evaluations can flip rapidly. This directly explains the clinical picture of borderline personality: the intense but unstable relationships in which the same person is idealised one moment ('you are perfect, you save me') and devalued the next ('you are cruel, I hate you'), the volatile emotions, and the unstable sense of identity. Because the world is split into extremes with no stable middle, relationships lurch between idealisation and devaluation. Splitting is classed as an immature or borderline-level defence, more primitive than the neurotic defences (repression, rationalization) and far below the mature ones (sublimation). Knowing the defence-to-disorder link is a common exam requirement: splitting maps onto borderline personality disorder just as, for other items, projection maps onto paranoid presentations. The signature instability of borderline relationships is driven by splitting, making it the correct answer.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which therapy focuses on changing maladaptive thought patterns?", "options": {"a": "Humanistic therapy", "b": "Cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT)", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Play therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "Cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT) is the umbrella name for structured, time-limited, present-focused treatments built on one premise: it is not events themselves but how we interpret them that shapes emotions and behaviour, so changing maladaptive thinking changes feelings and actions. Two founders matter. Albert Ellis developed rational therapy in the mid-1950s (later rational emotive behaviour therapy, REBT), with the ABC model: an Activating event, Beliefs about it, and emotional and behavioural Consequences; irrational beliefs such as 'I must be approved of by everyone' are disputed directly. Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy for depression in the 1960s (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979), describing the cognitive triad (negative views of self, world and future), automatic thoughts, underlying schemas or core beliefs, and cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation and catastrophising. Techniques include thought records, Socratic questioning, cognitive restructuring and behavioural experiments that test predictions, along with behavioural methods such as activity scheduling and exposure. Donald Meichenbaum added self-instructional training and stress inoculation. Third-wave therapies such as DBT, ACT and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy focus more on changing one's relationship to thoughts than their content. CBT has the largest evidence base among psychotherapies for depression and anxiety disorders. Contrast it with insight-oriented (psychodynamic) and growth-oriented (humanistic) therapies, which work through the past or the therapeutic relationship rather than directly targeting thoughts. Option b is right because identifying and modifying maladaptive thought patterns is the defining mechanism of CBT.", "topic": "The cognitive turn and the three waves", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which structure allows communication between the hemispheres?", "options": {"a": "Reticular formation", "b": "Temporal lobe", "c": "Contralateral bridge", "d": "Corpus callosum"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the corpus callosum is the band of fibres that lets the two cerebral hemispheres communicate. It is the largest commissure (crossing pathway) in the brain, a thick bundle of about 200 million axons of white matter lying in the midline above the thalamus. The smaller anterior commissure also links the two sides. Through the corpus callosum, what one hemisphere perceives or learns is quickly shared with the other. The classic evidence comes from split-brain patients. To control severe epilepsy, surgeons cut the corpus callosum. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1962 onwards) then flashed pictures to only one visual field. When an object was shown to the right hemisphere (left visual field), the patient could not name it, because speech is usually in the left hemisphere, yet his left hand could pick the object out by touch. Each hemisphere was now working on its own. Sperry shared the Nobel Prize in 1981 for this work. For example, if Meera, a split-brain patient, holds a key in her left hand out of sight, she can find it again among other objects with that hand but cannot say 'key', because the information stays in her right hemisphere. Option d is right because the corpus callosum is the main pathway joining and integrating the two hemispheres. Exam trap: 'contralateral bridge' is an invented term built from two real ideas (contralateral means opposite side; pons means bridge) so that it sounds plausible. The temporal lobe is a region inside each hemisphere, and the reticular formation is a brainstem arousal network; neither connects the hemispheres.", "topic": "Corpus callosum and split brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The corpus callosum connects :", "options": {"a": "Brainstem and cerebellum", "b": "Left and right hemispheres", "c": "Frontal and temporal lobes", "d": "Thalamus and cortex"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain: a thick, arched band of white matter, roughly 200 million axons, lying at the bottom of the longitudinal fissure and connecting corresponding areas of the left and right cerebral hemispheres. Its parts, from front to back, are the rostrum, genu, body and splenium; the splenium carries visual information between the occipital lobes. Neuroanatomy classifies white-matter fibres into three types, and this question tests the distinction. Commissural fibres cross from one hemisphere to the other (corpus callosum, the smaller anterior commissure, hippocampal commissure). Association fibres connect regions within the same hemisphere (arcuate fasciculus, uncinate fasciculus, cingulum). Projection fibres connect the cortex with lower structures such as the thalamus, brainstem and spinal cord (internal capsule, corona radiata). The importance of the callosum is shown by split-brain research. Surgeons cut it to control severe epilepsy, and Roger W. Sperry (Nobel Prize, 1981) with Michael S. Gazzaniga showed that each disconnected hemisphere could perceive and learn independently. An object flashed to the left visual field (right hemisphere) could not be named, because speech is usually in the left hemisphere, yet the left hand could pick it out by touch. People born without a corpus callosum (agenesis) show milder effects because other routes compensate. Option b is right because the corpus callosum is the main commissure linking the left and right cerebral hemispheres.", "topic": "Fibre systems: wiring the parts together", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The famous patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) underwent surgical removal of the medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampus, to treat severe epilepsy. While he retained most of his intellectual abilities and working memory, his primary and most profound deficit was in:", "options": {"a": "Procedural memory", "b": "Semantic memory", "c": "Anterograde amnesia", "d": "Retrograde amnesia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is anterograde amnesia, because after Henry Molaison had his medial temporal lobes, including much of the hippocampus, surgically removed in 1953 to control intractable epilepsy, his most profound and defining deficit was the inability to form new long-term declarative memories, which is by definition anterograde amnesia. Understand what his case taught neuroscience, because it is one of the most important case studies in the field, documented extensively by Brenda Milner and later Suzanne Corkin. Anterograde amnesia means loss of the ability to lay down new memories going forward from the injury, as opposed to retrograde amnesia, which is loss of memories formed before the injury. H.M. could hold information briefly in working memory (he could carry on a conversation and repeat a string of digits) and his intelligence, language, and personality were preserved, but the moment his attention shifted, new experiences vanished; he could not remember people he had met minutes earlier or events of the day. This dissociation proved several landmark points: that the ability to form new long-term memories is separable from short-term or working memory, that it is separable from general intelligence, and that the hippocampus and adjacent medial temporal structures are critical for consolidating new declarative memories into long-term storage, while long-established remote memories are stored elsewhere in the cortex. His relatively preserved memories from well before surgery, and his intact learning of motor skills, further refined the picture. But the headline deficit, the inability to form new conscious memories, is anterograde amnesia.", "topic": "H.M. and the medial temporal lobes", "fig": "Henry Molaison"}, {"q": "Which brain structure is primarily involved in forming new memories?", "options": {"a": "Amygdala", "b": "Hippocampus", "c": "Cerebellum", "d": "Thalamus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The hippocampus, option b, is primarily involved in forming new memories. The hippocampus is a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system, and it is essential for consolidating new explicit, declarative memories, the conscious memories of facts and events, transferring them from short-term into long-term storage. The most famous evidence comes from the patient Henry Molaison, known as H.M., who had the front half of both hippocampi, with nearby medial temporal tissue, removed in 1953 to treat epilepsy and was thereafter unable to form new long-term declarative memories, a profound anterograde amnesia, even though his old memories, his intelligence, and his ability to learn new motor skills remained intact. This dissociation revealed that the hippocampus is critical for encoding new declarative memories but not for storing them permanently or for procedural learning. The hippocampus also supports spatial memory and navigation, evidenced by place cells discovered by O'Keefe, and it is one of the few regions where adult neurogenesis occurs. It is among the earliest structures damaged in Alzheimer's disease, which is why memory loss is an early sign. The exam link is hippocampus equals new memory formation, so option b is correct.", "topic": "H.M. and the medial temporal lobes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which area of the brain is primarily associated with the consolidation of long-term memories?", "options": {"a": "Hippocampus", "b": "Amygdala", "c": "Prefrontal Cortex", "d": "Cerebellum"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The correct answer is Hippocampus. The hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, plays a central role in consolidating new information from short-term into long-term declarative memory, that is, memory for facts and events. Its importance was dramatically revealed by the case of patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), studied by Brenda Milner, whose bilateral hippocampal removal left him unable to form new long-term memories (anterograde amnesia) while sparing older memories and motor learning. This showed the hippocampus is essential for encoding and consolidating new declarative memories, even though the memories are eventually stored in the neocortex. Option A is thus correct. The amygdala (option B), also in the temporal lobe, processes emotion, especially fear, and modulates how strongly emotional experiences are remembered, but it is not the primary consolidation site. The prefrontal cortex (option C) supports working memory, attention, and executive control rather than the consolidation of new long-term memories. The cerebellum (option D) governs motor coordination, balance, and procedural (implicit) learning such as classically conditioned responses, not declarative memory. The concept of synaptic consolidation, involving long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo, 1973) described by Donald Hebb's principle that cells that fire together wire together, underlies how the hippocampus stabilises new memories.", "topic": "H.M. and the medial temporal lobes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Functional Analysis is considered under:", "options": {"a": "Classical Conditioning", "b": "Humanistic Principles", "c": "Operant Conditioning", "d": "Adlerian Principles"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: functional analysis is considered under operant conditioning. B. F. Skinner (1938) showed that operant behaviour is controlled by its consequences: behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and behaviour followed by punishment or no reward becomes less likely. Functional analysis applies this idea to a person's problem behaviour. Instead of asking only what the behaviour looks like, it asks what function it serves, that is, what keeps it going. The simplest form is the ABC model: Antecedents (what happens just before), Behaviour (described in observable terms) and Consequences (what follows). Frederick Kanfer and George Saslow (1965) expanded behavioural analysis into the SORKC model (stimulus, organism, response, contingency, consequence). Common functions are gaining attention, escaping a demand, getting a tangible item and automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Once the function is clear, treatment removes the reinforcer for the problem behaviour and teaches a better behaviour that earns the same result. Kabir, 6, is told to start his homework every evening (antecedent), throws his book (behaviour) and is sent to his room, so homework is avoided (consequence). The function is escape, so the time-out actually rewards him; the plan is to break homework into short tasks, praise effort and let him ask for a break with a card. Option c is right because functional analysis comes from Skinner's operant analysis of behaviour and its consequences. Exam trap: classical conditioning also belongs to behaviour therapy, so a looks plausible; but classical conditioning pairs stimuli to produce reflexive responses and does not analyse consequences, which are the heart of functional analysis.", "topic": "Functional analysis (ABC)", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Humanistic principles", "c": "Operant conditioning", "d": "Psychodynamic principles"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Functional analysis is a core method of behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis, grounded in the operant conditioning principles of B. F. Skinner. It involves systematically examining a target behaviour in terms of its antecedents (the situations, cues, or triggers that precede it), the behaviour itself (its form, frequency, intensity, and duration), and its consequences (what follows the behaviour and thereby reinforces or maintains it), the so-called ABC (Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence) model. The central assumption is Skinnerian: behaviour is controlled by its consequences, so understanding the reinforcement contingencies that maintain a problem behaviour allows the therapist to change those contingencies, altering antecedents and consequences to reduce maladaptive behaviour and strengthen desired behaviour. For example, a functional analysis might reveal that a child's tantrums (behaviour) are triggered by task demands (antecedent) and reinforced by escape from the task or by attention (consequence), pointing to an intervention that changes those contingencies. This consequence-focused, contingency-based approach is definitively operant, distinguishing it from classical conditioning (reflexive stimulus associations), humanistic therapy (self-actualization and unconditional positive regard, per Carl Rogers), and psychodynamic therapy (unconscious conflict). Because functional analysis rests on analysing and modifying the consequences of behaviour, it is based on operant conditioning, making option c correct.", "topic": "Origins, assumptions and functional analysis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Involuntary blocking unpleasant feelings and experiences from one's awareness is known as", "options": {"a": "Repression", "b": "Rationalisation", "c": "Sublimation", "d": "Suppression"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is repression. Repression, one of the central defence mechanisms in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, is the unconscious and involuntary exclusion of distressing thoughts, feelings, memories, or impulses from conscious awareness. The key word in the question is involuntary: repression happens automatically and outside of awareness, which distinguishes it from suppression (option d), the closely related but conscious and deliberate act of pushing an unwanted thought out of mind, for example choosing not to think about an exam until later. Freud considered repression the foundation of all other defences and called the theory of repression the cornerstone on which psychoanalysis rests (1914), arguing that repressed material does not disappear but continues to influence behaviour, dreams, and symptoms. Rationalisation generates false but acceptable justifications, and sublimation redirects impulses into valued activity, so neither matches the definition of blocking feelings from awareness. This distinction between repression (unconscious) and suppression (conscious) is a favourite exam point and is clinically important, because uncovering repressed material through free association and interpretation is a core aim of psychodynamic therapy, helping clients gain insight into hidden conflicts that drive their distress.", "topic": "Repression, the primary defence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A man who yells at his dog when he's upset with his girlfriend is experiencing _____________.", "options": {"a": "Denial", "b": "Displacement", "c": "Sublimation", "d": "Repression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is displacement. Displacement is a defense mechanism, in the tradition of Sigmund Freud and systematized by Anna Freud, in which an emotional impulse, usually aggression, is redirected from a threatening or unavailable target onto a safer, less threatening substitute. Here the man is angry with his girlfriend, but because expressing that anger directly may feel dangerous to the relationship, he unconsciously transfers the emotion onto the dog, a harmless and available target. The original feeling is preserved and discharged, but its object is shifted. This is the textbook illustration of displacement, often exemplified by the person who is criticized by a boss and then comes home and snaps at family members (the kick the dog scenario). It differs from the other defenses listed: denial refuses to acknowledge a painful reality; sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially constructive outlets such as art, sport, or work; and repression banishes distressing thoughts and feelings from conscious awareness entirely. Because the man's anger keeps its aggressive quality but is aimed at a substitute object rather than being transformed, hidden, or denied, the mechanism at work is specifically displacement, making option b correct.", "topic": "Five defences that redirect or disguise", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, which defense mechanism involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others?", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Repression", "c": "Sublimation", "d": "Displacement"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: projection is the defence mechanism that involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others. Defence mechanisms are the ego's unconscious strategies for protecting itself from anxiety. Sigmund Freud described many of them, and his daughter Anna Freud set them out systematically in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). In projection, an impulse or feeling that would be too threatening to acknowledge as one's own, such as hostility, envy or sexual desire, is disowned and perceived instead in another person. The feeling does not change; only its owner does. Freud linked projection to paranoia, and George Vaillant later placed it among the immature defences in his hierarchy, below neurotic defences such as repression and displacement and mature ones such as sublimation and humour. Freud also used the word in a broader sense for any colouring of perception by the inner world, which is the basis of projective tests such as the Rorschach. Example: a man attracted to a colleague, who cannot accept the feeling, accuses his wife of flirting with everyone; or a student who dislikes her roommate insists, 'She hates me'. Option a is right because attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others is the textbook definition of projection. Exam trap: in displacement the feeling stays yours but moves to a safer target, whereas in projection the feeling is placed in someone else; ask whether the owner or the target has changed.", "topic": "Projection as a defence mechanism", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Damage to the hippocampus is most likely to impair:", "options": {"a": "Memory", "b": "Balance", "c": "Sleep", "d": "Breathing"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: damage to the hippocampus mainly impairs memory, especially the ability to form new long-term memories. The hippocampus is a curved structure in the medial temporal lobe, named after the Greek word for seahorse, and it is part of the limbic system. Its main job is consolidation: turning new experiences and facts (declarative or explicit memory) into lasting long-term memories, which are later stored across the cortex. It also builds maps of space; John O'Keefe (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one particular location. The classic evidence is the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Beecher Scoville removed much of both medial temporal lobes, including most of the hippocampus, to stop his epilepsy. Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported severe anterograde amnesia: H.M. could not form new declarative memories, yet his intelligence, short-term memory, older memories and procedural learning were spared (he improved at mirror drawing while denying he had ever practised). So the hippocampus is needed for new explicit memories, not for every kind of memory. For example, if Kabir suffers hippocampal damage after encephalitis, he may chat normally and recall his childhood, but forget a visitor within minutes and fail to learn the route to his new ward. Option a is right because the hippocampus is the brain's key structure for consolidating new long-term memories. Exam trap: examiners offer the jobs of nearby structures. Balance belongs to the cerebellum and the vestibular system, sleep to the hypothalamus, reticular formation and pons, and breathing to the medulla. Also do not confuse the hippocampus (memory) with the amygdala (fear and emotional memory), its neighbour in the limbic system.", "topic": "Hippocampus and memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person suffered a head injury in a road accident several years ago. Since the accident, he is unable to form new memories. He can remember many things which he did before the accident, but he cannot remember what he said ten minutes ago. The part of the brain which is most probably injured is:", "options": {"a": "Brain stem", "b": "Hippocampus", "c": "Spinal cord", "d": "Reticular formation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The brain region most likely injured is the hippocampus, so the answer is b. The case describes anterograde amnesia: the man can recall events from before his accident (intact remote and old memories) but cannot form new long term memories, forgetting what he said ten minutes ago. This is the classic signature of bilateral hippocampal damage, made famous by the patient H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), who after surgical removal of his medial temporal lobes, including about the front half of each hippocampus, was unable to form new declarative memories while retaining older memories and normal short term memory and intelligence. The hippocampus is essential for consolidation, the process of converting new short term and working memories into durable long term memories that can later be retrieved. It is not, however, the storage site for old memories, which is why remote memories laid down before the injury survive: those have already been consolidated and distributed to cortical stores, so they remain accessible even when the hippocampus is damaged. Hence damage to the hippocampus selectively impairs the formation of new memories while sparing old ones, exactly the pattern described. The exam discriminator is the dissociation between preserved old memories and an inability to lay down new ones, which points unmistakably to the hippocampus and its role in memory consolidation rather than to brainstem, spinal, or arousal structures.", "topic": "The hippocampus: consolidation and space", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Anterograde amnesia results from accidental injury of the", "options": {"a": "Hippocampus", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Pituitary gland", "d": "Cerebelum (sic)"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new long-term declarative memories after the moment of injury, while memories laid down before the damage often remain intact. The structure most responsible is the hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped region in the medial temporal lobe that is essential for consolidating new experiences from short-term into stable long-term memory. The classic evidence comes from the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), studied by Brenda Milner: after bilateral removal of his medial temporal lobes, including about the front half of each hippocampus, to treat epilepsy, H.M. could no longer form new conscious memories of facts and events, though his older memories and his ability to learn new motor skills survived. This dissociation showed that the hippocampus is critical specifically for declarative memory consolidation, not for storage of already-consolidated memories or for procedural learning (which depends more on the cerebellum and basal ganglia). So injury to the hippocampus leaves a person stuck in the present, unable to encode new facts and events, the defining picture of anterograde amnesia. The distractors are all nearby or well-known brain structures with unrelated jobs: homeostasis for the hypothalamus, hormone control for the pituitary, and coordination for the cerebellum. Anchor it on H.M.: hippocampus damaged, new declarative memories cannot form. The correct answer is the hippocampus.", "topic": "H.M. and the medial temporal lobes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about obsessional thoughts?", "options": {"a": "They are best treated by thought-stopping", "b": "They always give rise to compulsions", "c": "They are ego-dystonic", "d": "They are rarely of a sexual nature"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: obsessional thoughts are ego-dystonic. An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive thought, image or impulse that the person experiences as unwanted, senseless and out of character, yet recognises as a product of his or her own mind. Kurt Schneider's classic definition describes contents of consciousness that carry a feeling of subjective compulsion and cannot be got rid of, although on quiet reflection they are seen as senseless. \"Ego-dystonic\" means the experience clashes with the person's values and self-image, so it causes distress and is resisted. Think of a devoted young mother who keeps getting the thought \"I might stab my baby\" while cutting vegetables: she is horrified, hides the knives and fights the thought, which is exactly why it is an obsession and not a wish. ICD-10 adds that at least one thought is still resisted, even if unsuccessfully, and that carrying out a related act is not in itself pleasurable. Obsessive-compulsive personality traits such as perfectionism are different: they feel ego-syntonic, natural and right to the person. Option c is right because distress, resistance and the sense that the thought is alien to the self are the core of an obsession. Exam trap: \"recognised as one's own\" (obsession) is not \"inserted from outside\" (thought insertion in schizophrenia); the obsessional patient owns the thought but rejects its content.", "topic": "Obsessions are ego-dystonic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The basic difference between obsession and compulsion is:", "options": {"a": "The former involves preoccupation with certain thoughts while the latter involves preoccupation with certain actions", "b": "The former is a mood disorder whereas the latter is an anxiety disorder", "c": "Actually, there is no difference between the two disorders", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: an obsession is a preoccupation with certain thoughts, while a compulsion is a preoccupation with certain actions. Both are symptoms of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Obsessions are recurrent, persistent and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that intrude into the mind and cause marked anxiety or distress, such as a fear of contamination or a nagging doubt that the door is unlocked. The person usually recognises them as products of his or her own mind and tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (hand washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, silently repeating a prayer) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules, to reduce distress or prevent a feared event. The relief is brief, so the cycle repeats and strengthens. Example: Arjun keeps thinking that his hands carry germs (obsession), so he washes them twenty times before every meal (compulsion). DSM-5 (2013) requires obsessions, compulsions or both that are time-consuming (for example more than one hour a day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Option a is right because it states the classic distinction: the obsession is the thought, the compulsion is the act that answers it. Exam trap: obsession and compulsion are two symptoms of one disorder, not two separate disorders; and compulsions can be silent mental acts such as counting, so a ritual that happens in the head is still a compulsion if it is done to reduce anxiety. OCD was never a mood disorder; it sat with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) and got its own chapter only in DSM-5 (2013).", "topic": "Obsessions versus compulsions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term IQ was invented by", "options": {"a": "Lewis Termon (sic).", "b": "Gordon Allport.", "c": "William Stern.", "d": "John Watson."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The concept of the intelligence quotient grew out of the mental-testing movement begun by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon in France, who introduced the idea of a child's 'mental age' relative to chronological age. The actual term intelligence quotient, and the formula for it, came from the German psychologist William Stern in 1912. Stern proposed dividing mental age by chronological age to yield a ratio that stayed comparable across ages; multiplying by 100 (to remove the decimal) gives the familiar IQ score, so a child whose mental age matches their chronological age scores 100. Lewis Terman, the distractor spelled 'Termon' in option a, later revised Binet's test at Stanford University to create the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale and helped spread IQ testing in the United States, but he adopted and applied Stern's quotient rather than inventing it. Gordon Allport is associated with the trait approach to personality (cardinal, central, and secondary traits), and John Watson is the founder of behaviorism, who famously downplayed inner mental constructs; neither is connected with the origin of IQ. So the credit for coining the term belongs to William Stern, with Binet supplying the mental-age foundation and Terman supplying the influential American test. This is a standard history-of-psychology fact examined frequently in entrance papers.", "topic": "From Galton to Binet: The First Tests and the IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "options": {"a": "That two tests are done at the same time", "b": "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", "c": "The items on the test consistently relate to each other.", "d": "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute (assessed at the same time). Concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion-related validity, the family of validity concerned with how well a test's scores correspond to an external criterion. Criterion validity has two forms distinguished by timing. Predictive validity assesses how well a test predicts a criterion measured in the future (for example, whether an aptitude test predicts later job performance). Concurrent validity assesses how well a test correlates with an established criterion or an existing validated measure of the same construct assessed at roughly the same point in time. For instance, a new, shorter depression scale shows concurrent validity if its scores correlate highly with scores on an already-established depression measure administered concurrently. The essence is agreement with a criterion in the present. Option A is a trap because it takes 'concurrent' too literally as merely testing simultaneously, without the crucial requirement of correlation with a valid criterion measuring the same attribute. Option B describes inter-rater reliability, and option C describes internal consistency reliability, both of which are reliability rather than validity. This item tests the correct definition of concurrent validity as present-time correlation with a criterion measure of the same construct.", "topic": "Criterion-related validity: concurrent and predictive", "fig": ""}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "options": {"a": "Trait; Humanistic.", "b": "Psychoanalytic; Behaviorist.", "c": "Psychoanalytic; Humanistic.", "d": "Trait; Behaviorist."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "topic": "Unconscious across personality approaches", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "options": {"a": "'Eccentric' behaviour marked by odd patterns of thinking and communication.", "b": "An enduring pattern of instability in personal relationships.", "c": "A lack of a well-defined and stable self-image.", "d": "Regular and unpredictable changes in mood."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder features", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "options": {"a": "Verbal fluency.", "b": "Visual perception.", "c": "Memory.", "d": "Attention."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "topic": "Stroop test and attention", "fig": "John Ridley Stroop"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "options": {"a": "Token economies", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Aversion therapies", "d": "Systematic desensitization."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: token economies are not built on classical conditioning; they come from operant conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with one that already triggers a reflex comes to trigger a similar response on its own. Several therapies apply this to emotional responses. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy so that relaxation replaces anxiety (counterconditioning, or reciprocal inhibition). Flooding exposes the person to the feared stimulus at full strength, with escape blocked, until the conditioned fear extinguishes. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus like a nausea-inducing drug so that the behaviour itself becomes aversive. A token economy works differently: it uses consequences. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin ran one on a psychiatric ward, described in The Token Economy (1968): patients earned tokens (generalised secondary reinforcers) for self-care and ward jobs and exchanged them for privileges, exactly as B. F. Skinner's operant principles predict. A child earning stars for homework that buy screen time is a token economy at home. Option a is right because tokens strengthen behaviour through reinforcement after the response, not through stimulus pairing. Exam trap: ask whether the technique changes what follows a behaviour (operant) or what a stimulus is paired with (classical).", "topic": "Token economy as operant technique", "fig": "Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin"}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client centred therapist most likely to use?", "options": {"a": "Analysis", "b": "Giving advice", "c": "Reflection", "d": "Aversive conditioning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "Client-centred therapy, also called person-centred therapy, was developed by Carl Rogers as a humanistic alternative to both psychoanalysis and behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913). Its guiding assumption is that people possess an innate drive toward growth and self-actualisation, and that the therapist's job is not to direct or diagnose but to create a facilitative relationship in which clients can find their own answers. Rogers specified three core conditions the therapist must provide: unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement), genuineness or congruence (being authentic rather than hiding behind a professional facade), and empathic understanding (accurately sensing the client's inner world). The characteristic technique that expresses this empathy is reflection: the therapist mirrors back the feelings and meanings the client expresses, for example responding 'It sounds like you feel trapped and exhausted,' so the client feels deeply heard and can explore their experience further. Reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client rather than the expert, which is the essence of the non-directive method. The distractors all violate this stance: analysis and interpretation belong to Freudian psychoanalysis, giving advice is directive and imposes the therapist's judgement, and aversive conditioning is a behaviourist procedure. Only reflection embodies Rogers's empathic, non-directive style, so it is the correct choice.", "topic": "The core conditions and person-centred practice", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses caused by long-term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease", "b": "Huntington's disease", "c": "Parkinson's disease", "d": "Korsakoff's disease"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: Korsakoff's disease (Korsakoff's syndrome) is the alcohol-related illness with profound retrograde amnesia. The Russian neuropsychiatrist Sergei Korsakoff described it in the 1880s in long-term heavy drinkers. Chronic alcohol abuse, with poor diet and impaired absorption, causes thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. The acute phase is Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke, with confusion, ataxia and eye-movement problems (ophthalmoplegia, nystagmus). If thiamine is not given quickly it can progress to Korsakoff's syndrome, so the two are often named together as Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal nucleus of the thalamus. The memory picture is severe anterograde amnesia (inability to form new memories) plus extensive retrograde amnesia with a temporal gradient: recent decades are lost more than early life. Patients often confabulate, filling gaps with invented but sincere stories, and lack insight. Example: a man in his fifties insists he still works at a job he left years ago and cheerfully describes a meeting there \"this morning\". Immediate memory and general intelligence are relatively spared. Option d is right because Korsakoff's is the only option caused by alcohol abuse, and its retrograde amnesia is classically profound. Exam trap: Korsakoff's is not a dementia like Alzheimer's; it is an amnestic disorder caused by a vitamin deficiency, and early thiamine can prevent it.", "topic": "Korsakoff's syndrome and retrograde amnesia", "fig": "Sergei Korsakoff"}, {"q": "Who is considered the father of experimental psychology?", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Wundt", "c": "Watson", "d": "Skinner"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Wilhelm Wundt is considered the father of experimental psychology. Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician who had assisted Hermann von Helmholtz, argued that the mind could be studied with the experimental methods of physiology. His Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) announced a new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig his students began original research in what is conventionally called the first psychological laboratory; that year is taken as the birth of psychology as an independent science. He founded the journal Philosophische Studien (1881) and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell, Emil Kraepelin and Charles Spearman. His method was controlled introspection: trained observers reacted to precise stimuli, such as a metronome beat or a flash of light, and reported their immediate experience while reaction times were measured. He also wrote the ten-volume Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) on language, myth and custom, which he thought needed non-experimental methods. Example: in a Leipzig reaction-time study an observer pressed a key as soon as he heard a click, and Wundt compared this simple reaction with one that required recognising the sound first. Option b is right because Wundt gave psychology its first laboratory, journal and trained students, the marks of an experimental science. Exam trap: read the qualifier: Gustav Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860) is the father of psychophysics, William James of American psychology, John B. Watson of behaviourism and Sigmund Freud of psychoanalysis; only experimental psychology belongs to Wundt.", "topic": "Wundt, father of experimental psychology", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "The primary function of the limbic system is:", "options": {"a": "Motor coordination", "b": "Emotion regulation and memory", "c": "Visual processing", "d": "Language production"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the primary function of the limbic system is emotion regulation and memory. The name comes from the Latin limbus, 'border', because these structures form a ring around the thalamus at the inner edge of the cerebral hemispheres. In 1937 James Papez proposed an emotional circuit running from the hippocampus through the fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus and back to the hippocampus. Paul MacLean added the amygdala and other structures and named the whole the limbic system in 1952. Its main members are the hippocampus, which forms new declarative memories and spatial maps; the amygdala, which detects threat and drives fear and emotional memory; the cingulate cortex; the fornix; the septum; and the mammillary bodies, with the hypothalamus closely linked. Evidence comes from H.M., who lost both medial temporal lobes in 1953 surgery and could no longer form new declarative memories, and from the monkeys of Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy, which became tame and fearless after removal of the temporal lobes including the amygdala. Example: you remember exactly where you were when you heard shocking news because the amygdala tags the event as emotional and the hippocampus stores it strongly. Option b is right because emotion and memory are the two functions every textbook assigns to the limbic system. Exam trap: the basal ganglia sit close by and help select movements, but motor coordination belongs to the cerebellum, not to the limbic system.", "topic": "Functions of the limbic system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Spinal cord", "c": "Frontal lobe", "d": "Medulla"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the pathway behind it, the reflex arc. It has five links: a receptor in the skin picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord; an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out; and an effector, a muscle or gland, acts. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is left out of the decision. Messages do travel up to the brain, but they arrive after the reflex has already happened, which is why you feel the pain a moment after your hand has jerked away. The simplest arc, the knee-jerk (patellar) reflex, has no interneuron at all: the sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron, a monosynaptic reflex. When Riya touches a hot iron, her hand pulls back before she has consciously registered the heat; the spinal cord has acted first and told the brain afterwards. Option b is right because the spinal cord is the centre of the classic reflex arc for the limb and body reflexes. Exam trap: some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, gagging and swallowing, are run by the medulla in the brainstem, so d looks attractive; but the word \"usually\" points to the spinal cord, which handles the typical reflexes taught in textbooks.", "topic": "Reflex arc", "fig": "Charles Sherrington"}, {"q": "Which one is called non-probability sampling?", "options": {"a": "Quota sampling", "b": "Cluster sampling", "c": "Systematic sampling", "d": "Stratified random sampling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: quota sampling is a non-probability method. In non-probability sampling the chance of any member being selected is unknown, because selection depends on the researcher's judgement or convenience rather than on chance. In quota sampling the researcher first decides how many people of each type are needed, usually to mirror the population on characteristics such as gender, age or religion, and then fills each quota with whoever is available. For example, an interviewer told to survey 50 men and 50 women at a mall will stop passers-by until both quotas are full; the proportions look right, but who gets picked within each quota is not random. Other non-probability methods are convenience (accidental), purposive (judgement) and snowball sampling. Option a is right because quota sampling fills subgroups by convenience, so it lacks random selection. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into subgroups. The difference is the last step: stratified sampling selects randomly within each stratum (probability), while quota sampling does not (non-probability).", "topic": "Quota sampling as non-probability sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who first described autism?", "options": {"a": "Kanner", "b": "Asperger", "c": "Chess", "d": "Benham"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: Leo Kanner. The Austrian-American child psychiatrist Leo Kanner, working at Johns Hopkins in Baltimore, published \"Autistic Disturbances of Affective Contact\" in 1943, describing 11 children who from early life showed extreme aloneness, an inability to relate normally to people, odd or delayed language (echolalia, pronoun reversal, very literal speech) and an \"anxiously obsessive desire for the maintenance of sameness\". Such a child might become distressed if a single piece of furniture was moved. He called the condition early infantile autism, and for decades it was known as Kanner's syndrome. A year later, in 1944, the Austrian paediatrician Hans Asperger independently described boys with \"autistic psychopathy\": fluent speech and normal intelligence but poor social understanding, narrow interests and clumsiness. His German-language paper became widely known only after Lorna Wing's 1981 paper on \"Asperger's syndrome\". DSM-5-TR now merges both pictures into autism spectrum disorder. Option a is right because Kanner's 1943 paper is the first standard description of autism as a childhood syndrome. Exam trap: the word \"autism\" was coined earlier by Eugen Bleuler (1911), but for withdrawal in schizophrenia, not for the developmental disorder; and Asperger came after Kanner, not before.", "topic": "First description of autism", "fig": "Leo Kanner"}, {"q": "The \"empty chair\" technique is commonly used in:", "options": {"a": "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy", "b": "Gestalt Therapy", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Behavioral Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the empty chair technique is commonly used in Gestalt therapy. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (Gestalt Therapy, 1951). It is an experiential, here-and-now therapy that aims to increase awareness and help clients finish unfinished business, feelings from past relationships that were never expressed and still shape present life. In the empty chair technique, the client imagines a person (often a parent, a former partner or someone who has died) or a disowned part of the self sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly, in the present tense. The client may then switch chairs and answer as the other person or part. This turns talk about feelings into direct experience of them, so that anger, grief or longing can be expressed and integrated. A related form is the two-chair dialogue between the 'topdog' (the critical 'you should' voice) and the 'underdog' (the apologetic voice that wins by stalling). Example: a man who never told his late father how hurt he felt by his criticism speaks to the empty chair, then moves into it and answers as his father; he ends the session tearful but relieved. Option b is right because the empty chair is the best-known signature technique of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: emotion-focused therapy (Leslie Greenberg) and psychodrama (Jacob Moreno) also use chair work, but in exam options the empty chair points to Gestalt therapy, never to REBT, psychoanalysis or behaviour therapy.", "topic": "Empty chair technique: Gestalt therapy", "fig": "Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "The zone of proximal development was given by –", "options": {"a": "Erik Erickson", "b": "Jean Piaget", "c": "Lev Vygotsky", "d": "Sigmund Freud"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the zone of proximal development (ZPD) was given by the Russian psychologist Lev Vygotsky. Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934) developed sociocultural theory, which holds that higher mental functions begin in social interaction and are then internalised by the child. His ideas reached English readers mainly through Mind in Society (1978). The ZPD is the gap between what a child can do alone (the actual developmental level) and what the child can do with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer (the potential developmental level). Teaching works best inside this zone, on tasks the child cannot yet do alone but can manage with help. If the task is too easy nothing new is learnt; if it is too hard, help cannot bridge the gap. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) later coined 'scaffolding' for the support a tutor gives and gradually withdraws as competence grows. Vygotsky also stressed private speech (children talking aloud to guide their own actions) and the role of language and culture in shaping thought. Example: Arjun, aged six, cannot finish a 30-piece jigsaw alone, but when his older sister suggests starting with the edges and sorting pieces by colour, he completes it. A week later he does it by himself. The jigsaw was in his ZPD, and his sister's hints were scaffolding. Option c is right because the ZPD is Vygotsky's central educational concept. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) is the common wrong answer because both theorists studied cognitive development; Piaget saw the child as a lone scientist moving through fixed stages, while Vygotsky stressed social guidance. Also, the word 'scaffolding' is not Vygotsky's own; it came from Wood, Bruner and Ross.", "topic": "Zone of proximal development", "fig": "Lev Vygotsky"}, {"q": "An IQ test does NOT provide which of the following?", "options": {"a": "High test retest reliability", "b": "Good predictor of behaviour", "c": "High internal consistency", "d": "Good validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is that an IQ test does NOT provide a good predictor of behaviour. Standardised intelligence tests such as the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, developed by David Wechsler, and the Stanford-Binet scale, developed from the work of Alfred Binet and revised by Lewis Terman, are among the most psychometrically sound instruments in psychology. They have excellent reliability, including high test-retest reliability (IQ scores are quite stable over time) and high internal consistency (their subtests and items cohere well in measuring the same underlying general ability). They also have good validity for what they are designed to assess, and notably strong criterion validity for predicting academic achievement and, to a meaningful degree, occupational and educational outcomes. However, the key limitation, and the intended answer here, is that an IQ score is a relatively poor predictor of specific individual behaviour in everyday life. Intelligence tests measure cognitive abilities and problem-solving skills, but real-world behaviour is shaped by many additional factors such as motivation, personality, emotional intelligence (a concept popularised by Daniel Goleman), social context, and situational variables, so IQ alone does not reliably predict how a person will behave. Thus while an IQ test delivers strong reliability and validity, it does not serve as a good general predictor of behaviour. This item tests awareness of the strengths and limits of intelligence testing.", "topic": "What an intelligence test can and cannot do", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:", "options": {"a": "Seven standard deviations below the mean.", "b": "One standard deviations below the mean.", "c": "Two standard deviations below the mean.", "d": "Four standard deviations below the mean"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The correct answer is two standard deviations below the mean. IQ tests such as the Wechsler scales, developed by David Wechsler, are standardised to a mean of 100 with a standard deviation of 15. The DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation (the older term for what the DSM-5 calls Intellectual Disability) specified significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, operationalised as an IQ of approximately 70 or below. An IQ of 70 lies exactly two standard deviations below the mean (100 minus 2 times 15 equals 70), which is why the two-standard-deviation cutoff is the cardinal criterion. Below that threshold roughly the bottom 2 to 3 percent of the population falls, reflecting the properties of the normal distribution. Importantly, the diagnosis required more than a low IQ alone: it also demanded concurrent deficits in adaptive functioning (such as communication, self-care, and social skills) with onset before age 18. The severity levels (mild, moderate, severe, profound) were graded by IQ ranges. The distractors give cutoffs that are either far too lenient (one SD, IQ 85) or implausibly extreme (four or seven SD). This item tests knowledge of the statistical basis of the intellectual disability threshold, anchored at two standard deviations below the mean.", "topic": "Intellectual disability: criteria and severity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The MMPI is used to measure:", "options": {"a": "Leadership potential", "b": "Personality and psychological disorders", "c": "The Big Five traits", "d": "Unconscious drives"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the MMPI measures personality and psychological disorders. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was developed at the University of Minnesota by the psychologist Starke R. Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley and published in 1943. They used empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if a psychiatric patient group (for example, people with depression) answered it differently from ordinary people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The result was ten clinical scales, including Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia and Social Introversion, plus validity scales (L for lying, F for infrequent responding, K for defensiveness) that show whether the person answered honestly. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 true/false items; the MMPI-2-RF and MMPI-3 are shorter revisions, and the MMPI-A is for adolescents. Clinical example: a psychologist assessing a patient with vague physical complaints may find Hypochondriasis and Hysteria raised well above Depression, the classic \"conversion V\" profile, which guides diagnosis and treatment planning. Option b is right because the MMPI is the most widely used objective inventory for assessing personality and psychopathology. Exam trap: the MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test, and it was built empirically, not from the Big Five or any theory of traits.", "topic": "MMPI: purpose and construction", "fig": "Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley"}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "options": {"a": "Algorithm", "b": "Metacognition", "c": "Analogies", "d": "Constructive process"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to learn more effectively by correcting oneself, is metacognition. The American developmental psychologist John H. Flavell introduced the term in the 1970s (key paper 1979), describing it as \"thinking about thinking\". It has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about your own mind, tasks and strategies: for example, that you remember diagrams better than lists. Metacognitive regulation is the active use of that knowledge: planning how to study, monitoring whether you understand, and correcting your approach when you do not. Its memory part, metamemory, includes the feeling of knowing and judging how well you have learned something. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts academic success. Example: while preparing for an entrance exam, you notice you keep getting psychophysics questions wrong, realise that rereading notes is not working, and switch to self-testing with past papers; noticing and fixing the problem is metacognition in action. In clinical work, Adrian Wells's metacognitive therapy targets beliefs about worrying itself, such as \"worry keeps me safe\". Option b is right because the question describes metacognition exactly: monitoring and correcting one's own thinking. Exam trap: metacognition is not ordinary cognition or general intelligence; \"meta\" means one level up, so it is cognition about cognition.", "topic": "Flavell's concept of metacognition", "fig": "John Flavell"}, {"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the:", "options": {"a": "Somatic nervous system.", "b": "Central nervous system.", "c": "Autonomic nervous system.", "d": "Cerebral cortex."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event, such as a pounding heart, fast breathing, sweating and a dry mouth, are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the internal organs (heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut and glands) largely without conscious control. It has two branches that work in opposite directions. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for emergencies, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: it raises heart rate and blood pressure, widens the pupils, slows digestion and makes the adrenal medulla release adrenaline. The parasympathetic branch calms the body afterwards (rest and digest), slowing the heart and restarting digestion. Imagine Arjun in a road accident: within seconds his heart races, his palms sweat and his stomach knots, all sympathetic effects, and an hour later his parasympathetic branch brings him back to baseline. The hypothalamus and amygdala in the brain trigger these changes, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. The polygraph (lie detector) works by recording exactly these autonomic changes. Option c is right because the stem asks which system most directly controls the emotional reaction, and the involuntary arousal of fear and stress is autonomic. Exam trap: the somatic nervous system sounds bodily, but it carries sensation and controls the voluntary skeletal muscles; the gut-level, involuntary arousal of emotion belongs to the autonomic system.", "topic": "Autonomic system and emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence:", "options": {"a": "Fluid, Fixed.", "b": "Fixed, Fluid.", "c": "Fluid, Crystalized.", "d": "Crystalized, Fluid."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Cattell (proposed 1943, tested 1963) and his student John Horn (1966) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, such as spotting the rule in a matrix puzzle or working out an unfamiliar gadget. It depends heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience. Because it keeps growing with use, it holds steady or even rises into old age. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) followed adults for decades and found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood, while perceptual speed declines from early adulthood. Timothy Salthouse (1996) traced much of the fluid loss to a general slowing of processing. Example: Riya's grandmother, aged 72, finishes the newspaper crossword before anyone else and knows every rare word (crystallised), but takes far longer than Riya to learn a new phone app (fluid). Option c is right because it puts the declining ability (fluid) in the first blank and the stable one (crystallised) in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: examiners reverse the order (option d) to catch students who remember the pair but not which one falls; think 'fluid flows away, crystals last'. They also invent a 'fixed' intelligence, which is not a type in any theory; it echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, a belief about ability rather than a kind of ability.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence in ageing", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "options": {"a": "Block design.", "b": "Mazes.", "c": "Non-verbal cues.", "d": "Drawing a man."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "topic": "Porteus Maze Test", "fig": "Stanley Porteus"}, {"q": "Which of the statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "options": {"a": "CBT and sertraline are not significantly different in terms of efficacy.", "b": "CBT improves outcome in around 60% of cases.", "c": "CBT and sertraline combined produce the best outcome.", "d": "All of these."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the general findings on treating childhood anxiety. The landmark trial is the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) of John Walkup and colleagues (2008), published in the New England Journal of Medicine. It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT, based on Philip Kendall's (1990) Coping Cat programme), the SSRI sertraline, the two combined, or a pill placebo. Rated much or very much improved were 80.7% with the combination, 59.7% with CBT, 54.9% with sertraline and 23.7% with placebo. Three findings follow. CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other (statement a). CBT helped about 60% of children (statement b). The combination beat both single treatments (statement c). Philip Kendall's own randomised trial (1994) had already shown that CBT, which teaches children to recognise anxious feelings and thoughts, use coping plans and then face feared situations through graded exposure, helps roughly two thirds of anxious children. Clinical practice therefore usually offers CBT first and considers adding an SSRI for moderate to severe anxiety or when CBT alone is not enough. Example: Meera, 11, panics before school and cannot sleep away from her mother; a CAMS-style plan might start weekly CBT and add sertraline if she stays stuck. Option d is right because each of the three statements matches the CAMS results. Exam trap: students who know that the combination won may wrongly reject statement a, assuming the two single treatments must also differ; in CAMS they did not.", "topic": "Treating childhood anxiety: CAMS", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of:", "options": {"a": "Delinquency as a factor affecting the development of intelligence.", "b": "Mental age versus chronological age.", "c": "A z-score.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the z-score. A z-score tells how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies above or below the mean of a group: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The older ratio IQ, from William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), was mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It worked for children but failed for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a perfectly ordinary adult's IQ would fall with every birthday. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, replaced it with the deviation IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the mean and SD of his or her own age group in a large norm sample, turned into a z-score, and then rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. So scoring exactly at your age group's mean gives 100 at any age, one SD above (z = +1) gives 115, and one SD below gives 85. Because the deviation IQ is a standard score on the normal curve, it converts directly to a percentile: 115 is about the 84th percentile, 130 about the 98th. Modern Wechsler tests and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition all use it. Example: Arjun, 30, scores 12 points above the mean for his age group on a test whose SD at that age is 6; his z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 30 = 130. Option c is right because the deviation IQ is a z-score rescaled to mean 100 and SD 15. Exam trap: option b describes the ratio IQ that the deviation IQ replaced, so students who remember 'mental age' pick the wrong formula.", "topic": "Deviation IQ as a z-score", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because:", "options": {"a": "Each involves a crisis or dilemma.", "b": "Each signals a new stage of cognitive development.", "c": "Each is an expression of biological programming.", "d": "Their failure to appear is evidence of psychopathology."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson (1950), life stages matter because each one involves a crisis or dilemma. In Childhood and Society, Erikson described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage is defined by a psychosocial crisis: a turning point between two opposite tendencies, set off when a growing person meets new social demands. They run trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). 'Crisis' here means a choice of direction, not a disaster. A favourable resolution, tilted towards the positive pole, leaves the ego with a basic strength or virtue, such as hope from trust or fidelity from identity. An unresolved crisis makes later ones harder but not hopeless, because development continues across the whole lifespan. Erikson called the built-in sequence the epigenetic principle: the stages unfold on a timetable, but society shapes how each one turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician while his parents push medicine; he is working through identity versus role confusion. Option a is right because a crisis or dilemma is the defining feature of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: examiners offer cognitive development, which is the stage logic of Jean Piaget (1936), and biological programming, which fits the libido-driven psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud better (Three Essays 1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923); Erikson's stages are psychosocial, defined by social conflicts.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial crises", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "REM sleep behaviour disorder is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterised as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "options": {"a": "Atonia.", "b": "Rapid eye movements.", "c": "High frequency, low amplitude EEG.", "d": "Penile erections (in males)."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "topic": "REM sleep behaviour disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of +1?", "options": {"a": "50 Percent.", "b": "16 Percent.", "c": "25 Percent.", "d": "10 Percent."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "topic": "Normal curve areas above z", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of:", "options": {"a": "Taste.", "b": "Sight.", "c": "Hearing.", "d": "Touch."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "topic": "Somatosensory cortex and touch", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of:", "options": {"a": "Negative reinforcement.", "b": "Punishment.", "c": "Positive reinforcement.", "d": "Secondary reinforcement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both forms of punishment. In operant conditioning, Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) experiments showed that consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Two questions classify every consequence. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Here positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour: a scolding, a slap, extra homework. Negative punishment removes something pleasant: time-out from play, a fine (response cost), losing phone time. Both make the behaviour less likely, so both are punishment. By contrast, positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, money), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet, the seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up); both make the behaviour more likely. Skinner argued that punishment suppresses behaviour only temporarily, teaches nothing about what to do instead and can breed fear and aggression, so reinforcing a better behaviour usually works better. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him when he hits his sister (positive punishment) and also takes away his cricket bat for a day (negative punishment); both aim to reduce hitting. Option b is right because adding an aversive stimulus and removing a pleasant one are the two forms of punishment. Exam trap: negative reinforcement (option a) is the classic confusion; it removes something unpleasant and so increases behaviour, the reverse of punishment.", "topic": "Positive and negative punishment", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Counter conditioning is an exposure therapy technique that involves:", "options": {"a": "Learning that one negative event may be linked to another", "b": "Exposing the client to the feared stimulus", "c": "Learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening", "d": "Helping the client to see that their behaviour is counterproductive"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening. Counterconditioning is a behaviour therapy technique, based on classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) principles, in which a previously feared or aversive stimulus is repeatedly paired with a new response that is incompatible with fear, so that the stimulus comes to elicit the new response instead. The principle rests on reciprocal inhibition, articulated by Joseph Wolpe: since relaxation and anxiety cannot coexist, deliberately pairing the feared stimulus with a relaxed, calm state gradually weakens and replaces the fear response. Systematic desensitization is the classic application: the client is taught deep relaxation (following the progressive relaxation method of Edmund Jacobson) and then, while relaxed, is exposed to a hierarchy of increasingly anxiety-provoking versions of the feared stimulus, learning to remain calm rather than afraid. Through this process the person effectively relearns that the event or situation is no longer threatening, extinguishing the conditioned fear. This distinguishes counterconditioning from mere exposure, which simply presents the feared stimulus, and from cognitive insight-based approaches. Because counterconditioning replaces the fear response so that the client comes to experience the once-feared situation as no longer threatening, option c is correct.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called:", "options": {"a": "Proactive memories.", "b": "Reactive memories.", "c": "Explicit memories.", "d": "Implicit memories."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: memories that operate outside conscious awareness are called implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) defined implicit memory as an influence of past experience on behaviour, feeling or thought without conscious recollection. Explicit memory is the opposite: you deliberately and consciously retrieve something, as when you recall a list or recognise a face, so it is tested by recall and recognition. Larry Squire (1992) used the parallel terms declarative (explicit) and nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Implicit memory includes procedural skills (typing, cycling), priming (a word seen earlier is completed faster on a word-fragment test even if you do not remember seeing it), classically conditioned responses and habituation. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) could not consciously remember new events after hippocampal surgery in 1953, yet Brenda Milner (1962) showed that he improved day by day at mirror drawing while insisting he had never done the task. So explicit memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, while implicit memory relies on other systems such as the striatum and cerebellum. Example: Arjun types his email password perfectly every morning without thinking, but if asked to say the characters aloud he has to mime typing to recover them; his fingers hold an implicit memory. Option d is right because working outside conscious awareness is the defining feature of implicit memory. Exam trap: options a and b borrow from interference theory, where proactive interference (old learning disrupts new) and retroactive interference (new disrupts old) cause forgetting; 'proactive memories' and 'reactive memories' are not memory systems at all.", "topic": "Implicit versus explicit memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a", "options": {"a": "Bell curve", "b": "Scatterplot", "c": "Bimodal distribution", "d": "Skewed distribution"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a graphical representation of correlational data is a scatterplot, also called a scatter diagram. Correlation describes how two variables vary together. To draw a scatterplot, you put one variable on the X axis and the other on the Y axis, and each person becomes a single dot placed at their pair of scores. The pattern of dots then shows three things at a glance. Direction: dots rising from lower left to upper right show a positive correlation; dots falling from upper left to lower right show a negative one. Strength: the tighter the dots cluster around a straight line, the stronger the correlation; a shapeless cloud means r is near zero. Form: the dots reveal whether the relation is linear or curved, and whether there are outliers or uneven spread (heteroscedasticity). Francis Galton (1886) used such plots of parents' and children's heights to discover regression towards the mean, and Karl Pearson (1896) then formalised the correlation coefficient r. A line of best fit, the regression line, can be drawn through the dots to predict one variable from the other. Example: Aarav plots hours of sleep against exam marks for his 40 classmates. If the dots rise steadily, more sleep goes with higher marks, a positive correlation, though this does not prove that sleep causes better marks. Option b is right because a scatterplot is the graph designed to show the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Exam trap: the other three options are all shapes of a frequency distribution of a single variable. A bell curve, a bimodal curve and a skewed curve each show how scores on one variable are spread, not how two variables relate to each other.", "topic": "Scatterplot for correlational data", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which non-parametric test is equivalent to the independent samples t-test?", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Kruskal-Wallis test", "c": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test", "d": "Friedman test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is the Mann-Whitney U test. The independent samples t-test is a parametric procedure that compares the means of two unrelated groups, assuming interval data and normality. When those assumptions are violated, for example with ordinal data or non-normal distributions, the appropriate non-parametric alternative for comparing two independent groups is the Mann-Whitney U test, developed by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (and equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test). It ranks all scores across both groups and tests whether the ranks differ systematically, so it directly parallels the independent samples t-test without requiring normality. Hence option A is correct. The Kruskal-Wallis test (option B), named after William Kruskal and Wilson Allen Wallis, is the non-parametric counterpart of a one-way ANOVA and is used to compare three or more independent groups, not two. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C), from Frank Wilcoxon, is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired-samples (dependent) t-test, used when the two sets of scores are related, such as before-and-after measures on the same subjects. The Friedman test (option D) is the non-parametric equivalent of a repeated-measures ANOVA, for comparing three or more related conditions. Matching each non-parametric test to its parametric partner is a staple of statistics: two independent groups map to Mann-Whitney U.", "topic": "Rank tests and choosing the right test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "How many Rorschach cards are coloured?", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "5", "c": "3", "d": "4"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is 5. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach and published in 1921 in his work Psychodiagnostik, is a projective personality assessment consisting of ten standardized inkblot cards shown one at a time, with the examinee asked what each might be. Of the ten cards, five are achromatic (black and grey only), and five contain colour. Among the coloured cards, two (Cards II and III) combine black with red, and three (Cards VIII, IX, and X) are fully multicoloured. So the count of cards containing any colour is five. This distinction matters interpretively: in scoring systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner, responses to the chromatic cards, especially colour-based determinants, are linked to how a person processes and modulates emotion and affect, whereas responses to the achromatic cards and shading relate to other dimensions such as dysphoria and anxiety. Test-takers sometimes confuse this with the total of ten cards or with the three fully coloured cards, but the precise answer to how many cards are coloured is five, making option b correct.", "topic": "The Rorschach: ten cards and how they are given", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "The visible spectrum wavelength ranges from", "options": {"a": "380 to 760 nanometres", "b": "200 to 700 nanometres", "c": "400 to 1000 nanometres", "d": "100 to 1000 nanometres"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the visible spectrum, the band of electromagnetic energy the human eye can detect, runs from about 380 to 760 nanometres. A nanometre is one billionth of a metre. Light is only a thin slice of the electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes gamma rays, X-rays, microwaves and radio waves. Within the visible band, wavelength is experienced as hue: the shortest visible waves (about 380 to 490 nanometres) look violet and blue, the middle ones green and yellow, and the longest (about 620 to 760 nanometres) red. Just below the short end lies ultraviolet, which bees can see but we cannot; just beyond the long end lies infrared, which we feel as heat rather than see. Isaac Newton, in prism experiments he began in 1666, showed that white light is a mixture of these colours by spreading sunlight into a band and then recombining it. Textbooks round the limits differently, giving 380 or 400 at the short end and 700 or 760 at the long end, but every version keeps both ends between ultraviolet and infrared. Riya remembers it as 'roughly 400 to 700, VIBGYOR from short to long'. Option a is right because 380 to 760 nanometres is the standard textbook range for human vision. Exam trap: each wrong option contains a real stretch of visible light but extends it into invisible regions, so check both ends of the range, not just one.", "topic": "The visible spectrum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term schizophrenia was coined by", "options": {"a": "Eugen Bleuler", "b": "David Rosenhan", "c": "Thomas Szasz", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler. The Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (1857 to 1939) introduced the word in a 1908 lecture and set it out fully in his 1911 book Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. Before him, Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) had grouped several pictures under 'dementia praecox' (premature dementia), stressing early onset and a deteriorating course. Bleuler disagreed on two points: not every patient deteriorates, and onset is not always early. He built 'schizophrenia' from the Greek schizein (to split) and phren (mind). The split he meant is a splitting of psychic functions, so that thought, emotion and behaviour lose their normal connection; it does not mean a split or multiple personality. He also wrote of a 'group' of schizophrenias, an early form of today's spectrum idea. Bleuler's fundamental symptoms are remembered as the four As: loosening of associations, disturbed affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into a private inner world). He regarded hallucinations and delusions as accessory symptoms. Example: Rohan's relatives say he has a 'split personality' because of his schizophrenia. The clinician corrects them: Bleuler's split is between Rohan's thoughts and feelings, as when he smiles while describing his father's death. Option a is right because both the word and the idea of a group of schizophrenias come from Bleuler. Exam trap: Emil Kraepelin is the classic distractor because he described the illness first, as dementia praecox; Kraepelin named and classified it, and Bleuler renamed it. Also remember that 'split mind' never means dissociative identity disorder.", "topic": "Origin of the term schizophrenia", "fig": "Eugen Bleuler"}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is responsible for vision?", "options": {"a": "Parietal", "b": "Frontal", "c": "Temporal", "d": "Occipital"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the occipital lobe is responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, divided by the central sulcus and the lateral (Sylvian) fissure: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) along the calcarine sulcus. Signals travel from the retina through the optic nerve, the optic chiasm (where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross) and the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to V1, so each hemisphere sees the opposite visual field. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel shared the 1981 Nobel Prize for showing that V1 cells respond to edges and lines of particular orientations. Beyond V1, processing splits into two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal 'where' (or 'how') stream into the parietal lobe for location and movement. Example: a stroke in the right occipital lobe leaves a person blind to the left side of space in both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Option d is right because primary visual processing takes place in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: damage outside the occipital lobe can still disturb seeing, since temporal damage causes prosopagnosia (failure to recognise faces) and parietal damage causes neglect; these are higher visual functions, but vision itself begins in the occipital cortex.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "options": {"a": "Interference", "b": "Selective attention", "c": "Decay", "d": "Retrieval inhibition"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting. Forgetting is the loss of, or inability to retrieve, information that was once stored, and the main explanations are these. Decay theory, traced to Edward L. Thorndike's law of disuse, holds that memory traces fade with time if unused. Interference theory holds that other learning gets in the way: proactive interference when old learning disrupts new, and retroactive interference when new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, studied by Endel Tulving, holds that the memory is still there but the right cue is missing. Retrieval inhibition, or retrieval-induced forgetting, shown by Michael C. Anderson, Robert A. Bjork and Elizabeth Ligon Bjork (1994), holds that practising some items suppresses related ones. Freud's motivated forgetting (repression) and consolidation failure complete the usual list. Selective attention, studied by Donald Broadbent with his filter model (1958), is an encoding process: choosing which input to process and ignoring the rest. Information you never attended to was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Option b is right because selective attention operates before storage, not on stored memories. Exam trap: some texts list \"encoding failure\" as a reason we cannot remember, as when you cannot draw a coin you have handled thousands of times; that is a failure to store, and selective attention itself is not a theory of forgetting.", "topic": "Theories of forgetting", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "options": {"a": "Visuospatial disorientation", "b": "Visual blindness", "c": "Loss of coordination", "d": "Personality changes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most often causes loss of coordination (ataxia). The cerebellum, or little brain, sits at the back of the hindbrain behind the pons and holds more than half of all the brain's neurons. It does not start movements. It compares the movement the motor cortex intended with feedback from muscles, joints and the inner ear, and corrects errors so that movement is smooth, accurate and well timed. It also keeps balance and posture and helps us learn motor skills. Gordon Holmes (1917) mapped its signs in soldiers with gunshot wounds of the cerebellum in the First World War. The classic signs are ataxia (an unsteady, wide-based, drunken gait), intention tremor (shaking that grows as the hand nears a target), dysmetria (over- or under-shooting, seen on the finger-nose test), dysdiadochokinesia (clumsy rapid alternating movements), nystagmus and slurred, scanning speech. The signs appear on the same side of the body as the damage. Alcohol affects the cerebellum early, which is why police roadside tests ask drivers to walk a straight line. Example: after a stroke in his right cerebellum, Arjun reaches for a glass of water with his right hand, overshoots it, and his hand shakes more the closer it gets. Option c is right because coordinating, timing and correcting movement is the cerebellum's main job, so its damage shows up as clumsy, unsteady action rather than paralysis. Exam trap: resting tremor and slowness belong to the basal ganglia (Parkinson's disease), whereas the cerebellum gives intention tremor. Jeremy Schmahmann (1998) also described a cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome with subtle planning, spatial and mood changes, but the most likely and classic result is still loss of coordination.", "topic": "Cerebellar damage and ataxia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in:", "options": {"a": "Parietal lobe", "b": "Frontal lobe", "c": "Occipital lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the frontal lobe. The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex in front of the motor areas, handle planning, judgement, inhibition of impulses and foreseeing consequences, together called executive functions. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in an explosion in 1848. He survived and could walk, talk and remember, but his physician, John Harlow, reported that he became fitful, impulsive, profane, obstinate and unable to carry out plans; his friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Modern neuropsychology describes several frontal syndromes. Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition: impulsivity, tactlessness, poor social judgement and indifference to consequences. Dorsolateral damage produces a dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, rigidity and perseveration, and medial damage produces apathy. Amit's new impulsiveness, irresponsibility and lack of concern for consequences after an accident fit the disinhibited picture, a common result of head injury because the undersides of the frontal lobes bruise easily against the bony skull base. Option b is right because personality change with impulsivity and poor judgement is the hallmark of frontal damage. Exam trap: memory loss points to the temporal lobes; personality change and poor judgement point to the frontal lobes.", "topic": "Frontal lobe damage and personality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:", "options": {"a": "primary, secondary, tertiary", "b": "cognitive, emotional, physiological", "c": "id, ego, superego", "d": "cardinal, central, secondary"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is cardinal, central, and secondary. Gordon Allport, a pioneer of the trait approach to personality, proposed that traits, enduring dispositions that make a person's behaviour consistent across situations, could be organised into three hierarchical levels. Cardinal traits are rare, dominant traits that shape virtually every aspect of a person's life and behaviour, so pervasive that the person may become known for them (for example, a personality so generous it is called Christlike); most people do not have a cardinal trait. Central traits are the general characteristics that form the basic building blocks of personality, the handful of core qualities (such as honest, kind, or anxious) one might use to describe someone. Secondary traits are more situation-specific and peripheral, such as preferences or attitudes that appear only under particular circumstances. The distractors belong to other frameworks: id, ego, and superego (option c) are Sigmund Freud's structural components of the psyche, not traits; while primary/secondary/tertiary (option a) and cognitive/emotional/physiological (option b) are generic labels not used by Allport. Understanding Allport's hierarchy matters because he helped establish the trait perspective and the idiographic study of individual uniqueness, foundational to modern personality psychology and later trait models like the Big Five.", "topic": "Allport: traits as real structures", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Behaviour analysis is based upon the principles of:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive learning theories", "b": "Operant conditioning", "c": "Dream analysis", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Behaviour analysis, and its practical arm applied behaviour analysis (ABA), is the scientific study of behaviour grounded in the principles of operant conditioning as developed by Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) within the framework of radical behaviourism. Its central premise is that behaviour is a function of its consequences and environmental contingencies, and it focuses on observable, measurable behaviour rather than on inferred internal mental states. The core operant principles it applies include positive and negative reinforcement (which increase behaviour), positive and negative punishment (which decrease behaviour), extinction, stimulus control and discrimination, and shaping through successive approximations, all organised around the three-term contingency of antecedent, behaviour, and consequence (the A-B-C model). Behaviour analysts conduct functional analyses to identify the environmental variables maintaining a behaviour and then rearrange contingencies to change it. Applied behaviour analysis is widely used in treating autism spectrum disorder, developmental disabilities, and in education and organisational settings. Because behaviour analysis rests specifically on operant conditioning and deliberately sets aside cognitive constructs and psychoanalytic dream interpretation, options A and C do not apply, which rules out 'all of the above.' This item tests the operant-conditioning foundation of behaviour analysis.", "topic": "Origins, assumptions and functional analysis", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Where was the first psychology laboratory established?", "options": {"a": "Leipzig", "b": "Romania", "c": "London", "d": "California"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a, Leipzig. In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt set up the first formal laboratory for experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in Germany. It is treated as the birthplace of scientific psychology because, for the first time, mental processes such as sensation, perception, attention, feeling and reaction time were studied with controlled experiments in a setting devoted to that purpose. The laboratory trained many of the first professional psychologists, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell and Emil Kraepelin, who carried its methods to America and into psychiatry. Other early landmarks are often confused with it: Francis Galton opened an anthropometric laboratory in London in 1884 to measure people's physical and sensory abilities, an early step in mental testing; G. Stanley Hall founded the first American psychology laboratory at Johns Hopkins University in 1883; and Lewis Terman at Stanford University in California published the Stanford-Binet test in 1916. Option a is right because Leipzig is the city where Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory. The exam trap is option c: London did have an early laboratory, Galton's, but it came five years later and measured individual differences rather than founding experimental psychology.", "topic": "Leipzig: site of the first psychology laboratory", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "Choose the correct order of Freud's psychosexual stages of development:", "options": {"a": "Genital – Phallic – Latency – Anal – Oral", "b": "Anal – Latency – Phallic – Genital – Oral", "c": "Oral – Anal – Phallic – Latency – Genital", "d": "Anal – Oral – Phallic – Genital – Latency"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Freud's psychosexual stages run oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923), proposed that personality develops as the libido (sexual or life energy) becomes focused on different body areas, called erogenous zones, in a fixed order. In the oral stage (birth to about 1 year) pleasure centres on the mouth through sucking, biting and feeding, and the key conflict is weaning. In the anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) pleasure comes from holding and releasing faeces, and toilet training is the conflict. In the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) the genitals become the focus, and the child passes through the Oedipus complex (for girls, the Electra complex, Carl Jung's 1913 term), resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent and forming the superego. In the latency period (about 6 years to puberty) sexual energy is dormant and channelled into school, friendships and skills. The genital stage (puberty onwards) brings mature sexual interest directed at others. If the conflict at any stage is badly handled, part of the libido stays fixated there, leaving adult traits. For instance, Kabir, harshly toilet trained, may grow into a very orderly, stubborn and stingy adult, which Freud called an anal character. Option c is right because it is the only option that follows the developmental sequence from infancy (the mouth) to adolescence (mature sexuality). Exam trap: latency is often misplaced after genital or before phallic. Remember that latency is a quiet pause between the Oedipal storm of the phallic stage and the arrival of puberty; Freud called it a period rather than a true stage, since no new erogenous zone appears. A handy mnemonic is 'Old Age Pensioners Love Gardening'.", "topic": "Freud's psychosexual stages", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Comorbidity explains the notion that:", "options": {"a": "People suffering with the same psychopathology die at the same time", "b": "Sufferers exhibit a chronic fear of death", "c": "Depression always happens in winter", "d": "Differentially defined disorders can co-occur"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: comorbidity is the notion that differently defined disorders can occur together in the same person. In psychiatry, comorbidity means the presence of two or more distinct disorders at the same time, such as major depressive disorder with generalised anxiety disorder, or alcohol use disorder with post-traumatic stress disorder. In mental health it is the rule rather than the exception. The National Comorbidity Survey of Ronald Kessler and colleagues (1994) in the United States found that more than half of all lifetime disorders occurred in the roughly one person in seven who had a history of three or more disorders. Comorbidity matters for three reasons. It complicates diagnosis, because symptoms overlap. It worsens prognosis, since comorbid cases are usually more severe and harder to treat. And it raises questions about classification: if anxiety and depression so often travel together, perhaps they share causes, which is one reason for transdiagnostic treatments. Example: Meera is diagnosed with panic disorder and, a year later, also meets the criteria for major depression; her clinician has to plan treatment for both, not just one. Option d is right because the co-occurrence of separately defined disorders in one individual is exactly what comorbidity means. Exam trap: 'morbidity' sounds like 'mortality', so examiners offer options about dying or death; comorbidity is about illnesses co-existing in a living person, and it is also different from prevalence, which is how common a disorder is in a population.", "topic": "Comorbidity", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["repeats-2"] = {"id": "repeats-2", "title": "Repeats Drill 2", "fullTitle": "Repeats Drill 2: asked in 2 papers (part 1)", "sub": "62 real past-paper Qs, each asked in 2 different papers · all 9 subjects · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "______ is the process which involves detecting, translating, and transmitting from the external environment to the brain.", "options": {"a": "adaptation", "b": "sensation", "c": "selective attention", "d": "perception"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: sensation is the process of detecting, translating and transmitting energy from the external environment to the brain. Sensation has three steps. First, specialised receptor cells detect a physical stimulus: light in the eye, sound waves in the ear, chemicals on the tongue and in the nose, pressure and temperature on the skin. Second, the receptors translate that energy into nerve impulses, a step called transduction. Third, sensory nerves transmit the impulses to the brain, where most senses are relayed through the thalamus to the cortex. The scientific study of this link between physical energy and experience is psychophysics, founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), building on Ernst Heinrich Weber's work on the just noticeable difference. Example: when you step into sunlight, light strikes the rods and cones of your retina, which pass signals through the bipolar cells to the ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve; recognising that the shape ahead is a friend's face is the next stage, perception. Option b is right because detecting, transducing and transmitting raw stimulus energy is the textbook definition of sensation. Exam trap: sensation is the raw input from receptors to brain, while perception is the organising and interpreting of that input; the words \"to the brain\" mark sensation. Key note: the coaching book prints a here, but its own explanation describes sensation, so b is used.", "topic": "Sensation: detection, transduction, transmission", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A large body of research indicates that violence and aggression is learned through observation. Reason (R): Learning through observation takes place due to exposure to similar situation and experienced reinforcement.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "Both (A) and (R) are false"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false. Assertion: a large body of research shows that aggression is learned through observation. True. In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hammer an inflatable doll later copied the model's exact acts and words far more than children who saw a calm model or none, without any reward. Their 1963 follow-up found filmed and cartoon models worked too, and decades of media-violence research point the same way. Reason: observational learning happens because of exposure to the same situation and experienced reinforcement. False. Observational learning needs neither. The observer learns by watching someone else, often in a situation they have never been in, and need not be reinforced. Bandura's four processes are attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation; the first two govern learning (acquisition) and the last two govern performance. In Bandura's 1965 study, children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet when offered a reward for showing what the model had done, the differences between the groups all but disappeared: they had learned without reinforcement. Seeing a model rewarded or punished (vicarious reinforcement) changes whether an act is performed, not whether it is learned. Example: Kabir has never been in a fight, but after watching a film hero slap a rival he tries the same move on a classmate. Option c is right because A is well supported while R describes direct, operant learning rather than observational learning. Exam trap: R uses the language of reinforcement, which fits the earlier imitation theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941), who held that imitation must itself be reinforced; Bandura showed it need not be.", "topic": "Observational learning of aggression", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "The contention that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience attests to which of the following hypotheses?", "options": {"a": "Universality hypothesis of facial expressions", "b": "Emotional specificity hypothesis of ANS activity", "c": "Emotional generality hypothesis of ANS activity", "d": "Facial feedback hypothesis"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the facial feedback hypothesis. This idea says the face does not just display an emotion; feedback from the facial muscles to the brain also helps create or strengthen the feeling. Charles Darwin and William James hinted at it in the nineteenth century, and Silvan Tomkins and Carroll Izard developed it in the 1960s and 1970s. In a well-known 1988 study, Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper had people hold a pen between their teeth, which forces a smile, or between their lips, which blocks one; the 'smilers' rated cartoons as funnier. Paul Ekman's lab similarly found that people told to move particular facial muscles, without being told which emotion they were posing, showed autonomic changes and reported a trace of that emotion. Everyday example: forcing a smile at a tiring party can make you feel slightly better. Later large replications found the pen effect weaker than first reported, so the effect is real but modest. Option d is right because the stem says our expressions influence our emotional experience, which is exactly the direction this hypothesis proposes. Exam trap: students pick universality because it also concerns faces, but universality is about expressions being produced and recognised the same way across cultures, not about faces changing feelings.", "topic": "Facial feedback hypothesis of emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When a light paired with a bell comes to elicit a conditioned response (CR) such as salivation, the phenomenon is called:", "options": {"a": "Anomalous conditioning.", "b": "Distinctive conditioning.", "c": "Counterconditioning.", "d": "Higher-order conditioning."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: when a light paired with a bell comes to elicit salivation, the phenomenon is higher-order conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, a neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly followed by an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes an unconditioned response (salivation). After pairing, the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation, now a conditioned response (CR). Higher-order conditioning adds a further layer. Once the bell is a strong CS, a new neutral stimulus, a light, is paired with the bell, and no food is given. The light then comes to make the dog salivate as well. The bell has acted as if it were the unconditioned stimulus; this is second-order conditioning, and a third stimulus paired with the light would give third-order conditioning. Each layer is weaker, and because the bell is now presented without food it slowly extinguishes, so higher-order responses fade. The idea explains how human fears and likings spread far beyond any original experience. Example: Arjun once failed a test and felt sick with dread, so the word 'exam' became a CS for anxiety. Later the school's long bell, which always rang just before the teacher announced an exam, came to make his heart race too, although the bell itself was never linked to failure. Option d is right because the light gains its power from a previously conditioned stimulus, not from food. Exam trap: do not confuse it with sensory preconditioning (Wilfred Brogden, 1939), where the two neutral stimuli are paired first and only afterwards is one of them conditioned; in higher-order conditioning the first CS is trained before the new stimulus is added.", "topic": "Higher-order conditioning", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "The famous book 'Principles of psychology' was authored by ------.", "options": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt", "b": "E B Titchener", "c": "William James", "d": "J B Watson"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: The Principles of Psychology was written by William James (1890). William James (1842 to 1910) trained in medicine at Harvard and taught physiology, philosophy and psychology there. He worked on the Principles for about twelve years and published it in two volumes in 1890, followed by a shorter version, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892). In the book he described consciousness as a continuous, ever-changing stream of consciousness rather than a set of elements; called habit 'the enormous fly-wheel of society'; split the self into the 'I' (the self as knower) and the 'me' (the self as known, with material, social and spiritual parts); and set out his theory of emotion, proposed independently by Carl Lange (1885), that we feel afraid because we tremble and run, not the other way round. He defined psychology as 'the science of mental life'. The Principles made James the father of American psychology and the inspiration of functionalism, which asked what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Option c is right because The Principles of Psychology is William James's book. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (a) wrote a book with a look-alike title, Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), the founding text of experimental psychology, so a hurried reader picks a.", "topic": "William James's Principles", "fig": "William James"}, {"q": "Internal standards used to judge stimuli are referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Adaptation level.", "b": "Intuition.", "c": "Frames of reference.", "d": "Context."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: internal standards used to judge stimuli are called frames of reference. We never judge a stimulus in absolute terms; we rate it against a standard we carry inside, built up from past experience and from the surroundings, and that standard is the frame of reference. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) showed one forming in the autokinetic experiment: a still point of light in a dark room seems to move, each person judging alone settled on a personal range, groups then converged on a shared norm, and members kept using that norm when later tested alone. Harry Helson (1947, 1964) made the idea measurable in adaptation-level theory; his 1948 paper is titled 'Adaptation-level as a basis for a quantitative theory of frames of reference'. The adaptation level is the neutral point of one such frame, pooled from the focal stimulus, the background stimuli around it and residual stimuli (traces of past experience); a stimulus above it feels heavy, bright or warm, one below it light, dim or cool. A classic demonstration: put one hand in hot water and the other in cold, then both in lukewarm water; the same water feels cool to the first hand and warm to the second. Example: Kabir lifts a 20 kg sack of rice in his uncle's shop, then picks up a 5 kg bag, which feels surprisingly light, because his frame of reference for weight has moved up. Option c is right because 'frames of reference' is the general name for the internal standards, and adaptation level is Helson's name for the neutral point inside one. Exam trap: adaptation level is the planted near miss. Key note: WBJEEB's final answer keys mark c both times this question was set, JEMAS(PG) 2023 and JEMAS(PG) 2024. This site keyed a (adaptation level) before the official keys were found; a is the narrower Helson term, so c is the answer to give.", "topic": "Frames of reference (Sherif, Helson)", "fig": "Harry Helson"}, {"q": "Basic speech sounds are called:", "options": {"a": "Morphemes.", "b": "Syllables.", "c": "Phonemes.", "d": "Syntax."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the basic speech sounds of a language are called phonemes. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, although it carries no meaning of its own. Swap /p/ for /b/ and 'pat' becomes 'bat'; swap /k/ for /h/ and 'cat' becomes 'hat'. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and each language uses its own set, so two sounds that are separate phonemes in one language may be heard as one sound in another. Hindi, for instance, separates aspirated /kh/ from plain /k/, so 'kaana' (one-eyed) and 'khaana' (food) are different words, while English speakers barely notice the puff of air. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units of meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness) and 'cats' has two (cat and the plural -s). Morphemes build words, words build sentences by the rules of syntax, and the meaning of the whole is studied by semantics. Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984) found that English-learning infants could tell apart Hindi dental and retroflex 't' sounds at 6 to 8 months but had mostly lost this by 10 to 12 months, as their ears tuned to the phonemes of their own language. Example: Meera's cousin from London cannot hear the difference between 'kaana' and 'khaana', while Meera hears two quite separate words. Option c is right because the stem asks for the basic units of speech sound, which is the definition of a phoneme. Exam trap: morpheme (option a) is the classic near miss; the morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning, the phoneme the smallest unit of sound.", "topic": "Phonemes and morphemes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The fact that a nearer object may obscure a more distant object from the view is due to:", "options": {"a": "Figure and ground", "b": "Interposition", "c": "Proximity", "d": "Closure"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: interposition, also called overlap or occlusion. Interposition is a monocular depth cue: when one object partly covers another, we see the covering object as nearer. It is one of the pictorial cues that painters have used for centuries, along with linear perspective, relative size, texture gradient, aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field. Because it needs only one eye, it works in photographs and paintings. It tells us only relative order (which object is in front), not absolute distance. Everyday example: at a cricket match, a spectator's head blocks part of the scoreboard, so you instantly know the head is closer without judging any distance in metres. In a class photo, the students whose bodies cut off others look nearer. Hermann von Helmholtz treated such cues as the basis of unconscious inference about depth, whereas James J. Gibson argued that the information lies directly in the light reaching the eye. Binocular cues, by contrast, need two eyes: retinal disparity and convergence. Option b is right because the stem describes a nearer object hiding part of a farther one, which is the exact definition of interposition. Exam trap: students confuse it with figure and ground or closure because these are also perceptual terms, but those are Gestalt principles of organising shapes, not cues for depth.", "topic": "Interposition as a depth cue", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Working memory can hold a large amount of information even though it can retain only seven to nine items. Reason (R): Items in working memory contain several separate bits of information grouped together called chunks.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and chunking (R) is exactly why a store limited to about seven items can still hold a large amount of information (A). George Miller (1956), in \"The magical number seven, plus or minus two\", showed that immediate memory holds about 7 units, give or take 2, so 5 to 9. Judge A: the limit is set in units, not in raw information. Seven words carry far more information than seven single digits, yet both fill the span, so a store of seven to nine items can hold a lot. A is true. Judge R: a chunk is a meaningful unit formed by grouping separate bits with the help of knowledge already in long-term memory, such as a word, a date or an acronym. William Chase and Herbert Simon (1973) found that chess masters recall whole board positions at a glance because they see familiar patterns of pieces as single chunks. R is true. Does R explain A? Yes: capacity is counted in chunks, and each chunk packs several bits, so the total information held can be large even though the number of items is small. Example: Meera cannot repeat the 12 letters B J P I S R O R B I U N heard once, but regrouped as BJP, ISRO, RBI and UN they become four easy chunks. Option a is right because R gives the very mechanism that makes A possible. Exam trap: \"seven items\" and \"large amount of information\" seem to clash, so students mark A false; the clash disappears once items are understood as chunks. Note too that Miller wrote of short-term memory; the working memory model came later (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974), and Nelson Cowan (2001) puts the limit nearer 4 chunks.", "topic": "Chunking and memory span", "fig": "George Miller"}, {"q": "Fluid intelligence tends to _____ with age while crystallized intelligence tends to _____ with age.", "options": {"a": "increase, decrease", "b": "increase, stay the same", "c": "decrease, increase", "d": "decrease, stay the same"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: fluid intelligence tends to decrease with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to increase. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with his student John Horn (1966), split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on learned knowledge: seeing patterns, abstract reasoning, working out unfamiliar tasks. It is measured by tests such as John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It relies heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in early adulthood (around the early twenties) and then declines gradually. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. It keeps rising into late middle age and holds up well into the sixties. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and what is learned crystallises as Gc. Longitudinal work such as K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) broadly supports the pattern, though most declines come later and are smaller than early cross-sectional studies suggested. For example, Meera's 68-year-old grandfather beats her easily at crosswords and knows far more history, but she solves a new puzzle app or a matrix-reasoning test much faster. Option c is right because Gf falls from early adulthood while Gc continues to grow with experience. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse; remember 'fluid flows away, crystals build up'. Option d is tempting because Gc does level off and finally dips in very old age, but across most of adulthood it rises.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "The concept of primary mental abilities was given by:", "options": {"a": "Spearman", "b": "Thurstone", "c": "Guilford", "d": "Piaget"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the concept of primary mental abilities was given by Louis Leon Thurstone (1938). Earlier, Charles Spearman (1904) had found that scores on different mental tests all correlate positively, and explained this by one general factor, g, plus specific factors, s. Thurstone, at the University of Chicago, gave a large battery of tests to students and analysed the scores with his own method of multiple factor analysis. Instead of one dominant g, he found about seven fairly separate group factors, which he called primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning (induction). He argued that a person is better described by a profile across these abilities than by a single IQ. Example: Kabir may be strong in number and space but weak in word fluency, while his sister Meera shows the opposite profile, though both earn the same overall IQ. Later work found that the seven abilities themselves correlate, so g returned at the top of hierarchical models. Option b is right because the term primary mental abilities comes from Thurstone's 1938 monograph of that name. Exam trap: Spearman also used factor analysis, so a student who remembers only 'factor theory' may pick him; Spearman's answer was g and s, Thurstone's was several primary abilities.", "topic": "Thurstone's primary mental abilities", "fig": "Louis Leon Thurstone"}, {"q": "ERG theory of motivation was given by:", "options": {"a": "Alderfer.", "b": "Maslow.", "c": "Herzberg.", "d": "McClelland."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: ERG theory of motivation was given by Clayton Alderfer (1969). Clayton Alderfer, an American organisational psychologist, reworked Abraham Maslow's (1943) five-level hierarchy and tested it on employees in organisations. He reduced the five needs to three. Existence needs are material and physical (food, water, pay, safe working conditions), matching Abraham Maslow's physiological and safety needs. Relatedness needs are for meaningful relationships with family, friends, colleagues and supervisors, matching belongingness and the social part of esteem. Growth needs are for personal development and full use of one's abilities, matching self-esteem and self-actualisation. ERG theory differs from Abraham Maslow's in three ways. First, the needs are not a rigid ladder, and several can be active at once. Second, a lower need does not have to be satisfied before a higher one can motivate. Third, it adds frustration-regression: when a higher need is blocked, the person falls back and seeks more of a lower need, whereas Abraham Maslow described only satisfaction-progression, moving up once a need is met. Example: Arjun is passed over for promotion and for the chance to lead a project (growth frustrated); instead of pushing for development, he starts demanding a pay rise and spending more time chatting with colleagues (existence and relatedness), which is frustration-regression. Option a is right because Clayton Alderfer proposed the existence, relatedness and growth model. Exam trap: all four names are need theorists of motivation, and David McClelland (1961) also had three needs (achievement, affiliation and power), so 'three needs' alone can pull students away from Clayton Alderfer; Frederick Herzberg (1959) had two factors and Abraham Maslow five levels.", "topic": "Alderfer's ERG theory", "fig": "Clayton Alderfer"}, {"q": "Among the following schedules of reinforcement, which typically produces the highest rate of response and is most resistant to extinction?", "options": {"a": "Fixed Ratio", "b": "Variable Ratio", "c": "Fixed Interval", "d": "Variable Interval"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is variable ratio, because among the schedules of reinforcement the variable ratio schedule produces the highest rate of responding and the greatest resistance to extinction. Understand why this schedule is so powerful within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning framework. A variable ratio schedule reinforces a response after an unpredictable number of responses that varies around some average; for instance, on a VR-10 schedule reinforcement comes on average every tenth response, but the actual number required fluctuates unpredictably from one reinforcement to the next. Two properties combine to make this schedule uniquely potent. First, because it is a ratio schedule, reinforcement depends directly on how many responses the organism makes, so the faster the organism responds, the more reinforcement it earns, which drives a very high, sustained rate of responding. Second, because the ratio is variable and unpredictable, the organism can never know which particular response will be the one that pays off, so the very next response might be rewarded, and this uncertainty keeps the organism responding vigorously and continuously without the post-reinforcement pauses seen in fixed schedules. This same unpredictability makes variable ratio schedules extraordinarily resistant to extinction: when reinforcement stops, the organism cannot easily tell the difference between an unusually long unrewarded run (which was normal during training) and actual extinction, so it keeps responding for a long time. The prototypical real-world example is gambling on a slot machine, which pays off on a variable ratio schedule and hooks players into persistent, rapid play. So for the highest and most extinction-resistant responding, the answer is variable ratio.", "topic": "Schedules and other operant effects", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the notion of the confirmatory bias?", "options": {"a": "Clinicians ignore information that does not support their hypothesis", "b": "Clinicians ignore information that does not support their stereotypes.", "c": "Clinicians interpret ambiguous information as supporting their hypothesis.", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is all of the above. Confirmatory bias (confirmation bias) is the pervasive tendency to seek, interpret, favour, and recall information in a way that confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, hypotheses, or expectations, while giving disproportionately little attention to information that contradicts them. The concept was demonstrated experimentally by Peter Wason, whose selection task and 2-4-6 rule-discovery task showed that people tend to test hypotheses by looking for confirming rather than disconfirming evidence. In clinical assessment and diagnosis, confirmation bias is a serious source of error: a clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may then ignore or discount symptoms and information that do not fit that hypothesis (option A), may disregard evidence that contradicts a stereotype about a patient group (option B), and may interpret genuinely ambiguous or neutral information as though it supports the favoured hypothesis (option C). All three describe real manifestations of the same underlying bias, so the inclusive answer is correct. This bias contributes to premature closure and diagnostic overshadowing, which is why clinicians are trained to actively consider alternative hypotheses and seek disconfirming evidence, and why structured assessment reduces error. This item tests recognition that confirmation bias operates through several complementary mechanisms in clinical judgment.", "topic": "Clinical judgement and its errors", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is test-retest reliability", "options": {"a": "The test is measuring what it claims to be measuring", "b": "The test will produce consistent results", "c": "The client will improve performance second time round", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is that the test will produce consistent results. Reliability refers to the consistency, stability, or repeatability of measurement, and test-retest reliability is one specific form of it. Test-retest reliability is assessed by administering the same test to the same group of people on two separate occasions and then correlating the two sets of scores. If the test is reliable in this sense, individuals should obtain similar scores across the two administrations, yielding a high correlation (the test-retest reliability coefficient, or coefficient of stability). In other words, a reliable test produces consistent, reproducible results over time. This is especially important for measures of stable traits such as intelligence or personality, which should not fluctuate substantially over short intervals. A key complication is the practice or carryover effect: if respondents remember items or improve simply through repeated exposure, the second scores may rise, artificially inflating or distorting the reliability estimate, so an appropriate time interval must be chosen. Note that option A actually defines validity (measuring what it claims to measure), and option C describes a practice effect, which is a threat to, not a definition of, test-retest reliability. The broader concept of reliability is central to classical test theory. This item tests the definition of test-retest reliability as consistency of scores across time.", "topic": "Stability and equivalence: test-retest and parallel forms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Projective tests claim to reveal information about:", "options": {"a": "intellectual attainment", "b": "unconscious processes", "c": "parenting style", "d": "career aptitude"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is unconscious processes. Projective tests are personality assessment tools built on the projective hypothesis: the idea that when a person is presented with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they will project their own inner needs, feelings, conflicts, motives, and unconscious processes onto it, so their interpretations reveal aspects of their hidden psychological makeup. This approach is rooted in psychoanalytic theory, tracing to Sigmund Freud's emphasis on the unconscious. Classic examples include the Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by Hermann Rorschach, in which respondents describe what they see in inkblots, and the Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, in which respondents tell stories about ambiguous pictures. Because the stimuli are ambiguous, there are no right or wrong answers, and the assumption is that responses spring from unconscious material the person cannot or would not consciously report. The distractors point to other assessment domains: intellectual attainment (option a) is measured by intelligence and achievement tests; parenting style (option c) by specific scales or observation; and career aptitude (option d) by vocational interest and aptitude inventories. Understanding what projective tests claim to reveal matters because it clarifies their theoretical purpose, to access unconscious dynamics, while also inviting critical evaluation, since projective tests have been criticised for low reliability and validity compared with objective measures.", "topic": "The projective hypothesis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The MMPI measures which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Mood", "b": "Physical concerns", "c": "Social attitudes", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is all of the above. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is one of the most widely used and researched objective personality and psychopathology assessment instruments. It was developed in the late 1930s and published in 1943 by Starke Hathaway, a psychologist, and J. Charnley McKinley, a psychiatrist, at the University of Minnesota. It is a broad-band, empirically keyed self-report inventory consisting of hundreds of true/false items. The original MMPI contains ten clinical scales that assess a wide range of psychological domains, and these scales span exactly the areas listed: mood and emotional disturbance (for example the Depression and Hypomania scales), physical or somatic concerns (the Hypochondriasis and Hysteria scales, which tap bodily preoccupation), and social attitudes and interpersonal orientation (the Social Introversion scale, plus scales tapping social deviance). It also includes validity scales (such as the L, F, and K scales) to detect defensiveness, exaggeration, or careless responding. Because the MMPI casts a wide net across mood, somatic, and social-attitudinal content, all three options are genuinely assessed, making the inclusive answer correct. The instrument was later revised as the MMPI-2 (1989) and MMPI-2-RF, and the current version is the MMPI-3 (2020). This item tests broad awareness of the MMPI's comprehensive, multi-domain coverage of personality and psychopathology.", "topic": "The MMPI and its validity scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to Inter-rater reliability?", "options": {"a": "The degree to which two tests measure the same construct.", "b": "The degree to which a clinician can predict future behaviour.", "c": "The degree to which two clinicians will agree on interpretation or scoring of a test.", "d": "The degree to which the items in the test relate to each other"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is the degree to which two clinicians will agree on the interpretation or scoring of a test. Reliability refers to the consistency or repeatability of measurement, and it comes in several forms. Inter-rater reliability (also called inter-observer or inter-scorer reliability) specifically concerns the degree of agreement between two or more independent raters, observers, or clinicians when they score, rate, or interpret the same responses, behaviour, or test. High inter-rater reliability means different clinicians reach the same judgments, indicating that the scoring is objective and not idiosyncratic to a particular rater. It is especially important for subjectively scored instruments such as projective tests, behavioural observations, and structured clinical interviews, where scorer judgment plays a large role. It is commonly quantified using statistics such as Cohen's kappa, developed by Jacob Cohen, which corrects for chance agreement, or the intraclass correlation coefficient. The distractors describe other psychometric properties: option A refers to construct or convergent validity, option B to predictive (criterion) validity, and option D to internal consistency reliability, which is measured by Cronbach's alpha and concerns how well the items hang together. This item tests the ability to distinguish inter-rater reliability from other reliability and validity concepts.", "topic": "Agreement between scorers", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is Rorschach's projective test designed to measure?", "options": {"a": "Unconscious intentions.", "b": "Dreams", "c": "Conscious desires.", "d": "Brain size"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is unconscious intentions. The Rorschach Inkblot Test was created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921. It is the archetypal projective test, a category of personality assessment based on the projective hypothesis, which holds that when a person is presented with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they will project their own inner needs, conflicts, feelings, and unconscious processes onto it in the way they interpret it. The Rorschach consists of ten symmetrical inkblot cards; the examinee is asked what each blot might be, and responses are scored on dimensions such as location (which part of the blot was used), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), and content, using systems such as John Exner's Comprehensive System. The underlying assumption, rooted in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory of the unconscious, is that because there is no correct answer, the person's interpretations reveal hidden aspects of personality, unconscious motives, and emotional functioning that they could not or would not report directly on a self-report questionnaire. The distractors are wrong because dreams belong to psychodynamic dream analysis, conscious desires contradict the projective aim of accessing unconscious material, and brain size is a physical measure. Although the Rorschach's reliability and validity are debated, it remains standard exam content. This item tests the projective purpose of the Rorschach in accessing unconscious material.", "topic": "The Rorschach: ten cards and how they are given", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "_____ is a sub process of _____:", "options": {"a": "Psychological testing; Observation.", "b": "Psychological testing; Interviewing.", "c": "Interviewing; Psychological assessment.", "d": "Psychological assessment; Observation."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: interviewing is a sub-process of psychological assessment. Psychological assessment is the broad process of collecting and combining information from many sources to answer a referral question about a person: Is this a learning disability? How severe is the depression? Which treatment suits? Its sources include the clinical interview, behavioural observation, psychological tests, the case history, school or medical records and reports from family. Psychological testing is narrower. Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour; it yields a score. Assessment, as Gary Groth-Marnat stresses in his Handbook of Psychological Assessment, is the clinician's work of integrating test scores with everything else into a reasoned conclusion and recommendations. So testing, interviewing and observation are all sub-processes, or tools, within assessment; none of them contains the others. Example: Kabir, 9, is referred for falling marks. The psychologist interviews his parents and teacher, talks with and observes Kabir, gives an intelligence test and a reading test, reads his school reports, and then writes a report that ties it all together. The whole sequence is assessment; the interview is one step in it. Option c is right because it is the only option that puts a component (interviewing) inside the umbrella process (psychological assessment). Exam trap: testing and assessment are often used loosely as synonyms, so options that put testing above interviewing or observation look plausible; assessment is always the umbrella, and the other three are its parts. Key note: JEPAS(PG) 2020 asks the same item with the same options. Neither paper has an official key, and the JEPAS 2020 mock on this site is keyed a, after Lee Cronbach's view of a test as a systematic way of observing behaviour. c is the reading modern texts back (Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik, Gary Groth-Marnat: interviewing, observation and testing are all tools inside assessment), so it is used here.", "topic": "Assessment, testing and interviewing", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A salesperson knocks on your door and requests you to buy the latest technology refrigerator of his \"internationally famed\" organization. When you turn him down, he requests you to buy a portable ice box. You find yourself obliged to comply. He has used, to near success:", "options": {"a": "Foot-in-the-door technique.", "b": "Door-in-the-face technique.", "c": "Low ball technique.", "d": "Reciprocity."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: asking first for an expensive refrigerator, which you refuse, and then for a cheap ice box, which you feel obliged to buy, is the door-in-the-face technique. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1975) demonstrated it with students. Asked to counsel juvenile delinquents two hours a week for two years, every student refused. Asked next to take the delinquents on a single two-hour trip to the zoo, about 50% agreed, against under 17% of students asked only the small favour. The large request is meant to be refused; it makes the second, real request look like a concession. Because of the reciprocity norm (we repay favours and concessions), the target feels pressure to concede in turn, and by contrast the second request also seems smaller than it is. Guilt about the first refusal may add to the pull. The technique works best when the same person makes both requests, one soon after the other, and the first is not absurdly large. In the stem, the salesman's real goal was always the ice box. By retreating from the refrigerator he seemed to give something up, and you felt you owed him a yes. Option b is right because the order is a large refused request followed by the smaller target request. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door uses the reverse order (small then large), so check which request came first. Key note: the door-in-the-face works through the reciprocity norm (a concession is returned with a concession), so d names the mechanism, not the technique; b stands.", "topic": "Door-in-the-face technique", "fig": "Robert Cialdini"}, {"q": "______ technique involves presenting target people with small requests and then following up with larger requests, the one that is desired all along.", "options": {"a": "Low ball.", "b": "Foot in the door.", "c": "Door in the face.", "d": "That's not all."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: making a small request first and then following it with the larger request wanted all along is the foot-in-the-door technique. (JEMAS(PG) 2022 garbles this option as 'Put in the door'; it means foot in the door.) Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966) showed it with Californian homeowners. Asked cold to put a large, ugly 'Drive Carefully' sign on their lawns, about 17% agreed. But about 76% agreed if, two weeks earlier, they had accepted a small 'Be a safe driver' sign. Two explanations are offered: consistency (having said yes once, refusing now feels inconsistent) and self-perception (watching yourself agree, you infer that you are the kind of person who supports such causes). The first request must be small enough that nearly everyone accepts it. Its relatives work differently. Door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) reverses the order. Low-ball (Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins agreement to a good deal and then worsens the terms. That's-not-all (Jerry Burger, 1986) sweetens the offer before the person has answered. Example: a volunteer asks Riya to sign a petition for a cleaner lake; a week later he asks her to give up a Sunday to clear rubbish from it, and she agrees. Option b is right because small-then-large is the defining sequence of the foot-in-the-door. Exam trap: door-in-the-face is the mirror image (large, refused, then small), and examiners swap the two; fix the order of the requests before you answer.", "topic": "Foot-in-the-door technique", "fig": "Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser"}, {"q": "Which term refers to the tendency to attribute one's successes to internal factors and failures to external factors?", "options": {"a": "Fundamental attribution error", "b": "Self-serving bias", "c": "Actor-observer bias", "d": "Attributional bias"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: self-serving bias. The self-serving bias is the tendency to take credit for our successes by attributing them to internal causes (ability, effort) while blaming our failures on external causes (bad luck, an unfair examiner, a noisy hall). A student who tops a test says \"I worked hard\", but after failing says \"the paper was out of syllabus\". The usual explanation is motivational: the bias protects and enhances self-esteem, and it also helps us present ourselves well to others. It is one of several attributional biases studied in social psychology, along with the fundamental attribution error and the actor-observer bias. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly the success-internal, failure-external pattern that defines the self-serving bias. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (named by Lee Ross in 1977) is about how we explain other people's behaviour, overestimating their dispositions and underestimating the situation; the self-serving bias is about explaining our own outcomes and depends on whether the outcome was good or bad. If the stem mentions success versus failure, think self-serving bias; if it mentions judging others' behaviour as personality-driven, think fundamental attribution error.", "topic": "Self-serving bias in attribution", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Adler, b. Bandura, c. Jung, d. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "options": {"a": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4", "c": "a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1", "d": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (a-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (b-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (c-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (d-1). Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), founder of individual psychology, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal, never fully real, that pulls a person's behaviour forward, such as \"I must be the best\". Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) described reciprocal determinism (1978), later called triadic reciprocal causation (1986): person factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) treated libido as general psychic energy governed by principles borrowed from physics. By the principle of equivalence, energy withdrawn from one interest reappears in another of equal value; by the principle of entropy, energy flows toward a balance that is never reached. Karen Horney (The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, 1937) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives the strategies of moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Kabir retires from cricket and pours the same passion into coaching, Jung would call it the principle of equivalence. Option c is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs are correct. Exam trap: option d gets Adler and Horney right but swaps Bandura and Jung; \"reciprocal\" and \"equivalence\" both sound like a balance of forces, so students trade them. Bandura is the social-cognitive theorist of three interacting factors; Jung is the energy theorist.", "topic": "Personality theorists and concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Moving towards people, moving away from people, moving against people\" are constituents of whose classification of pattern of needs:", "options": {"a": "Dollard-Miller.", "b": "Klein.", "c": "Horney.", "d": "Fromm."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: moving towards, against and away from people are Karen Horney's three neurotic trends. Karen Horney (1937), in The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, argued that personality is shaped mainly by social relationships, not sexual instincts. A child whose parents show indifference (no genuine warmth, broken promises, favouritism, ridicule) feels basic hostility, represses it out of helplessness or fear, and is left with basic anxiety: a pervasive feeling of being lonely and helpless in a hostile world. To cope, the child adopts strategies that harden into ten neurotic needs, such as needs for affection, a dominant partner, power, prestige, self-sufficiency and perfection. In Our Inner Conflicts (1945) she grouped these needs into three trends. The compliant type moves towards people, seeking love and approval; the aggressive type moves against people, seeking power and admiration; the detached type moves away from people, seeking independence and distance. A healthy person uses all three flexibly; a neurotic person is locked into one, and the clash among them is the core of neurosis. For example, Arjun agrees with everyone and panics if a friend seems annoyed (towards), while his brother Kabir turns every conversation into a contest (against). Option c is right because the stem quotes Horney's own triad. Exam trap: Erich Fromm also listed social strategies (mechanisms of escape in 1941, character orientations in 1947), and both were neo-Freudians of the cultural school, so d is the near miss; but the towards, against and away wording is Horney's alone.", "topic": "Horney's neurotic trends", "fig": "Karen Horney"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a mature defense mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Altruism", "b": "Intellectualisation", "c": "Repression", "d": "Rationalisation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "Defence mechanisms are unconscious ego strategies that reduce anxiety arising from conflict between impulses, conscience and reality. Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea, and Anna Freud (1936) catalogued the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. George E. Vaillant (1977), drawing on the Grant Study, a decades-long follow-up of Harvard men reported in Adaptation to Life, ranked defences in four levels of maturity. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Level II, immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out. Level III, neurotic: intellectualisation, repression, displacement, reaction formation, dissociation. Level IV, mature: altruism, sublimation, suppression, anticipation and humour. Men who relied on mature defences fared better in later health, work and relationships. Mature defences keep the feeling in awareness and turn it to adaptive use. Altruism is gaining real satisfaction from constructive service to others: Meera, whose brother died by suicide, volunteers at a crisis helpline. Separate it from reaction formation, where a hidden hostile wish is replaced by exaggerated, rigid kindness. Two pairs drive variant questions: suppression (a conscious choice to postpone a worry, mature) versus repression (unconscious exclusion from awareness, neurotic), and sublimation (an impulse redirected into a valued outlet, such as aggression into competitive boxing, mature) versus displacement (the feeling shifted onto a safer target, neurotic). Rationalisation sits with the neurotic defences in most textbook tables; the DSM-IV Defensive Functioning Scale placed it at the lower disavowal level. Option a is right because altruism is one of Vaillant's five mature defences, while intellectualisation, repression and rationalisation all sit lower.", "topic": "Vaillant's hierarchy of maturity", "fig": "George Vaillant"}, {"q": "The ___ complex is to girls as the ___ complex is to boys.", "options": {"a": "electra, oedipus", "b": "oedipus, electra", "c": "oral, phallic", "d": "phallic, oral"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is electra, oedipus. In Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development, during the phallic stage (roughly ages 3 to 6) the child experiences unconscious sexual desire for the opposite-sex parent and rivalry with the same-sex parent. For boys, Freud called this the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek myth of Oedipus, who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother; the boy desires his mother, views his father as a rival, and fears castration, eventually resolving the conflict by identifying with the father. For girls, the parallel is the Electra complex, a term actually coined by Carl Jung (though the concept is Freud's), named after the Greek figure Electra who sought revenge against her mother; the girl is said to desire her father and experience penis envy, resolving the conflict by identifying with the mother. Thus the Electra complex is to girls as the Oedipus complex is to boys, making option a correct; option b reverses them. Options c and d list psychosexual stages (oral, phallic) rather than the named complexes. Although these ideas are heavily criticised and no longer scientifically accepted, they remain staple exam content because they are central to Freud's account of how gender identity, morality (the superego), and adult relationships supposedly develop through the resolution of these childhood conflicts.", "topic": "The psychosexual stages", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "According to Eysenck, extraverts seek to _____ their arousal while introverts seek to _____ their arousal.", "options": {"a": "decrease, increase", "b": "hide, reveal", "c": "increase, decrease", "d": "reveal, hide"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to Hans Eysenck, extraverts seek to increase their arousal, while introverts seek to decrease it. Hans Eysenck (1967), in The Biological Basis of Personality, linked his extraversion-introversion dimension to the brain. He proposed that the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) in the brainstem sets each person's baseline level of cortical arousal, and that everyone feels and performs best at a moderate, optimal level, an idea close to the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908). Introverts, Eysenck argued, have a higher resting arousal, so they already sit near or above their optimum; extra stimulation such as noise, crowds or novelty overloads them, and they choose quiet settings to bring arousal down. Extraverts have a lower resting arousal, so they are under-aroused and seek parties, risk, variety and loud music to push it up. The evidence fits: in the lemon juice test, introverts salivate more than extraverts when drops of lemon juice are placed on the tongue, showing stronger physiological reactivity, and introverts are also more sensitive to pain and faint stimuli and condition more easily. Jeffrey Gray (1970) later revised the biology in his reinforcement sensitivity theory. For example, after a long week Arjun, an extravert, recharges at a crowded cricket match, while his introverted friend Kabir recharges by reading alone in a quiet room. Option c is right because extraverts are chronically under-aroused and seek stimulation to raise arousal, while introverts are over-aroused and avoid it. Exam trap: it seems natural to assume extraverts are 'more aroused' because they look lively. In Eysenck's theory the opposite is true: their lively behaviour makes up for low cortical arousal.", "topic": "Eysenck's arousal theory", "fig": "Hans Eysenck"}, {"q": "Which brain structure is primarily involved in regulating emotions such as fear and aggression?", "options": {"a": "Hippocampus", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Amygdala", "d": "Cerebellum"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The amygdala, option c, is the brain structure primarily involved in regulating emotions such as fear and aggression. The amygdala is an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei located deep within the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. It is the brain's threat-detection and emotional-significance hub, rapidly evaluating sensory input for danger and triggering the physiological and behavioural components of fear, including the fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) response via its connections to the hypothalamus and brainstem. Joseph LeDoux's research mapped a fast, low road from the thalamus directly to the amygdala that allows a near-instant fear response before the cortex has fully processed the stimulus, and a slower high road through the cortex that permits more deliberate appraisal. The amygdala is central to fear conditioning, the learning of associations between neutral cues and aversive events, and damage to it produces blunted fear and reduced aggression, as in the classic studies of the patient who could not recognise fearful facial expressions. It is implicated in anxiety disorders, PTSD, and aggression. The tight link between the amygdala and fear, threat, and aggression is the key exam fact, making option c correct.", "topic": "The amygdala and fear", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Surgery is one of the methods to understand brain behaviour relations in humans. Reason (R): Lesion method destroys part of the brain.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this item, in both the 2019 and the 2021 paper, marks (A) false and (R) true. Reason: the lesion method destroys part of the brain. True. A lesion is damage to or removal of tissue, and the lesion (ablation) method infers a region's function from what is lost, as in the ablation studies of Pierre Flourens (1820s) and the cortical lesions Karl Lashley made in rats (Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence, 1929). Assertion: surgery is one of the methods to understand brain-behaviour relations in humans. On the key's reading, false: as a research method, surgery means experimental lesioning, deliberately destroying or removing tissue to see what changes, and that is done only in animals. No one may operate on a human brain to find out what it does. In humans, brain-behaviour relations are studied through naturally occurring damage (strokes, tumours, head injuries), neuropsychological testing, electrical recording (EEG, event-related potentials), neuroimaging (CT, MRI, fMRI, PET) and non-invasive stimulation (transcranial magnetic stimulation). Patients who need surgery for epilepsy or a tumour can be tested before and after, and some of the best known findings came that way: Henry Molaison (H.M.), whose medial temporal lobes William Scoville removed in 1953 and whom Brenda Milner tested, and the split-brain patients of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s). But there the surgery is treatment, and the research method is the clinical study of a lesion. Example: a researcher who wants to know what the hippocampus does can lesion it in rats, but in people has to find patients whose hippocampus was damaged by illness or by a necessary operation. Option d is right on the key's reading because R correctly defines the lesion method, while A wrongly lists surgery as a method that can be used on humans. Exam trap: H.M. and the split-brain cases make A look obviously true, so most students pick a. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because therapeutic surgery such as H.M.'s has taught a great deal about the human brain and is a lesion in all but name. LBS's official key marks d for this question in both 2019 and 2021, on the reading that surgery is never a research method in humans. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "topic": "Surgery and the lesion method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Put the parts of the brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: 1. Metencephalon 2. Mesencephalon 3. Diancephalon 4. Telencephalon 5. Myelencephalon", "options": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "b": "2, 4, 1, 3, 5", "c": "4, 3, 2, 1, 5", "d": "3, 2, 1, 5, 4"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: from higher to lower the order is telencephalon (4), diencephalon (3), mesencephalon (2), metencephalon (1) and myelencephalon (5). The names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube forms three swellings: the forebrain (prosencephalon), the midbrain (mesencephalon) and the hindbrain (rhombencephalon). Before birth the forebrain divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: cortex, basal ganglia, limbic structures and corpus callosum. The diencephalon ('between-brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with its tectum (the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow-brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. Example: Arjun remembers the order with a simple rule: telencephalon on top, then the other four in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: metencephalon and mesencephalon differ by one letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it. Option d keeps the middle of the order but drops the telencephalon to the bottom.", "topic": "Five divisions of the brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Serotonin.", "b": "Dopamine.", "c": "Epinephrine.", "d": "Insulin."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: insulin is not a neurotransmitter; it is a hormone made by the pancreas. A neurotransmitter is a chemical that a neuron stores in vesicles at its axon terminal and releases into the synaptic cleft, where it acts within milliseconds on receptors of the next cell, only a fraction of a micrometre away. A hormone is released by an endocrine (ductless) gland into the blood and travels to distant target cells, acting over minutes to hours. Insulin is a protein hormone released by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas when blood glucose rises after a meal. It lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose, so blood sugar falls; glucagon from the alpha cells does the opposite. Insulin also reaches the brain from the blood and tells the hypothalamus that the body is fed, but it is not released by neurons at synapses, so it is not counted as a neurotransmitter. Serotonin (from the raphe nuclei: mood, sleep, appetite) and dopamine (from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area: movement and reward) are classic monoamine transmitters. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the tricky one: it is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but a few brainstem neurons also release it as a transmitter, so it is both. Example: when Riya eats a plate of jalebis, her pancreas pours insulin into her blood, while inside her brain dopamine is released at synapses of the reward circuit. Option d is right because insulin is the only substance listed that works purely as a blood-borne hormone. Exam trap: epinephrine is the near miss. When a list offers only dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline and adrenaline, adrenaline is marked as the odd one out because it acts mainly as a hormone; once a pure hormone such as insulin is offered, adrenaline counts as a neurotransmitter and insulin is the answer.", "topic": "Hormones versus neurotransmitters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Procedural memory can be affected by damage to the:", "options": {"a": "Cerebrum.", "b": "Cerebellum.", "c": "Motor cortex.", "d": "Limbic system."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: procedural memory can be affected by damage to the cerebellum. Procedural memory is memory for how to do things: motor skills such as cycling, typing or playing an instrument, and habits learned by repetition. It is a kind of implicit (nondeclarative) memory, shown in better performance rather than in conscious recall, and it is separate from declarative memory for facts and events, which depends on the hippocampus. The best evidence comes from patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed his medial temporal lobes in 1953 he could form no new conscious memories, yet Brenda Milner (1962) found that he improved day by day at mirror drawing, tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror, though each time he denied having done the task before. The operation spared his cerebellum and basal ganglia, though years of the anti-epileptic drug phenytoin later left his cerebellum markedly atrophied (Suzanne Corkin, 2002). The cerebellum compares intended with actual movement and corrects the error, so it is vital for learning and timing motor skills; Richard Thompson (1980s) showed that eyeblink conditioning depends on it. Damage causes ataxia and poor motor learning. Example: after a cerebellar stroke, Meera, a tabla player, can still describe every rhythm she knows, but her strokes become uneven and she struggles to relearn even simple patterns. Option b is right because the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie most closely to procedural skill learning. Exam trap: the limbic system (hippocampus) is the memory answer for facts and events, not for skills; H.M. is the proof. Key note: procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (striatum) and the cerebellum, with the motor cortex involved; the basal ganglia are not offered, and the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie to motor skill learning (H.M. still learned mirror drawing), so b stands.", "topic": "Procedural memory and cerebellum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The principal function of myelin is to:", "options": {"a": "Increase the amount of neurotransmitter released into a synapse.", "b": "Decrease the distance of the synaptic cleft.", "c": "Increase the rate of propagation of an action potential.", "d": "Increase the amplitude of an action potential."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the principal function of myelin is to increase the speed at which an action potential travels along the axon. Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped in many layers around axons. In the central nervous system it is made by oligodendrocytes, each of which can wrap segments of several axons; in the peripheral nervous system it is made by Schwann cells, each wrapping one segment of one axon. Between the segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1835 to 1922), where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated. Because ions cannot leak across the myelinated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes. The impulse seems to jump from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap). Thin unmyelinated axons conduct at about 1 metre per second; large myelinated axons at up to about 100 metres per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions must be pumped back after each impulse. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, and the prefrontal cortex is among the last regions to finish, in the twenties. Example: Meera, 29, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling in her legs. An MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord: multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks CNS myelin, so conduction slows or fails. Option c is right because myelin's job is faster propagation of the action potential. Exam trap: myelin changes the speed, not the size, of the impulse; by the all-or-none law, every action potential in a given neuron has the same amplitude.", "topic": "Myelin and conduction speed", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An elderly acquaintance of yours has suffered from partial blindness since she had a stroke. Apparently the stroke damaged her:", "options": {"a": "Occipital lobe.", "b": "Parietal lobe.", "c": "Temporal lobes.", "d": "Reticular formation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: partial blindness after a stroke points to damage in the occipital lobe. The occipital lobe, at the back of the cerebral cortex, holds the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) around the calcarine fissure. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve, partly cross at the optic chiasm, relay in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and arrive in V1. Because of this crossing, each occipital lobe sees the opposite half of the visual field from both eyes. A stroke in the posterior cerebral artery, which feeds the occipital lobe, therefore often leaves a person blind in the same half of the field in both eyes (contralateral homonymous hemianopia), or with smaller blind islands (scotomas), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Damage to both occipital lobes can produce cortical blindness. Beyond V1, visual information flows along two streams: a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe (Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin, 1982). Suppose Meera's grandmother can read the right half of a newspaper line but keeps missing the words on the left; a right occipital stroke would explain it. Option a is right because partial loss of sight with intact eyes means the visual cortex is damaged, and the visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: parietal damage can make a person ignore the left side of space (hemispatial neglect), which looks like blindness but is a failure of attention, not of seeing.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following hormone is being secreted by adrenal gland?", "options": {"a": "Cortisol", "b": "Epinephrine", "c": "(A) and (B)", "d": "Melatonin"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The adrenal glands secrete both cortisol and epinephrine, so option c, both A and B, is correct. The adrenal glands sit atop each kidney and have two functionally distinct parts. The outer adrenal cortex produces steroid hormones, including glucocorticoids such as cortisol, mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone, and small amounts of sex steroids. Cortisol is the primary stress hormone of the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; it mobilises glucose, suppresses inflammation, and helps the body sustain a prolonged stress response, with secretion following a circadian rhythm peaking in the morning. The inner adrenal medulla, functionally an extension of the sympathetic nervous system, produces the catecholamines epinephrine, also called adrenaline, and norepinephrine. These are the hormones of the fast fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) response, raising heart rate, blood pressure, and blood sugar within seconds of a threat. Because both cortisol from the cortex and epinephrine from the medulla originate in the adrenal gland, both listed hormones are correct, which is exactly why the combined answer is the right one. Melatonin, by contrast, is made elsewhere, so option c is the complete and accurate choice.", "topic": "The other endocrine glands", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The thyroid gland controls:", "options": {"a": "Glucose absorption", "b": "Emotions", "c": "Metabolism", "d": "Sexuality"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the thyroid gland controls metabolism. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the front of the neck, below the larynx. It releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), hormones made with iodine, which set the body's metabolic rate: how fast cells burn fuel and produce heat and energy. The thyroid is controlled by a feedback loop: the hypothalamus releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), which makes the anterior pituitary release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which drives the thyroid; high thyroid hormone levels then switch the loop down. Too much thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) speeds everything up: weight loss, a racing heart, heat intolerance, restlessness and anxiety. Too little (hypothyroidism) slows everything down: weight gain, tiredness, feeling cold, slowed thinking and low mood, which is why doctors check thyroid function in depression. In infancy, untreated thyroid deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism (once called cretinism), with stunted growth and intellectual disability; iodine deficiency is a major preventable cause, which is why salt is iodised in India. Example: Riya feels low, has gained weight and always feels cold; a blood test shows low thyroxine. Option c is right because setting the metabolic rate is the thyroid's main job. Exam trap: thyroid problems change mood, which tempts students towards \"emotions\", but emotion is organised by the limbic system and the adrenal stress hormones; and blood glucose is regulated by insulin and glucagon from the pancreas, not by the thyroid.", "topic": "Thyroid gland and metabolism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The link between Nervous System and Endocrine System is possible through:", "options": {"a": "Corpus Callosum", "b": "Hypothalamus", "c": "Reticular formation", "d": "Cerebellum"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the link between the nervous system and the endocrine system is the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is a small structure, about the size of an almond, at the base of the brain just below the thalamus. It controls hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the stress response, and it does much of this through hormones. It sits directly above the pituitary gland, often called the master gland, and is joined to it by a stalk. The link works in two ways. First, some hypothalamic neurons make oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) and send them down their axons into the posterior pituitary, which releases them into the blood. Second, the hypothalamus secretes releasing and inhibiting hormones into a tiny network of blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system, which carries them to the anterior pituitary; there they switch on or off the hormones that control the thyroid, adrenal glands, gonads and growth. Geoffrey Harris (1955) established this neural control of the anterior pituitary. A clear example is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Before her board exam, Riya's hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone, her pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and her adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which raises her blood sugar and keeps her alert: a nervous signal (worry) has become a hormonal one. Option b is right because the hypothalamus is the brain structure that turns neural signals into hormonal ones through its control of the pituitary. Exam trap: the pituitary is the gland that gives orders to the other glands, but it is itself ruled by the hypothalamus, so the bridge between the two systems is the hypothalamus.", "topic": "Hypothalamus and endocrine link", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the resolution point of Erik Erikson's fourth stage of psychosocial development?", "options": {"a": "Fidelity.", "b": "Competence.", "c": "Will.", "d": "Purpose."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the fourth stage of Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994), industry versus inferiority, resolves in the virtue of competence. In Childhood and Society (1950) Erikson gave each of his eight psychosocial stages a crisis between two poles, and in Insight and Responsibility (1964) he named the virtue, or basic strength, that a good resolution leaves. Stage 4 covers the school years, about 6 to 11 or puberty, matching the latency period of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). School brings tools, tasks and classmates to compare oneself with. A child who finishes tasks, learns skills and earns recognition develops industry, a sense of being able to make things work; repeated failure, harsh comparison or being made to feel behind brings inferiority. Competence is the free use of skill and intelligence in completing tasks, unimpaired by a sense of inferiority. Teachers and peers become key relationships as the child's world widens beyond the family. The virtues to memorise, in order: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care, wisdom. Example: nine-year-old Kabir struggles with long division, but his teacher breaks it into steps and praises each success; by the end of term he volunteers to solve problems at the board, the sense of competence Erikson described. Option b is right because competence is the virtue of the fourth stage. Exam trap: the other three options are also Erikson virtues, from stage 2 (will), stage 3 (purpose) and stage 5 (fidelity), so count the stages carefully.", "topic": "Erikson's industry stage virtue", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "Grief refers to:", "options": {"a": "The period of emotional adjustment that follows the death of a loved one.", "b": "The intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend, or relative.", "c": "The period during which a person seems dazed or numb and shows little emotion.", "d": "Demoralization and discouragement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: grief is the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend or relative. Three words are often mixed up. Bereavement is the objective state of having lost someone close through death: it describes your situation. Grief is the emotional response to that loss: sorrow, yearning, anger, guilt, anxiety and loneliness, often with bodily signs such as poor sleep and loss of appetite. Mourning is the period of adjustment and the culturally shaped rituals through which people express grief and adapt, such as a funeral, a set time of wearing white, or the thirteen days of rites many Hindu families observe. John Bowlby (1980) saw grief as the response to a broken attachment and described phases: numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, and reorganisation. Colin Murray Parkes (1972), studying London widows, described a similar course. Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) later replaced fixed phases with a dual process model, in which the bereaved move back and forth between facing the loss and rebuilding daily life. Example: when Arjun's father dies, Arjun is bereaved (his state); the heavy sadness and longing he feels is grief; the cremation, the prayers and the year of family observances make up mourning. Option b is right because it names the emotion itself, an intense emotional state, rather than a period, a phase or a general mood. Exam trap: option a sounds almost identical but describes a period of adjustment, which is mourning, not grief; option c describes only the first numb phase within grief.", "topic": "Grief, bereavement and mourning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An example of a cognitive therapy is rational-emotive therapy, which was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Carl Rogers", "b": "Joseph Wolpe", "c": "Albert Ellis", "d": "Allen Bergin"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: rational-emotive therapy was developed by Albert Ellis. Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007), an American psychologist first trained in psychoanalysis, grew frustrated with its slowness and introduced rational therapy in 1955. It was later called rational-emotive therapy (RET) and, from 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). He set it out in Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962), and it is usually counted as the first of the modern cognitive therapies. Its centre is the ABC model. A is an activating event, B is the belief about it, C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. People assume A causes C, but Ellis argued that B does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('I must succeed', 'others must treat me fairly'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it') and global self-rating. The therapist then actively Disputes the belief (D), which leads to an Effective new philosophy (E). Example: Arjun fails one test (A) and feels worthless (C). Ellis would expose the belief 'I must never fail, or I am a total failure' (B) and dispute it until Arjun holds a preference instead: 'I would like to pass, but failing once does not make me worthless.' Option c is right because Ellis created RET, the classic cognitive therapy named in the stem. Exam trap: Aaron T. Beck (1967) developed the other main cognitive therapy (cognitive triad, automatic thoughts), and exam setters often swap the two. Joseph Wolpe (1958) belongs to behaviour therapy and Carl Rogers (1951) to person-centred therapy.", "topic": "Ellis and rational-emotive therapy", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "An analogue observation enables:", "options": {"a": "Surreptitious observation of the client", "b": "Observations of non-clinical population", "c": "Observation of more than one client at a time", "d": "Observation that require three or more clinicians"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: analogue observation enables surreptitious observation of the client. In behavioural assessment the clinician watches behaviour directly rather than relying only on what the client reports. Naturalistic observation happens in the real setting (home, classroom, ward). Analogue observation happens in a contrived, controlled setting built to resemble the real one: a clinic playroom where a parent and child carry out a set task, or a role-played social situation in which a client must refuse a request or start a conversation. Because the setting is arranged in advance, the clinician can watch from behind a one-way mirror or through a video link, unseen and unobtrusive, which is what 'surreptitious' means. This reduces reactivity (people behaving differently because they know they are being watched) and lets behaviour be coded in a standard way. The cost is generalisation: behaviour in a clinic room may not match behaviour at home. Example: Meera's parents say she refuses every instruction, so the psychologist asks her mother to have Meera tidy toys in a playroom and records Meera's compliance through a one-way mirror. Option a is right because the controlled analogue setting is what makes hidden, unobtrusive observation of the client possible. Exam trap: the word 'analogue' also appears in analogue research, where non-clinical people stand in for patients; that is a research strategy, not an observation method. Key note: IMHANS 2019 prints 'Superstitious', plainly a slip for 'surreptitious' (unseen, unobtrusive), and the option is shown corrected; b describes analogue research, a different use of the word, so a stands.", "topic": "Analogue observation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Dereflection\" is a component of:", "options": {"a": "Existential Therapy.", "b": "Person Centered Therapy.", "c": "Logotherapy.", "d": "Gestalt Therapy."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: dereflection is a technique of logotherapy, the meaning-centred therapy of Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997). Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi concentration camps, described his approach in Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German). Logotherapy (from logos, meaning) holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning, and that a frustrated search for meaning produces an existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis. Frankl gave it two signature techniques. Dereflection counters hyper-reflection, excessive attention to oneself or one's symptom, which often makes the symptom worse, as in insomnia or sexual dysfunction. The client is helped to turn attention away from the self and towards a meaningful task or other people, so the problem fades for lack of feeding. Paradoxical intention, the second technique, asks the client to wish for or do the very thing he fears, which breaks anticipatory anxiety. Socratic dialogue then helps the client find meaning in work, love or suffering. Example: Arjun lies awake checking whether sleep is coming. His therapist uses dereflection, asking him to spend that time planning the music class he teaches, and sleep arrives once he stops watching for it. Option c is right because dereflection is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: logotherapy is one of the existential therapies, so a is the near miss; but when the specific school is offered, a named technique goes to its own school, not to the broad family.", "topic": "Dereflection in logotherapy", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "Which of the following defines Beck's cognitive triad?", "options": {"a": "Negative beliefs about self, world and future.", "b": "Negative beliefs about self, world and others.", "c": "Negative beliefs about self, others and future.", "d": "Negative beliefs about present, past, and future."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the cognitive triad of Aaron Beck (1967) is a set of negative beliefs about the self, the world and the future. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' dreams and thoughts were full of automatic negative themes about loss and failure rather than hidden anger turned inwards. In his 1967 book on depression and in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues), he proposed that depression rests on three linked negative views. The self: 'I am worthless, unlovable, a failure.' The world, or current experience: 'Everything is against me; no one helps.' The future: 'It will never get better.' These views flow from dysfunctional schemas laid down by early experience and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. Hopelessness, the negative view of the future, is the corner most closely tied to suicide, which Beck measured with his Hopelessness Scale (1974). Cognitive therapy targets the triad through collaborative empiricism: client and therapist treat each belief as a hypothesis, test it with thought records and behavioural experiments, and build more balanced alternatives. Example: after failing one exam, Arjun thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'my teachers are unfair and nobody cares' (world) and 'I will never get a job' (future). Option a is right because it lists all three corners correctly: self, world and future. Exam trap: examiners swap one corner for a near miss, putting 'others' in place of the world or the future, or substituting a time sequence (past, present, future); the mnemonic is self, world, future.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}, {"q": "The technique used to extinguish fear by forcing clients to remain in anxiety arousing situation is called –", "options": {"a": "Time-out", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Inhibition", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: flooding is the technique that extinguishes fear by keeping the client in the anxiety-arousing situation until the fear fades. Flooding is a form of exposure therapy. The client is exposed, usually in real life (in vivo), to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, all at once, and is prevented from escaping. It rests on classical extinction: if the conditioned stimulus (the feared object) appears again and again without the feared outcome, the conditioned fear response weakens. It also blocks avoidance. In the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947), fears are learnt by classical conditioning and kept alive by operant avoidance, because each escape is rewarded by relief, and that relief stops the person from ever learning that the danger is not real. When escape is prevented, anxiety rises to a peak and then falls through habituation, and the client learns that nothing terrible happens. Sessions are long, often hours, because ending while anxiety is still high could strengthen the fear. A related method, implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl, 1967), floods the client in imagination with exaggerated scenes. Flooding is quicker than graded exposure but more distressing, so it needs full informed consent. Example: Rohan is terrified of lifts. With his therapist he rides a lift up and down a tall building for two hours. His panic peaks in the first 20 minutes, then slowly subsides until he feels bored. Option b is right because keeping the client in the feared situation with no escape is the definition of flooding. Exam trap: students confuse flooding with systematic desensitisation; both are exposure-based, but desensitisation is gradual and paired with relaxation, while flooding starts at the top of the hierarchy with no relaxation.", "topic": "Flooding in behaviour therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The behavioural technique for reducing anxiety in which patients practise relaxation while visualising anxiety-provoking situations of increasing intensity is called:", "options": {"a": "Symptom substitution", "b": "Time-out", "c": "Systematic Desensitisation", "d": "Token Economy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the technique is systematic desensitisation, developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition. It rests on reciprocal inhibition: relaxation and anxiety cannot occur at the same time, so if deep relaxation is paired with a feared stimulus, the relaxation inhibits the anxiety. This is a form of counterconditioning, replacing a fear response with a calm one. Therapy has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive muscle relaxation, tensing and releasing one muscle group after another. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated on Joseph Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when the current step no longer causes anxiety. Example: Riya fears flying; she first imagines looking at a photograph of a plane, then booking a ticket, then waiting at the gate and finally take-off, relaxing at every stage. Option c is right because the stem names all three parts: relaxation, visualisation and situations of increasing intensity. Exam trap: graded exposure while relaxed is desensitisation, but exposure to the most feared situation at full intensity from the start is flooding (implosion when the scene is imagined); and desensitisation works by reciprocal inhibition, not by operant reinforcement, extinction alone or catharsis.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:", "options": {"a": "The client is asked to talk about anything that comes to mind", "b": "The client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores reasons for this", "c": "The therapist interprets the dreams of the client", "d": "The client transfers their feelings towards significant others onto the therapist"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: analysis of resistance is where the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895), in Studies on Hysteria, noticed that patients struggled against remembering painful material even while asking to be cured. Freud called this opposition resistance: anything in the patient that blocks the work of treatment and access to the unconscious. It is the defence of repression showing itself in the consulting room. Resistance can look like arriving late, missing sessions, forgetting to pay, long silences, changing the subject just as something painful comes up, intellectualising, or suddenly feeling cured (a flight into health). Freud came to see resistance not as a nuisance but as information: what the patient avoids points to what matters most. So the analyst gently points out the obstruction and explores with the patient what it is protecting against, rather than pushing past it. Kabir, three months into therapy, arrives fifteen minutes late every time his father is due to come up; the therapist notes the pattern and wonders aloud what it might be like to talk about his father, opening the topic he has been avoiding. Option b is right because it describes both halves of the technique: the client's obstruction and the therapist's exploration of why it happens. Exam trap: the other three options are also psychoanalytic techniques, so they all sound right; match each to its name. Saying whatever comes to mind is free association, interpreting dreams is dream analysis, and redirecting feelings about significant others onto the therapist is transference.", "topic": "Analysis of resistance", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "The first step in the counselling process is:", "options": {"a": "Administering tools", "b": "Discussing problem", "c": "Building rapport", "d": "Solving client's problem"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first step in the counselling process is building rapport, a warm, trusting and accepting relationship between counsellor and client. The counsellor builds it by greeting the client warmly, giving full attention (eye contact, an open posture, nodding), listening without judging, reflecting feelings, explaining what counselling involves and promising confidentiality within its limits. Carl Rogers (1957) argued that the counsellor's empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness are the conditions that make change possible, and Gerard Egan (1975), in The Skilled Helper, likewise opens the helping process with attending to the client and helping him explore his concerns. Indian guidance texts give the process as building rapport, exploring the problem, assessment (including tests where needed), setting goals, intervention, and termination with follow-up. Without trust, clients hide or soften the real problem, so later steps rest on half the facts. Example: Farah, a Class 9 student, is sent to the school counsellor for missing classes. Asked at once 'Why are you bunking?', Farah says nothing. If the counsellor first chats about Farah's favourite subject, listens patiently and promises confidentiality, Farah soon admits that she is being teased on the school bus. Option c is right because exploring, testing and problem solving all depend on the trust built at the start. Exam trap: discussing the problem feels like the obvious opening, since the client came with a problem, but real discussion begins only once rapport exists; and solving the problem is not a step the counsellor performs for the client.", "topic": "First step of counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which option is not commonly diagnosed in criminals?", "options": {"a": "Depersonalization Disorder", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "Narcissistic Personality", "d": "Anti-Social Personality"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: depersonalization disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals. Depersonalization/derealization disorder (DSM-5-TR) involves persistent or recurrent experiences of feeling detached from one's own thoughts, feelings, body or actions, as if watching oneself from outside (depersonalization), or of the surroundings seeming dreamlike or unreal (derealization). Reality testing stays intact: the person says \"it is as if I am in a dream\", knows it is not so, and is usually frightened by the feeling. It is a dissociative disorder with no special link to offending. By contrast, forensic research shows antisocial personality disorder is the most common diagnosis among offenders; a large review of prison surveys by Seena Fazel and John Danesh (2002) found that nearly half of male prisoners met its criteria. Narcissistic traits are over-represented among violent offenders, and psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, though present in only a minority of prisoners, occur at several times the general-population rate. Clinical example: a college student who, under exam stress, feels her hands belong to someone else and her room looks like a film set has depersonalization and derealization, not a criminal tendency. Option a is right because depersonalization is an inward, distressing dissociative experience with no established link to crime. Exam trap: do not pick schizophrenia just because most people with schizophrenia are not violent; the question asks about rates among criminals, where it is elevated.", "topic": "Depersonalization disorder and criminality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "options": {"a": "Affective flattening.", "b": "Bizarre behaviour.", "c": "Circumstantiality.", "d": "Delusions."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: affective flattening is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to or taken away from normal experience. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to an acute Type I illness that responds to antipsychotics, and mainly negative symptoms to a chronic Type II illness with a poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen turned the split into two rating scales, the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The SAPS covers hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and positive formal thought disorder, and the last includes derailment, tangentiality, incoherence and circumstantiality. Affective flattening, the first domain of the SANS, is reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture. Example: Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for ten years, speaks in a flat monotone, rarely smiles or makes eye contact, and shows no change of expression when told of his sister's wedding. Option a is right because flattening is the absence of something normal, which makes it negative. Exam trap: circumstantiality looks like the odd one out, since it is mild and is also seen in obsessional personalities and in epilepsy; but Andreasen rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, because it adds needless detail rather than taking something away.", "topic": "Positive versus negative symptoms", "fig": "Nancy Andreasen"}, {"q": "Which of these is not an information provided in DSM-IV-TR?", "options": {"a": "Essential features of the disorder.", "b": "Associated features.", "c": "A list of symptoms that must be present for diagnosis.", "d": "Information on treatment options."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: DSM-IV-TR does not give information on treatment options. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Since DSM-III, directed by Robert Spitzer (1980), the manual has been descriptive and atheoretical: it says what a disorder looks like and how to diagnose it reliably, stays neutral about causes and is silent on treatment. DSM-IV-TR (2000) is a text revision of DSM-IV (1994): the criteria stayed almost the same, but the descriptive text was updated. For each disorder the text covers diagnostic features (the essential features), subtypes and specifiers, recording procedures, associated features and disorders (including laboratory findings), specific culture, age and gender features, prevalence, course, familial pattern and differential diagnosis, followed by the diagnostic criteria, the list of symptoms that must be present and for how long. DSM-IV-TR also used five axes: clinical disorders; personality disorders and mental retardation; general medical conditions; psychosocial and environmental problems; and the Global Assessment of Functioning. Treatment guidance lives elsewhere, in the American Psychiatric Association's separate practice guidelines, in national guidelines and in textbooks. Example: a clinical psychologist, Kabir, uses DSM-IV-TR to confirm that his client meets the criteria for a major depressive episode and to rule out bipolar disorder, but turns to a practice guideline to choose between CBT and an antidepressant. Option d is right because the manual is a diagnostic and descriptive tool, not a treatment manual. Exam trap: the size of the book and the word 'Statistical' tempt students to think it covers everything about a disorder; it covers diagnosis and description, never therapy.", "topic": "Contents of DSM-IV-TR", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Lifetime prevalence rate for OCD is:", "options": {"a": "Around 2.5%.", "b": "Around 1.5%.", "c": "Around 4.5%.", "d": "Around 1%."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the lifetime prevalence of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is around 2.5%. OCD is defined by obsessions, recurrent intrusive and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety (fear of germs, doubts about locking the door, taboo images), and/or compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, praying) done to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. It must be time-consuming (more than an hour a day) or cause marked distress or impairment. The classic figure comes from the Epidemiologic Catchment Area (ECA) study of the 1980s, which interviewed more than 18,000 adults at five US sites with a structured diagnostic interview. Marvin Karno and colleagues (1988) reported a lifetime OCD prevalence of about 2.5%, far higher than clinicians had assumed, since many sufferers hide their rituals. Lifetime prevalence is the proportion of people who have had the disorder at any point in their life; 12-month prevalence counts only recent cases and is therefore lower. OCD usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood, earlier in males. Example: Arjun, 20, checks the gas knob 30 times before leaving home and is late for college every day; he is one of the 2 to 3 people in every 100 who will have OCD at some point. Option a is right because about 2.5% is the standard lifetime figure. Exam trap: 'around 1%' (option d) is close to the 12-month figure, so it catches students who mix up lifetime and 12-month prevalence. Key note: the later National Comorbidity Survey Replication (Ayelet Meron Ruscio and colleagues, 2010) gives about 2.3%, and DSM-5 gives a 12-month figure of 1.2% (1.1% to 1.8% internationally), so a '1%' option is a 12-month trap; around 2.5% stands.", "topic": "Lifetime prevalence of OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which aspect of the definition of abnormality includes unhappiness?", "options": {"a": "Distress", "b": "Deviance", "c": "Dysfunction", "d": "Danger to self or others"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the part of the definition of abnormality that includes unhappiness is distress. Distress refers to unpleasant, upsetting feelings: sadness, unhappiness, anxiety, fear, guilt or emotional pain. It is one of the four Ds that Ronald Comer (1992) uses to sum up abnormality, along with deviance, dysfunction and danger. The personal distress criterion treats behaviour as abnormal when it causes the person great suffering. It matters because many disorders, such as depression and the anxiety disorders, are felt mainly as suffering, and DSM-5 (2013) builds 'clinically significant distress or impairment' into its definition of a mental disorder. But distress is neither necessary nor sufficient on its own. Some people with a disorder feel little distress: a person in a manic episode may feel wonderful, and someone with antisocial personality disorder may feel no guilt. And distress can be normal: grief after a parent's death, or nerves before a viva, is not a disorder. That is why clinicians weigh all four Ds together and ask whether the distress is out of proportion to its cause and lasting. Example: Riya has felt deeply unhappy and hopeless for two months since moving to a new city, cries most evenings and has lost interest in everything. Her unhappiness is the distress feature of abnormality. Option a is right because unhappiness is a form of personal suffering, which is exactly what the distress criterion describes. Exam trap: dysfunction can sound close, because unhappy people often function worse, but dysfunction refers to impaired daily functioning (work, study, relationships, self-care), not to the feeling itself.", "topic": "Four Ds: distress", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "options": {"a": "Nonassociative fear acquisition", "b": "Learned fear response", "c": "Biological preparedness", "d": "Specific phobic acquisition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the leading evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn fears of things that threatened our ancestors, such as snakes, spiders, heights, deep water and angry faces, far more easily than fears of modern dangers such as electric sockets or cars. Prepared fears can form after a single bad experience, or even from watching someone else, and they resist extinction. This explains why phobias cluster around a short list of ancestral objects. Strong evidence comes from rhesus monkeys: Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) showed laboratory-reared monkeys videos of other monkeys reacting with fear; they learned to fear toy snakes and toy crocodiles, but not flowers or toy rabbits edited into the same footage. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) later described an evolved \"fear module\" in the brain that such cues trigger selectively. Example: Riya has never been bitten, yet one scream from her cousin at a garden snake leaves her with a lasting snake phobia, while a far more dangerous shock from a faulty switch leaves no phobia at all. Option c is right because preparedness is the evolutionary explanation that dominates textbook accounts of phobias. Exam trap: a learned fear response (option b) sounds scientific but is plain conditioning with no evolutionary claim, while the non-associative account (option a) is also evolutionary, so students who spot only that word may pick it. Key note: Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies (2002) proposed non-associative fear acquisition, in which some fears (of heights, water, strangers) are innate and appear without any learning; it is evolutionary too, but a minority view, and preparedness remains the predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "topic": "Preparedness theory of phobias", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "‘Reinforcement will have positive effect on performance,’ which type of hypothesis is this?", "options": {"a": "Null and directional", "b": "Alternate and directional", "c": "Null and non-directional", "d": "Alternate and non-directional"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is alternate and directional. In hypothesis testing, researchers distinguish between the null hypothesis and the alternate (alternative) hypothesis, and between directional and non-directional forms. The null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or no relationship, for example that reinforcement has no effect on performance. The alternate hypothesis (H1) states that there is an effect or relationship. The statement 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' asserts that an effect exists, so it is an alternate hypothesis, not a null one. Furthermore, it specifies the direction of that effect: not merely that reinforcement affects performance, but that it improves it (a positive effect). When a hypothesis specifies the direction of the expected effect (such as 'greater than,' 'less than,' 'improves,' or 'reduces'), it is a directional hypothesis, tested with a one-tailed test. When it merely predicts a difference or relationship without specifying which way it will go (such as 'reinforcement will affect performance'), it is non-directional, tested with a two-tailed test. Because this statement both claims an effect (alternate) and specifies that the effect is positive (directional), it is an alternate and directional hypothesis. This is a standard research-methodology classification.", "topic": "Types of hypotheses", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following variable(s) can be labelled as organismic variable(s)? 1. Task complexity 2. Intelligence 3. Errors made while learning task 4. Heart rate", "options": {"a": "4 only", "b": "2 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 4 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: intelligence and heart rate are organismic variables, so 2 and 4 only. Robert Woodworth (1869 to 1962), whose Dynamic Psychology (1918) put the organism and its drives at the centre, expanded the behaviourist stimulus-response (S-R) formula into S-O-R, placing the organism between stimulus and response, and experimental psychology still sorts variables this way. Stimulus or task variables are features of the situation or task the experimenter presents: task complexity, list length, noise, instructions. Organismic (O, or subject) variables are characteristics or states that the participant brings to the study: age, sex, intelligence, personality, motivation, fatigue, and physiological states such as heart rate or blood sugar. Response variables are the behaviour measured as the outcome, the dependent variable: errors, reaction time, trials to criterion. Organismic variables usually cannot be manipulated, so they are measured, selected or controlled (by matching or random assignment); left uncontrolled, they act as extraneous variables. Example: Arjun learns a finger maze. The number of turns in the maze is a task variable, his IQ and resting heart rate are organismic variables, and the errors he makes are the response variable. Option b is right because only intelligence (a trait) and heart rate (a physiological state) belong to the organism. Exam trap: errors made while learning come from the person, so they feel organismic, but they are the response being measured (the DV); task complexity is a property of the task. Key note: heart rate is recorded as a response measure in some experiments, for example in psychophysiology, but as a state of the organism it is an O variable here; b stands.", "topic": "Organismic variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "How many subjects would be required in a 3 × 3×2 factorial design with ten subjects per cell?", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "90", "c": "18", "d": "180"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 180 subjects. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at once, crossing every level of each factor with every level of the others. The notation tells you the structure: each number is one factor, and its value is that factor's number of levels. So 3 × 3 × 2 means three factors, two with three levels each and one with two levels. The number of cells (conditions) is the product of the levels: 3 × 3 × 2 = 18. With ten subjects in each cell, the total is 18 × 10 = 180. Example: a researcher studies anxiety reduction by therapy type (CBT, mindfulness, waiting list), session frequency (weekly, twice weekly, daily) and gender (male, female); that gives 18 combinations, and with ten participants in each, 180 people are needed. Factorial designs, analysed with factorial analysis of variance (ANOVA), are efficient because one study tests every main effect and every interaction. Option d is right because you multiply the levels to get the cells and then multiply by the subjects per cell. Exam trap: students add the levels (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) or stop at the number of cells (18, option c). The calculation assumes a between-subjects design, the standard reading, in which each participant serves in only one cell.", "topic": "Factorial design sample size", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Type II error occurs :", "options": {"a": "whenever a true null hypothesis is accepted", "b": "whenever a false null hypothesis is rejected", "c": "whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted", "d": "whenever a true null hypothesis is rejected"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is that a Type II error occurs whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted. In the null-hypothesis significance-testing framework formalized by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, two kinds of decision errors are possible. A Type I error (alpha error) occurs when a true null hypothesis is rejected, that is, the researcher concludes there is an effect when in fact there is none, a false positive; its probability is set by the significance level alpha. A Type II error (beta error) occurs when a false null hypothesis is accepted (not rejected), that is, the researcher fails to detect a real effect that actually exists, a false negative; its probability is denoted beta. The complement of a Type II error, 1 minus beta, is the statistical power of a test, the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis and detecting a true effect. Power increases with larger sample size, larger effect size, lower measurement error, and a higher alpha. Correctly accepting a true null and correctly rejecting a false null are the two right decisions, not errors, which rules out options a and b. Because a Type II error is specifically the failure to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, option c is correct.", "topic": "Type I and Type II errors", "fig": ""}, {"q": "After the recent heavy catastrophic floods in a given state, a psychologist interviewed 120 participants to understand the psychological consequences of the event. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "Ex post facto field research", "b": "Field experiment", "c": "Controlled experiment", "d": "Psychometric research"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: interviewing flood survivors after the disaster is ex post facto field research. 'Ex post facto' is Latin for 'after the fact'. Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, described it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred or cannot be manipulated, so the researcher works backward from the observed effects to their presumed cause. Here the independent variable is the flood. No psychologist can create a flood, or decide at random who is hit and who is spared, so there is no manipulation and no random assignment. What the psychologist can do is go to the affected area afterwards and measure outcomes such as anxiety, sleep problems, grief or post-traumatic stress, perhaps comparing badly hit and lightly hit villages. Because the data are gathered in people's real surroundings rather than a laboratory, it is a field study. The design has known weaknesses: exposed and unexposed groups differ in many ways besides the flood, so causal conclusions are weak, and it is tempting to assume that whatever came first caused whatever followed. Example: Kabir, a clinical psychologist, interviews 120 survivors in Kerala three months after the floods to record their distress. Option a is right because the event came first, the researcher had no control over it, and the work took place in the community. Exam trap: 'field' appears in two options, and what separates them is manipulation. A field experiment still has the researcher deliberately change something, such as assigning some villages to a counselling programme; here nothing was changed.", "topic": "Ex post facto field research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is :", "options": {"a": "Left of the median", "b": "Right of the median", "c": "Same as median", "d": "Same as a mode"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is left of the median. Skewness describes the asymmetry of a distribution. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution, the long tail points toward the low (left) end of the scale. Extreme low scores in that tail pull the arithmetic mean downward more than they move the median, because the mean is sensitive to every value while the median depends only on rank position. The result is the ordering mean less than median less than mode. So the mean sits to the left of the median. The mirror image holds for a positively (right) skewed distribution, where the ordering is mode less than median less than mean, placing the mean to the right. This relationship among the three measures of central tendency was popularized through the empirical work of Karl Pearson, who also defined coefficients of skewness using the gap between mean and mode or mean and median. Understanding it lets you infer the shape of a distribution from summary statistics alone, which is why it is a staple of psychology statistics papers.", "topic": "Skewness and kurtosis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a measure of central tendency?", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Median", "c": "Mean Deviation", "d": "Mode"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "Mean deviation is not a measure of central tendency, so c is the correct answer. Central tendency refers to a single value that represents the centre or typical score of a distribution, summarising where the bulk of the data cluster. The three classic measures of central tendency are the mean, the arithmetic average obtained by summing all scores and dividing by their number; the median, the middle score when all values are arranged in order, which divides the distribution into two equal halves and is robust to extreme scores; and the mode, the most frequently occurring score, which is the only measure usable with nominal data. Mean deviation, by contrast, belongs to an entirely different family of statistics called measures of dispersion or variability, which describe how spread out the scores are around the centre rather than where the centre lies. Mean deviation is calculated as the average of the absolute differences of each score from the mean (or median). Other measures of dispersion include the range, the variance, the standard deviation, and the quartile deviation. The conceptual point the question tests is the distinction between location and spread: central tendency answers where is the centre, while dispersion answers how scattered are the data. Because mean deviation quantifies scatter, it cannot be a measure of central tendency, making it the correct exception.", "topic": "Measures of central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a goodness of fit test?", "options": {"a": "Chi-Square", "b": "t-test", "c": "ANOVA", "d": "Runs Test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the chi-square test is the classic goodness of fit test. A goodness of fit test asks how well observed data match the distribution that a hypothesis predicts. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced chi-square for exactly this purpose. You sort cases into categories, count the observed frequency (O) in each, work out the expected frequency (E) if the hypothesis were true, and add up (O minus E) squared divided by E across the categories. A small chi-square means the data fit the expected pattern; a large one, read against the chi-square table with (number of categories minus 1) degrees of freedom, means they do not. Because it works on counts in categories, it needs no normal distribution and is classed as non-parametric. Example: Meera asks 120 students which of four counselling slots they prefer. If there is no preference, each slot should get 30. She observes 50, 30, 25 and 15, and chi-square tells her whether that spread departs from the even split more than chance allows. Applied to a two-way table (are gender and choice of stream related?), the same statistic becomes the chi-square test of independence, a different use. Option a is right because chi-square compares observed counts with the counts a model expects, which is what fit means. Exam trap: the runs test is also non-parametric and also works on counts, so it looks similar, but it tests whether a sequence is random, not whether data fit a distribution. The Kolmogorov-Smirnov one-sample test is the other well-known goodness of fit test, but it is not offered here.", "topic": "Chi-square goodness of fit", "fig": "Karl Pearson"}]};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["repeats-3"] = {"id": "repeats-3", "title": "Repeats Drill 3", "fullTitle": "Repeats Drill 3: asked in 2 papers (part 2)", "sub": "62 more real past-paper Qs, each asked in 2 different papers · all 9 subjects · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Rejecting the concept of S-R connectionism, the S-O-R concept was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Woodworth", "b": "C. G. Jung", "c": "E. B. Titchener", "d": "Sigmund Freud"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Robert S. Woodworth (1869 to 1962) of Columbia University rejected the plain stimulus-response (S-R) formula and proposed S-O-R, stimulus-organism-response. Edward Thorndike's (1898) connectionism, and later John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism, explained behaviour as learned bonds between a stimulus (S) and a response (R). Woodworth argued that the same stimulus produces different responses depending on what is going on inside the organism (O): its drives, needs, past learning, expectations, attention and bodily state. In Dynamic Psychology (1918) he stressed drive and motivation, and in his textbook Psychology (1929) he set out the S-O-R formula. This mediational view, in which inner states stand between stimulus and response, was later taken up by neobehaviourists and by cognitive psychology. Woodworth was a functionalist and is remembered for making psychology 'dynamic', concerned with why organisms act as well as how. Example: a plate of hot samosas (S) makes hungry Kabir reach for one (R), but after a big lunch the same plate gets no response; the difference lies in the organism (O), his state of hunger. Option a is right because the S-O-R concept is Woodworth's, introduced to correct the S-R connectionist view. Exam trap: Edward B. Titchener (1867 to 1927) (c) tempts because he is another early founder working in America, but he was a structuralist who analysed conscious experience into elements by introspection, not a critic of S-R bonds; and Thorndike is the connectionist the stem rejects, not the reformer.", "topic": "Woodworth's S-O-R model", "fig": "Robert S. Woodworth"}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Free recall and recognition, b. Sentence verification task, c. Priming, d. Scanning task. 1. Semantic memory, 2. Sensory memory, 3. Episodic memory, 4. Implicit memory", "options": {"a": "a-4, b-2, c-1, d-3", "b": "a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4", "c": "a-1, b-4, c-2, d-3", "d": "a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition test episodic memory (3), sentence verification tests semantic memory (1), priming tests implicit memory (4) and the scanning task, by elimination, sensory memory (2). Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory into episodic memory, personal events tied to a time and place, and semantic memory, general knowledge of facts and concepts. Free recall (write down every word from the list you just heard) and recognition (which of these words were on the list?) ask about one specific study episode, so they are episodic tasks. In a sentence verification task the person judges sentences such as 'A canary is a bird' as true or false as fast as possible; Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) used the reaction times to map the network of stored facts, so the task taps semantic memory. Priming is an influence of earlier exposure without conscious recollection: having seen BUTTER, a person completes BUT___ as BUTTER more often, even without remembering the word; Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) called this implicit memory. Scanning a briefly flashed display, as in George Sperling's (1960) partial report, taps the brief iconic store of sensory memory. Example: Riya recognises yesterday's list words (episodic), confirms that a sparrow is a bird (semantic) and fills BUT___ with BUTTER without knowing why (implicit). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: recall and recognition feel like knowledge tests, so students send them to semantic memory; they test memory for an episode. Key note: Saul Sternberg's (1966) memory scanning task is really a short-term memory task, while scanning a briefly flashed display, as in Sperling's partial report, taps sensory (iconic) memory; with the other three pairs fixed, d stands.", "topic": "Memory tasks and memory systems", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In signal-detection research, when no stimulus is presented and the subject reports seeing one, it is called a:", "options": {"a": "Hit.", "b": "Miss.", "c": "False alarm.", "d": "False positive."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in signal-detection research, reporting a stimulus when none was presented is called a false alarm. Classical psychophysics assumed a fixed threshold, but wartime radar operators showed that whether a person says 'yes' depends both on what they can sense and on how willing they are to say yes. Signal detection theory, set out by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates the two. On every trial a signal is either present or absent, and the observer says either 'yes' or 'no', which gives four outcomes. Signal present and 'yes' is a hit. Signal present and 'no' is a miss. Signal absent and 'yes' is a false alarm. Signal absent and 'no' is a correct rejection. From the hit and false alarm rates the researcher works out sensitivity (d prime, how well the person tells signal from noise) and the criterion (beta, how ready they are to say yes). A liberal observer, like a touchy smoke detector, gives many hits but also many false alarms; a conservative one gives few false alarms but more misses. Example: Kabir, an airport security screener, flags a bag as holding a knife when it holds only a steel tiffin box; no target was present but he said yes, so that is a false alarm. Option c is right because 'false alarm' is the signal-detection name for a yes response on a trial with no signal. Exam trap: students swap the two errors. A miss is saying no when the signal was there; a false alarm is saying yes when it was not. Key note: 'false positive' names the same event in diagnostic-testing language, so d is not wrong in plain terms; in signal-detection research the term is false alarm, and c stands.", "topic": "Signal detection outcomes", "fig": "David Green and John Swets"}, {"q": "Hermann Ebbinghaus, had done the pioneering experiments on:", "options": {"a": "Perception.", "b": "Psychophysics.", "c": "Serial Position Effect.", "d": "Memory."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1850 to 1909) did the pioneering experiments on memory, reported in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory, 1885). Ebbinghaus showed that a higher mental process could be measured experimentally, something Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) had doubted. Acting as his own sole participant, he invented the nonsense syllable (consonant-vowel-consonant strings such as DAX or BUP) to avoid the meanings and associations of real words. He learned lists until he could recite them twice without error and then relearned them after delays. Relearning was quicker even when nothing could be recalled, and he measured this with the savings method: original trials minus relearning trials, divided by original trials, times 100. His forgetting curve drops steeply, losing more than half within an hour and about two thirds within a day, then levels off without reaching zero. He also found the spacing effect (spread-out practice beats massed practice) and noticed that items at the start and end of a list are learned more easily. Example: Meera learns a list of nonsense syllables in 20 readings; a week later she recalls none, but relearns the list in 12 readings, a 40% saving. Option d is right because nonsense syllables, savings and the forgetting curve are all memory research, and the serial position finding is one result within it. Exam trap: the serial position effect is a real Ebbinghaus observation, so it baits the half-informed; but it is one part of his memory work, and memory is the full answer. Key note: WBJEEB dropped this question (as JEMAS(PG) 2024 Q59) from its final answer key for JEMAS(PG) 2024 (Nov 2024), so it was not scored in the real exam. c can be argued too, since Ebbinghaus did first describe the serial position effect; d stays the answer here.", "topic": "Ebbinghaus and memory", "fig": "Hermann Ebbinghaus"}, {"q": "Among the following pioneering psychologists who is not associated with Behaviourism?", "options": {"a": "A.T Beck.", "b": "E.R Guthrie.", "c": "B.F Skinner.", "d": "C.L Hull."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021) is not associated with behaviourism; he founded cognitive therapy. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), held that psychology should study only observable behaviour and its environmental causes, leaving out mind and consciousness. The neobehaviourists who followed refined it. Edwin Ray Guthrie (1886 to 1959) explained learning by contiguity alone: a stimulus that accompanied a movement will tend to be followed by that movement again, at full strength after one trial. Clark Leonard Hull (1884 to 1952) built a hypothetico-deductive system of drive reduction in which reaction potential equals habit strength times drive (Principles of Behavior, 1943). Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist, studied operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement. Beck, a psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, moved in a different direction. In the 1960s he argued that depression grows from negative automatic thoughts and schemas, summed up in the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), and he built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). His therapy targets thoughts, which strict behaviourists refused to treat as causes. Example: Riya fails a test and thinks 'I am useless and always will be'. A Beck therapist tests that thought against the evidence, while a Skinnerian would look at how studying is reinforced. Option a is right because Beck's theory is cognitive, from the school that rose against behaviourism. Exam trap: cognitive behaviour therapy uses behavioural techniques, so Beck's name sits near behaviour therapy in textbooks, but he is a cognitive pioneer, not a behaviourist.", "topic": "Behaviourists versus Beck", "fig": "Aaron T. Beck"}, {"q": "A physical stimulus is converted into a neural impulse by a process known as:", "options": {"a": "Transposition.", "b": "Transaction.", "c": "Transduction.", "d": "Transition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the conversion of a physical stimulus into a neural impulse is called transduction. Every sense organ contains receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy. Their job is to translate that energy into the only language the nervous system understands: changes in membrane voltage that lead to nerve impulses. In the eye, light is absorbed by photopigments such as rhodopsin in the rods and cones; this changes the receptor's membrane potential, and the signal passes through bipolar cells to ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. In the ear, sound waves bend the stereocilia of hair cells in the cochlea, opening ion channels. In the skin, pressure deforms mechanoreceptors; in the nose and tongue, chemicals bind to receptor proteins. In each case the first change is a graded receptor potential whose size follows the strength of the stimulus; this then triggers all-or-none impulses whose rate codes intensity. Because all impulses look alike, Johannes Peter Muller (1801 to 1858) proposed the doctrine of specific nerve energies: the quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, which is why pressing on a closed eye makes you see flashes of light. Sensation is detection and transduction; perception is the later interpretation by the brain. Example: Riya steps into sunlight; within milliseconds her rods and cones turn light into electrical signals, and only then can her brain recognise her friend's face across the road. Option c is right because transduction is the technical term for converting stimulus energy into neural signals at the receptor. Exam trap: all four options begin with \"trans-\"; only transduction is the sensory term, while transposition and transaction belong to other areas of psychology.", "topic": "Sensory transduction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "______ is the study of the meaning of words and language.", "options": {"a": "Linguistics.", "b": "Encoding.", "c": "Semantics.", "d": "Syntax."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: semantics is the study of the meaning of words and language. Language can be analysed at several levels. Phonology studies the sound system (phonemes); morphology studies how words are built from morphemes, the smallest units of meaning; syntax studies the rules that combine words into sentences; semantics studies meaning, of words, phrases and whole sentences; and pragmatics studies how language is used in social context, such as knowing that 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request, not a question about ability. Semantics covers word meanings, synonyms and opposites, ambiguity, and how the meaning of a sentence is built from its parts. Noam Chomsky (1957) showed that syntax and meaning can come apart with his sentence 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously', which is grammatically perfect but meaningless. Charles Hockett (1960) listed semanticity, the link between symbols and the things they stand for, among the design features of human language. The same root appears in memory research: Endel Tulving's (1972) semantic memory is our store of facts and word meanings. Example: Riya reads 'the boat drifted to the bank' and 'she deposited cash at the bank' and picks the right sense of 'bank' each time; that skill is semantic. Option c is right because the stem asks specifically about meaning, and meaning is what semantics studies. Exam trap: linguistics (option a) is the broad scientific study of all aspects of language, of which semantics is only one branch, so it is too wide; syntax (option d) is grammar, the rules of word order, not meaning.", "topic": "Semantics and levels of language", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Individual differences in intelligence are due to", "options": {"a": "only genetic factors", "b": "only environmental factors", "c": "genetic and economic factors", "d": "many factors including genetic and environmental"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: individual differences in intelligence arise from many factors, both genetic and environmental, acting together. Behaviour genetics estimates the genetic share with twin and adoption studies. Identical twins, who share all their genes, have more similar IQ scores than fraternal twins, who share about half, and adopted children come to resemble their biological parents more than their adoptive parents as they grow. Heritability, the proportion of differences within a population linked to genetic differences, rises with age: a combined analysis of about 11,000 twin pairs led by Claire Haworth and Robert Plomin (2010) found about 0.4 at age 9 rising to about 0.66 by age 17. But heritability is a population statistic, not a fixed limit for any one person. The environment matters too: prenatal health, nutrition, schooling, a stimulating home, family income and freedom from toxins such as lead. James Flynn (1984, 1987) showed that average IQ scores rose by about three points per decade across many nations, far too fast for genes to explain. Genes and environment also work together: a curious child seeks out books, and the books then sharpen her abilities. Example: identical twins Riya and Meera are adopted into different homes; their scores stay close, yet the twin given better schooling scores a few points higher. Option d is right because it is the only option that includes both genetic and environmental causes without narrowing them. Exam trap: option c sounds balanced, but economic status is only one environmental factor; 'many factors including genetic and environmental' is the full interactionist answer.", "topic": "Heredity and environment in intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The \"law of effect,\" formulated by Edward Thorndike, states that:", "options": {"a": "Behaviors followed by pleasurable consequences are more likely to be repeated.", "b": "Organisms learn by observing and imitating models.", "c": "Insight occurs when the elements of a problem are suddenly restructured.", "d": "All behavior is motivated by unconscious drives."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is that behaviors followed by pleasurable consequences are more likely to be repeated, because that is the essence of Edward Thorndike's law of effect. Understand the law and its place in the history of learning theory. Edward Thorndike, working around 1900, conducted classic experiments placing hungry cats in puzzle boxes from which they could escape to reach food by performing some action, such as pulling a loop or pressing a lever. At first the cat clawed and struggled randomly, but eventually it happened upon the correct response by accident and escaped. Over repeated trials, the time to escape gradually decreased, and Thorndike plotted these learning curves. Crucially, the improvement was gradual and incremental, not sudden, which led him to conclude that the cat was not reasoning or gaining insight but rather learning by trial and error, with successful responses being gradually stamped in. From this he formulated the law of effect: responses that produce a satisfying or pleasurable consequence in a given situation become more strongly connected to that situation and are more likely to recur, whereas responses followed by discomfort become weakened and less likely to recur. This idea, that consequences shape the future probability of behavior, was the direct forerunner of B. F. Skinner's principle of reinforcement in operant conditioning. Thorndike also proposed the law of exercise, that connections strengthen with practice. Contrast his consequence-driven, incremental account with Bandura's observational learning, Kohler's insight learning, and Freud's unconscious motivation. The statement that satisfying consequences make behavior more likely to repeat is the law of effect, Thorndike's signature contribution.", "topic": "Thorndike, Skinner and consequences", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "Thorndike's Multifactor theory of intelligence is at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of:", "options": {"a": "Motor organizations", "b": "Intellectual organizations", "c": "Mental organizations", "d": "Reasoning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike's (1927) multifactor theory sits at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of mental organisation, that is, how the abilities that make up intelligence are structured. Theorists who correlated many tests asked whether the abilities behind them form one general power, a few broad group factors or a host of separate bits. Thorndike gave the most splintered answer. For him intelligence is made up of a multitude of separate, minute elements of ability, each a set of stimulus-response connections. Any mental act uses a number of these elements together, two tasks correlate only to the extent that they share elements, and there is no general intelligence: a brighter person simply has more connections. The theory is therefore called atomistic. Even so, Thorndike grouped tasks into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence. At the other extreme stands Charles Spearman's (1904) two-factor theory, in which all intellectual activity depends mainly on one general factor, g, plus a specific factor, s, for each task; group-factor theories such as that of Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) sit in between. Example: Kabir repairs engines brilliantly but is average at abstract puzzles; Thorndike would call these largely separate abilities. Option c is right because the range from multifactor to two-factor theories is a range of views on how the mind's abilities are organised, which is exactly the phrase the textbook uses. Exam trap: the opposite pole is Spearman's g, not Thurstone's primary mental abilities, which are a middle position; and do not mix up Thorndike (multifactor) with Thurstone (primary mental abilities). Key note: the stem copies a sentence from Frank S. Freeman's Theory and Practice of Psychological Testing (third edition, 1962), which reads 'the nature of mental organization', so c is the answer both Jammu papers intend. Option b, intellectual organisation, means much the same thing here; if you picked it you understood the idea, but on these papers c is the safe mark, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Thorndike's multifactor theory", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "The unconscious perception of stimuli that are too weak to exceed the absolute threshold for detection is known as:", "options": {"a": "Just noticeable difference", "b": "Difference threshold", "c": "Subliminal perception", "d": "Response bias"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: subliminal perception is the unconscious perception of stimuli too weak or brief to exceed the absolute threshold for detection. 'Limen' is Latin for threshold, so subliminal means below the threshold. The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus a person detects 50 per cent of the time (Gustav Fechner, 1860). A subliminal stimulus is not consciously reported, yet it can still be processed. The best evidence comes from masked priming: Anthony Marcel (1983) flashed words so briefly, followed by a pattern mask, that people could not say what they had seen, yet an unseen word still speeded their responses to a related word. Such effects are real but small and short-lived. The famous claim of James Vicary (1957) that flashing 'Eat popcorn' and 'Drink Coca-Cola' during a film raised cinema sales was a hoax that he admitted in 1962, and there is no good evidence that subliminal advertising changes what people buy. Example: Riya sees a smiling face for a few milliseconds, instantly masked; she reports seeing nothing, but then rates an unfamiliar Chinese character a little more favourably. Option c is right because the stem defines subliminal perception exactly. Exam trap: a and b are one and the same thing, the just noticeable difference or difference threshold (Ernst Weber, 1834), which concerns noticing a change between two stimuli, not perceiving a stimulus below the absolute threshold.", "topic": "Subliminal perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who has emphasized the linguistic relativity hypothesis?", "options": {"a": "Noam Chomsky", "b": "Jean Piaget", "c": "Steven Pinker", "d": "Whorf B.L."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the linguistic relativity hypothesis is tied to Benjamin Lee Whorf (1956, Language, Thought, and Reality, published after his death), who built on the ideas of his teacher Edward Sapir (1929), so it is also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. It claims that the language you speak shapes how you think about and perceive the world. It comes in two strengths. The strong form, linguistic determinism, says language fixes thought, so speakers of very different languages live in different mental worlds. The weak form, linguistic relativity proper, says language only nudges what we notice, remember and find easy to think about. Whorf argued, for example, that the Hopi language treats time differently from English, so Hopi speakers think about time differently. Later research challenged the strong form. Eleanor Rosch (1972, 1973) studied the Dani of New Guinea, whose language has only two basic colour terms, and found they still learned and remembered the focal colours more easily than other shades, much as English speakers do. Weaker effects of language on memory and attention do find support. Example: Kabir speaks a language with separate words for a mother's brother (mama) and a father's brother (chacha), so he may notice that family distinction more readily than an English speaker who says only \"uncle\". Option d is right because Whorf is the name most closely tied to the claim that language shapes thought. Exam trap: Noam Chomsky and Steven Pinker are famous language theorists, but both argue for a universal, inborn capacity for language that does not lock thought into a particular language, the reverse of Whorf.", "topic": "Linguistic relativity hypothesis", "fig": "Benjamin Lee Whorf"}, {"q": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for:", "options": {"a": "Housing benefit", "b": "Disability allowance", "c": "Suicide risk", "d": "Special educational needs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) can be used to help assess eligibility for disability allowance, because establishing intellectual functioning is part of documenting a qualifying disability such as intellectual disability or cognitive impairment. The WAIS, originally developed by David Wechsler (the first version, the Wechsler-Bellevue, appeared in 1939, with the WAIS proper in 1955 and now in its fourth and fifth editions), is the most widely used individually administered adult intelligence test. It yields a Full Scale IQ along with index scores for verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed. Because a diagnosis of intellectual disability requires demonstrating significantly subaverage intellectual functioning (roughly an IQ below 70) alongside deficits in adaptive behavior, a standardized IQ measure like the WAIS provides exactly the objective intellectual data that disability determinations require. The question is testing whether the student understands the practical, applied use of intelligence testing in clinical and medico-legal contexts: an IQ score can substantiate a claim for a disability benefit by quantifying cognitive limitation. The other options describe contexts where an adult intelligence test is either irrelevant or the wrong instrument entirely.", "topic": "What an intelligence test can and cannot do", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "The first attempt for standardization of mental tests in India was made by:", "options": {"a": "Dr. S. M. Mohsin", "b": "V. V. Kamat", "c": "Dr. C.H. Rice", "d": "Dr. Sohen Lal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first attempt to standardise a mental test in India was made by C. Herbert Rice (1922). Standardisation means giving a test to a large, representative sample under uniform conditions, so that norms can be built and any one score can be read against them. Intelligence testing began with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) in Paris, and Binet-type scales soon spread across the world. In India, C. Herbert Rice, working in Lahore, began adapting the Binet scale for Indian children in 1922, work that produced the Hindustani Binet Performance Point Scale. As its name says, the scale put Binet-type items into Hindustani, added performance tasks and scored the whole by points, and it was tried out on Indian school children to give local norms. That makes it the first Indian adaptation and standardisation of a Binet-type test. Later Indian work built on it. V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test), and S. M. Mohsin and Sohan Lal developed Hindi group tests of general mental ability. Example: if Meera's teacher wants to know whether Meera's score is high, she needs norms from Indian children like Meera, and Rice's scale was the first to offer them. Option c is right because Rice's work, begun in 1922, came before the work of Kamat, Mohsin and Sohan Lal. Exam trap: the Binet-Kamat test is the best known Indian Binet, so it tempts as 'the first'; it was a later revision, not the first attempt.", "topic": "First Indian test standardisation", "fig": "C. Herbert Rice"}, {"q": "Which of the following are not Factor Analytically derived personality inventories? 1. 16 PF 2. Comrey Personality Scales 3. Jenkins Activity Survey 4. Edwards Personal Preference Schedule", "options": {"a": "1 and 2 only", "b": "3 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 3 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey (3) and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (4) are not factor-analytically derived. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content strategy writes items whose content obviously fits the trait; the theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory; the empirical criterion-group strategy keeps whatever items separate a criterion group from controls; and the factor-analytic strategy correlates many items, keeps those that load on each factor and names the factors afterwards. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949) came from factor analyses of trait terms, and Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970; 180 items, eight factors such as trust and orderliness) were built the same way, so 1 and 2 are factor-analytic. The Jenkins Activity Survey of C. David Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Ray Rosenman measures the Type A behaviour pattern; its Type A scale items were chosen because they agreed with Structured Interview classifications, a criterion approach. Allen Edwards's Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (revised edition, 1959) is theory-based: it measures 15 needs from Henry Murray's (1938) list, using forced-choice pairs matched for social desirability. Example: Arjun gives 300 new items to 1,000 students and keeps only the clusters that emerge from factor analysis; that is Cattell's and Comrey's route, not Edwards's. Option b is right because 3 and 4 are the only two not built by factor analysis. Exam trap: the EPPS controls social desirability so carefully that it looks statistical, but its items were written for Murray's need theory. Key note: the Jenkins Activity Survey also reports subscales (speed and impatience, job involvement, hard-driving competitiveness) found by factor analysis, but its main Type A scale was built to match interview classifications, so it is not classed as a factor-analytic inventory; b stands.", "topic": "Inventory construction strategies", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Minnesota Multiphasic personality inventory yields score on how many clinical scales?", "options": {"a": "12", "b": "15", "c": "10", "d": "11"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) yields scores on 10 clinical scales. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI at the University of Minnesota by empirical criterion keying: an item went onto a scale if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. The original test had 550 true-false items; the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567. The 10 clinical scales are known by number: 1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania and 0 Social Introversion. Eight were keyed against psychiatric groups; scales 5 and 0 were built later from other criterion groups. Scales 1, 2 and 3 form the 'neurotic triad', and 6, 7, 8 and 9 the 'psychotic tetrad'. Scores are T scores with a mean of 50 and an SD of 10; on the MMPI-2 a T of 65 or more is clinically significant, and clinicians read the profile as a pattern, often by two-point code types. Separate validity scales, ? (cannot say), L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (correction), check for faking or careless answering. Example: Rakesh's profile peaks on scales 2 and 7, a 2-7 code type typical of anxious depression. Option c is right because the standard MMPI and MMPI-2 profile has exactly ten clinical scales. Exam trap: 13 is the number of 'basic scales', the 10 clinical scales plus the 3 validity scales L, F and K, and the MMPI-2-RF (Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen, 2008) has 9 Restructured Clinical scales. None of 12, 15 or 11 is the clinical-scale count.", "topic": "MMPI clinical scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Projective tests originated from:", "options": {"a": "Freud's idea that all behaviour was caused by unconscious motivational effects.", "b": "Jung's theory of psychological types.", "c": "The attempt to develop tests that did not rely on language.", "d": "Accidentally spilling ink on a test booklet."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: projective tests originated from the idea of Sigmund Freud (1896) that behaviour is driven by unconscious motives. In his early papers Freud described projection as a defence in which a person attributes his or her own unacceptable feelings or wishes to others. More broadly, psychoanalysis held that unconscious motives shape everything we do, including how we see ambiguous things. Lawrence Frank (1939) turned this into the projective hypothesis and coined the term 'projective methods': when people meet an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus with minimal instructions, they impose their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing on it, revealing what they could not or would not report directly. Hence the tests: Hermann Rorschach's (1921) ten inkblots, Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray's (1935) Thematic Apperception Test of ambiguous pictures, sentence completion and drawing tests. Rorschach did not stumble on his blots; he deliberately designed and selected them, perhaps inspired by klecksography, a popular game of making pictures from blots. Projective tests are prized for clinical richness but criticised for weak reliability and validity; John Exner's (1974) Comprehensive System was a major attempt to standardise Rorschach scoring. Example: shown a picture of a boy staring at a violin, Riya tells a story about a child forced to practise by a harsh father; the clinician wonders whether she is projecting her own feelings about pressure at home. Option a is right because the logic of projection and hidden motives comes from Freud's psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung is a tempting near miss because his word association test (from 1904) was an early projective-style method, but option b names his theory of psychological types, which led to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, an objective self-report inventory.", "topic": "Origins of projective tests", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "In administering the Rorschach, the inquiry refers to that part of the examination in which:", "options": {"a": "The examiner obtains complete demographic information", "b": "The examiner exposes the cards to the test taker for the first time", "c": "The examiner exposes the cards to the test taker for the second time", "d": "The test taker is permitted to ask the examiner any question at all"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach, the inquiry is the part of the examination in which the cards are shown to the test taker for the second time. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published ten inkblots: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) administration has two main phases. In the response phase (free association) the examiner hands over the cards one at a time in a fixed order, asks 'What might this be?', and records every response word for word without suggesting answers. In the inquiry phase, after all ten cards, the examiner goes back through each card and each response to learn where on the blot it was seen (location), what made it look that way, such as form, colour, shading or movement (determinants), and what it was (content), using non-leading questions such as 'What made it look like that?'. An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow, in which the examiner asks directly about popular responses or determinants the person never used. Option c is right because the inquiry depends on re-exposing the same cards so that each response can be scored. For example, if Riya said 'a bat' to Card I, in the inquiry the examiner hands her Card I again and asks her to show where the bat is and what made it look like one. Exam trap: option b, the first exposure, is the response phase; the inquiry always comes after it and never uses leading questions.", "topic": "Rorschach inquiry phase", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Which is not a projective test", "options": {"a": "Word association test", "b": "Rorschach's ink lot test", "c": "Thematic apperception test", "d": "Sentence completion test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The intended answer is the word association test, though this is a somewhat debatable item and deserves care. Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli onto which a person projects unconscious thoughts and feelings. The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach), the Thematic Apperception Test or TAT (developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, in which the person tells stories about ambiguous pictures), and the sentence completion test (such as the Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank by Julian Rotter, in which the person finishes stems like 'I feel...') are all classic projective techniques. The word association test, in which a person responds to a stimulus word with the first word that comes to mind, was developed by Francis Galton and used clinically by Carl Gustav Jung to detect emotionally charged complexes, and it is often itself classified as a projective technique. However, in many entrance-exam answer keys the word association test is treated as the odd one out, being regarded as more of a controlled association or diagnostic-of-complexes method rather than a full storytelling or interpretive projective task, so it is the intended answer here. (The item is contested: word association is arguably projective too.) This item tests classification of projective techniques, and the examiners intend word association as the exception.", "topic": "Lindzey's five families", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term social loafing refers to:", "options": {"a": "Playing when expected to be working.", "b": "Working harder in groups than individually.", "c": "Working harder individually than in groups.", "d": "The opposite of social inhibition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: social loafing means people work harder individually than in groups. Social loafing is the drop in effort that occurs when people's contributions are pooled in a group, compared with when each works alone and can be identified. The first evidence came from the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913): when men pulled on a rope, the group's total pull was less than the sum of the members pulling separately, and each person's share fell as the group grew. Part of this could be poor coordination, but Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) showed that blindfolded people who merely believed others were pulling with them also pulled less, so motivation drops too. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: blindfolded students wearing headphones clapped and shouted less loudly per person in supposed groups than alone. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard when they believe their effort matters and will be noticed. Loafing shrinks when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful or the group is close-knit. Example: in Riya's four-member college project, two members contribute little because the teacher grades only the final report; when the teacher asks each student to submit a named section, everyone works harder. Option c is right because it captures the essence: less effort in the group than alone. Exam trap: option a uses the everyday meaning of 'loafing' (idling), and option d describes social facilitation, a different group effect.", "topic": "Social loafing", "fig": "Bibb Latane"}, {"q": "People are likely to invest less effort in a task when they are working with others. What is this phenomenon called?", "options": {"a": "Social Facilitation", "b": "Social Loafing", "c": "Deindividuation", "d": "Sleeper Effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: investing less effort when working with others on a shared task is social loafing. Social loafing is the tendency of individuals to exert less effort when their contributions are pooled into a group product than when they work alone. Its forerunner was the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913), who found that each person pulled a rope less hard as the team grew. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: students asked to clap or shout as loudly as they could made less noise per person in groups, even when they only believed that others were shouting with them, which showed that motivation was lost, not just coordination. Loafing happens because individual effort cannot be identified or evaluated, responsibility is spread across members, and people feel their own effort makes little difference. It shrinks when each person's output is identifiable, the task matters to them, the group is cohesive or each member has a distinct role. Example: in a group project, Kabir does very little because he knows the teacher will mark only the final report, not each member's part. Option b is right because the stem describes reduced individual effort in a group, the definition of social loafing. Exam trap: social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965) is the look-alike, but it runs the other way: the presence of others improves performance on simple, well-learned tasks, especially when each person's effort is visible.", "topic": "Social loafing and deindividuation", "fig": "Bibb Latane"}, {"q": "Cognitive dissonance theory was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Festinger", "b": "Bandura", "c": "Ellis", "d": "Piaget"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive dissonance theory was developed by Leon Festinger. Festinger (1919 to 1989), an American social psychologist and a student of Kurt Lewin, proposed it in A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance (1957). Dissonance is the uncomfortable tension of holding two inconsistent cognitions, especially when behaviour contradicts an attitude. Because the tension is unpleasant, people try to reduce it: by changing the attitude, changing the behaviour, adding consonant cognitions or trivialising the inconsistency. Dissonance grows with the importance of the cognitions and the ratio of dissonant to consonant ones. The classic test is Festinger and James Carlsmith's (1959) study: students did a boring task, then were paid either $1 or $20 to tell the next participant it was fun. The $1 group later rated the task as more enjoyable, because $1 was too little to justify lying, so they changed their attitude to fit their behaviour; the $20 group had an external justification. Other paradigms include effort justification (Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills, 1959) and post-decision dissonance (Jack Brehm, 1956). Example: a smoker who knows smoking causes cancer tells himself 'my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety'. Option a is right because Festinger named and formulated cognitive dissonance theory. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's self-perception theory (1967) explains the same $1 and $20 result without any tension, so if a question asks for the rival explanation of dissonance findings, the answer is Bem, not Festinger.", "topic": "Festinger's cognitive dissonance theory", "fig": "Leon Festinger"}, {"q": "According to psychodynamic theory, individuals are sometimes motivated to respond to the world through the perspectives they have learnt from other important people in their developmental past. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Object permanence theory", "b": "Distorted perspective theory", "c": "Object-relations theory", "d": "Ego defence theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: this is object relations theory. In psychodynamic language an 'object' is not a thing but a person, or part of a person, towards whom feelings and wishes are directed, usually the mother or another early caregiver. Object relations theory holds that the infant takes in (internalises) mental pictures of the self and of important others from early relationships, along with the feelings that link them. These internal objects then act as templates: later in life the person sees and responds to partners, bosses and therapists through the perspectives learnt from those early figures. The school grew in Britain after Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960) described splitting of the world into good and bad objects and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; W. R. D. Fairbairn (1952) argued that the infant is object-seeking rather than pleasure-seeking; Donald Winnicott (1953) described the transitional object and the good-enough mother; Otto Kernberg (1975) linked split internal object relations to borderline personality organisation. Example: Kabir grew up with a harsh, critical father, and as an adult he hears criticism in every neutral remark from his manager, reacting to the internal father image rather than to the manager. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this internalised, relationship-based template learnt from important others. Exam trap: 'object permanence' shares the word 'object' but belongs to Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) sensorimotor stage, where the infant learns that things go on existing when out of sight.", "topic": "Object relations theory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Replacing the fragmented self with the cohesive self is the primary developmental and therapeutic goal of:", "options": {"a": "Kernberg.", "b": "Kohut.", "c": "Malan.", "d": "Horowitz."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: replacing a fragmented self with a cohesive self is the central developmental and therapeutic goal of Heinz Kohut's self psychology. Heinz Kohut (1971), in The Analysis of the Self and later The Restoration of the Self (1977), argued that narcissism has its own healthy line of development, not only a pathological one. The child's self grows through selfobjects: people experienced as part of the self because they perform functions the child cannot yet perform alone. Three selfobject needs must be met: mirroring (the parent's delighted response that confirms the child's 'Look at me!'), idealising (merging with a calm, strong parent) and twinship (feeling essentially alike with others). When parents respond well but imperfectly, each bearable failure lets the child take over the soothing function, which Kohut called transmuting internalisation, and this builds a cohesive self. When mirroring and idealising fail chronically, the self stays fragmented and enfeebled, and narcissistic problems follow. In therapy the analyst offers empathic attunement; the client forms selfobject transferences (mirror, idealising, twinship), and the therapist's small, understood empathic failures repeat the healthy process, so the self becomes cohesive. Imagine Kabir, who feels he falls apart whenever his boss ignores him; a Kohutian therapist would read this as a fragile, fragmenting self and work through empathy to strengthen its cohesion. Option b is right because 'fragmented self' and 'cohesive self' are Kohut's signature terms. Exam trap: Otto Kernberg (1975) also wrote on narcissism and borderline states, so a is the near miss; but Kernberg's goal is integrating split good and bad images of self and others, not building self cohesion through selfobjects.", "topic": "Kohut's self psychology", "fig": "Heinz Kohut"}, {"q": "The concept of 'borderline personality organization' was given by:", "options": {"a": "Heinz Kohut.", "b": "Margaret Mahler.", "c": "Carl Jung.", "d": "Otto Kernberg."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the concept of borderline personality organisation was given by Otto Kernberg (1967, 1975). Otto Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, combined Melanie Klein's (1946) object relations theory with ego psychology. He described three levels of personality organisation, judged on identity, level of defences and reality testing. Neurotic organisation has an integrated identity, higher-level defences such as repression, and intact reality testing. Borderline organisation has identity diffusion (an unstable, contradictory sense of self and others), primitive defences centred on splitting (with projective identification, idealisation, devaluation, denial and omnipotence), and reality testing that is mostly intact but can slip under stress. Psychotic organisation has identity diffusion, primitive defences and a loss of reality testing. Borderline organisation is wider than the DSM diagnosis of borderline personality disorder: it also takes in narcissistic, antisocial, paranoid and schizoid personalities. Otto Kernberg traced it to intense early aggression that keeps 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others apart, and later developed transference-focused psychotherapy to treat it. Example: Meera praises her new manager as the best person she has ever worked with, then, after one critical remark, calls her cruel and worthless, yet she stays fully in touch with reality; this is splitting with intact reality testing, the borderline level. Option d is right because borderline personality organisation is Otto Kernberg's structural model. Exam trap: Heinz Kohut (1971) and Otto Kernberg both wrote on narcissism in the 1970s and famously disagreed, and Margaret Mahler's (1975) rapprochement crisis is often linked to the origins of borderline problems, so all three names look plausible; the structural term itself is Otto Kernberg's alone.", "topic": "Kernberg's borderline organisation", "fig": "Otto Kernberg"}, {"q": "In the early work of Hartshorne and May, designed to assess children's moral behavior, it was found that:", "options": {"a": "Moral children always adhere to generally recognized moral standards.", "b": "Children seem to apply situational morality, acting differently in separate situations involving the same moral principle.", "c": "Moral children never cheat.", "d": "Boys generally have stronger conscience than girls."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928) found that children's honesty depended heavily on the situation, so the same child could cheat in one setting and stay honest in another that tested the same moral principle. In the Character Education Inquiry at Teachers College, Columbia University, reported in Studies in Deceit, they asked a simple question: is honesty a general trait that a child carries everywhere? They gave thousands of American schoolchildren disguised chances to lie, cheat and steal in class tests, party games, athletic contests and home tasks: for instance, marking their own test papers when they could secretly change answers, or keeping coins from a box. Almost every child cheated somewhere, and few cheated everywhere. Honesty in one situation predicted honesty in another only weakly, and knowing the moral rule, or attending character education or Sunday school, barely predicted conduct. What mattered was the situation: the risk of being caught, the effort needed and what classmates were doing. They called this the doctrine of specificity. Walter Mischel (1968) later built on it in his critique of trait consistency, which started the person-situation debate; Seymour Epstein (1979) replied that behaviour averaged over many occasions is far more stable. Example: Riya peeks at a classmate's answer sheet when the teacher steps out, yet on the way home returns the extra change a shopkeeper gave her by mistake. Option b is right because it states the inquiry's central finding of situation-specific (situational) morality. Exam trap: the absolute words \"always\" and \"never\" in a and c describe exactly the consistent moral character that the study failed to find.", "topic": "Hartshorne and May: situational honesty", "fig": "Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five Traits?", "options": {"a": "sense of humour", "b": "openness to experience", "c": "conscientiousness", "d": "extraversion"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is sense of humour, which is NOT one of the Big Five traits. The Big Five, or five-factor model, developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa, Robert McCrae, and Lewis Goldberg, describes personality in terms of five broad dimensions summarised by the acronym OCEAN: Openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), Extraversion (outgoing and energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (compassionate and cooperative versus antagonistic), and Neuroticism (emotionally reactive and anxious versus stable). Among the options, openness to experience (b), conscientiousness (c), and extraversion (d) are all genuine factors within this model. Sense of humour (a), while an important and appealing personal quality, is not one of the five factors; it is a specific characteristic that may relate loosely to traits like extraversion or openness but does not constitute a distinct dimension in the model. The trap lies in the fact that sense of humour sounds like a plausible personality descriptor, but the Big Five is a specific empirical taxonomy, and only the five OCEAN dimensions qualify. Knowing the exact five factors matters because the Big Five is the most widely researched and cross-culturally validated framework in personality psychology, and students must be able to distinguish the genuine factors from everyday trait words that do not belong to the model.", "topic": "The Big Five: discovery and the five factors", "fig": "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae"}, {"q": "The 16PF test is based on:", "options": {"a": "Eysenck's theory", "b": "Cattell's theory", "c": "Allport's theory", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the 16PF is based on Raymond Cattell's theory. Raymond Cattell, a British-born psychologist who worked in the United States, built his trait theory by factor analysis, a statistical method that finds the few underlying factors that explain the correlations among many measures. He started from the trait words Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had collected from the dictionary, merged synonyms, and factor-analysed three kinds of data: L-data (life records and observer ratings), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (behaviour on objective tests). He separated surface traits, clusters of behaviour that go together, from source traits, the deeper underlying causes. He found 16 source traits, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire, the 16PF (1949), such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance and liveliness, each scored as a bipolar dimension. Factor-analysing the 16 again gives broader second-order (global) factors such as extraversion and anxiety. Example: Riya's 16PF profile shows high warmth and liveliness but low emotional stability, which helps her counsellor see a sociable young woman who is easily upset under pressure. Option b is right because Cattell designed the 16PF to measure the source traits of his own factor-analytic theory. Exam trap: Hans Eysenck (a) also used factor analysis, but he arrived at only three broad dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism), measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975); Gordon Allport (c) preferred the idiographic study of the individual and did not build the 16PF.", "topic": "16PF and Cattell", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are ______", "options": {"a": "Sympathetic and parasympathetic", "b": "Voluntary and involuntary", "c": "Thalamus and hypothalamus", "d": "Cerebrospinal and cortical"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the two parts of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. The nervous system splits into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system. The peripheral system has a somatic branch, which carries sensation and voluntary movement of skeletal muscles, and an autonomic branch, which runs the heart, glands, blood vessels and the smooth muscle of the gut largely without conscious control. The British physiologist John Newport Langley named the autonomic nervous system in 1898. Its sympathetic division, arising from the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, prepares the body for action in what Walter B. Cannon called the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils dilate, digestion slows and glucose is released. The parasympathetic division, arising from the brainstem and sacral cord, does the rest-and-digest work: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and restarts digestion. Picture a near-miss on a scooter: the pounding heart is sympathetic, and the slow calming afterwards is parasympathetic. Option a is right because these two divisions are the standard textbook parts of the autonomic system. Exam trap: voluntary versus involuntary is the split between somatic and autonomic, not a division within the autonomic system; some modern texts add a third, enteric division in the gut wall, but exam keys expect sympathetic and parasympathetic.", "topic": "Divisions of the autonomic nervous system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Find out the correct answers from the codes given below: Topographical organization: 1. Applies to sensory cortical areas 2. High degree of topographical organisation shows in the auditory pathway 3. Fibres in this pathway originate from the ganglion cells of the retina 4. Fibres then synapse at the lateral geniculate body of the hypothalamus", "options": {"a": "1, 3 and 4 are correct", "b": "2 and 3 are correct", "c": "1 and 3 are correct", "d": "1 and 4 are correct"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 3 are correct. Topographical (topographic) organisation means that neighbouring points on a sensory surface are represented by neighbouring points in the brain, so each sensory cortex carries an orderly map: retinotopic in vision (the primary visual cortex maps the retina, with the small fovea given a disproportionately large share), tonotopic in hearing (each sound frequency has its own place in auditory cortex) and somatotopic in touch (the body map, or homunculus, of the postcentral gyrus). So statement 1, that it applies to sensory cortical areas, is true. Statement 3 describes the visual pathway: the fibres of the optic nerve are the axons of the retina's ganglion cells, which carry the signal out of the eye, so it is true. Statement 4 is false: optic tract fibres synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (body) of the thalamus, not of the hypothalamus, before the optic radiations carry the signal to the striate cortex (area 17). The hypothalamus receives only a small retinal input, to its suprachiasmatic nucleus, for setting circadian rhythms. Example: a small stroke in one spot of Arjun's left primary visual cortex blinds one matching patch of his right visual field, because the map keeps retinal neighbours together. Option c is right because 1 and 3 are true and 4 is false. Exam trap: the lateral geniculate body sounds hypothalamic because both lie in the diencephalon, but the geniculate bodies are thalamic relays (lateral for vision, medial for hearing). Key note: statement 2 names the auditory pathway, which is tonotopic in reality, but statement 3 then says 'this pathway' starts in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot both describe the same pathway; the paired statements describe the visual pathway and c (1 and 3) is the only fully correct set; c stands.", "topic": "Topographic maps, visual pathway", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which area is known as the satiety centre?", "options": {"a": "Lateral hypothalamus", "b": "Ventromedial hypothalamus", "c": "Cerebrum", "d": "Basal ganglia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "Twentieth-century hunger research produced the dual-centre model, which entrance exams still test. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that bilateral lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) in rats caused hyperphagia (overeating) and marked obesity; later studies showed that stimulating the VMH stopped eating. Bal Anand and John Brobeck (1951) found the reverse for the lateral hypothalamus (LH): lesions produced aphagia (refusal to eat), sometimes fatal without tube feeding; later work added adipsia and showed that LH stimulation made sated animals eat. Eliot Stellar (1954) combined these into the theory that the LH is the hunger or feeding centre and the VMH is the satiety centre. VMH obesity has a dynamic phase of rapid weight gain, then a static phase in which the animal defends its new, higher weight; lesioned rats also become finicky eaters. John P. J. Pinel's Biopsychology warns that the model is too simple: VMH lesions raise insulin and fat storage, part of their effect comes from damage to nearby structures such as the paraventricular nucleus, and LH lesions cause wider motor problems. Current models stress the arcuate nucleus, where leptin from fat cells and stomach ghrelin (a hunger signal) act, while gut cholecystokinin signals satiety mainly via the vagus nerve. Memory aid: lesion the Lateral and the animal Lacks appetite; lesion the Ventromedial and it Very Much overeats. Exam neighbours: the suprachiasmatic nucleus is the circadian clock, and the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make vasopressin and oxytocin. Option b is right because the ventromedial hypothalamus is the structure classically named the satiety centre.", "topic": "The hypothalamus and the arcuate nucleus", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following chemical substances is NOT classified as an opioid?", "options": {"a": "Cocaine.", "b": "Codeine.", "c": "Heroin.", "d": "Morphine."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: cocaine is not an opioid; it is a stimulant. Opioids are substances that act on the brain's opioid receptors, chiefly the mu receptor, the same receptors used by the body's own endorphins and enkephalins. They relieve pain, suppress coughing and cause euphoria and drowsiness, and in overdose they stop breathing; naloxone reverses an overdose by blocking the receptors. Opioids come in three groups. Natural opiates are taken from the sap of the opium poppy: morphine and codeine, a weaker relative used for cough and mild pain. Semi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically changing a natural one: heroin is diacetylmorphine, morphine with two acetyl groups added so that it enters the brain faster. Fully synthetic opioids, such as methadone and fentanyl, are built in the laboratory. Cocaine comes from a different plant, the coca leaf of South America. It blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (and serotonin), so these transmitters pile up in the synapse, giving alertness, confidence and euphoria, followed by a crash; heavy use can cause paranoia and cocaine psychosis. It is also a local anaesthetic, first used in eye surgery by Carl Koller (1884). Example: Arjun, who misuses codeine cough syrup, becomes drowsy with pinpoint pupils; his friend Kabir, who snorts cocaine at a party, becomes restless and talkative with dilated pupils. Option a is right because cocaine is a stimulant that works on monoamine reuptake, not on opioid receptors. Exam trap: in law, cocaine has long been grouped with heroin and morphine as a 'narcotic' drug, and 'cocaine' sounds like 'codeine', so students lump it with the opioids; pharmacologically the two classes have opposite effects.", "topic": "Opioids versus stimulants", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Most current Alzheimer treatments work by altering levels of which neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Dopamine.", "b": "GABA.", "c": "Noradrenaline.", "d": "Acetylcholine."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: most current Alzheimer treatments work by raising levels of acetylcholine. The cholinergic hypothesis, set out by Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982), grew from the finding that Alzheimer brains lose cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, at the base of the forebrain, which send acetylcholine to the cortex and hippocampus. Acetylcholine is vital for attention, learning and memory, so its loss fits the memory decline. Normally the enzyme acetylcholinesterase breaks acetylcholine down in the synapse soon after release. Cholinesterase inhibitors block that enzyme, so acetylcholine lingers longer and signals more strongly. Three are in wide use: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. They modestly improve or steady symptoms for a time in mild to moderate disease, but they do not stop the underlying plaques and tangles. The exception is memantine, used in moderate to severe disease, which blocks NMDA glutamate receptors and so protects neurons from excess glutamate stimulation. Newer antibody drugs that clear amyloid act on the disease process itself rather than on any transmitter. Example: Kabir's grandmother is diagnosed with mild Alzheimer's disease and started on donepezil; her neurologist explains that the tablet keeps more acetylcholine available at her synapses. Option d is right because the main drug class for Alzheimer's disease targets acetylcholine. Exam trap: dopamine (option a) is the transmitter targeted in Parkinson's disease (L-dopa) and schizophrenia, so students who mix up the neurodegenerative diseases pick it; GABA loss marks Huntington's disease.", "topic": "Cholinergic treatment of Alzheimer's", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What hormone secreted by the pineal gland increases when periods of darkness begin and promotes a feeling of drowsiness?", "options": {"a": "Testosterone.", "b": "Melatonin.", "c": "Cortisol.", "d": "Epinephrine."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, rises when darkness begins and promotes drowsiness. The pineal gland is a small, pine-cone-shaped gland near the centre of the brain; Rene Descartes (1649) thought it was the seat of the soul. Aaron Lerner (1958) and colleagues isolated its hormone, melatonin, from cattle pineal glands. Melatonin is made from serotonin and is released mainly at night. Its timing is set by the body's master clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus. Light falling on special melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract; in daylight the SCN keeps the pineal quiet, and as darkness falls the brake comes off, so melatonin climbs through the evening, peaks in the middle of the night and falls by morning. Rising melatonin lowers body temperature and alertness and signals 'night' to the body, so it works as a darkness hormone that opens the gate to sleep rather than as a strong sedative. Bright screens at night can suppress it and delay sleep, and low-dose melatonin is used to shift the clock in jet lag and delayed sleep phase. Example: Aditya scrolls on his phone until 2 a.m.; the bright light holds back his melatonin, so he lies awake even though he is tired. Option b is right because melatonin is the pineal hormone tied to darkness and drowsiness. Exam trap: cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, so it tempts; but cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, peaks in the early morning and helps wake us, the opposite timing.", "topic": "Melatonin and the pineal gland", "fig": ""}, {"q": "White matter is primarily composed of", "options": {"a": "Myelinated axons.", "b": "Myelinated dendrites.", "c": "Cell bodies.", "d": "Unmyelinated axons."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "White matter gets its name from the pale, glossy appearance of myelin, the lipid-rich sheath wrapped around axons by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system (and by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system; Pio del Rio Hortega named oligodendroglia in 1921 and in 1922 likened them to Schwann cells as the myelin-forming cells of the CNS). Myelin acts as electrical insulation. Because current cannot leak across the insulated segments, the action potential effectively jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next, a process Ichiji Tasaki described as saltatory conduction. This dramatically speeds signalling and is why long-distance tracts, such as the corpus callosum or spinal columns, are white. Grey matter, by contrast, is where the computation happens: it is packed with neuronal cell bodies, dendrites, and synapses, and its lack of heavy myelination gives it a duller, greyish-brown tone. So the organising principle is simple: grey matter is where neurons integrate information; white matter is the myelinated wiring that carries the results elsewhere. When you read that a lesion is in white matter (as in multiple sclerosis, an autoimmune demyelinating disease), you should picture damaged insulation on axonal cables, producing slowed or blocked conduction rather than direct loss of cell bodies. For the exam, anchor on the phrase myelinated axons whenever white matter is described.", "topic": "Grey matter, white matter and the spinal cord", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Choose the odd one for pattern of sympathetic activation during emotions:", "options": {"a": "Increased heart rate", "b": "Pupil constriction", "c": "Lowered skin resistance", "d": "Increased breathing rate"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: pupil constriction is the odd one out, because it is a parasympathetic response, while the other three are signs of sympathetic activation. The autonomic nervous system has two branches that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for action, the fight or flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). During strong emotions such as fear or anger it raises heart rate and blood pressure, speeds breathing and widens the airways, dilates the pupils (mydriasis) to let in more light, releases glucose for energy, slows digestion and increases sweating. Sweat is salty water that conducts electricity, so sweating lowers skin resistance (raises skin conductance); this galvanic skin response is one of the channels a polygraph records. The parasympathetic branch is the rest and digest system: it slows the heart, stimulates digestion and constricts the pupils (miosis) through fibres in the oculomotor nerve. Example: when a dog suddenly lunges at Meera on the street, her heart pounds, she breathes fast, her palms sweat and her pupils widen; once she is safe at home, parasympathetic activity slows her heart and her pupils return to normal. Option b is right because constriction of the pupil is the parasympathetic opposite of the sympathetic dilation seen in emotion. Exam trap: \"lowered skin resistance\" sounds like a decrease and so like calming, but it reflects more sweating and is a classic sign of sympathetic arousal.", "topic": "Sympathetic arousal in emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A part of the brain that sends signals \"alert\" to higher centres of the brain in response to incoming message is:", "options": {"a": "Amygdala", "b": "Hippocampus", "c": "Limbic system", "d": "Reticular formation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the reticular formation alerts the higher centres of the brain to incoming messages. The reticular formation ('reticulum' means little net) is a net-like column of neurons running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain. Sensory pathways heading for the cortex send side branches (collaterals) into it, so it samples nearly all incoming information. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), projects through the thalamus to the whole cortex and keeps it awake and alert. The classic evidence came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrically stimulating the brainstem reticular formation in a drowsy or anaesthetised cat changed the slow, synchronised EEG of sleep into the fast, desynchronised EEG of alert waking. Earlier, Frederic Bremer (1935) had shown that cutting the brainstem at the midbrain (cerveau isolé) left the forebrain in permanent sleep-like EEG, while cutting lower, between medulla and spinal cord (encéphale isolé), left normal sleep and waking. Damage to the RAS causes coma. The system also filters input, passing on novel or important signals and damping repetitive ones, and its descending fibres adjust muscle tone and reflexes. Example: Meera dozes through the drone of the fan in a lecture, but jolts awake the moment the teacher calls her name; her reticular formation passed on the important signal and roused her cortex. Option d is right because sending an 'alert' signal to the cortex in response to incoming input is the defining job of the reticular activating system. Exam trap: the limbic system and amygdala also respond to incoming signals, but they add emotional meaning; general arousal and wakefulness belong to the reticular formation.", "topic": "Reticular activating system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Erik Erikson’s psychosocial theory, the key challenge in adolescence is :", "options": {"a": "Trust vs. Mistrust", "b": "Industry vs. Inferiority", "c": "Identity vs. Role Confusion", "d": "Autonomy vs. Shame"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "According to Erikson's psychosocial theory, the key challenge in adolescence is identity versus role confusion, option c. This is the fifth stage, occurring roughly between ages twelve and eighteen. The central task is for the adolescent to develop a coherent and stable sense of self, an identity, by integrating their various roles, values, beliefs, talents, and goals into a unified whole and answering the question of who they are and what they want to become. Adolescents experiment with different roles, ideologies, and relationships in a period Erikson called a psychosocial moratorium, a sanctioned time of exploration before committing to adult roles. Successful resolution yields a firm sense of identity and the ego strength of fidelity, the ability to commit to values and others. Failure produces role confusion, an unclear, fragmented, or unstable sense of self, uncertainty about one's place in the world. James Marcia later extended this into four identity statuses based on the presence of exploration and commitment: identity achievement, moratorium, foreclosure, and diffusion. The exam mapping is adolescence equals identity versus role confusion, making option c correct over the infancy, school-age, and toddler stages listed.", "topic": "The eight stages", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "Elizabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of:", "options": {"a": "Eating disorders.", "b": "Adolescent behavior.", "c": "Coping with stress.", "d": "Death and dying."}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969) is known for her research on death and dying. She was a Swiss-born psychiatrist who interviewed terminally ill patients at a Chicago hospital. In her book On Death and Dying she proposed five stages that people often pass through after learning that their illness is terminal: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live to see my son's wedding'), depression (grieving the losses to come) and acceptance (a calm, often quiet readiness). The mnemonic is DABDA. Her work changed medical care: it encouraged doctors to talk honestly with dying patients and supported the hospice movement, which Cicely Saunders (1967) had begun with St Christopher's Hospice in London. Later research found that people do not move through the stages in a fixed order; they skip, repeat or mix them, so the stages are best read as common reactions rather than a timetable. The model was also stretched, with little evidence, to the grief of the bereaved. Example: when Meera, 58, is told her cancer has spread, she first insists the scans are wrong (denial), later snaps at the nurses (anger), and weeks later promises to give up sweets if she can see her granddaughter born (bargaining). Option d is right because her classic book and five-stage model are about how people face their own death. Exam trap: coping with stress (option c) is the near miss, because dying is a stressor; but stress and coping research belongs to Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), not Kubler-Ross.", "topic": "Kübler-Ross stages of dying", "fig": "Elisabeth Kübler-Ross"}, {"q": "Which of the following expresses a correct sequence in cognitive development?", "options": {"a": "Voluntary movement, intuitive thought, object permanence.", "b": "Concrete operations, mental representation, information processing.", "c": "Mental representation, symbolic thought, intuitive thought.", "d": "Conservation, object permanence, intuitive thought."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, mental representation appears first, at the end of infancy, then symbolic thought, then intuitive thought. Jean Piaget (1952) described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards), passed through in an invariant order. The sensorimotor stage has six substages. In the last one, at about 18 to 24 months, the child can for the first time represent objects and actions in the mind instead of only acting on them; Piaget called this the beginning of thought, and it makes deferred imitation and full object permanence possible. The preoperational stage is then divided into two substages. In the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4 years) the child lets one thing stand for another: words, pretend play and drawings. In the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7 years) the child asks endless \"why\" questions and reasons by intuition rather than logic, still unable to conserve or to explain answers. Example: at 20 months Arjun copies his father's shaving the next day (mental representation); at 3 he uses a banana as a phone (symbolic thought); at 5 he insists the taller glass holds more juice but cannot say why (intuitive thought). Option c is right because it is the only option that lists achievements in the order in which Piaget's child reaches them. Exam trap: each distractor mixes real Piagetian terms in the wrong positions. Voluntary movement and object permanence belong to infancy, so object permanence cannot follow intuitive thought; conservation is a concrete operational gain that cannot precede object permanence; and information processing is a separate theory, not a Piagetian stage.", "topic": "Piaget: sequence of cognitive development", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "The term Clinical Psychology was first proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Witmer", "b": "Binet", "c": "Cattell", "d": "Weber"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Lightner Witmer first proposed the term clinical psychology. Lightner Witmer (1867 to 1956) studied with James McKeen Cattell at the University of Pennsylvania, took his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt (founder of the Leipzig laboratory, 1879) in 1892, and then returned to Pennsylvania. In March 1896 he opened the first psychological clinic there, after a schoolteacher brought him a boy who could not learn to spell. Later that year he described his 'clinical method' to the American Psychological Association, and in 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic, whose first issue carried his article 'Clinical Psychology', the first printed use of the name. His clinic worked mainly with children who had learning and behaviour problems, combining assessment with remedial teaching, so early clinical psychology was closer to what we now call school psychology and special education than to adult psychotherapy. For founding both the name and the first clinic, Witmer is called the father of clinical psychology. Option a is right because Witmer coined the term and set up the first clinic in which it was practised. Exam trap: Alfred Binet and James McKeen Cattell are famous for mental testing, which clinical psychologists later relied on heavily, so they look like founders of the field; but neither of them named it. Ernst Heinrich Weber is a psychophysicist with no link to clinics. Remember the pair: 1896, the first clinic; 1907, the name in print.", "topic": "Founder of clinical psychology", "fig": "Lightner Witmer"}, {"q": "Self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation involving which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Clients measuring their own galvanic skin responses.", "b": "Clients measuring their own heart rate", "c": "Clients observing and recording their own behaviour", "d": "Clients recording their dreams"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is clients observing and recording their own behaviour. Self-monitoring is an assessment and intervention technique, central to behavioural and cognitive-behavioural approaches, in which clients systematically observe, track, and record their own behaviours, thoughts, feelings, and the surrounding circumstances (antecedents and consequences) as they occur in everyday life. For example, a client might keep a diary of every cigarette smoked, each panic attack and its triggers, food intake, or automatic negative thoughts. Self-monitoring serves two purposes. As assessment, it yields ecologically valid, real-time data about the frequency, intensity, and context of the target behaviour that would be hard to capture in the clinic and less subject to retrospective memory bias. As intervention, the very act of monitoring often produces reactivity, meaning the behaviour changes simply because it is being observed (undesirable behaviours tend to decrease and desirable ones increase), so it is therapeutic in itself. It fits within the functional analysis tradition of behaviour therapy associated with B.F. Skinner and is a staple of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy through thought records. The distractors describe physiological self-measurement (galvanic skin response, heart rate), which belongs to biofeedback, or dream recording, which belongs to psychodynamic work, none of which is behavioural self-observation. This item tests the definition of self-monitoring.", "topic": "Relaxation, biofeedback and self-management", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which therapy technique is based on the concept of \"unconditional positive regard\"?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Client-centered therapy", "c": "Behavioral therapy", "d": "Gestalt therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: client-centred therapy is built on the concept of unconditional positive regard. It was developed by the American psychologist Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), first as non-directive counselling in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and then as client-centred therapy in his 1951 book of that name; it is now usually called person-centred therapy. Rogers held that every person has an actualising tendency, a drive to grow. Problems arise from conditions of worth: a child who is loved only when she meets others' standards learns to deny feelings that do not fit, creating incongruence between self-concept and experience. In a 1957 paper Rogers named the 'necessary and sufficient' conditions for change, three of which are the therapist's core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance of the client as a person, without judgement or conditions) and accurate empathic understanding. When the client feels accepted, the need to defend the self falls and he can own denied feelings. Example: a client admits he resents his ill mother. The therapist neither praises nor criticises but accepts him and reflects, 'You feel angry, and guilty about feeling angry', so he can explore the feeling rather than hide it. Option b is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions and the defining attitude of client-centred therapy. Exam trap: unconditional positive regard is an attitude, not a technique, and it does not mean approving of every behaviour; it means valuing the person, which separates it from praise or reinforcement in behaviour therapy.", "topic": "Unconditional positive regard: Rogers", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Token Economy is an influential intervention based upon:", "options": {"a": "Operant conditioning.", "b": "Implicit learning.", "c": "Classical conditioning.", "d": "Client centred therapy."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the token economy is based on operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938) in The Behavior of Organisms, holds that behaviour is shaped by its consequences: a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more frequent. A token economy applies this to a whole group, such as a hospital ward, a classroom or a hostel. Staff define clear target behaviours (making one's bed, attending a group, finishing homework), and each time one occurs the person receives a token: a point, a star or a plastic chip. Tokens have no value in themselves; they are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers that are later exchanged for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. Because they can buy many things they act as generalised reinforcers, like money, and they bridge the delay between a behaviour and the final reward. Tokens can also be taken back for problem behaviour, which is response cost. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) ran the classic programme at Anna State Hospital, Illinois, where long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs. When tokens were handed out regardless of work, the work almost stopped; when the contingency returned, so did the work. Example: in a children's home, Kabir earns a star each morning he is dressed and ready for school on time, and ten stars buy an extra hour of cricket on Sunday. Option a is right because the method works through contingent reinforcement, the heart of operant conditioning. Exam trap: tokens gain their value by being paired with backup rewards, which sounds like classical pairing, but the programme as a whole changes voluntary behaviour through its consequences, so it is operant; and Skinner supplied the principles, while Ayllon and Azrin built the token economy.", "topic": "Token economy", "fig": "Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT an example of primary prevention of mental illness?", "options": {"a": "Drug Abuse Resistance Education (DARE)", "b": "Genetic Screening", "c": "Narcotics Anonymous (NA)", "d": "Prenatal health care"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Narcotics Anonymous is not primary prevention; it serves people who already have an addiction, so it is tertiary prevention. Gerald Caplan (1964), in Principles of Preventive Psychiatry, adapted the public health levels of prevention to mental illness. Primary prevention aims to stop a disorder from arising at all, lowering its incidence (new cases) through education, stress reduction, prenatal care, nutrition and genetic counselling. Secondary prevention aims at early detection and prompt treatment of cases that have already begun, shortening them and lowering prevalence, as in screening schoolchildren for depression. Tertiary prevention aims to reduce the disability and relapse of people who are already ill, through rehabilitation, aftercare and self-help groups. Narcotics Anonymous, founded in 1953 on the twelve-step model of Alcoholics Anonymous, is a self-help fellowship for people already dependent on drugs, helping them stay abstinent and rebuild their lives: relapse prevention and rehabilitation. The other three options act before any disorder exists. DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education), begun in Los Angeles schools in 1983, teaches children to resist drugs before they start using them. Genetic screening and counselling identify carriers or risks so that a disorder can be avoided. Prenatal care protects the foetus from infection, malnutrition, alcohol and birth complications that can cause intellectual disability. Arjun, recovering from heroin dependence and attending weekly NA meetings, is receiving tertiary prevention; his younger sister, in a DARE class at school, is receiving primary prevention. Option c is right because NA begins only after addiction exists. Exam trap: DARE tempts as the 'not primary' answer because evaluations found it largely ineffective, but effectiveness does not change its level: it still targets children who are not yet using.", "topic": "Levels of prevention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Behaviour therapies apply learning principles to:", "options": {"a": "Identify and treat the underlying causes of dysfunction", "b": "Improve learning and insight", "c": "Eliminate the unwanted behaviour", "d": "Improve communication and social sensitivity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: behaviour therapies apply the principles of learning to eliminate unwanted behaviour (and to build desirable behaviour in its place). Behaviour therapy treats problem behaviour as learned, so it can be unlearned or replaced. It grew in the 1950s from three roots: the reciprocal inhibition and systematic desensitisation of Joseph Wolpe (1958) in South Africa, the group of Hans Eysenck (1952) at the Maudsley Hospital in London, and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953) in the United States. Behaviour therapists focus on the symptom itself, here and now, instead of hunting for hidden causes; Eysenck's motto was that if you get rid of the symptom you have eliminated the neurosis. A functional analysis asks what triggers the behaviour and what consequences keep it going. Classical conditioning methods include systematic desensitisation, exposure, flooding and aversion therapy; operant methods include token economies, shaping, extinction and time out; modelling, from Albert Bandura (1969), adds learning by observation. Example: Kabir is terrified of dogs. His therapist builds an anxiety hierarchy from a picture of a puppy up to stroking a real dog, and pairs each step with relaxation until the fear response is gone. Option c is right because the stated goal of behaviour therapy is to change, and usually remove, the unwanted behaviour through relearning. Exam trap: option a, treating the underlying causes of dysfunction, is the aim of psychoanalysis. Behaviourists argued that the symptoms are the disorder, and research has found little evidence that removing a symptom simply produces a new one (symptom substitution).", "topic": "Aim of behaviour therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Illnesses that seem to result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called:", "options": {"a": "Somatic", "b": "Hysterical", "c": "Psychosomatic", "d": "Conversion disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: illnesses that come from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called psychosomatic. 'Psychosomatic' joins psyche (mind) and soma (body); the term is credited to the German physician Johann Christian Heinroth (1818). A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical disease, with measurable tissue damage or physiological change, whose onset or course is worsened by psychological factors such as stress, conflict or emotion. Franz Alexander (1950), in Psychosomatic Medicine, proposed that a specific unconscious conflict acting on a vulnerable organ produced each of seven classic psychosomatic disorders, the 'holy seven': peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, bronchial asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. The specificity idea failed (most ulcers proved to be caused by Helicobacter pylori bacteria), but the broader point survived: chronic stress raises cortisol, suppresses immunity and worsens many diseases. George Engel (1977) widened this into the biopsychosocial model. DSM-5 now calls the category 'psychological factors affecting other medical conditions', and ICD-10 codes it F54. Example: Kabir has genuine asthma, confirmed by lung function tests; his attacks become much more frequent in the weeks before his board exams and after quarrels at home. The disease is real, and stress is making it worse. Option c is right because the stem stresses an interaction of physical and psychological factors, which defines psychosomatic illness. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary or 'all in the mind'; there is real bodily disease. In conversion disorder, by contrast, the symptom (paralysis, blindness, seizures) has no physical disease behind it, which is why examiners pair the two.", "topic": "Psychosomatic disorders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these has been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence?", "options": {"a": "Criminal behaviour leading to a prison sentence.", "b": "Psychosis.", "c": "Antisocial personality disorder.", "d": "All of these."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: criminal behaviour leading to prison, psychosis and antisocial personality disorder have all been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence. Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive, persistent pattern of violating others' basic rights or major age-appropriate rules: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations such as truancy and running away. DSM requires 3 of 15 criteria in the past 12 months and separates a childhood-onset type (a symptom before 10) from an adolescent-onset type. The links run in three directions. First, antisocial personality disorder (ASPD): DSM requires evidence of conduct disorder before age 15 for an adult ASPD diagnosis, and Lee Robins (1966), in Deviant Children Grown Up, found that adult antisocial personality almost never appeared without childhood conduct problems. Second, crime: Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated a rare life-course-persistent pattern, starting in early childhood and continuing into adult offending and imprisonment, from a common adolescence-limited pattern that fades in early adulthood. Third, psychosis: birth-cohort studies such as the Dunedin study in New Zealand found that children with conduct problems had higher rates of adult psychotic and schizophreniform disorders, so CD is a broad risk marker, not only a route to crime. Example: Kabir, 14, has been setting fires, stealing and bullying since age 9; his risk of adult offending, of ASPD and even of later psychosis is raised. Option d is right because each of the three outcomes has research support. Exam trap: students know the conduct disorder to ASPD link and pick c, overlooking the evidence for imprisonment and psychosis.", "topic": "Outcomes of adolescent conduct disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Hallucinations\" are examples of disorders of:", "options": {"a": "Judgement.", "b": "Perception.", "c": "Intelligence.", "d": "Thinking."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: hallucinations are disorders of perception. Descriptive psychopathology, laid out by Karl Jaspers (1913) in General Psychopathology, sorts symptoms by the mental function they disturb: consciousness, perception, thought (form and content), mood, memory and so on. A hallucination is a perception without an object: the person sees, hears, smells, tastes or feels something when no matching external stimulus is present, yet it is as vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside. Jean-Etienne Esquirol (1838) gave this modern definition and separated it from an illusion, which is a misperception of a real stimulus (a coat on a hook seen as a man). Auditory hallucinations, such as voices commenting on the person, are the commonest in schizophrenia; visual ones point more towards delirium or other organic causes. Example: Riya hears a man's voice calling her name in an empty room every night. Nothing in the room produced the sound, so it is a hallucination, a fault in perception. Option b is right because a hallucination is by definition a false perception, so in a mental status examination it is recorded under perception. Exam trap: examiners offer 'thinking' because hallucinations and delusions travel together in psychosis. A delusion is a false, fixed belief, a disorder of thought content; a hallucination is a false perception. Judgement (deciding how to act) and intelligence are separate headings altogether.", "topic": "Hallucination as perceptual disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"First rank symptoms\" of schizophrenia given by Schneider include all except", "options": {"a": "Depersonalization", "b": "Hallucinatory voice in the form of running commentary", "c": "Delusional perception", "d": "Thought Broad casting"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "Kurt Schneider, a German psychiatrist, set out first-rank symptoms (FRS) in Clinical Psychopathology (English edition 1959) as experiences that, in the absence of organic brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia. There are eleven, in four groups. Auditory hallucinations of three kinds: audible thoughts (hearing one's own thoughts spoken aloud), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity (made) experiences: made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts, and somatic passivity, where bodily sensations are felt as imposed from outside. Finally, delusional perception: a normal perception given a sudden delusional meaning, such as Raju seeing a red car and instantly knowing he has been chosen to save the nation. Schneider called symptoms such as other hallucinations, perplexity, depressive or euphoric mood change and emotional impoverishment second-rank. FRS are not pathognomonic: they also occur in mania and other psychoses. DSM-IV let one bizarre delusion or one running-commentary voice satisfy Criterion A, but DSM-5 and DSM-5-TR removed this special weighting, so two Criterion A symptoms are needed, at least one being delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. ICD-11 keeps experiences of influence, passivity and control among its core symptoms. Depersonalisation is a nonspecific self-experience found in anxiety, depression, fatigue and depersonalization/derealization disorder, and the person knows the change is only 'as if', so it is not an FRS. Option a is right because depersonalisation is the only option absent from Schneider's list of first-rank symptoms.", "topic": "From dementia praecox to schizophrenia", "fig": "Kurt Schneider"}, {"q": "Which one of these statements is TRUE?", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia makes people violent.", "b": "If you hear voices or hold delusional beliefs you have schizophrenia.", "c": "Siblings of people who are diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to suffer mental health problems in their lifetime.", "d": "People with a diagnosis of schizophrenia are more likely to commit murder than their non-schizophrenic peer group."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: siblings of people diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to have mental health problems in their lifetime. Schizophrenia runs in families because risk is partly genetic and polygenic, carried by many common gene variants of small effect. Irving Gottesman (1991) pooled about 40 family and twin studies: lifetime risk is about 1% in the general population, about 9% for a sibling, about 13% for a child of one affected parent and about 48% for an identical co-twin. These genes overlap with those for bipolar disorder, depression and autism, and siblings also share family stress, so first-degree relatives show raised rates of several disorders, not only schizophrenia. The other statements are popular myths. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent and are far more often victims of violence than perpetrators. Hearing voices or holding odd beliefs does not by itself mean schizophrenia: Marius Romme and Sandra Escher (1989) showed that many voice-hearers in the general population cope well and never seek help, and hallucinations also occur in bereavement, post-traumatic stress disorder, bipolar disorder, severe depression, drug use and on falling asleep. Example: Arjun's elder brother has schizophrenia; Arjun's own lifetime risk of schizophrenia is several times the average, and his risk of depression is also higher, yet most siblings never develop schizophrenia. Option c is right because family and twin studies consistently show raised risk in relatives. Exam trap: options a and d play on media stereotypes, and b equates a symptom with a diagnosis. Key note: c is the clearly supported statement, though d is not baseless: meta-analyses such as Seena Fazel and colleagues (2009) find a raised relative risk of violent crime, including homicide, in schizophrenia, mostly explained by co-occurring substance misuse. The absolute risk is tiny and textbooks treat the blanket claim as a myth, so c stands.", "topic": "Myths and genetics of schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The violation of a society's explicit and implicit norms can be viewed as not normal in the sense that the violation is:", "options": {"a": "Deviant", "b": "Distressful", "c": "Dysfunctional", "d": "A danger to one's self or to others"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: behaviour that violates a society's explicit and implicit norms is called deviant. There is no single test for abnormality, so textbooks such as Abnormal Psychology by Ronald Comer (1992) sum up the usual features as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Deviance means behaviour that is different, extreme, unusual or bizarre. It can be judged in two ways. The statistical criterion calls abnormal whatever is rare, far from the average; its flaw is that rare talent, such as an IQ of 150, would also count. The social-norm criterion calls abnormal whatever breaks a society's norms, both the explicit rules (laws) and the implicit ones (unspoken customs). Its flaw is cultural relativity: norms differ across cultures and eras. A possession trance at a temple festival may be accepted custom in one setting and a symptom in another, and homosexuality was listed as a disorder in DSM-II (1968) until 1973. So deviance alone never proves a disorder. Example: Kabir walks to college in winter wearing only shorts and talks loudly to himself on the bus. People stare because he is violating norms; his behaviour is deviant, but a clinician would still ask whether he is distressed, dysfunctional or dangerous. Option a is right because 'deviant' is the term for breaking a society's norms. Exam trap: the other three Ds are also features of abnormality, so all four options look correct. The test is which D the stem defines: norm violation is deviance, suffering is distress, inability to cope with daily life is dysfunction, and risk of harm is danger.", "topic": "Four Ds: deviance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term “dementia praecox” was coined by", "options": {"a": "Emil Kraepelin", "b": "Eugene Bleuler", "c": "Karl Jaspers", "d": "Adolf Meyer"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: Emil Kraepelin. The German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926) is the name attached to \"dementia praecox\", Latin for early (precocious) dementia. He used the term in the fourth edition of his textbook in 1893, and in the sixth edition of 1899 he drew his famous dividing line: dementia praecox, which begins in youth and runs a deteriorating course, versus manic-depressive insanity, which comes in episodes with good recovery between them. He united pictures described by others, hebephrenia (Ewald Hecker), catatonia (Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum) and paranoid states, as forms of one illness defined above all by its course. Think of a 19-year-old student who withdraws, hears voices and over the years loses his drive and social skills: to Kraepelin, the downhill course itself was diagnostic. In 1911 Eugen Bleuler renamed the group schizophrenia because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. Option a is right because Kraepelin made the Latin term famous and built the concept around it. Exam trap: the French physician Benedict Morel used the French phrase \"démence précoce\" earlier, in the 1850s, for young patients who deteriorated, and Arnold Pick used the Latin term in 1891; neither is offered here. Key note: strictly, Kraepelin popularised rather than coined the term, but among these options he is the standard answer.", "topic": "Origin of dementia praecox", "fig": "Emil Kraepelin"}, {"q": "Which one is NOT a compulsive behaviour in obsessive-compulsive disorder?", "options": {"a": "Tapping", "b": "Hoarding", "c": "Washing", "d": "Checking"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is hoarding because, in this classic framing, hoarding is not classified as a compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder; the others (tapping, washing, checking) are textbook compulsions. To understand this, you must grasp the structure of OCD. It has two components. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images, or urges that cause marked anxiety (for example, fears of contamination, doubts about safety, or a need for symmetry). Compulsions are repetitive behaviors or mental acts that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession, in order to reduce the anxiety or prevent a feared event. The most common compulsions are precisely washing and cleaning (in response to contamination fears), checking (in response to doubt, like checking locks or the stove), ordering and arranging, counting, and repeating actions such as tapping. So tapping, washing, and checking are all standard compulsive rituals. Hoarding, the difficulty discarding possessions and the distress at parting with them, was historically considered a symptom or subtype of OCD, but in the DSM-5 it was reclassified as its OWN distinct condition, Hoarding Disorder, within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter but separate from OCD itself. That is why hoarding is the odd one out: it is not a compulsive ritual performed to neutralize an obsession in the way washing or checking are; it now stands as a separate diagnosis. The conceptual point to retain is that classic compulsions neutralize obsessions, while hoarding is a distinct disorder of acquisition and difficulty discarding. Key note: RINPAS 2024 asks this same item with the same four options, and its printed key gives a (tapping), counting hoarding as a compulsion the older way. DSM-5 (2013) made hoarding disorder a diagnosis of its own, which is why hoarding is used here.", "topic": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What assumption does ANCOVA have that ANOVA does not?", "options": {"a": "Homogeneity of variance", "b": "Homoscedasticity", "c": "Homogeneity of sample size", "d": "Homogeneity of regression slopes"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is homogeneity of regression slopes. Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA), an extension of the analysis of variance developed by Ronald Fisher, combines ANOVA and regression: it compares group means on a dependent variable while statistically controlling for one or more continuous covariates that are related to the dependent variable, thereby reducing error variance and removing confounding. ANCOVA shares ANOVA's basic assumptions of normality, independence of observations, and homogeneity of variance (homoscedasticity). But it adds a crucial extra assumption not required by ordinary ANOVA: homogeneity of regression slopes. This means the relationship (the regression slope) between the covariate and the dependent variable must be the same across all the treatment groups. In practice this is tested by checking that there is no significant interaction between the independent variable and the covariate. The assumption matters because ANCOVA adjusts each group's mean using a common (pooled) slope; if the slopes differ across groups, that single adjustment is invalid and the covariate's effect depends on group membership, making the adjusted means misleading. Because homogeneity of regression slopes is the assumption unique to ANCOVA and absent from ANOVA, option d is correct.", "topic": "Factorial, repeated-measures and covariance designs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which non-parametric test is used for comparing medians of three or more independent groups?", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Kruskal-Wallis test", "c": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test", "d": "Friedman test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is the Kruskal-Wallis test. The Kruskal-Wallis test, named after William Kruskal and Wilson Allen Wallis, is the non-parametric alternative to the one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA). It is used to compare three or more independent groups when the data are ordinal or when the assumptions of ANOVA (such as normality and homogeneity of variance) are not met. By ranking all observations together and comparing the mean ranks across groups, it tests whether the samples come from the same distribution, effectively comparing the medians of three or more independent groups. Because the question specifies three or more independent groups and a non-parametric procedure, option B is correct. The Mann-Whitney U test (option A) is limited to comparing exactly two independent groups, so it cannot handle three or more. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C), from Frank Wilcoxon, compares two related or paired samples and so applies to dependent rather than independent data. The Friedman test (option D) is also non-parametric and handles three or more conditions, but it is designed for related or repeated-measures data (the same subjects measured under multiple conditions), not independent groups. The key mapping is that independent groups with three or more levels call for Kruskal-Wallis, the rank-based analogue of one-way ANOVA.", "topic": "Rank tests and choosing the right test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Parameter is characteristic of:", "options": {"a": "The sample", "b": "The population", "c": "An individual", "d": "A test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The correct answer is the population. In statistics, a clear distinction is drawn between a parameter and a statistic. A parameter is a numerical value that describes a characteristic of an entire population, the complete set of all individuals or observations of interest, for example the true mean height of all adults in a country or the true proportion of voters favouring a candidate. A statistic, by contrast, is a numerical value computed from a sample, a subset drawn from the population, for example the mean height of 500 surveyed adults. The reason this distinction matters is that we usually cannot measure an entire population directly (it is too large, costly, or inaccessible), so we draw a sample, calculate sample statistics, and use them to estimate the unknown population parameters, a process called statistical inference. Parameters are typically denoted by Greek letters (mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation), while sample statistics use Roman letters (x-bar for the sample mean, s for the sample standard deviation). The accuracy of estimating a parameter from a statistic depends on sampling methods and sample size. Because a parameter by definition describes the population, the population is the correct answer.", "topic": "Populations, samples and parameters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "'Ethics' can be defined as the formulation of principles to:", "options": {"a": "Reduce inappropriate behaviour.", "b": "Punish inappropriate behaviour.", "c": "Reinforce appropriate behaviour.", "d": "Guide behaviour."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: ethics is the formulation of principles that guide behaviour, telling people what they ought and ought not to do. The word comes from the Greek ethos, meaning character or custom. Ethics is wider than any one tool for changing behaviour: it is a set of standards of right conduct that people use to decide how to act, especially when interests conflict. Professional ethics turns these standards into codes. In research, modern codes grew out of abuse: the Nuremberg Code (1947) after Nazi experiments, the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), which named three principles: respect for persons (applied through informed consent), beneficence (do no harm and maximise benefit) and justice (fair selection of participants). Psychologists follow the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016), and Indian researchers follow the Indian Council of Medical Research's National Ethical Guidelines (2017). These codes do not mainly punish or reward; they guide decisions in advance. Example: Meera plans to study anxiety in college students. Ethical principles guide her to obtain informed consent, tell students they may withdraw at any time, keep their answers confidential, and offer debriefing and help if a questionnaire upsets anyone. Option d is right because guiding behaviour is the purpose of ethical principles; reducing bad behaviour or rewarding good behaviour are at most side effects. Exam trap: the distractors borrow the language of operant conditioning (reinforce, punish, reduce behaviour), which makes them sound psychological and precise; but ethics is a guide to conduct, not a reinforcement schedule.", "topic": "Definition of ethics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When the assumption of normal distribution is met and you want to compare the means of two independent groups, which statistical test is most appropriate?", "options": {"a": "Mann-Whitney U test", "b": "Independent samples t-test", "c": "Chi-square test", "d": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is the independent-samples t-test because it is the standard parametric test for comparing the means of exactly two separate, unrelated groups when the parametric assumptions, especially normality, are satisfied. The t-test was developed by William Sealy Gosset, who published under the pseudonym 'Student' in 1908 while working at the Guinness brewery, which is why it is often called Student's t-test. It works by calculating a t-statistic, essentially the difference between the two group means divided by an estimate of the variability (standard error) of that difference, and then judging whether that difference is large enough, relative to the noise, to be unlikely under the null hypothesis of no true difference. Because it uses the actual means and standard deviations rather than just ranks, it is more powerful than its non-parametric counterpart when its assumptions hold. Choosing the right test depends on three questions: how many groups, whether they are independent or related, and whether parametric assumptions are met. Two independent groups with normality gives the independent-samples t-test; two related or paired groups gives the paired-samples t-test; and if normality fails you drop to the non-parametric versions, Mann-Whitney U for independent groups and Wilcoxon signed-rank for paired ones. Since the question specifies normality met, two independent groups, and comparison of means, the independent-samples t-test is correct.", "topic": "Rank tests and choosing the right test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "_____ is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even though they are measured in different units.", "options": {"a": "Range", "b": "Standard deviation", "c": "Coefficient of variation", "d": "Mean deviation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the coefficient of variation (CV) is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even when they are measured in different units. Karl Pearson (1896) defined it as the standard deviation expressed as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Because the SD and the mean are in the same units, the units cancel and the CV is a pure percentage. That allows comparisons the SD cannot make: heights in centimetres against weights in kilograms, or the spread of marks in a class whose mean is 30 against one whose mean is 80. The set with the lower CV is the more consistent (more uniform, less variable). Example: in Riya's class, height has a mean of 160 cm and an SD of 8 cm (CV = 5 per cent), while weight has a mean of 50 kg and an SD of 5 kg (CV = 10 per cent); weight varies relatively twice as much, even though 8 looks larger than 5. Because it divides by the mean, the CV strictly needs ratio data with a true zero. Option c is right because it is the standard relative, unit-free measure of dispersion. Exam trap: the standard deviation is the best-known measure of spread, so it attracts guesses; but it is stated in the original units, so comparing an SD in centimetres with one in kilograms is meaningless.", "topic": "Coefficient of variation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following can reduce experimenter bias?", "options": {"a": "Using control groups", "b": "Demand characteristics", "c": "Barnum effect", "d": "Double blinding"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: double blinding reduces experimenter bias. Experimenter bias, or the experimenter expectancy effect, is the way a researcher's expectations can unintentionally shape results: through subtle cues to participants, through how ambiguous responses are scored, or through how data are recorded. Robert Rosenthal (1966) made the effect famous. In Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode's (1963) study, students were told their rats were 'maze-bright' or 'maze-dull', though the rats had been assigned at random; the 'bright' rats learned faster, apparently because the students handled them differently. In a single-blind study the participants do not know which condition they are in, which controls their own expectations, such as placebo effects. In a double-blind study neither the participants nor the researchers who deal with them and record the data know who is in which condition. Because the experimenter does not know, their expectations cannot leak into the participants' behaviour or the scoring. Double-blind designs are standard in drug trials, where an independent person holds the code that links each participant to drug or placebo. Example: Dr Anil tests a new antidepressant. A pharmacist labels identical capsules with codes, and neither Dr Anil, who rates the patients' mood, nor the patients know which capsules hold the drug. The code is broken only after rating ends. Option d is right because double blinding removes the experimenter's knowledge of the conditions, which is the very source of experimenter bias. Exam trap: control groups are vital, but they control extraneous variables and placebo effects, not the experimenter's own expectations; an experimenter who knows which group is which can still bias the results. Demand characteristics and the Barnum effect are sources of bias, not cures for it.", "topic": "Double blinding and experimenter bias", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of the mean?", "options": {"a": "It is affected by extreme scores", "b": "It minimizes the sum of squared deviations", "c": "The sum of the deviations about the mean is zero", "d": "It is best used with the ordinal data"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'it is best used with ordinal data' is not a characteristic of the mean. The arithmetic mean (M = ΣX ÷ N) is the sum of the scores divided by their number. It has three classic properties. First, it is the balance point of the distribution: deviations above and below it cancel, so the sum of deviations about the mean is always zero. Second, the sum of squared deviations about the mean is smaller than about any other value, the least-squares property that makes the mean the basis of variance, SD and regression. Third, because it uses every score, it is affected by extreme scores: the marks 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8, but adding one score of 40 lifts it to about 13.3. The mean assumes equal distances between scores, so it needs interval or ratio data. Ordinal data, such as class ranks or finishing positions in a race, show only order; the gap between first and second need not equal the gap between second and third, so the median is the proper average, and for nominal data the mode. Example: if Arjun's teacher records only class ranks, the median rank is a fair summary, but a mean rank assumes that the gap between 1st and 2nd equals the gap between 9th and 10th, which ranks cannot tell us. Option d is right because ordinal data suit the median, not the mean. Exam trap: the item asks for what is NOT true; options a, b and c are all genuine properties, so a hurried reader picks the first true statement.", "topic": "Properties of the mean", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"repeats-1": [{"q": "Psychoanalytical theory about human personality was proposed by", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory of personality was proposed by Sigmund Freud. Freud (1856 to 1939), a Viennese neurologist, developed psychoanalysis from his work with hysterical patients, first with Josef Breuer in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and then in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). He held that much of mental life is unconscious and that behaviour is driven by instincts, chiefly the sexual drive (libido) and later aggression. His topographic model divides the mind into conscious, preconscious and unconscious levels; his structural model (The Ego and the Id, 1923) divides personality into the pleasure-seeking id, the superego or internalised conscience, and the ego, which mediates between them and reality. Anxiety signals danger, and the ego protects itself with defence mechanisms such as repression and projection. Personality develops through the oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital psychosexual stages, and unresolved conflict at a stage can leave a fixation. His methods were free association, dream analysis and the study of slips of the tongue. Example: a man who calls his wife by his mother's name may, in Freudian terms, be revealing an unconscious wish. Option b is right because Freud founded and named psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung's theory is analytical psychology and Alfred Adler's is individual psychology; both are psychodynamic, but only Freud's is psychoanalysis.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Gustav Jung broke with Freud in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, known for the collective unconscious, archetypes and introversion-extraversion.", "c": "B. F. Skinner was a radical behaviourist who explained personality as learned patterns of operant behaviour and rejected unconscious forces.", "d": "Alfred Adler left Freud's circle in 1911 and founded individual psychology, centred on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, style of life and social interest."}}, {"q": "Which theory of personality was developed by Sigmund Freud?", "concept": "The correct answer is psychoanalytic. Sigmund Freud founded the psychoanalytic theory of personality, which holds that behaviour is driven largely by unconscious forces, early childhood experiences, and the interplay of three structures: the id (primitive drives operating on the pleasure principle), the ego (the reality-oriented mediator), and the superego (the moral conscience). Freud also proposed psychosexual stages of development and the mechanisms of repression and defence. The distractors are each associated with different founders: behaviourism was developed by John Watson and extended by B.F. Skinner, emphasising observable stimulus-response learning and rejecting the unconscious; humanistic psychology was led by Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, who stressed free will, self-actualisation, and the drive toward growth; and psychosocial theory was created by Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian who expanded Freud's stages across the whole lifespan with eight crises. Knowing that Freud equals psychoanalytic is foundational because his ideas shaped a century of clinical practice, grew out of the talking cure (Anna O.'s name for Josef Breuer's method), and remain the basis for psychodynamic therapy. It also anchors students so they can correctly attribute the many competing personality theories they will encounter.", "traps": {"b": "The behaviouristic approach was developed by John Watson and B.F. Skinner, focusing on observable behaviour, not Freud.", "c": "The humanistic theory belongs to Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, emphasising self-actualisation.", "d": "The psychosocial theory of development was proposed by Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian, not Freud himself."}}, {"q": "Insight learning is basically learning by", "concept": "The answer is a: insight learning is basically learning by cognition. Insight learning was described by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler, who studied chimpanzees at the anthropoid station on Tenerife between 1913 and 1920 and reported the work in The Mentality of Apes (1917 in German, 1925 in English). A banana was hung out of reach or placed outside the cage. After failed attempts, a chimpanzee named Sultan would pause, seem to survey the situation, and then suddenly join two short sticks into a long one, or stack boxes, to get the fruit. Kohler argued that the animal had mentally restructured the perceptual field, seeing the relationship between the parts and the goal. Insight has typical marks: the solution comes suddenly (the \"aha\" experience), it is performed smoothly once found, it is remembered, and it transfers to similar problems. Everyday example: you struggle with a riddle, stop, and the answer suddenly clicks, after which you can solve others like it. Option a is right because insight depends on thinking and understanding relationships, a cognitive process, rather than on reinforced associations. Exam trap: insight is the opposite of Edward L. Thorndike's gradual trial-and-error learning in the puzzle box, and Kohler's work was a Gestalt challenge to behaviourism.", "traps": {"b": "\"Conditions\" points to conditioning, classical or operant, where learning comes from associations between stimuli, responses and consequences rather than sudden understanding.", "c": "Trial and error is Edward L. Thorndike's account of cats in puzzle boxes, where correct responses are stamped in gradually by their consequences; insight is sudden, not gradual.", "d": "Imitation is observational learning, studied by Albert Bandura in the Bobo doll experiments, where behaviour is copied from a model."}}, {"q": "Who is the most valuable contributor in the theory of insight learning?", "concept": "The answer is d: Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) is the main contributor to the theory of insight learning. Kohler, one of the three founders of Gestalt psychology, was stranded at the anthropoid research station on Tenerife from 1913 to 1920 and studied chimpanzees there (The Mentality of Apes, 1917 in German, 1925 in English). His best-known chimpanzee, Sultan, could not reach a banana outside the cage with either of two short sticks. After a pause he fitted one stick into the other and at once raked in the fruit. Other apes stacked boxes to reach fruit hung from the roof. Kohler called this insight: a sudden reorganisation of the perceptual field in which the animal sees how the parts of the problem relate to the goal. Insight learning is abrupt rather than gradual, smooth once found, well remembered and readily transferred to new problems, and the moment of understanding is the 'aha' experience. Example: Arjun stares at a puzzle for ten minutes, then suddenly sees that turning one piece sideways solves it, and next time he repeats the solution at once. Option d is right because Kohler's ape studies are the founding evidence and theory of insight learning. Exam trap: Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941) was Kohler's Gestalt colleague, so the shared school makes c tempting, and Edward Thorndike (1898) is offered because his cats in puzzle boxes are the classic contrast: their learning was gradual trial and error, the opposite of insight.", "traps": {"a": "Edward Thorndike (1898) studied cats in puzzle boxes and proposed trial-and-error learning and the law of effect, the gradual learning that Kohler set insight against.", "b": "Edward Chace Tolman (1886 to 1959) is the purposive behaviourist of latent learning and cognitive maps in rats (1930, 1948), a cognitive learning theory but not insight learning.", "c": "Kurt Koffka was a Gestalt founder who wrote Growth of the Mind (1921) and Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935) and spread the idea of insight, but the ape experiments and the theory are Kohler's."}}, {"q": "During insight learning there is the sudden realization of the solution to a problem. This is known as the:", "concept": "The correct answer is the aha experience. Insight learning refers to the sudden grasp of the solution to a problem without overt trial and error, a concept demonstrated by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler in his experiments with chimpanzees, most famously Sultan. Kohler placed bananas out of reach and provided sticks or stackable boxes; rather than gradually fumbling toward the answer, the chimps would pause, appear to study the situation, and then suddenly assemble the tools or stack the boxes in one smooth, correct sequence. This abrupt restructuring of the perceptual field, in which the elements of a problem are suddenly reorganised into a meaningful whole, is the hallmark of insight. The subjective moment of sudden comprehension is popularly called the aha experience (or sometimes the eureka moment). It contrasts sharply with Thorndike's trial-and-error learning, where solutions emerge gradually through reinforcement of successful responses. Insight learning highlights the cognitive, perceptual reorganisation that Gestalt psychologists emphasised against the purely associationist view, showing that learning can be sudden, all-or-none, and transferable to new but similar problems.", "traps": {"b": "Light bulb is only an informal cartoon metaphor for having an idea, not the technical psychological term for insight.", "c": "Momentary merely describes brief duration and is not a named concept for the moment of insight.", "d": "Learning is the general superordinate term and is far too broad to name the specific sudden realisation in insight."}}, {"q": "The prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by Binet. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction asked for a way to identify children who could not benefit from ordinary schooling and needed special classes. Alfred Binet, working with the physician Theodore Simon, rejected Francis Galton's approach of measuring reaction time and sensory acuity and instead tested higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning. The Binet-Simon scale of 1905 had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, creating the concept of mental age, and a further revision followed in 1911, the year Binet died. Later work built on this prototype: William Stern proposed the intelligence quotient (mental age divided by chronological age), and Lewis M. Terman of Stanford University adapted the test as the Stanford-Binet (1916), multiplying the ratio by 100. Example: on the 1908 scale, a seven-year-old who passed the items most nine-year-olds pass had a mental age of nine. Option b is right because the Binet-Simon scale was the first practical intelligence test and the model for later tests. Exam trap: Terman popularised the IQ in America and Wechsler introduced the deviation IQ, but neither created the first test, and \"Stanford\" is a university, not a person.", "traps": {"a": "David Wechsler created the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), later the WAIS and WISC, and introduced the deviation IQ, decades after Binet.", "c": "Lewis M. Terman revised Binet's scale as the Stanford-Binet (1916) and popularised the IQ, but he adapted an existing test rather than creating the prototype.", "d": "Stanford is the university where Terman worked; it lent its name to the Stanford-Binet but is not a person who developed a test."}}, {"q": "Which of the following psychologists is associated with the development of the first intelligence test?", "concept": "The first practical intelligence test was developed by the French psychologist Alfred Binet, together with his collaborator Theodore Simon. In 1904 the French government commissioned Binet to devise a way to identify schoolchildren who needed special educational assistance, and the resulting Binet-Simon Scale (1905) measured children's performance on tasks of judgment, comprehension, and reasoning. Its 1908 revision grouped the tasks by age and introduced the mental level (later called mental age), the age level at which a child was performing. This scale was the foundation of modern intelligence testing. It was later revised by Lewis Terman at Stanford University into the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, which adopted the intelligence quotient (IQ), a term from William Stern, computed as mental age divided by chronological age times 100. The distractors are unrelated to this achievement: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, Jean Piaget studied stages of cognitive development (though he briefly worked in Binet's laboratory), and B. F. Skinner pioneered operant conditioning. Because Alfred Binet developed the first intelligence test, option c is correct.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis and had nothing to do with intelligence testing.", "b": "Jean Piaget studied cognitive development in children, not the creation of the first intelligence test (though he briefly worked in Binet's lab).", "d": "B. F. Skinner was a behaviorist focused on operant conditioning, not intelligence testing."}}, {"q": "Which intelligence test includes the Pattern Drawing Test?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Pattern Drawing Test is one of the five subtests of Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence. Amit Panwar's Master Guide lists this stem as a repeat across CET and RML papers. C. M. Bhatia assembled the battery in 1955 and standardised it on Indian children, so that intelligence could be measured without reading, writing or any one language. Its five subtests are: Kohs Block Design, after Samuel C. Kohs (1920), where coloured cubes are arranged to copy a printed design; Alexander Pass-Along, after W. P. Alexander (1932), where blocks are slid along a tray, never lifted, to reach a target pattern; Pattern Drawing, where the person copies line figures; Immediate Memory, recalling digits forward and backward, the one task that is not a performance task; and Picture Construction, where cut pieces are put together into a picture. Because it needs no literacy, it is the test of choice for an illiterate child or adult from a rural area: it keeps cultural and educational bias low, while the WISC-IV and the Stanford-Binet carry Western verbal content. Example: a 12-year-old from a village who never went to school cannot be fairly tested on vocabulary, but can copy patterns, build block designs and assemble pictures. Option a is right because Pattern Drawing belongs to Bhatia's battery and to none of the other scales listed. Exam trap: block design appears in both Bhatia's battery (as Kohs blocks) and the Wechsler scales, so a block item cannot tell them apart; Pattern Drawing and Picture Construction can. The Seguin Form Board, the other performance test Indian clinics use, is not part of Bhatia's battery.", "traps": {"b": "David Wechsler's WAIS-IV (2008) has Block Design, which descends from Kohs' blocks, plus Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles, Digit Span and other subtests, but no Pattern Drawing; that shared block task is what makes it tempting.", "c": "John C. Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (1938) is one non-verbal test of 60 matrix items in five sets of 12: you choose the piece that completes a pattern, you never draw one.", "d": "The Stanford-Binet, Lewis Terman's 1916 revision of the Binet-Simon scale (fifth edition by Gale Roid, 2003), is an individually given scale with verbal and nonverbal tasks across five factors (fluid reasoning, knowledge, quantitative reasoning, visual-spatial processing, working memory); Pattern Drawing is not among them."}}, {"q": "Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing:", "concept": "The answer is b: Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing intelligence. C. M. Bhatia (1955) built the Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence for Indian conditions, including people who cannot read. It has five performance subtests: Kohs block design (copying coloured designs with cubes), Alexander's pass-along test (sliding blocks in a tray into a target pattern), pattern drawing, immediate memory for digits, and picture construction (assembling cut pieces of pictures). Because it needs no reading and little language, and was standardised with separate norms for literate and illiterate children aged 11 to 16, it is used with illiterate and less schooled people and minimises the educational bias of verbal tests. The subtest scores are combined into one performance IQ. It stands beside other Indian intelligence tests such as the Binet-Kamat Test (V. V. Kamat, 1930s) and Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (Arthur Malin, 1969). For example, a psychologist assessing Arjun, a 12-year-old from a village with little schooling, chooses Bhatia's Battery over the WISC, which leans on schooling and foreign norms. Option b is right because the battery is a non-verbal performance test of general intelligence. Exam trap: because its subtests look like practical, manipulative tasks, students mistake it for an aptitude test, but it yields one intelligence score, not a profile of specific aptitudes.", "traps": {"a": "Personality is assessed by inventories (the EPI, 16PF, MMPI) and projective tests (Rorschach, TAT); Bhatia's subtests are timed problem-solving tasks, not measures of traits.", "c": "Attitudes are measured by Thurstone, Likert or Bogardus scales of agreement with statements; the battery contains no attitude items.", "d": "Aptitude tests measure specific potentials (the eight subtests of the Differential Aptitude Tests, the Minnesota Clerical Test); Bhatia's Battery, although made of performance tasks, gives one general intelligence score."}}, {"q": "A 10-year-old child from a rural area, with no formal schooling, is brought for an intellectual assessment due to academic struggles. The child is illiterate and has had minimal exposure to formal testing materials. Which of the following tests would be the MOST appropriate to use to minimize cultural and educational bias?", "concept": "The answer is Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence, because for an illiterate, unschooled child it minimizes cultural and educational bias by relying on nonverbal performance tasks rather than language or academic knowledge. Understand the principle at stake, which is the distinction between verbal and performance (nonverbal) measures of intelligence and the problem of test bias. Any intelligence test that depends on reading, vocabulary, general information, or arithmetic taught in school will systematically underestimate the ability of a child who has had no formal education and cannot read, not because the child is less intelligent but because the test measures acquired schooling rather than underlying reasoning; this is cultural and educational bias, a threat to validity. Performance tests sidestep this by assessing reasoning through hands-on, nonverbal tasks such as assembling blocks, completing patterns, or manipulating objects, which do not require literacy or culturally specific verbal knowledge. Bhatia's battery, developed by C. M. Bhatia in 1955 specifically for the Indian population, is a set of five performance subtests (including Koh's block design, Alexander's pass-along, pattern drawing, immediate memory, and picture construction) designed to be usable with illiterate and rural children by minimizing verbal and educational demands. Because the vignette emphasizes illiteracy, no schooling, and minimal exposure to formal testing, the appropriate choice is the purely performance-based, Indian-standardized Bhatia's battery. The distractors, WISC-IV, Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916), and even the Indian MISIC, all include verbal components and presuppose schooling, so they carry more bias, making Bhatia's the best answer.", "traps": {"b": "The WISC-IV is a Wechsler scale that relies heavily on verbal subtests and assumes familiarity with formal testing and language, making it culturally and educationally loaded and unsuitable for an illiterate, unschooled child.", "c": "The Stanford-Binet is a Western-developed, verbally weighted intelligence test standardized on other populations, so it carries substantial cultural and educational bias for a rural, illiterate Indian child.", "d": "Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is an Indian adaptation of the Wechsler scale but still contains verbal subtests and presupposes some schooling and language competence, so it remains less suitable than a purely performance-based battery for an illiterate child."}}, {"q": "In a 3 × 4 contingency table, using the chi-square test, the degrees of freedom associated with the chi-square will be:", "concept": "The answer is d: 6 degrees of freedom. The chi-square test was introduced by Karl Pearson in 1900 to compare observed frequencies with the frequencies expected if the null hypothesis is true. In a contingency table, the test of independence asks whether two categorical variables are related, for example diagnosis (three categories) and treatment setting (four categories). Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of cell values that are free to vary once the row and column totals are fixed. For a contingency table the formula is df = (r - 1)(c - 1), where r is the number of rows and c the number of columns. Here, (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 × 3 = 6. Intuition: if all row and column totals are known, you can fill only 2 cells in each of the first 3 columns freely; every remaining cell is then fixed by the totals. The df matter because the critical value depends on them: at the .05 level, chi-square needs to exceed 12.59 when df is 6. Option d is right because applying (r - 1)(c - 1) to a 3 × 4 table gives 6. Exam trap: students multiply rows by columns (12, the number of cells), add them (7), or subtract one from only one dimension (9), instead of subtracting one from each dimension before multiplying.", "traps": {"a": "12 is simply 3 × 4, the number of cells in the table; it ignores the constraints imposed by the fixed row and column totals.", "b": "7 is 3 + 4, the number of rows plus columns; degrees of freedom for a contingency table are never found by adding the dimensions.", "c": "9 comes from subtracting one from only one dimension, (4 - 1) × 3; the formula requires subtracting one from both rows and columns, giving 2 × 3 = 6."}}, {"q": "What would be the degrees of freedom for chi-square based on a 4 x 5 contingency table?", "concept": "For a chi-square test on a contingency table the degrees of freedom equal (rows minus one) multiplied by (columns minus one), so for a 4 by 5 table the calculation is (4 minus 1) times (5 minus 1), which is 3 times 4, equalling 12, making c correct. To understand why, recall what degrees of freedom mean: they represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints are imposed. In a contingency table the row totals and column totals (the marginal totals) are treated as fixed. Given those fixed marginals, once you know all but the last cell in each row and each column, the remaining cells are determined automatically, because each row and each column must sum to its known marginal. This is why one degree of freedom is subtracted from both the number of rows and the number of columns. The chi-square test of independence itself compares observed frequencies in each cell against the frequencies expected if the two categorical variables were independent, summing the squared differences divided by expected counts. The degrees of freedom then determine which chi-square distribution to compare the statistic against in order to find the critical value and significance. Getting the degrees of freedom right is essential because using the wrong value leads to an incorrect critical threshold and a wrong conclusion about statistical significance.", "traps": {"a": "Five would only be correct if you mistakenly used the larger dimension minus nothing or confused degrees of freedom with the number of columns; it ignores the (r-1)(c-1) formula entirely.", "b": "Nine is the product of the raw dimensions minus an incorrect single adjustment, or 3 times 3; it would be right only for a 4 by 4 table, not a 4 by 5.", "d": "Twenty is simply the total number of cells (4 times 5) with no subtraction for the fixed marginals, which overstates the degrees of freedom."}}, {"q": "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:", "concept": "The answer is b: Cronbach's alpha measures internal consistency, how well the items of a test hang together in measuring one thing. Lee Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha. The idea is simple: if all the items of a scale tap the same attribute, a person who scores high on one item should tend to score high on the others. Alpha summarises these inter-item relationships in one number, usually between 0 and 1. It equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the problem that different ways of splitting a test give different answers. It generalises the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several response points, such as Likert items. Only one administration of the test is needed. Values of about .70 and above are usually treated as acceptable for research, and higher values are expected for clinical decisions about individuals. Alpha rises as items are added, so a long test can have a high alpha even if it measures more than one thing; a high alpha does not prove the scale is unidimensional. Example: Arjun builds a 20-item exam stress questionnaire and gives it once to 200 students; an alpha of .86 tells him the items move together. Option b is right because alpha is calculated from the relationships among items within a single sitting, which is the definition of internal consistency reliability. Exam trap: Cronbach's name is also on the classic construct validity paper with Paul Meehl (1955), so students drift to validity, but alpha is a reliability coefficient.", "traps": {"a": "Inter-rater consistency is agreement between two or more scorers rating the same responses, measured with Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) or the intraclass correlation. Alpha looks at items, not raters.", "c": "Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice to the same people and correlates the two sets of scores, showing stability over time. Alpha needs only one sitting.", "d": "Construct validity asks whether a test measures the theoretical trait it claims to (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955). Alpha shows consistency, and a test can be highly consistent yet measure the wrong thing."}}, {"q": "Borderline Personality Disorder is most characteristically associated with which primary defense mechanism?", "concept": "The answer is splitting because it is the primitive defence mechanism most characteristically associated with Borderline Personality Disorder. Splitting, a concept emphasised by object-relations theorists such as Melanie Klein and later Otto Kernberg in his work on borderline organisation, refers to the inability to hold together the good and bad qualities of oneself or others into a single, integrated, realistic whole. Instead, people and situations are experienced in black-and-white, all-good or all-bad terms, and these evaluations can flip rapidly. This directly explains the clinical picture of borderline personality: the intense but unstable relationships in which the same person is idealised one moment ('you are perfect, you save me') and devalued the next ('you are cruel, I hate you'), the volatile emotions, and the unstable sense of identity. Because the world is split into extremes with no stable middle, relationships lurch between idealisation and devaluation. Splitting is classed as an immature or borderline-level defence, more primitive than the neurotic defences (repression, rationalization) and far below the mature ones (sublimation). Knowing the defence-to-disorder link is a common exam requirement: splitting maps onto borderline personality disorder just as, for other items, projection maps onto paranoid presentations. The signature instability of borderline relationships is driven by splitting, making it the correct answer.", "traps": {"a": "Repression, the pushing of unacceptable thoughts and impulses out of awareness, is a neurotic-level defence and not the hallmark of borderline personality.", "c": "Sublimation is a mature defence that channels impulses into constructive activity, the opposite of the primitive defences typical of borderline pathology.", "d": "Rationalization justifies behaviour with plausible but false reasons; it is a neurotic defence, not the characteristic all-or-nothing splitting seen in borderline."}}, {"q": "Which therapy focuses on changing maladaptive thought patterns?", "concept": "Cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT) is the umbrella name for structured, time-limited, present-focused treatments built on one premise: it is not events themselves but how we interpret them that shapes emotions and behaviour, so changing maladaptive thinking changes feelings and actions. Two founders matter. Albert Ellis developed rational therapy in the mid-1950s (later rational emotive behaviour therapy, REBT), with the ABC model: an Activating event, Beliefs about it, and emotional and behavioural Consequences; irrational beliefs such as 'I must be approved of by everyone' are disputed directly. Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy for depression in the 1960s (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979), describing the cognitive triad (negative views of self, world and future), automatic thoughts, underlying schemas or core beliefs, and cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation and catastrophising. Techniques include thought records, Socratic questioning, cognitive restructuring and behavioural experiments that test predictions, along with behavioural methods such as activity scheduling and exposure. Donald Meichenbaum added self-instructional training and stress inoculation. Third-wave therapies such as DBT, ACT and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy focus more on changing one's relationship to thoughts than their content. CBT has the largest evidence base among psychotherapies for depression and anxiety disorders. Contrast it with insight-oriented (psychodynamic) and growth-oriented (humanistic) therapies, which work through the past or the therapeutic relationship rather than directly targeting thoughts. Option b is right because identifying and modifying maladaptive thought patterns is the defining mechanism of CBT.", "traps": {"a": "Humanistic therapy, most famously Carl Rogers's client-centred (person-centred) therapy, assumes people have an innate drive toward self-actualisation. The therapist provides three core conditions, empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, and the client grows without being directed or having thoughts challenged. Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls) is also humanistic. It tempts because humanistic therapy does change self-concept, but indirectly, not by disputing thoughts.", "c": "Psychoanalysis, developed by Sigmund Freud, aims to make unconscious conflicts conscious through free association, dream analysis, interpretation of resistance and analysis of transference, so that the patient gains insight. Change comes from insight and working through, usually over a long period. It tempts because psychoanalysis deals with mental content, but it targets unconscious drives and defences, not conscious maladaptive thoughts.", "d": "Play therapy uses toys, drawing and play as the natural language of children to express feelings and work through conflicts. It grew from the child analysts Anna Freud and Melanie Klein, and Virginia Axline (1947) developed a non-directive, Rogerian version. It is defined by its medium and population, children, rather than by targeting thought patterns. It tempts because some play therapies borrow cognitive techniques."}}, {"q": "Which structure allows communication between the hemispheres?", "concept": "The answer is d: the corpus callosum is the band of fibres that lets the two cerebral hemispheres communicate. It is the largest commissure (crossing pathway) in the brain, a thick bundle of about 200 million axons of white matter lying in the midline above the thalamus. The smaller anterior commissure also links the two sides. Through the corpus callosum, what one hemisphere perceives or learns is quickly shared with the other. The classic evidence comes from split-brain patients. To control severe epilepsy, surgeons cut the corpus callosum. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1962 onwards) then flashed pictures to only one visual field. When an object was shown to the right hemisphere (left visual field), the patient could not name it, because speech is usually in the left hemisphere, yet his left hand could pick the object out by touch. Each hemisphere was now working on its own. Sperry shared the Nobel Prize in 1981 for this work. For example, if Meera, a split-brain patient, holds a key in her left hand out of sight, she can find it again among other objects with that hand but cannot say 'key', because the information stays in her right hemisphere. Option d is right because the corpus callosum is the main pathway joining and integrating the two hemispheres. Exam trap: 'contralateral bridge' is an invented term built from two real ideas (contralateral means opposite side; pons means bridge) so that it sounds plausible. The temporal lobe is a region inside each hemisphere, and the reticular formation is a brainstem arousal network; neither connects the hemispheres.", "traps": {"a": "The reticular formation is a network in the brainstem core that controls arousal, attention and sleep-wake transitions; it does not link the hemispheres.", "b": "The temporal lobe is one of the four lobes within each hemisphere, handling hearing, language comprehension and (with the hippocampus) memory; it is not a connecting pathway.", "c": "'Contralateral bridge' is not a real brain structure; the name borrows from the pons ('bridge') and from the fact that each hemisphere controls the opposite (contralateral) side."}}, {"q": "The corpus callosum connects :", "concept": "The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain: a thick, arched band of white matter, roughly 200 million axons, lying at the bottom of the longitudinal fissure and connecting corresponding areas of the left and right cerebral hemispheres. Its parts, from front to back, are the rostrum, genu, body and splenium; the splenium carries visual information between the occipital lobes. Neuroanatomy classifies white-matter fibres into three types, and this question tests the distinction. Commissural fibres cross from one hemisphere to the other (corpus callosum, the smaller anterior commissure, hippocampal commissure). Association fibres connect regions within the same hemisphere (arcuate fasciculus, uncinate fasciculus, cingulum). Projection fibres connect the cortex with lower structures such as the thalamus, brainstem and spinal cord (internal capsule, corona radiata). The importance of the callosum is shown by split-brain research. Surgeons cut it to control severe epilepsy, and Roger W. Sperry (Nobel Prize, 1981) with Michael S. Gazzaniga showed that each disconnected hemisphere could perceive and learn independently. An object flashed to the left visual field (right hemisphere) could not be named, because speech is usually in the left hemisphere, yet the left hand could pick it out by touch. People born without a corpus callosum (agenesis) show milder effects because other routes compensate. Option b is right because the corpus callosum is the main commissure linking the left and right cerebral hemispheres.", "traps": {"a": "The brainstem and cerebellum are connected by three pairs of cerebellar peduncles (superior, middle and inferior), which carry input from the spinal cord, vestibular system and pons and output toward the thalamus and motor cortex. The corpus callosum lies far above them, between the hemispheres. It tempts because both are named white-matter bridges and the word 'connects' invites any pairing of structures.", "c": "Frontal and temporal lobes within one hemisphere are joined by association fibres, mainly the uncinate fasciculus and the arcuate fasciculus (which links Broca's and Wernicke's areas). These run within a hemisphere; the corpus callosum runs between hemispheres. It tempts because the arcuate fasciculus is famous from aphasia questions, and students blur 'connects two cortical regions' with 'connects two hemispheres'.", "d": "The thalamus and cortex are linked by projection fibres, the thalamocortical radiations, which pass through the internal capsule and fan out as the corona radiata. They relay sensory and motor information to and from the cortex of the same hemisphere. It tempts because the thalamus is a central relay and these radiations are large white-matter tracts, but they are projection, not commissural, fibres."}}, {"q": "The famous patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) underwent surgical removal of the medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampus, to treat severe epilepsy. While he retained most of his intellectual abilities and working memory, his primary and most profound deficit was in:", "concept": "The answer is anterograde amnesia, because after Henry Molaison had his medial temporal lobes, including much of the hippocampus, surgically removed in 1953 to control intractable epilepsy, his most profound and defining deficit was the inability to form new long-term declarative memories, which is by definition anterograde amnesia. Understand what his case taught neuroscience, because it is one of the most important case studies in the field, documented extensively by Brenda Milner and later Suzanne Corkin. Anterograde amnesia means loss of the ability to lay down new memories going forward from the injury, as opposed to retrograde amnesia, which is loss of memories formed before the injury. H.M. could hold information briefly in working memory (he could carry on a conversation and repeat a string of digits) and his intelligence, language, and personality were preserved, but the moment his attention shifted, new experiences vanished; he could not remember people he had met minutes earlier or events of the day. This dissociation proved several landmark points: that the ability to form new long-term memories is separable from short-term or working memory, that it is separable from general intelligence, and that the hippocampus and adjacent medial temporal structures are critical for consolidating new declarative memories into long-term storage, while long-established remote memories are stored elsewhere in the cortex. His relatively preserved memories from well before surgery, and his intact learning of motor skills, further refined the picture. But the headline deficit, the inability to form new conscious memories, is anterograde amnesia.", "traps": {"a": "Procedural memory, the memory for skills and habits, was actually preserved in H.M., as shown by his ability to improve on motor tasks, so it was a spared ability rather than his deficit.", "b": "Semantic memory for facts learned before the surgery was largely intact; H.M. retained general knowledge acquired earlier in life, so this was not his defining deficit.", "d": "Retrograde amnesia, loss of memories from before the surgery, was relatively limited in H.M. and mostly confined to the years just prior to the operation, far less profound than his inability to form new memories."}}, {"q": "Which brain structure is primarily involved in forming new memories?", "concept": "The hippocampus, option b, is primarily involved in forming new memories. The hippocampus is a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system, and it is essential for consolidating new explicit, declarative memories, the conscious memories of facts and events, transferring them from short-term into long-term storage. The most famous evidence comes from the patient Henry Molaison, known as H.M., who had the front half of both hippocampi, with nearby medial temporal tissue, removed in 1953 to treat epilepsy and was thereafter unable to form new long-term declarative memories, a profound anterograde amnesia, even though his old memories, his intelligence, and his ability to learn new motor skills remained intact. This dissociation revealed that the hippocampus is critical for encoding new declarative memories but not for storing them permanently or for procedural learning. The hippocampus also supports spatial memory and navigation, evidenced by place cells discovered by O'Keefe, and it is one of the few regions where adult neurogenesis occurs. It is among the earliest structures damaged in Alzheimer's disease, which is why memory loss is an early sign. The exam link is hippocampus equals new memory formation, so option b is correct.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala attaches emotional significance to memories and processes fear, but it does not perform the core encoding of new declarative memories that the hippocampus does.", "c": "The cerebellum supports motor learning and coordination, a form of procedural memory, not the formation of new explicit memories.", "d": "The thalamus is a sensory relay station and contributes to memory circuits, but the hippocampus is the structure primarily responsible for forming new memories."}}, {"q": "Which area of the brain is primarily associated with the consolidation of long-term memories?", "concept": "The correct answer is Hippocampus. The hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, plays a central role in consolidating new information from short-term into long-term declarative memory, that is, memory for facts and events. Its importance was dramatically revealed by the case of patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), studied by Brenda Milner, whose bilateral hippocampal removal left him unable to form new long-term memories (anterograde amnesia) while sparing older memories and motor learning. This showed the hippocampus is essential for encoding and consolidating new declarative memories, even though the memories are eventually stored in the neocortex. Option A is thus correct. The amygdala (option B), also in the temporal lobe, processes emotion, especially fear, and modulates how strongly emotional experiences are remembered, but it is not the primary consolidation site. The prefrontal cortex (option C) supports working memory, attention, and executive control rather than the consolidation of new long-term memories. The cerebellum (option D) governs motor coordination, balance, and procedural (implicit) learning such as classically conditioned responses, not declarative memory. The concept of synaptic consolidation, involving long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo, 1973) described by Donald Hebb's principle that cells that fire together wire together, underlies how the hippocampus stabilises new memories.", "traps": {"b": "The amygdala processes emotion and modulates the emotional strength of memories, but it is not the primary consolidation structure.", "c": "The prefrontal cortex supports working memory and executive function rather than the consolidation of new long-term memories.", "d": "The cerebellum is chiefly involved in motor coordination and procedural learning, not declarative memory consolidation."}}, {"q": "Functional Analysis is considered under:", "concept": "The answer is c: functional analysis is considered under operant conditioning. B. F. Skinner (1938) showed that operant behaviour is controlled by its consequences: behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and behaviour followed by punishment or no reward becomes less likely. Functional analysis applies this idea to a person's problem behaviour. Instead of asking only what the behaviour looks like, it asks what function it serves, that is, what keeps it going. The simplest form is the ABC model: Antecedents (what happens just before), Behaviour (described in observable terms) and Consequences (what follows). Frederick Kanfer and George Saslow (1965) expanded behavioural analysis into the SORKC model (stimulus, organism, response, contingency, consequence). Common functions are gaining attention, escaping a demand, getting a tangible item and automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Once the function is clear, treatment removes the reinforcer for the problem behaviour and teaches a better behaviour that earns the same result. Kabir, 6, is told to start his homework every evening (antecedent), throws his book (behaviour) and is sent to his room, so homework is avoided (consequence). The function is escape, so the time-out actually rewards him; the plan is to break homework into short tasks, praise effort and let him ask for a break with a card. Option c is right because functional analysis comes from Skinner's operant analysis of behaviour and its consequences. Exam trap: classical conditioning also belongs to behaviour therapy, so a looks plausible; but classical conditioning pairs stimuli to produce reflexive responses and does not analyse consequences, which are the heart of functional analysis.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) explains how a neutral stimulus comes to trigger a reflexive response through pairing; it looks at what comes before a response, not at the consequences that maintain it.", "b": "Humanistic principles (Carl Rogers, 1951; Abraham Maslow, 1954) focus on the self, growth and the therapeutic relationship, and do not break behaviour down into antecedents and consequences.", "d": "Adlerian principles come from Alfred Adler's (1912) individual psychology: inferiority feelings, style of life and social interest. Adlerians ask about the purpose of behaviour, but functional analysis as a method belongs to operant behaviour analysis."}}, {"q": "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:", "concept": "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Functional analysis is a core method of behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis, grounded in the operant conditioning principles of B. F. Skinner. It involves systematically examining a target behaviour in terms of its antecedents (the situations, cues, or triggers that precede it), the behaviour itself (its form, frequency, intensity, and duration), and its consequences (what follows the behaviour and thereby reinforces or maintains it), the so-called ABC (Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence) model. The central assumption is Skinnerian: behaviour is controlled by its consequences, so understanding the reinforcement contingencies that maintain a problem behaviour allows the therapist to change those contingencies, altering antecedents and consequences to reduce maladaptive behaviour and strengthen desired behaviour. For example, a functional analysis might reveal that a child's tantrums (behaviour) are triggered by task demands (antecedent) and reinforced by escape from the task or by attention (consequence), pointing to an intervention that changes those contingencies. This consequence-focused, contingency-based approach is definitively operant, distinguishing it from classical conditioning (reflexive stimulus associations), humanistic therapy (self-actualization and unconditional positive regard, per Carl Rogers), and psychodynamic therapy (unconscious conflict). Because functional analysis rests on analysing and modifying the consequences of behaviour, it is based on operant conditioning, making option c correct.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) concerns reflexive stimulus-response associations, whereas functional analysis examines consequences of voluntary behaviour.", "b": "Humanistic principles emphasize self-actualization and the therapeutic relationship, not the behavioural ABC analysis.", "d": "Psychodynamic principles focus on unconscious conflict, unrelated to the antecedent-behaviour-consequence framework."}}, {"q": "Involuntary blocking unpleasant feelings and experiences from one's awareness is known as", "concept": "The correct answer is repression. Repression, one of the central defence mechanisms in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, is the unconscious and involuntary exclusion of distressing thoughts, feelings, memories, or impulses from conscious awareness. The key word in the question is involuntary: repression happens automatically and outside of awareness, which distinguishes it from suppression (option d), the closely related but conscious and deliberate act of pushing an unwanted thought out of mind, for example choosing not to think about an exam until later. Freud considered repression the foundation of all other defences and called the theory of repression the cornerstone on which psychoanalysis rests (1914), arguing that repressed material does not disappear but continues to influence behaviour, dreams, and symptoms. Rationalisation generates false but acceptable justifications, and sublimation redirects impulses into valued activity, so neither matches the definition of blocking feelings from awareness. This distinction between repression (unconscious) and suppression (conscious) is a favourite exam point and is clinically important, because uncovering repressed material through free association and interpretation is a core aim of psychodynamic therapy, helping clients gain insight into hidden conflicts that drive their distress.", "traps": {"b": "Rationalisation invents acceptable reasons for behaviour; it does not block feelings from awareness.", "c": "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into productive activity rather than blocking them.", "d": "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate pushing away of thoughts, whereas the question specifies an involuntary process."}}, {"q": "A man who yells at his dog when he's upset with his girlfriend is experiencing _____________.", "concept": "The correct answer is displacement. Displacement is a defense mechanism, in the tradition of Sigmund Freud and systematized by Anna Freud, in which an emotional impulse, usually aggression, is redirected from a threatening or unavailable target onto a safer, less threatening substitute. Here the man is angry with his girlfriend, but because expressing that anger directly may feel dangerous to the relationship, he unconsciously transfers the emotion onto the dog, a harmless and available target. The original feeling is preserved and discharged, but its object is shifted. This is the textbook illustration of displacement, often exemplified by the person who is criticized by a boss and then comes home and snaps at family members (the kick the dog scenario). It differs from the other defenses listed: denial refuses to acknowledge a painful reality; sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially constructive outlets such as art, sport, or work; and repression banishes distressing thoughts and feelings from conscious awareness entirely. Because the man's anger keeps its aggressive quality but is aimed at a substitute object rather than being transformed, hidden, or denied, the mechanism at work is specifically displacement, making option b correct.", "traps": {"a": "Denial is refusing to accept a painful reality, not redirecting an emotion onto a safer target.", "c": "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activities, not into yelling at a pet.", "d": "Repression pushes distressing material out of awareness rather than redirecting the anger toward a substitute object."}}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, which defense mechanism involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others?", "concept": "The answer is a: projection is the defence mechanism that involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others. Defence mechanisms are the ego's unconscious strategies for protecting itself from anxiety. Sigmund Freud described many of them, and his daughter Anna Freud set them out systematically in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). In projection, an impulse or feeling that would be too threatening to acknowledge as one's own, such as hostility, envy or sexual desire, is disowned and perceived instead in another person. The feeling does not change; only its owner does. Freud linked projection to paranoia, and George Vaillant later placed it among the immature defences in his hierarchy, below neurotic defences such as repression and displacement and mature ones such as sublimation and humour. Freud also used the word in a broader sense for any colouring of perception by the inner world, which is the basis of projective tests such as the Rorschach. Example: a man attracted to a colleague, who cannot accept the feeling, accuses his wife of flirting with everyone; or a student who dislikes her roommate insists, 'She hates me'. Option a is right because attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others is the textbook definition of projection. Exam trap: in displacement the feeling stays yours but moves to a safer target, whereas in projection the feeling is placed in someone else; ask whether the owner or the target has changed.", "traps": {"b": "Repression, which Freud called the cornerstone of psychoanalysis, pushes threatening thoughts and memories out of awareness altogether; nothing is attributed to anyone else.", "c": "Sublimation channels an unacceptable impulse into a socially valued activity, as when aggression is poured into boxing or surgery; Vaillant counts it as a mature defence.", "d": "Displacement redirects a feeling from a threatening target to a safer one, as when anger at the boss is taken out on a child; the person still owns the feeling, unlike in projection."}}, {"q": "Damage to the hippocampus is most likely to impair:", "concept": "The answer is a: damage to the hippocampus mainly impairs memory, especially the ability to form new long-term memories. The hippocampus is a curved structure in the medial temporal lobe, named after the Greek word for seahorse, and it is part of the limbic system. Its main job is consolidation: turning new experiences and facts (declarative or explicit memory) into lasting long-term memories, which are later stored across the cortex. It also builds maps of space; John O'Keefe (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one particular location. The classic evidence is the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Beecher Scoville removed much of both medial temporal lobes, including most of the hippocampus, to stop his epilepsy. Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported severe anterograde amnesia: H.M. could not form new declarative memories, yet his intelligence, short-term memory, older memories and procedural learning were spared (he improved at mirror drawing while denying he had ever practised). So the hippocampus is needed for new explicit memories, not for every kind of memory. For example, if Kabir suffers hippocampal damage after encephalitis, he may chat normally and recall his childhood, but forget a visitor within minutes and fail to learn the route to his new ward. Option a is right because the hippocampus is the brain's key structure for consolidating new long-term memories. Exam trap: examiners offer the jobs of nearby structures. Balance belongs to the cerebellum and the vestibular system, sleep to the hypothalamus, reticular formation and pons, and breathing to the medulla. Also do not confuse the hippocampus (memory) with the amygdala (fear and emotional memory), its neighbour in the limbic system.", "traps": {"b": "Balance and coordination depend on the cerebellum and the vestibular system of the inner ear, not on the hippocampus.", "c": "Sleep and waking are controlled by the hypothalamus (including the suprachiasmatic nucleus), the reticular activating system and the pons; hippocampal damage does not mainly disturb sleep.", "d": "Breathing is run by the respiratory centre of the medulla, with help from the pons, so damage there, not to the hippocampus, threatens breathing."}}, {"q": "A person suffered a head injury in a road accident several years ago. Since the accident, he is unable to form new memories. He can remember many things which he did before the accident, but he cannot remember what he said ten minutes ago. The part of the brain which is most probably injured is:", "concept": "The brain region most likely injured is the hippocampus, so the answer is b. The case describes anterograde amnesia: the man can recall events from before his accident (intact remote and old memories) but cannot form new long term memories, forgetting what he said ten minutes ago. This is the classic signature of bilateral hippocampal damage, made famous by the patient H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), who after surgical removal of his medial temporal lobes, including about the front half of each hippocampus, was unable to form new declarative memories while retaining older memories and normal short term memory and intelligence. The hippocampus is essential for consolidation, the process of converting new short term and working memories into durable long term memories that can later be retrieved. It is not, however, the storage site for old memories, which is why remote memories laid down before the injury survive: those have already been consolidated and distributed to cortical stores, so they remain accessible even when the hippocampus is damaged. Hence damage to the hippocampus selectively impairs the formation of new memories while sparing old ones, exactly the pattern described. The exam discriminator is the dissociation between preserved old memories and an inability to lay down new ones, which points unmistakably to the hippocampus and its role in memory consolidation rather than to brainstem, spinal, or arousal structures.", "traps": {"a": "The brain stem governs vital autonomic functions like breathing and heart rate; damage there would not selectively impair new memory formation.", "c": "The spinal cord conducts motor and sensory signals between body and brain; it has no role in forming memories.", "d": "The reticular formation regulates arousal, sleep, and alertness; its damage affects consciousness and attention, not the specific encoding of new memories."}}, {"q": "Anterograde amnesia results from accidental injury of the", "concept": "Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new long-term declarative memories after the moment of injury, while memories laid down before the damage often remain intact. The structure most responsible is the hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped region in the medial temporal lobe that is essential for consolidating new experiences from short-term into stable long-term memory. The classic evidence comes from the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), studied by Brenda Milner: after bilateral removal of his medial temporal lobes, including about the front half of each hippocampus, to treat epilepsy, H.M. could no longer form new conscious memories of facts and events, though his older memories and his ability to learn new motor skills survived. This dissociation showed that the hippocampus is critical specifically for declarative memory consolidation, not for storage of already-consolidated memories or for procedural learning (which depends more on the cerebellum and basal ganglia). So injury to the hippocampus leaves a person stuck in the present, unable to encode new facts and events, the defining picture of anterograde amnesia. The distractors are all nearby or well-known brain structures with unrelated jobs: homeostasis for the hypothalamus, hormone control for the pituitary, and coordination for the cerebellum. Anchor it on H.M.: hippocampus damaged, new declarative memories cannot form. The correct answer is the hippocampus.", "traps": {"b": "The hypothalamus governs homeostasis and drives, not the consolidation of new declarative memories.", "c": "The pituitary is an endocrine gland regulating hormones and has no direct role in forming new memories.", "d": "The cerebellum supports motor learning and coordination, and its damage causes ataxia rather than anterograde amnesia."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about obsessional thoughts?", "concept": "The answer is c: obsessional thoughts are ego-dystonic. An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive thought, image or impulse that the person experiences as unwanted, senseless and out of character, yet recognises as a product of his or her own mind. Kurt Schneider's classic definition describes contents of consciousness that carry a feeling of subjective compulsion and cannot be got rid of, although on quiet reflection they are seen as senseless. \"Ego-dystonic\" means the experience clashes with the person's values and self-image, so it causes distress and is resisted. Think of a devoted young mother who keeps getting the thought \"I might stab my baby\" while cutting vegetables: she is horrified, hides the knives and fights the thought, which is exactly why it is an obsession and not a wish. ICD-10 adds that at least one thought is still resisted, even if unsuccessfully, and that carrying out a related act is not in itself pleasurable. Obsessive-compulsive personality traits such as perfectionism are different: they feel ego-syntonic, natural and right to the person. Option c is right because distress, resistance and the sense that the thought is alien to the self are the core of an obsession. Exam trap: \"recognised as one's own\" (obsession) is not \"inserted from outside\" (thought insertion in schizophrenia); the obsessional patient owns the thought but rejects its content.", "traps": {"a": "Thought-stopping (saying \"Stop!\" when the thought appears) is an older behavioural technique, not the treatment of choice, and suppressing a thought often makes it rebound. Exposure and response prevention and SSRIs are the evidence-based treatments.", "b": "Obsessions often lead to compulsions, but not always; some patients have predominantly obsessional thoughts or ruminations without overt rituals (ICD-10 F42.0).", "d": "Sexual themes are among the commonest obsessions, alongside contamination, harm, symmetry and religious themes, so they are not rare."}}, {"q": "The basic difference between obsession and compulsion is:", "concept": "The answer is a: an obsession is a preoccupation with certain thoughts, while a compulsion is a preoccupation with certain actions. Both are symptoms of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Obsessions are recurrent, persistent and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that intrude into the mind and cause marked anxiety or distress, such as a fear of contamination or a nagging doubt that the door is unlocked. The person usually recognises them as products of his or her own mind and tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (hand washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, silently repeating a prayer) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules, to reduce distress or prevent a feared event. The relief is brief, so the cycle repeats and strengthens. Example: Arjun keeps thinking that his hands carry germs (obsession), so he washes them twenty times before every meal (compulsion). DSM-5 (2013) requires obsessions, compulsions or both that are time-consuming (for example more than one hour a day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Option a is right because it states the classic distinction: the obsession is the thought, the compulsion is the act that answers it. Exam trap: obsession and compulsion are two symptoms of one disorder, not two separate disorders; and compulsions can be silent mental acts such as counting, so a ritual that happens in the head is still a compulsion if it is done to reduce anxiety. OCD was never a mood disorder; it sat with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) and got its own chapter only in DSM-5 (2013).", "traps": {"b": "Neither is a mood disorder: obsessions and compulsions are both symptoms of OCD, which was grouped with the anxiety disorders through DSM-IV-TR (2000) and now has its own obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter in DSM-5 (2013).", "c": "There is a clear difference: obsessions are intrusive thoughts, urges or images, and compulsions are the repetitive acts performed to neutralise them; they are also symptoms, not two separate disorders.", "d": "This would only fit if no option gave the correct distinction, but option a states the standard textbook difference between thought and action."}}, {"q": "The term IQ was invented by", "concept": "The concept of the intelligence quotient grew out of the mental-testing movement begun by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon in France, who introduced the idea of a child's 'mental age' relative to chronological age. The actual term intelligence quotient, and the formula for it, came from the German psychologist William Stern in 1912. Stern proposed dividing mental age by chronological age to yield a ratio that stayed comparable across ages; multiplying by 100 (to remove the decimal) gives the familiar IQ score, so a child whose mental age matches their chronological age scores 100. Lewis Terman, the distractor spelled 'Termon' in option a, later revised Binet's test at Stanford University to create the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale and helped spread IQ testing in the United States, but he adopted and applied Stern's quotient rather than inventing it. Gordon Allport is associated with the trait approach to personality (cardinal, central, and secondary traits), and John Watson is the founder of behaviorism, who famously downplayed inner mental constructs; neither is connected with the origin of IQ. So the credit for coining the term belongs to William Stern, with Binet supplying the mental-age foundation and Terman supplying the influential American test. This is a standard history-of-psychology fact examined frequently in entrance papers.", "traps": {"a": "Terman adapted the Binet scale into the Stanford-Binet and popularised IQ scoring, but he did not originate the term.", "b": "Gordon Allport was a trait theorist of personality and had nothing to do with coining the intelligence quotient.", "d": "John Watson founded behaviorism and rejected mentalistic constructs, so he did not invent the IQ concept."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?", "concept": "The correct answer is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute (assessed at the same time). Concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion-related validity, the family of validity concerned with how well a test's scores correspond to an external criterion. Criterion validity has two forms distinguished by timing. Predictive validity assesses how well a test predicts a criterion measured in the future (for example, whether an aptitude test predicts later job performance). Concurrent validity assesses how well a test correlates with an established criterion or an existing validated measure of the same construct assessed at roughly the same point in time. For instance, a new, shorter depression scale shows concurrent validity if its scores correlate highly with scores on an already-established depression measure administered concurrently. The essence is agreement with a criterion in the present. Option A is a trap because it takes 'concurrent' too literally as merely testing simultaneously, without the crucial requirement of correlation with a valid criterion measuring the same attribute. Option B describes inter-rater reliability, and option C describes internal consistency reliability, both of which are reliability rather than validity. This item tests the correct definition of concurrent validity as present-time correlation with a criterion measure of the same construct.", "traps": {"a": "Merely administering two tests at the same time is a superficial reading of the word 'concurrent'; concurrent validity requires that the test correlate with an established criterion measured at the same time, not just simultaneous testing.", "b": "Agreement among clinicians is inter-rater reliability, not concurrent validity.", "c": "Items consistently relating to each other is internal consistency reliability, not validity."}}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "traps": {"a": "Trait theorists such as Gordon Allport and Raymond Cattell describe stable, measurable dispositions and say little about the unconscious, so trait theory cannot be the approach that maximised it.", "c": "Psychoanalytic is right for the first blank, but humanistic theory (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) only played down the unconscious; Rogers still accepted that experiences can be denied to awareness, whereas behaviourism set the unconscious aside entirely.", "d": "Behaviourist fits the second blank, but trait theory never maximised the unconscious; Gordon Allport even stressed that healthy adults are guided by conscious, present motives."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "concept": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "traps": {"b": "An enduring pattern of unstable, intense relationships, swinging between idealisation and devaluation, is a core DSM criterion for BPD, so it is a cardinal feature, not the exception.", "c": "A lack of a stable self-image is the DSM identity disturbance criterion and matches Otto Kernberg's identity diffusion, so it is a cardinal BPD feature.", "d": "Frequent, unpredictable mood swings describe affective instability (marked reactivity of mood lasting hours, rarely days), a core BPD criterion."}}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "traps": {"a": "Verbal fluency tests, such as the FAS (Controlled Oral Word Association) test, ask the person to generate as many words as possible from a letter or category in a minute; the Stroop asks for no word generation.", "b": "Visual perception tests, such as Arthur Benton's Judgment of Line Orientation, assess how a person sees shape and space; the Stroop assumes normal colour vision and measures the control of attention instead.", "c": "Memory is tested by tools such as the Wechsler Memory Scale or the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test; the Stroop stimuli stay in front of the person throughout, so nothing has to be remembered."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "concept": "The answer is a: token economies are not built on classical conditioning; they come from operant conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with one that already triggers a reflex comes to trigger a similar response on its own. Several therapies apply this to emotional responses. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy so that relaxation replaces anxiety (counterconditioning, or reciprocal inhibition). Flooding exposes the person to the feared stimulus at full strength, with escape blocked, until the conditioned fear extinguishes. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus like a nausea-inducing drug so that the behaviour itself becomes aversive. A token economy works differently: it uses consequences. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin ran one on a psychiatric ward, described in The Token Economy (1968): patients earned tokens (generalised secondary reinforcers) for self-care and ward jobs and exchanged them for privileges, exactly as B. F. Skinner's operant principles predict. A child earning stars for homework that buy screen time is a token economy at home. Option a is right because tokens strengthen behaviour through reinforcement after the response, not through stimulus pairing. Exam trap: ask whether the technique changes what follows a behaviour (operant) or what a stimulus is paired with (classical).", "traps": {"b": "Flooding is a classical conditioning (extinction) technique: prolonged, full-intensity exposure to the conditioned fear stimulus without the feared outcome lets the fear response die out; Thomas Stampfl's implosive therapy is its imaginal version.", "c": "Aversion therapy is classical conditioning: an unwanted behaviour or stimulus (alcohol, a fetish object) is paired with an aversive unconditioned stimulus such as a nausea-producing drug or mild shock until it evokes discomfort.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's counterconditioning technique, pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy, so it rests squarely on classical conditioning."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client centred therapist most likely to use?", "concept": "Client-centred therapy, also called person-centred therapy, was developed by Carl Rogers as a humanistic alternative to both psychoanalysis and behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913). Its guiding assumption is that people possess an innate drive toward growth and self-actualisation, and that the therapist's job is not to direct or diagnose but to create a facilitative relationship in which clients can find their own answers. Rogers specified three core conditions the therapist must provide: unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement), genuineness or congruence (being authentic rather than hiding behind a professional facade), and empathic understanding (accurately sensing the client's inner world). The characteristic technique that expresses this empathy is reflection: the therapist mirrors back the feelings and meanings the client expresses, for example responding 'It sounds like you feel trapped and exhausted,' so the client feels deeply heard and can explore their experience further. Reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client rather than the expert, which is the essence of the non-directive method. The distractors all violate this stance: analysis and interpretation belong to Freudian psychoanalysis, giving advice is directive and imposes the therapist's judgement, and aversive conditioning is a behaviourist procedure. Only reflection embodies Rogers's empathic, non-directive style, so it is the correct choice.", "traps": {"a": "Interpretation and analysis of unconscious material are psychoanalytic techniques belonging to Freud, not the client-centred approach.", "b": "Giving advice is directive and imposes the therapist's agenda, contradicting the non-directive stance Rogers required.", "d": "Aversive conditioning is a behaviour-therapy technique, entirely at odds with the warm, accepting person-centred method."}}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses caused by long-term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "concept": "The answer is d: Korsakoff's disease (Korsakoff's syndrome) is the alcohol-related illness with profound retrograde amnesia. The Russian neuropsychiatrist Sergei Korsakoff described it in the 1880s in long-term heavy drinkers. Chronic alcohol abuse, with poor diet and impaired absorption, causes thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. The acute phase is Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke, with confusion, ataxia and eye-movement problems (ophthalmoplegia, nystagmus). If thiamine is not given quickly it can progress to Korsakoff's syndrome, so the two are often named together as Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal nucleus of the thalamus. The memory picture is severe anterograde amnesia (inability to form new memories) plus extensive retrograde amnesia with a temporal gradient: recent decades are lost more than early life. Patients often confabulate, filling gaps with invented but sincere stories, and lack insight. Example: a man in his fifties insists he still works at a job he left years ago and cheerfully describes a meeting there \"this morning\". Immediate memory and general intelligence are relatively spared. Option d is right because Korsakoff's is the only option caused by alcohol abuse, and its retrograde amnesia is classically profound. Exam trap: Korsakoff's is not a dementia like Alzheimer's; it is an amnestic disorder caused by a vitamin deficiency, and early thiamine can prevent it.", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer in 1906, is a progressive dementia with amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles; it is not caused by alcohol abuse.", "b": "Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant genetic disorder (an expanded CAG repeat on chromosome 4) causing chorea, psychiatric symptoms and dementia; it has nothing to do with alcohol.", "c": "Parkinson's disease, described by James Parkinson in 1817, results from loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra and causes tremor, rigidity and slowed movement; it is not alcohol-induced."}}, {"q": "Who is considered the father of experimental psychology?", "concept": "The answer is b: Wilhelm Wundt is considered the father of experimental psychology. Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician who had assisted Hermann von Helmholtz, argued that the mind could be studied with the experimental methods of physiology. His Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) announced a new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig his students began original research in what is conventionally called the first psychological laboratory; that year is taken as the birth of psychology as an independent science. He founded the journal Philosophische Studien (1881) and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell, Emil Kraepelin and Charles Spearman. His method was controlled introspection: trained observers reacted to precise stimuli, such as a metronome beat or a flash of light, and reported their immediate experience while reaction times were measured. He also wrote the ten-volume Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) on language, myth and custom, which he thought needed non-experimental methods. Example: in a Leipzig reaction-time study an observer pressed a key as soon as he heard a click, and Wundt compared this simple reaction with one that required recognising the sound first. Option b is right because Wundt gave psychology its first laboratory, journal and trained students, the marks of an experimental science. Exam trap: read the qualifier: Gustav Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860) is the father of psychophysics, William James of American psychology, John B. Watson of behaviourism and Sigmund Freud of psychoanalysis; only experimental psychology belongs to Wundt.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud is the father of psychoanalysis; his clinical method of free association and case study was not experimental.", "c": "John B. Watson is the father of behaviourism (1913) and ran the Little Albert study (1920), but experimental psychology had begun with Wundt decades earlier.", "d": "B. F. Skinner was the leading radical behaviourist and developer of operant conditioning (1938), working long after psychology's experimental foundation."}}, {"q": "The primary function of the limbic system is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the primary function of the limbic system is emotion regulation and memory. The name comes from the Latin limbus, 'border', because these structures form a ring around the thalamus at the inner edge of the cerebral hemispheres. In 1937 James Papez proposed an emotional circuit running from the hippocampus through the fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus and back to the hippocampus. Paul MacLean added the amygdala and other structures and named the whole the limbic system in 1952. Its main members are the hippocampus, which forms new declarative memories and spatial maps; the amygdala, which detects threat and drives fear and emotional memory; the cingulate cortex; the fornix; the septum; and the mammillary bodies, with the hypothalamus closely linked. Evidence comes from H.M., who lost both medial temporal lobes in 1953 surgery and could no longer form new declarative memories, and from the monkeys of Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy, which became tame and fearless after removal of the temporal lobes including the amygdala. Example: you remember exactly where you were when you heard shocking news because the amygdala tags the event as emotional and the hippocampus stores it strongly. Option b is right because emotion and memory are the two functions every textbook assigns to the limbic system. Exam trap: the basal ganglia sit close by and help select movements, but motor coordination belongs to the cerebellum, not to the limbic system.", "traps": {"a": "Motor coordination is chiefly the work of the cerebellum, with the basal ganglia selecting and starting movements; the limbic system is not a motor system.", "c": "Visual processing takes place mainly in the occipital lobe, beginning in the primary visual cortex (area 17) and continuing in the ventral and dorsal streams.", "d": "Language production depends on Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus; the limbic system adds emotional tone to speech but does not produce language."}}, {"q": "Reflexes are usually controlled by the:", "concept": "The answer is b: reflexes are usually controlled by the spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic, unlearned response to a stimulus, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan. Charles Sherrington (1906), in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, described the pathway behind it, the reflex arc. It has five links: a receptor in the skin picks up the stimulus; a sensory (afferent) neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord; an interneuron in the grey matter of the cord passes it on; a motor (efferent) neuron carries the command out; and an effector, a muscle or gland, acts. Because the loop closes inside the spinal cord, the brain is left out of the decision. Messages do travel up to the brain, but they arrive after the reflex has already happened, which is why you feel the pain a moment after your hand has jerked away. The simplest arc, the knee-jerk (patellar) reflex, has no interneuron at all: the sensory neuron synapses straight onto the motor neuron, a monosynaptic reflex. When Riya touches a hot iron, her hand pulls back before she has consciously registered the heat; the spinal cord has acted first and told the brain afterwards. Option b is right because the spinal cord is the centre of the classic reflex arc for the limb and body reflexes. Exam trap: some reflexes, such as coughing, sneezing, gagging and swallowing, are run by the medulla in the brainstem, so d looks attractive; but the word \"usually\" points to the spinal cord, which handles the typical reflexes taught in textbooks.", "traps": {"a": "The hypothalamus controls homeostasis (hunger, thirst, body temperature) and the pituitary gland; it regulates drives and hormones, not the fast reflex arc.", "c": "The frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex and plans voluntary, deliberate movement; reflexes by definition bypass this conscious control.", "d": "The medulla does run a few brainstem reflexes (coughing, sneezing, gagging, swallowing) and the vital rhythms of breathing and heart rate, but most reflexes in the body close their loop in the spinal cord."}}, {"q": "Which one is called non-probability sampling?", "concept": "The answer is a: quota sampling is a non-probability method. In non-probability sampling the chance of any member being selected is unknown, because selection depends on the researcher's judgement or convenience rather than on chance. In quota sampling the researcher first decides how many people of each type are needed, usually to mirror the population on characteristics such as gender, age or religion, and then fills each quota with whoever is available. For example, an interviewer told to survey 50 men and 50 women at a mall will stop passers-by until both quotas are full; the proportions look right, but who gets picked within each quota is not random. Other non-probability methods are convenience (accidental), purposive (judgement) and snowball sampling. Option a is right because quota sampling fills subgroups by convenience, so it lacks random selection. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into subgroups. The difference is the last step: stratified sampling selects randomly within each stratum (probability), while quota sampling does not (non-probability).", "traps": {"b": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole groups such as schools or villages, so it is a probability method.", "c": "Systematic sampling picks every kth person from a list after a random start, which makes it a probability method.", "d": "Stratified random sampling selects randomly within each subgroup, so it is a probability method, unlike quota sampling."}}, {"q": "Who first described autism?", "concept": "The answer is a: Leo Kanner. The Austrian-American child psychiatrist Leo Kanner, working at Johns Hopkins in Baltimore, published \"Autistic Disturbances of Affective Contact\" in 1943, describing 11 children who from early life showed extreme aloneness, an inability to relate normally to people, odd or delayed language (echolalia, pronoun reversal, very literal speech) and an \"anxiously obsessive desire for the maintenance of sameness\". Such a child might become distressed if a single piece of furniture was moved. He called the condition early infantile autism, and for decades it was known as Kanner's syndrome. A year later, in 1944, the Austrian paediatrician Hans Asperger independently described boys with \"autistic psychopathy\": fluent speech and normal intelligence but poor social understanding, narrow interests and clumsiness. His German-language paper became widely known only after Lorna Wing's 1981 paper on \"Asperger's syndrome\". DSM-5-TR now merges both pictures into autism spectrum disorder. Option a is right because Kanner's 1943 paper is the first standard description of autism as a childhood syndrome. Exam trap: the word \"autism\" was coined earlier by Eugen Bleuler (1911), but for withdrawal in schizophrenia, not for the developmental disorder; and Asperger came after Kanner, not before.", "traps": {"b": "Hans Asperger described \"autistic psychopathy\" in 1944, a year after Kanner, in boys with good language and intelligence but poor social skills.", "c": "Stella Chess, with Alexander Thomas, ran the New York Longitudinal Study of temperament (easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up children); she also studied autism after congenital rubella, but she did not first describe it.", "d": "Benham is linked with Benham's top, a spinning black-and-white disc that produces illusory colours; the name has nothing to do with autism."}}, {"q": "The \"empty chair\" technique is commonly used in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the empty chair technique is commonly used in Gestalt therapy. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (Gestalt Therapy, 1951). It is an experiential, here-and-now therapy that aims to increase awareness and help clients finish unfinished business, feelings from past relationships that were never expressed and still shape present life. In the empty chair technique, the client imagines a person (often a parent, a former partner or someone who has died) or a disowned part of the self sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly, in the present tense. The client may then switch chairs and answer as the other person or part. This turns talk about feelings into direct experience of them, so that anger, grief or longing can be expressed and integrated. A related form is the two-chair dialogue between the 'topdog' (the critical 'you should' voice) and the 'underdog' (the apologetic voice that wins by stalling). Example: a man who never told his late father how hurt he felt by his criticism speaks to the empty chair, then moves into it and answers as his father; he ends the session tearful but relieved. Option b is right because the empty chair is the best-known signature technique of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: emotion-focused therapy (Leslie Greenberg) and psychodrama (Jacob Moreno) also use chair work, but in exam options the empty chair points to Gestalt therapy, never to REBT, psychoanalysis or behaviour therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Rational emotive behaviour therapy (Albert Ellis) disputes irrational beliefs through the ABC model and homework; it is didactic and argumentative, not built on chair dialogues.", "c": "Psychoanalysis uses free association, dream analysis and transference interpretation with a largely silent analyst; it does not ask the patient to address an imagined person in a chair.", "d": "Behavioural therapy uses conditioning methods such as exposure, reinforcement and modelling; role-play appears in assertiveness training, but the empty chair is not a behavioural technique."}}, {"q": "The zone of proximal development was given by –", "concept": "The answer is c: the zone of proximal development (ZPD) was given by the Russian psychologist Lev Vygotsky. Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934) developed sociocultural theory, which holds that higher mental functions begin in social interaction and are then internalised by the child. His ideas reached English readers mainly through Mind in Society (1978). The ZPD is the gap between what a child can do alone (the actual developmental level) and what the child can do with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer (the potential developmental level). Teaching works best inside this zone, on tasks the child cannot yet do alone but can manage with help. If the task is too easy nothing new is learnt; if it is too hard, help cannot bridge the gap. David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) later coined 'scaffolding' for the support a tutor gives and gradually withdraws as competence grows. Vygotsky also stressed private speech (children talking aloud to guide their own actions) and the role of language and culture in shaping thought. Example: Arjun, aged six, cannot finish a 30-piece jigsaw alone, but when his older sister suggests starting with the edges and sorting pieces by colour, he completes it. A week later he does it by himself. The jigsaw was in his ZPD, and his sister's hints were scaffolding. Option c is right because the ZPD is Vygotsky's central educational concept. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) is the common wrong answer because both theorists studied cognitive development; Piaget saw the child as a lone scientist moving through fixed stages, while Vygotsky stressed social guidance. Also, the word 'scaffolding' is not Vygotsky's own; it came from Wood, Bruner and Ross.", "traps": {"a": "Erik Erikson (1950) proposed eight psychosocial stages, each a crisis such as trust versus mistrust; his theory is about identity and social relationships, not guided learning within a zone.", "b": "Jean Piaget (1936) proposed four stages of cognitive development driven by the child's own exploration through assimilation and accommodation; the ZPD is the social rival to his view, not part of it.", "d": "Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed the psychosexual stages and the id, ego and superego; his theory concerns unconscious drives, not learning with adult help."}}, {"q": "An IQ test does NOT provide which of the following?", "concept": "The correct answer is that an IQ test does NOT provide a good predictor of behaviour. Standardised intelligence tests such as the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, developed by David Wechsler, and the Stanford-Binet scale, developed from the work of Alfred Binet and revised by Lewis Terman, are among the most psychometrically sound instruments in psychology. They have excellent reliability, including high test-retest reliability (IQ scores are quite stable over time) and high internal consistency (their subtests and items cohere well in measuring the same underlying general ability). They also have good validity for what they are designed to assess, and notably strong criterion validity for predicting academic achievement and, to a meaningful degree, occupational and educational outcomes. However, the key limitation, and the intended answer here, is that an IQ score is a relatively poor predictor of specific individual behaviour in everyday life. Intelligence tests measure cognitive abilities and problem-solving skills, but real-world behaviour is shaped by many additional factors such as motivation, personality, emotional intelligence (a concept popularised by Daniel Goleman), social context, and situational variables, so IQ alone does not reliably predict how a person will behave. Thus while an IQ test delivers strong reliability and validity, it does not serve as a good general predictor of behaviour. This item tests awareness of the strengths and limits of intelligence testing.", "traps": {"a": "Well-constructed IQ tests do show high test-retest reliability (stable scores over time), so this is something they provide, not what they fail to provide.", "c": "IQ tests have high internal consistency (their items cohere in measuring the same underlying ability), so this is a property they possess.", "d": "IQ tests demonstrate good validity for the cognitive abilities they measure, so this is a strength, not a shortcoming."}}, {"q": "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:", "concept": "The correct answer is two standard deviations below the mean. IQ tests such as the Wechsler scales, developed by David Wechsler, are standardised to a mean of 100 with a standard deviation of 15. The DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation (the older term for what the DSM-5 calls Intellectual Disability) specified significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, operationalised as an IQ of approximately 70 or below. An IQ of 70 lies exactly two standard deviations below the mean (100 minus 2 times 15 equals 70), which is why the two-standard-deviation cutoff is the cardinal criterion. Below that threshold roughly the bottom 2 to 3 percent of the population falls, reflecting the properties of the normal distribution. Importantly, the diagnosis required more than a low IQ alone: it also demanded concurrent deficits in adaptive functioning (such as communication, self-care, and social skills) with onset before age 18. The severity levels (mild, moderate, severe, profound) were graded by IQ ranges. The distractors give cutoffs that are either far too lenient (one SD, IQ 85) or implausibly extreme (four or seven SD). This item tests knowledge of the statistical basis of the intellectual disability threshold, anchored at two standard deviations below the mean.", "traps": {"a": "Seven standard deviations below the mean would correspond to an IQ near zero, far beyond any clinical cutoff and biologically implausible as a threshold.", "b": "One standard deviation below the mean gives an IQ of about 85, which is within the low-average range and not a criterion for intellectual disability.", "d": "Four standard deviations below the mean (IQ around 40) falls within the moderate-to-severe range but is far below the general diagnostic threshold, so it is too extreme as the cutoff."}}, {"q": "The MMPI is used to measure:", "concept": "The answer is b: the MMPI measures personality and psychological disorders. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was developed at the University of Minnesota by the psychologist Starke R. Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley and published in 1943. They used empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if a psychiatric patient group (for example, people with depression) answered it differently from ordinary people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The result was ten clinical scales, including Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia and Social Introversion, plus validity scales (L for lying, F for infrequent responding, K for defensiveness) that show whether the person answered honestly. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 true/false items; the MMPI-2-RF and MMPI-3 are shorter revisions, and the MMPI-A is for adolescents. Clinical example: a psychologist assessing a patient with vague physical complaints may find Hypochondriasis and Hysteria raised well above Depression, the classic \"conversion V\" profile, which guides diagnosis and treatment planning. Option b is right because the MMPI is the most widely used objective inventory for assessing personality and psychopathology. Exam trap: the MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test, and it was built empirically, not from the Big Five or any theory of traits.", "traps": {"a": "Leadership potential is judged with tools such as assessment centres, situational judgement tests or normal-range inventories like the California Psychological Inventory; the MMPI was not designed for this.", "c": "The Big Five (openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism) are measured by the NEO-PI-R of Paul T. Costa Jr. and Robert R. McCrae, a factor-analytic inventory of normal personality.", "d": "Unconscious drives are the target of projective techniques such as the Rorschach inkblots and the TAT, which assume people project hidden needs onto ambiguous stimuli."}}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to learn more effectively by correcting oneself, is metacognition. The American developmental psychologist John H. Flavell introduced the term in the 1970s (key paper 1979), describing it as \"thinking about thinking\". It has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about your own mind, tasks and strategies: for example, that you remember diagrams better than lists. Metacognitive regulation is the active use of that knowledge: planning how to study, monitoring whether you understand, and correcting your approach when you do not. Its memory part, metamemory, includes the feeling of knowing and judging how well you have learned something. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts academic success. Example: while preparing for an entrance exam, you notice you keep getting psychophysics questions wrong, realise that rereading notes is not working, and switch to self-testing with past papers; noticing and fixing the problem is metacognition in action. In clinical work, Adrian Wells's metacognitive therapy targets beliefs about worrying itself, such as \"worry keeps me safe\". Option b is right because the question describes metacognition exactly: monitoring and correcting one's own thinking. Exam trap: metacognition is not ordinary cognition or general intelligence; \"meta\" means one level up, so it is cognition about cognition.", "traps": {"a": "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution if followed correctly, like a mathematical formula; it is a problem-solving method, not awareness of one's own thinking.", "c": "Analogies solve a new problem by applying the solution of a similar, familiar one; this is a problem-solving strategy, not self-monitoring.", "d": "Constructive process refers to memory or perception actively building and filling in information (as in Frederic Bartlett's reconstructive memory), not awareness of one's own thought."}}, {"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the:", "concept": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event, such as a pounding heart, fast breathing, sweating and a dry mouth, are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the internal organs (heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut and glands) largely without conscious control. It has two branches that work in opposite directions. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for emergencies, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: it raises heart rate and blood pressure, widens the pupils, slows digestion and makes the adrenal medulla release adrenaline. The parasympathetic branch calms the body afterwards (rest and digest), slowing the heart and restarting digestion. Imagine Arjun in a road accident: within seconds his heart races, his palms sweat and his stomach knots, all sympathetic effects, and an hour later his parasympathetic branch brings him back to baseline. The hypothalamus and amygdala in the brain trigger these changes, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. The polygraph (lie detector) works by recording exactly these autonomic changes. Option c is right because the stem asks which system most directly controls the emotional reaction, and the involuntary arousal of fear and stress is autonomic. Exam trap: the somatic nervous system sounds bodily, but it carries sensation and controls the voluntary skeletal muscles; the gut-level, involuntary arousal of emotion belongs to the autonomic system.", "traps": {"a": "The somatic nervous system carries sensory input and commands to the voluntary skeletal muscles; it lets you run from danger but does not produce the involuntary racing heart and sweating of emotion.", "b": "The central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) contains the hypothalamus and amygdala that start an emotional reaction, but it acts on the body through the autonomic system, which is the more direct controller the stem asks for.", "d": "The cerebral cortex appraises and labels an emotion and can partly regulate it, but it does not directly drive heart rate, breathing or sweating."}}, {"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence:", "concept": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Cattell (proposed 1943, tested 1963) and his student John Horn (1966) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, such as spotting the rule in a matrix puzzle or working out an unfamiliar gadget. It depends heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience. Because it keeps growing with use, it holds steady or even rises into old age. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) followed adults for decades and found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood, while perceptual speed declines from early adulthood. Timothy Salthouse (1996) traced much of the fluid loss to a general slowing of processing. Example: Riya's grandmother, aged 72, finishes the newspaper crossword before anyone else and knows every rare word (crystallised), but takes far longer than Riya to learn a new phone app (fluid). Option c is right because it puts the declining ability (fluid) in the first blank and the stable one (crystallised) in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: examiners reverse the order (option d) to catch students who remember the pair but not which one falls; think 'fluid flows away, crystals last'. They also invent a 'fixed' intelligence, which is not a type in any theory; it echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, a belief about ability rather than a kind of ability.", "traps": {"a": "Fluid is correct for the first blank, but 'fixed' intelligence is not a type in Raymond Cattell and John Horn's theory or any other; the word borrows from Carol Dweck's fixed mindset, which is a belief about ability.", "b": "This invents a 'fixed' intelligence and also places fluid intelligence in the stable slot, when fluid reasoning is precisely the ability that declines with age.", "d": "This reverses the finding of Raymond Cattell and John Horn: crystallised knowledge holds or grows into old age, while fluid reasoning is what declines."}}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "traps": {"a": "Block design belongs to Samuel C. Kohs (1920), whose coloured cubes are arranged to copy printed patterns; David Wechsler later borrowed it as a subtest. It is a performance test, but not the one Porteus built.", "c": "Non-verbal cues are body language and facial signals studied in communication research; no classic performance test is built on them, and the phrase only sounds like \"non-verbal test\".", "d": "Drawing a person is Florence Goodenough's Draw-a-Man test (1926), revised by Dale Harris (1963); it estimates intellectual maturity from the details in a drawing, not from maze tracing."}}, {"q": "Which of the statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the general findings on treating childhood anxiety. The landmark trial is the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) of John Walkup and colleagues (2008), published in the New England Journal of Medicine. It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT, based on Philip Kendall's (1990) Coping Cat programme), the SSRI sertraline, the two combined, or a pill placebo. Rated much or very much improved were 80.7% with the combination, 59.7% with CBT, 54.9% with sertraline and 23.7% with placebo. Three findings follow. CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other (statement a). CBT helped about 60% of children (statement b). The combination beat both single treatments (statement c). Philip Kendall's own randomised trial (1994) had already shown that CBT, which teaches children to recognise anxious feelings and thoughts, use coping plans and then face feared situations through graded exposure, helps roughly two thirds of anxious children. Clinical practice therefore usually offers CBT first and considers adding an SSRI for moderate to severe anxiety or when CBT alone is not enough. Example: Meera, 11, panics before school and cannot sleep away from her mother; a CAMS-style plan might start weekly CBT and add sertraline if she stays stuck. Option d is right because each of the three statements matches the CAMS results. Exam trap: students who know that the combination won may wrongly reject statement a, assuming the two single treatments must also differ; in CAMS they did not.", "traps": {"a": "True on its own: in CAMS, CBT alone (59.7% improved) and sertraline alone (54.9%) did not differ significantly; but because b and c are also true, choosing a alone is incomplete.", "b": "True on its own: CBT alone produced a good response in about 60% (59.7%) of children in CAMS; but a and c are true as well, so this single option undersells the findings.", "c": "True on its own: combination treatment (80.7%) beat both CBT and sertraline alone; but a and b are also supported, so 'All of these' is the complete answer."}}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the z-score. A z-score tells how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies above or below the mean of a group: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The older ratio IQ, from William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), was mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It worked for children but failed for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a perfectly ordinary adult's IQ would fall with every birthday. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, replaced it with the deviation IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the mean and SD of his or her own age group in a large norm sample, turned into a z-score, and then rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. So scoring exactly at your age group's mean gives 100 at any age, one SD above (z = +1) gives 115, and one SD below gives 85. Because the deviation IQ is a standard score on the normal curve, it converts directly to a percentile: 115 is about the 84th percentile, 130 about the 98th. Modern Wechsler tests and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition all use it. Example: Arjun, 30, scores 12 points above the mean for his age group on a test whose SD at that age is 6; his z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 30 = 130. Option c is right because the deviation IQ is a z-score rescaled to mean 100 and SD 15. Exam trap: option b describes the ratio IQ that the deviation IQ replaced, so students who remember 'mental age' pick the wrong formula.", "traps": {"a": "Delinquency has no role in IQ scoring; this distractor plays on the word 'deviation', which in statistics means distance from the mean, not deviant behaviour.", "b": "Mental age versus chronological age is the ratio IQ of William Stern and Lewis Terman ((MA ÷ CA) × 100), the method the deviation IQ replaced.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores were the two halves of David Wechsler's older scales (dropped in the WAIS-IV); comparing them is profile analysis, not the basis of the deviation IQ."}}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because:", "concept": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson (1950), life stages matter because each one involves a crisis or dilemma. In Childhood and Society, Erikson described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage is defined by a psychosocial crisis: a turning point between two opposite tendencies, set off when a growing person meets new social demands. They run trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). 'Crisis' here means a choice of direction, not a disaster. A favourable resolution, tilted towards the positive pole, leaves the ego with a basic strength or virtue, such as hope from trust or fidelity from identity. An unresolved crisis makes later ones harder but not hopeless, because development continues across the whole lifespan. Erikson called the built-in sequence the epigenetic principle: the stages unfold on a timetable, but society shapes how each one turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician while his parents push medicine; he is working through identity versus role confusion. Option a is right because a crisis or dilemma is the defining feature of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: examiners offer cognitive development, which is the stage logic of Jean Piaget (1936), and biological programming, which fits the libido-driven psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud better (Three Essays 1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923); Erikson's stages are psychosocial, defined by social conflicts.", "traps": {"b": "Stages that mark new levels of cognitive development are Jean Piaget's (sensorimotor to formal operational); Erik Erikson's stages concern social and emotional conflicts, not reasoning ability.", "c": "Erik Erikson's epigenetic principle does assume a built-in ground plan, but each stage is defined by its social crisis; stages as pure biological programming fit Arnold Gesell's maturation theory or Sigmund Freud's libido-driven stages better.", "d": "Erik Erikson's stages are not symptoms that should 'appear'; everyone passes through all eight, and difficulty comes from a poorly resolved crisis, not from a stage failing to show up."}}, {"q": "REM sleep behaviour disorder is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterised as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "traps": {"b": "Rapid eye movements continue in RBD; they are how the stage is recognised on the sleep study, so they are not the missing feature.", "c": "The desynchronised, high-frequency, low-amplitude EEG of REM is still present in RBD; the brain is in genuine REM, and only the muscle paralysis fails.", "d": "Penile erections are a normal feature of REM sleep in males and are not what is lost in RBD; their presence during sleep is used instead in nocturnal penile tumescence testing for erectile dysfunction."}}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of +1?", "concept": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "traps": {"a": "50% is the area above the mean (z = 0), the whole upper half of the curve; by z = +1 another 34% has been passed, leaving about 16%.", "c": "25% is the area above the third quartile, which lies at about z = +0.67, not at z = +1.", "d": "10% is the area above about z = +1.28 (the 90th percentile), so it is too small for z = +1."}}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "traps": {"a": "Taste is processed in the gustatory cortex of the insula and frontal operculum, reached through cranial nerves VII, IX and X and the thalamus, not in the postcentral gyrus.", "b": "Sight is processed in the primary visual (striate) cortex around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe.", "c": "Hearing is processed in the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus of the temporal lobe."}}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of:", "concept": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both forms of punishment. In operant conditioning, Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) experiments showed that consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Two questions classify every consequence. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Here positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour: a scolding, a slap, extra homework. Negative punishment removes something pleasant: time-out from play, a fine (response cost), losing phone time. Both make the behaviour less likely, so both are punishment. By contrast, positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, money), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet, the seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up); both make the behaviour more likely. Skinner argued that punishment suppresses behaviour only temporarily, teaches nothing about what to do instead and can breed fear and aggression, so reinforcing a better behaviour usually works better. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him when he hits his sister (positive punishment) and also takes away his cricket bat for a day (negative punishment); both aim to reduce hitting. Option b is right because adding an aversive stimulus and removing a pleasant one are the two forms of punishment. Exam trap: negative reinforcement (option a) is the classic confusion; it removes something unpleasant and so increases behaviour, the reverse of punishment.", "traps": {"a": "Negative reinforcement removes an unpleasant stimulus when the behaviour occurs (switching off a shock, ending a nagging), so the behaviour increases; punishment does the opposite and decreases it.", "c": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus (praise, a sweet) after a behaviour to make it more likely; neither half of the stem adds anything pleasant.", "d": "Secondary (conditioned) reinforcement is a stimulus that gains its power by being paired with a primary reinforcer, such as money or tokens in a token economy; it strengthens behaviour rather than weakening it."}}, {"q": "Counter conditioning is an exposure therapy technique that involves:", "concept": "The correct answer is learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening. Counterconditioning is a behaviour therapy technique, based on classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) principles, in which a previously feared or aversive stimulus is repeatedly paired with a new response that is incompatible with fear, so that the stimulus comes to elicit the new response instead. The principle rests on reciprocal inhibition, articulated by Joseph Wolpe: since relaxation and anxiety cannot coexist, deliberately pairing the feared stimulus with a relaxed, calm state gradually weakens and replaces the fear response. Systematic desensitization is the classic application: the client is taught deep relaxation (following the progressive relaxation method of Edmund Jacobson) and then, while relaxed, is exposed to a hierarchy of increasingly anxiety-provoking versions of the feared stimulus, learning to remain calm rather than afraid. Through this process the person effectively relearns that the event or situation is no longer threatening, extinguishing the conditioned fear. This distinguishes counterconditioning from mere exposure, which simply presents the feared stimulus, and from cognitive insight-based approaches. Because counterconditioning replaces the fear response so that the client comes to experience the once-feared situation as no longer threatening, option c is correct.", "traps": {"a": "Learning that one negative event predicts another describes the acquisition of a fear, the opposite of counterconditioning.", "b": "Mere exposure to the feared stimulus describes exposure in general; counterconditioning specifically pairs it with an incompatible response.", "d": "Recognizing behaviour as counterproductive is a cognitive insight, not the associative relearning of counterconditioning."}}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called:", "concept": "The answer is d: memories that operate outside conscious awareness are called implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) defined implicit memory as an influence of past experience on behaviour, feeling or thought without conscious recollection. Explicit memory is the opposite: you deliberately and consciously retrieve something, as when you recall a list or recognise a face, so it is tested by recall and recognition. Larry Squire (1992) used the parallel terms declarative (explicit) and nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Implicit memory includes procedural skills (typing, cycling), priming (a word seen earlier is completed faster on a word-fragment test even if you do not remember seeing it), classically conditioned responses and habituation. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) could not consciously remember new events after hippocampal surgery in 1953, yet Brenda Milner (1962) showed that he improved day by day at mirror drawing while insisting he had never done the task. So explicit memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, while implicit memory relies on other systems such as the striatum and cerebellum. Example: Arjun types his email password perfectly every morning without thinking, but if asked to say the characters aloud he has to mime typing to recover them; his fingers hold an implicit memory. Option d is right because working outside conscious awareness is the defining feature of implicit memory. Exam trap: options a and b borrow from interference theory, where proactive interference (old learning disrupts new) and retroactive interference (new disrupts old) cause forgetting; 'proactive memories' and 'reactive memories' are not memory systems at all.", "traps": {"a": "'Proactive' belongs to proactive interference, where older learning disrupts recall of newer material (shown by Benton Underwood in 1957); it is a cause of forgetting, not a kind of unconscious memory.", "b": "There is no 'reactive memory'; the term echoes retroactive interference, where new learning disrupts recall of older material, a cause of forgetting rather than a memory system.", "c": "Explicit memory is the opposite: conscious, deliberate recollection of facts and events (Larry Squire's declarative memory), tested by recall and recognition."}}, {"q": "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a", "concept": "The answer is b: a graphical representation of correlational data is a scatterplot, also called a scatter diagram. Correlation describes how two variables vary together. To draw a scatterplot, you put one variable on the X axis and the other on the Y axis, and each person becomes a single dot placed at their pair of scores. The pattern of dots then shows three things at a glance. Direction: dots rising from lower left to upper right show a positive correlation; dots falling from upper left to lower right show a negative one. Strength: the tighter the dots cluster around a straight line, the stronger the correlation; a shapeless cloud means r is near zero. Form: the dots reveal whether the relation is linear or curved, and whether there are outliers or uneven spread (heteroscedasticity). Francis Galton (1886) used such plots of parents' and children's heights to discover regression towards the mean, and Karl Pearson (1896) then formalised the correlation coefficient r. A line of best fit, the regression line, can be drawn through the dots to predict one variable from the other. Example: Aarav plots hours of sleep against exam marks for his 40 classmates. If the dots rise steadily, more sleep goes with higher marks, a positive correlation, though this does not prove that sleep causes better marks. Option b is right because a scatterplot is the graph designed to show the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Exam trap: the other three options are all shapes of a frequency distribution of a single variable. A bell curve, a bimodal curve and a skewed curve each show how scores on one variable are spread, not how two variables relate to each other.", "traps": {"a": "A bell curve is the normal distribution (Abraham de Moivre, 1733; Carl Friedrich Gauss, 1809), the symmetrical shape of one variable's frequency distribution. It does not plot two variables against each other.", "c": "A bimodal distribution is a frequency distribution of one variable with two peaks, such as heights in a mixed group of men and women. It describes one variable, not a correlation.", "d": "A skewed distribution is an asymmetrical frequency distribution with a long tail to one side, such as income. It shows the spread of one variable, not the relation between two."}}, {"q": "Which non-parametric test is equivalent to the independent samples t-test?", "concept": "The correct answer is the Mann-Whitney U test. The independent samples t-test is a parametric procedure that compares the means of two unrelated groups, assuming interval data and normality. When those assumptions are violated, for example with ordinal data or non-normal distributions, the appropriate non-parametric alternative for comparing two independent groups is the Mann-Whitney U test, developed by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (and equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test). It ranks all scores across both groups and tests whether the ranks differ systematically, so it directly parallels the independent samples t-test without requiring normality. Hence option A is correct. The Kruskal-Wallis test (option B), named after William Kruskal and Wilson Allen Wallis, is the non-parametric counterpart of a one-way ANOVA and is used to compare three or more independent groups, not two. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C), from Frank Wilcoxon, is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired-samples (dependent) t-test, used when the two sets of scores are related, such as before-and-after measures on the same subjects. The Friedman test (option D) is the non-parametric equivalent of a repeated-measures ANOVA, for comparing three or more related conditions. Matching each non-parametric test to its parametric partner is a staple of statistics: two independent groups map to Mann-Whitney U.", "traps": {"b": "The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of a one-way ANOVA, comparing three or more independent groups, not two.", "c": "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired-samples t-test, for related samples.", "d": "The Friedman test is the non-parametric equivalent of a repeated-measures ANOVA, for three or more related conditions."}}, {"q": "How many Rorschach cards are coloured?", "concept": "The correct answer is 5. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach and published in 1921 in his work Psychodiagnostik, is a projective personality assessment consisting of ten standardized inkblot cards shown one at a time, with the examinee asked what each might be. Of the ten cards, five are achromatic (black and grey only), and five contain colour. Among the coloured cards, two (Cards II and III) combine black with red, and three (Cards VIII, IX, and X) are fully multicoloured. So the count of cards containing any colour is five. This distinction matters interpretively: in scoring systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner, responses to the chromatic cards, especially colour-based determinants, are linked to how a person processes and modulates emotion and affect, whereas responses to the achromatic cards and shading relate to other dimensions such as dysphoria and anxiety. Test-takers sometimes confuse this with the total of ten cards or with the three fully coloured cards, but the precise answer to how many cards are coloured is five, making option b correct.", "traps": {"a": "10 is the total number of Rorschach cards, not the number that are coloured.", "c": "3 corresponds to the fully multicoloured cards only, omitting the two black-red cards.", "d": "4 is not a correct count of the coloured cards in the standard set."}}, {"q": "The visible spectrum wavelength ranges from", "concept": "The answer is a: the visible spectrum, the band of electromagnetic energy the human eye can detect, runs from about 380 to 760 nanometres. A nanometre is one billionth of a metre. Light is only a thin slice of the electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes gamma rays, X-rays, microwaves and radio waves. Within the visible band, wavelength is experienced as hue: the shortest visible waves (about 380 to 490 nanometres) look violet and blue, the middle ones green and yellow, and the longest (about 620 to 760 nanometres) red. Just below the short end lies ultraviolet, which bees can see but we cannot; just beyond the long end lies infrared, which we feel as heat rather than see. Isaac Newton, in prism experiments he began in 1666, showed that white light is a mixture of these colours by spreading sunlight into a band and then recombining it. Textbooks round the limits differently, giving 380 or 400 at the short end and 700 or 760 at the long end, but every version keeps both ends between ultraviolet and infrared. Riya remembers it as 'roughly 400 to 700, VIBGYOR from short to long'. Option a is right because 380 to 760 nanometres is the standard textbook range for human vision. Exam trap: each wrong option contains a real stretch of visible light but extends it into invisible regions, so check both ends of the range, not just one.", "traps": {"b": "200 to 700 nanometres has an acceptable upper end, but its lower end reaches deep into ultraviolet (below about 380 nanometres), which the human eye cannot see.", "c": "400 to 1000 nanometres starts correctly but runs far into the infrared; wavelengths beyond about 760 nanometres are felt as heat, not seen as light.", "d": "100 to 1000 nanometres overshoots at both ends, taking in ultraviolet and infrared radiation that lie outside human vision."}}, {"q": "The term schizophrenia was coined by", "concept": "The answer is a: the term schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler. The Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (1857 to 1939) introduced the word in a 1908 lecture and set it out fully in his 1911 book Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. Before him, Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) had grouped several pictures under 'dementia praecox' (premature dementia), stressing early onset and a deteriorating course. Bleuler disagreed on two points: not every patient deteriorates, and onset is not always early. He built 'schizophrenia' from the Greek schizein (to split) and phren (mind). The split he meant is a splitting of psychic functions, so that thought, emotion and behaviour lose their normal connection; it does not mean a split or multiple personality. He also wrote of a 'group' of schizophrenias, an early form of today's spectrum idea. Bleuler's fundamental symptoms are remembered as the four As: loosening of associations, disturbed affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into a private inner world). He regarded hallucinations and delusions as accessory symptoms. Example: Rohan's relatives say he has a 'split personality' because of his schizophrenia. The clinician corrects them: Bleuler's split is between Rohan's thoughts and feelings, as when he smiles while describing his father's death. Option a is right because both the word and the idea of a group of schizophrenias come from Bleuler. Exam trap: Emil Kraepelin is the classic distractor because he described the illness first, as dementia praecox; Kraepelin named and classified it, and Bleuler renamed it. Also remember that 'split mind' never means dissociative identity disorder.", "traps": {"b": "David Rosenhan (1973) wrote 'On being sane in insane places', in which healthy pseudopatients were admitted with a diagnosis of schizophrenia; he criticised psychiatric diagnosis, he did not name the disorder.", "c": "Thomas Szasz (1961) wrote The Myth of Mental Illness, arguing that mental illness is a label for problems in living; he was a critic of psychiatry, not the coiner of the term.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because Eugen Bleuler is the correct and well-known answer."}}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is responsible for vision?", "concept": "The answer is d: the occipital lobe is responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, divided by the central sulcus and the lateral (Sylvian) fissure: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) along the calcarine sulcus. Signals travel from the retina through the optic nerve, the optic chiasm (where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross) and the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to V1, so each hemisphere sees the opposite visual field. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel shared the 1981 Nobel Prize for showing that V1 cells respond to edges and lines of particular orientations. Beyond V1, processing splits into two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal 'where' (or 'how') stream into the parietal lobe for location and movement. Example: a stroke in the right occipital lobe leaves a person blind to the left side of space in both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Option d is right because primary visual processing takes place in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: damage outside the occipital lobe can still disturb seeing, since temporal damage causes prosopagnosia (failure to recognise faces) and parietal damage causes neglect; these are higher visual functions, but vision itself begins in the occipital cortex.", "traps": {"a": "The parietal lobe, behind the central sulcus, holds the primary somatosensory cortex for touch and body sense and handles spatial attention; it receives the dorsal visual stream but is not the primary visual area.", "b": "The frontal lobe controls voluntary movement (primary motor cortex), planning and personality, and contains Broca's area for speech production; it does not process vision.", "c": "The temporal lobe houses the primary auditory cortex and Wernicke's area, and its lower part recognises objects and faces through the ventral 'what' stream, but basic vision is occipital."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "concept": "The answer is b: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting. Forgetting is the loss of, or inability to retrieve, information that was once stored, and the main explanations are these. Decay theory, traced to Edward L. Thorndike's law of disuse, holds that memory traces fade with time if unused. Interference theory holds that other learning gets in the way: proactive interference when old learning disrupts new, and retroactive interference when new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, studied by Endel Tulving, holds that the memory is still there but the right cue is missing. Retrieval inhibition, or retrieval-induced forgetting, shown by Michael C. Anderson, Robert A. Bjork and Elizabeth Ligon Bjork (1994), holds that practising some items suppresses related ones. Freud's motivated forgetting (repression) and consolidation failure complete the usual list. Selective attention, studied by Donald Broadbent with his filter model (1958), is an encoding process: choosing which input to process and ignoring the rest. Information you never attended to was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Option b is right because selective attention operates before storage, not on stored memories. Exam trap: some texts list \"encoding failure\" as a reason we cannot remember, as when you cannot draw a coin you have handled thousands of times; that is a failure to store, and selective attention itself is not a theory of forgetting.", "traps": {"a": "Interference is a major cause of forgetting: proactive interference (old learning blocks new) and retroactive interference (new learning blocks old).", "c": "Decay, related to Thorndike's law of disuse, explains forgetting as the fading of unused memory traces over time.", "d": "Retrieval inhibition (retrieval-induced forgetting) is a documented cause of forgetting: retrieving some items suppresses access to related, competing items."}}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "concept": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most often causes loss of coordination (ataxia). The cerebellum, or little brain, sits at the back of the hindbrain behind the pons and holds more than half of all the brain's neurons. It does not start movements. It compares the movement the motor cortex intended with feedback from muscles, joints and the inner ear, and corrects errors so that movement is smooth, accurate and well timed. It also keeps balance and posture and helps us learn motor skills. Gordon Holmes (1917) mapped its signs in soldiers with gunshot wounds of the cerebellum in the First World War. The classic signs are ataxia (an unsteady, wide-based, drunken gait), intention tremor (shaking that grows as the hand nears a target), dysmetria (over- or under-shooting, seen on the finger-nose test), dysdiadochokinesia (clumsy rapid alternating movements), nystagmus and slurred, scanning speech. The signs appear on the same side of the body as the damage. Alcohol affects the cerebellum early, which is why police roadside tests ask drivers to walk a straight line. Example: after a stroke in his right cerebellum, Arjun reaches for a glass of water with his right hand, overshoots it, and his hand shakes more the closer it gets. Option c is right because coordinating, timing and correcting movement is the cerebellum's main job, so its damage shows up as clumsy, unsteady action rather than paralysis. Exam trap: resting tremor and slowness belong to the basal ganglia (Parkinson's disease), whereas the cerebellum gives intention tremor. Jeremy Schmahmann (1998) also described a cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome with subtle planning, spatial and mood changes, but the most likely and classic result is still loss of coordination.", "traps": {"a": "Visuospatial disorientation, such as getting lost or misjudging where objects are, comes mainly from right parietal (parieto-occipital) damage. The cerebellum can add subtle spatial problems, but this is not its typical sign.", "b": "Visual blindness follows damage to the eyes, the optic nerves or the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe (cortical blindness). The cerebellum does not process vision.", "d": "Personality change is the hallmark of frontal lobe damage, especially to the orbitofrontal cortex, as in Phineas Gage. Cerebellar damage changes how smoothly a person moves, not who they are."}}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the frontal lobe. The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex in front of the motor areas, handle planning, judgement, inhibition of impulses and foreseeing consequences, together called executive functions. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in an explosion in 1848. He survived and could walk, talk and remember, but his physician, John Harlow, reported that he became fitful, impulsive, profane, obstinate and unable to carry out plans; his friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Modern neuropsychology describes several frontal syndromes. Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition: impulsivity, tactlessness, poor social judgement and indifference to consequences. Dorsolateral damage produces a dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, rigidity and perseveration, and medial damage produces apathy. Amit's new impulsiveness, irresponsibility and lack of concern for consequences after an accident fit the disinhibited picture, a common result of head injury because the undersides of the frontal lobes bruise easily against the bony skull base. Option b is right because personality change with impulsivity and poor judgement is the hallmark of frontal damage. Exam trap: memory loss points to the temporal lobes; personality change and poor judgement point to the frontal lobes.", "traps": {"a": "Parietal damage disturbs touch, body sense and spatial attention, producing astereognosis or neglect of one side of space, not impulsive personality change.", "c": "Occipital damage causes visual problems such as field defects, cortical blindness or visual agnosia, not changes in judgement or impulse control.", "d": "Temporal damage typically affects memory, hearing and language comprehension (Wernicke's aphasia), and bilateral lesions can cause Kluver-Bucy syndrome, but consequence-blind, irresponsible behaviour is the frontal picture."}}, {"q": "Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:", "concept": "The correct answer is cardinal, central, and secondary. Gordon Allport, a pioneer of the trait approach to personality, proposed that traits, enduring dispositions that make a person's behaviour consistent across situations, could be organised into three hierarchical levels. Cardinal traits are rare, dominant traits that shape virtually every aspect of a person's life and behaviour, so pervasive that the person may become known for them (for example, a personality so generous it is called Christlike); most people do not have a cardinal trait. Central traits are the general characteristics that form the basic building blocks of personality, the handful of core qualities (such as honest, kind, or anxious) one might use to describe someone. Secondary traits are more situation-specific and peripheral, such as preferences or attitudes that appear only under particular circumstances. The distractors belong to other frameworks: id, ego, and superego (option c) are Sigmund Freud's structural components of the psyche, not traits; while primary/secondary/tertiary (option a) and cognitive/emotional/physiological (option b) are generic labels not used by Allport. Understanding Allport's hierarchy matters because he helped establish the trait perspective and the idiographic study of individual uniqueness, foundational to modern personality psychology and later trait models like the Big Five.", "traps": {"a": "Primary, secondary, tertiary is a generic ranking, not Allport's specific trait terminology.", "b": "Cognitive, emotional, physiological describes components of responses, not Allport's levels of traits.", "c": "Id, ego, superego are Freud's structures of personality, not Allport's trait levels."}}, {"q": "Behaviour analysis is based upon the principles of:", "concept": "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Behaviour analysis, and its practical arm applied behaviour analysis (ABA), is the scientific study of behaviour grounded in the principles of operant conditioning as developed by Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) within the framework of radical behaviourism. Its central premise is that behaviour is a function of its consequences and environmental contingencies, and it focuses on observable, measurable behaviour rather than on inferred internal mental states. The core operant principles it applies include positive and negative reinforcement (which increase behaviour), positive and negative punishment (which decrease behaviour), extinction, stimulus control and discrimination, and shaping through successive approximations, all organised around the three-term contingency of antecedent, behaviour, and consequence (the A-B-C model). Behaviour analysts conduct functional analyses to identify the environmental variables maintaining a behaviour and then rearrange contingencies to change it. Applied behaviour analysis is widely used in treating autism spectrum disorder, developmental disabilities, and in education and organisational settings. Because behaviour analysis rests specifically on operant conditioning and deliberately sets aside cognitive constructs and psychoanalytic dream interpretation, options A and C do not apply, which rules out 'all of the above.' This item tests the operant-conditioning foundation of behaviour analysis.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive learning theories emphasise internal mental processes, which behaviour analysis (especially radical behaviourism) explicitly de-emphasises in favour of observable behaviour and its consequences.", "c": "Dream analysis is a psychoanalytic technique concerned with unconscious content, wholly separate from the behavioural focus of behaviour analysis.", "d": "Since only operant conditioning underlies behaviour analysis, the inclusive option that adds cognitive theories and dream analysis is incorrect."}}, {"q": "Where was the first psychology laboratory established?", "concept": "The answer is a, Leipzig. In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt set up the first formal laboratory for experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in Germany. It is treated as the birthplace of scientific psychology because, for the first time, mental processes such as sensation, perception, attention, feeling and reaction time were studied with controlled experiments in a setting devoted to that purpose. The laboratory trained many of the first professional psychologists, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell and Emil Kraepelin, who carried its methods to America and into psychiatry. Other early landmarks are often confused with it: Francis Galton opened an anthropometric laboratory in London in 1884 to measure people's physical and sensory abilities, an early step in mental testing; G. Stanley Hall founded the first American psychology laboratory at Johns Hopkins University in 1883; and Lewis Terman at Stanford University in California published the Stanford-Binet test in 1916. Option a is right because Leipzig is the city where Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory. The exam trap is option c: London did have an early laboratory, Galton's, but it came five years later and measured individual differences rather than founding experimental psychology.", "traps": {"b": "Romania has no connection with the founding of the first psychology laboratory; it is a pure distractor.", "c": "London is linked with Francis Galton's anthropometric laboratory (1884), which came after Leipzig and focused on measuring individual differences.", "d": "California is linked with later American work such as Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet test (1916), long after 1879."}}, {"q": "Choose the correct order of Freud's psychosexual stages of development:", "concept": "The answer is c: Freud's psychosexual stages run oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923), proposed that personality develops as the libido (sexual or life energy) becomes focused on different body areas, called erogenous zones, in a fixed order. In the oral stage (birth to about 1 year) pleasure centres on the mouth through sucking, biting and feeding, and the key conflict is weaning. In the anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) pleasure comes from holding and releasing faeces, and toilet training is the conflict. In the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) the genitals become the focus, and the child passes through the Oedipus complex (for girls, the Electra complex, Carl Jung's 1913 term), resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent and forming the superego. In the latency period (about 6 years to puberty) sexual energy is dormant and channelled into school, friendships and skills. The genital stage (puberty onwards) brings mature sexual interest directed at others. If the conflict at any stage is badly handled, part of the libido stays fixated there, leaving adult traits. For instance, Kabir, harshly toilet trained, may grow into a very orderly, stubborn and stingy adult, which Freud called an anal character. Option c is right because it is the only option that follows the developmental sequence from infancy (the mouth) to adolescence (mature sexuality). Exam trap: latency is often misplaced after genital or before phallic. Remember that latency is a quiet pause between the Oedipal storm of the phallic stage and the arrival of puberty; Freud called it a period rather than a true stage, since no new erogenous zone appears. A handy mnemonic is 'Old Age Pensioners Love Gardening'.", "traps": {"a": "This option lists the five stages in exact reverse order, from genital back to oral; it is a reversal trap, since genital is the last stage, not the first.", "b": "This order puts anal first, oral last and latency before phallic; in fact the oral stage comes first, in infancy, and latency follows the phallic stage.", "d": "This order swaps oral and anal and places genital before latency; latency must come before the genital stage, which begins at puberty."}}, {"q": "Comorbidity explains the notion that:", "concept": "The answer is d: comorbidity is the notion that differently defined disorders can occur together in the same person. In psychiatry, comorbidity means the presence of two or more distinct disorders at the same time, such as major depressive disorder with generalised anxiety disorder, or alcohol use disorder with post-traumatic stress disorder. In mental health it is the rule rather than the exception. The National Comorbidity Survey of Ronald Kessler and colleagues (1994) in the United States found that more than half of all lifetime disorders occurred in the roughly one person in seven who had a history of three or more disorders. Comorbidity matters for three reasons. It complicates diagnosis, because symptoms overlap. It worsens prognosis, since comorbid cases are usually more severe and harder to treat. And it raises questions about classification: if anxiety and depression so often travel together, perhaps they share causes, which is one reason for transdiagnostic treatments. Example: Meera is diagnosed with panic disorder and, a year later, also meets the criteria for major depression; her clinician has to plan treatment for both, not just one. Option d is right because the co-occurrence of separately defined disorders in one individual is exactly what comorbidity means. Exam trap: 'morbidity' sounds like 'mortality', so examiners offer options about dying or death; comorbidity is about illnesses co-existing in a living person, and it is also different from prevalence, which is how common a disorder is in a population.", "traps": {"a": "People with the same problem dying at the same time confuses comorbidity with mortality (death rates); comorbidity is about disorders co-existing in one living person.", "b": "A chronic fear of death is thanatophobia (death anxiety), a specific fear, not the co-occurrence of disorders.", "c": "Depression in winter refers to the seasonal pattern specifier of major depression (seasonal affective disorder), and even that is not 'always' in winter; it says nothing about two disorders occurring together."}}], "repeats-2": [{"q": "______ is the process which involves detecting, translating, and transmitting from the external environment to the brain.", "concept": "The answer is b: sensation is the process of detecting, translating and transmitting energy from the external environment to the brain. Sensation has three steps. First, specialised receptor cells detect a physical stimulus: light in the eye, sound waves in the ear, chemicals on the tongue and in the nose, pressure and temperature on the skin. Second, the receptors translate that energy into nerve impulses, a step called transduction. Third, sensory nerves transmit the impulses to the brain, where most senses are relayed through the thalamus to the cortex. The scientific study of this link between physical energy and experience is psychophysics, founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), building on Ernst Heinrich Weber's work on the just noticeable difference. Example: when you step into sunlight, light strikes the rods and cones of your retina, which pass signals through the bipolar cells to the ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve; recognising that the shape ahead is a friend's face is the next stage, perception. Option b is right because detecting, transducing and transmitting raw stimulus energy is the textbook definition of sensation. Exam trap: sensation is the raw input from receptors to brain, while perception is the organising and interpreting of that input; the words \"to the brain\" mark sensation. Key note: the coaching book prints a here, but its own explanation describes sensation, so b is used.", "traps": {"a": "Adaptation (sensory adaptation) is the drop in sensitivity to an unchanging stimulus, such as no longer smelling your own perfume after a few minutes; it is one feature of sensation, not the whole process.", "c": "Selective attention is focusing on some stimuli while filtering out others, as in the cocktail party effect studied by Colin Cherry; it acts on information already received rather than carrying it to the brain.", "d": "Perception is the brain's organisation and interpretation of sensory input into meaningful objects and events; it comes after sensation has delivered the signals."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): A large body of research indicates that violence and aggression is learned through observation. Reason (R): Learning through observation takes place due to exposure to similar situation and experienced reinforcement.", "concept": "The answer is c: the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false. Assertion: a large body of research shows that aggression is learned through observation. True. In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hammer an inflatable doll later copied the model's exact acts and words far more than children who saw a calm model or none, without any reward. Their 1963 follow-up found filmed and cartoon models worked too, and decades of media-violence research point the same way. Reason: observational learning happens because of exposure to the same situation and experienced reinforcement. False. Observational learning needs neither. The observer learns by watching someone else, often in a situation they have never been in, and need not be reinforced. Bandura's four processes are attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation; the first two govern learning (acquisition) and the last two govern performance. In Bandura's 1965 study, children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet when offered a reward for showing what the model had done, the differences between the groups all but disappeared: they had learned without reinforcement. Seeing a model rewarded or punished (vicarious reinforcement) changes whether an act is performed, not whether it is learned. Example: Kabir has never been in a fight, but after watching a film hero slap a rival he tries the same move on a classmate. Option c is right because A is well supported while R describes direct, operant learning rather than observational learning. Exam trap: R uses the language of reinforcement, which fits the earlier imitation theory of John Dollard and Neal Miller (1941), who held that imitation must itself be reinforced; Bandura showed it need not be.", "traps": {"a": "a fails because R is false, and a false statement cannot explain a true one; observational learning works without direct reinforcement or prior exposure to the same situation, as the Bobo doll children showed.", "b": "b needs both statements to be true, but R wrongly makes experienced reinforcement a condition of observational learning, the very claim Bandura's 1965 study disproved.", "d": "\"Both false\" fails because A is true: the Bobo doll studies and the wider media-violence literature support aggression being learned by watching models."}}, {"q": "The contention that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience attests to which of the following hypotheses?", "concept": "The answer is d: the facial feedback hypothesis. This idea says the face does not just display an emotion; feedback from the facial muscles to the brain also helps create or strengthen the feeling. Charles Darwin and William James hinted at it in the nineteenth century, and Silvan Tomkins and Carroll Izard developed it in the 1960s and 1970s. In a well-known 1988 study, Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper had people hold a pen between their teeth, which forces a smile, or between their lips, which blocks one; the 'smilers' rated cartoons as funnier. Paul Ekman's lab similarly found that people told to move particular facial muscles, without being told which emotion they were posing, showed autonomic changes and reported a trace of that emotion. Everyday example: forcing a smile at a tiring party can make you feel slightly better. Later large replications found the pen effect weaker than first reported, so the effect is real but modest. Option d is right because the stem says our expressions influence our emotional experience, which is exactly the direction this hypothesis proposes. Exam trap: students pick universality because it also concerns faces, but universality is about expressions being produced and recognised the same way across cultures, not about faces changing feelings.", "traps": {"a": "The universality hypothesis (Paul Ekman, building on Charles Darwin) says basic expressions such as happiness, fear and disgust are produced and recognised the same way across cultures; it treats the face as a universal signal, not as a cause of feelings.", "b": "The emotional specificity hypothesis says each emotion has its own distinct pattern of autonomic nervous system activity, a view linked to William James and Carl Lange; it concerns bodily arousal, not facial muscles.", "c": "The emotional generality hypothesis says autonomic arousal is much the same for every emotion, as Walter Cannon and later Stanley Schachter assumed; it too is about the ANS, not facial expressions."}}, {"q": "When a light paired with a bell comes to elicit a conditioned response (CR) such as salivation, the phenomenon is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: when a light paired with a bell comes to elicit salivation, the phenomenon is higher-order conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, a neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly followed by an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes an unconditioned response (salivation). After pairing, the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation, now a conditioned response (CR). Higher-order conditioning adds a further layer. Once the bell is a strong CS, a new neutral stimulus, a light, is paired with the bell, and no food is given. The light then comes to make the dog salivate as well. The bell has acted as if it were the unconditioned stimulus; this is second-order conditioning, and a third stimulus paired with the light would give third-order conditioning. Each layer is weaker, and because the bell is now presented without food it slowly extinguishes, so higher-order responses fade. The idea explains how human fears and likings spread far beyond any original experience. Example: Arjun once failed a test and felt sick with dread, so the word 'exam' became a CS for anxiety. Later the school's long bell, which always rang just before the teacher announced an exam, came to make his heart race too, although the bell itself was never linked to failure. Option d is right because the light gains its power from a previously conditioned stimulus, not from food. Exam trap: do not confuse it with sensory preconditioning (Wilfred Brogden, 1939), where the two neutral stimuli are paired first and only afterwards is one of them conditioned; in higher-order conditioning the first CS is trained before the new stimulus is added.", "traps": {"a": "'Anomalous conditioning' is not a recognised type of conditioning; it is an invented distractor that borrows the word 'anomalous' (irregular) to sound technical.", "b": "'Distinctive conditioning' is not a standard term; the nearest real idea is discrimination, where a subject learns to respond to one stimulus and not to a similar one, which is not what happens with the light.", "c": "Counterconditioning, shown by Mary Cover Jones (1924) with Peter and the rabbit, replaces an existing CR with an incompatible one (fear with relaxation or pleasure); it removes a response rather than spreading it to a new stimulus."}}, {"q": "The famous book 'Principles of psychology' was authored by ------.", "concept": "The answer is c: The Principles of Psychology was written by William James (1890). William James (1842 to 1910) trained in medicine at Harvard and taught physiology, philosophy and psychology there. He worked on the Principles for about twelve years and published it in two volumes in 1890, followed by a shorter version, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892). In the book he described consciousness as a continuous, ever-changing stream of consciousness rather than a set of elements; called habit 'the enormous fly-wheel of society'; split the self into the 'I' (the self as knower) and the 'me' (the self as known, with material, social and spiritual parts); and set out his theory of emotion, proposed independently by Carl Lange (1885), that we feel afraid because we tremble and run, not the other way round. He defined psychology as 'the science of mental life'. The Principles made James the father of American psychology and the inspiration of functionalism, which asked what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Option c is right because The Principles of Psychology is William James's book. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (a) wrote a book with a look-alike title, Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), the founding text of experimental psychology, so a hurried reader picks a.", "traps": {"a": "Wilhelm Wundt wrote Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), a different book with a similar title, and founded the Leipzig laboratory in 1879.", "b": "Edward B. Titchener, Wundt's student and the founder of structuralism at Cornell, wrote the Experimental Psychology manuals (1901 to 1905) and A Textbook of Psychology (1909), not The Principles of Psychology.", "d": "John B. Watson wrote 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' (1913) and Behaviorism (1925); he rejected the study of consciousness that the Principles centred on."}}, {"q": "Internal standards used to judge stimuli are referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is c: internal standards used to judge stimuli are called frames of reference. We never judge a stimulus in absolute terms; we rate it against a standard we carry inside, built up from past experience and from the surroundings, and that standard is the frame of reference. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) showed one forming in the autokinetic experiment: a still point of light in a dark room seems to move, each person judging alone settled on a personal range, groups then converged on a shared norm, and members kept using that norm when later tested alone. Harry Helson (1947, 1964) made the idea measurable in adaptation-level theory; his 1948 paper is titled 'Adaptation-level as a basis for a quantitative theory of frames of reference'. The adaptation level is the neutral point of one such frame, pooled from the focal stimulus, the background stimuli around it and residual stimuli (traces of past experience); a stimulus above it feels heavy, bright or warm, one below it light, dim or cool. A classic demonstration: put one hand in hot water and the other in cold, then both in lukewarm water; the same water feels cool to the first hand and warm to the second. Example: Kabir lifts a 20 kg sack of rice in his uncle's shop, then picks up a 5 kg bag, which feels surprisingly light, because his frame of reference for weight has moved up. Option c is right because 'frames of reference' is the general name for the internal standards, and adaptation level is Helson's name for the neutral point inside one. Exam trap: adaptation level is the planted near miss. Key note: WBJEEB's final answer keys mark c both times this question was set, JEMAS(PG) 2023 and JEMAS(PG) 2024. This site keyed a (adaptation level) before the official keys were found; a is the narrower Helson term, so c is the answer to give.", "traps": {"a": "Adaptation level is Harry Helson's name for the neutral point inside one frame of reference (his 1948 paper calls it a quantitative theory of frames of reference); it is a part of the frame, not the general name for internal standards, and WBJEEB's final key does not accept it.", "b": "Intuition is knowing or judging something quickly without conscious reasoning; it is not a standard or reference point against which stimuli are rated.", "d": "Context is the external setting around a stimulus; it feeds the frame of reference as background stimulation, but it is not itself the internal standard."}}, {"q": "Basic speech sounds are called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the basic speech sounds of a language are called phonemes. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, although it carries no meaning of its own. Swap /p/ for /b/ and 'pat' becomes 'bat'; swap /k/ for /h/ and 'cat' becomes 'hat'. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and each language uses its own set, so two sounds that are separate phonemes in one language may be heard as one sound in another. Hindi, for instance, separates aspirated /kh/ from plain /k/, so 'kaana' (one-eyed) and 'khaana' (food) are different words, while English speakers barely notice the puff of air. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units of meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness) and 'cats' has two (cat and the plural -s). Morphemes build words, words build sentences by the rules of syntax, and the meaning of the whole is studied by semantics. Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984) found that English-learning infants could tell apart Hindi dental and retroflex 't' sounds at 6 to 8 months but had mostly lost this by 10 to 12 months, as their ears tuned to the phonemes of their own language. Example: Meera's cousin from London cannot hear the difference between 'kaana' and 'khaana', while Meera hears two quite separate words. Option c is right because the stem asks for the basic units of speech sound, which is the definition of a phoneme. Exam trap: morpheme (option a) is the classic near miss; the morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning, the phoneme the smallest unit of sound.", "traps": {"a": "A morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning (free like 'cat' or bound like the plural -s); it is usually built from several phonemes, so it is a unit of meaning, not of sound.", "b": "A syllable is a unit of pronunciation organised around a vowel ('wa-ter' has two); it usually contains several phonemes, so it is not the basic sound unit.", "d": "Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into sentences; it is grammar, not sound, and works several levels above phonemes."}}, {"q": "The fact that a nearer object may obscure a more distant object from the view is due to:", "concept": "The answer is b: interposition, also called overlap or occlusion. Interposition is a monocular depth cue: when one object partly covers another, we see the covering object as nearer. It is one of the pictorial cues that painters have used for centuries, along with linear perspective, relative size, texture gradient, aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field. Because it needs only one eye, it works in photographs and paintings. It tells us only relative order (which object is in front), not absolute distance. Everyday example: at a cricket match, a spectator's head blocks part of the scoreboard, so you instantly know the head is closer without judging any distance in metres. In a class photo, the students whose bodies cut off others look nearer. Hermann von Helmholtz treated such cues as the basis of unconscious inference about depth, whereas James J. Gibson argued that the information lies directly in the light reaching the eye. Binocular cues, by contrast, need two eyes: retinal disparity and convergence. Option b is right because the stem describes a nearer object hiding part of a farther one, which is the exact definition of interposition. Exam trap: students confuse it with figure and ground or closure because these are also perceptual terms, but those are Gestalt principles of organising shapes, not cues for depth.", "traps": {"a": "Figure and ground is the Gestalt principle that we split a scene into an object (figure) standing out against a background (ground), as in Edgar Rubin's vase-faces picture; it organises the scene but is not the overlap cue to distance.", "c": "Proximity is the Gestalt law that elements close together are grouped as a unit, such as dots spaced in pairs; it concerns grouping, not depth.", "d": "Closure is the Gestalt tendency to fill gaps and see incomplete figures, such as a broken circle, as whole; it concerns completing form, not distance."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Working memory can hold a large amount of information even though it can retain only seven to nine items. Reason (R): Items in working memory contain several separate bits of information grouped together called chunks.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and chunking (R) is exactly why a store limited to about seven items can still hold a large amount of information (A). George Miller (1956), in \"The magical number seven, plus or minus two\", showed that immediate memory holds about 7 units, give or take 2, so 5 to 9. Judge A: the limit is set in units, not in raw information. Seven words carry far more information than seven single digits, yet both fill the span, so a store of seven to nine items can hold a lot. A is true. Judge R: a chunk is a meaningful unit formed by grouping separate bits with the help of knowledge already in long-term memory, such as a word, a date or an acronym. William Chase and Herbert Simon (1973) found that chess masters recall whole board positions at a glance because they see familiar patterns of pieces as single chunks. R is true. Does R explain A? Yes: capacity is counted in chunks, and each chunk packs several bits, so the total information held can be large even though the number of items is small. Example: Meera cannot repeat the 12 letters B J P I S R O R B I U N heard once, but regrouped as BJP, ISRO, RBI and UN they become four easy chunks. Option a is right because R gives the very mechanism that makes A possible. Exam trap: \"seven items\" and \"large amount of information\" seem to clash, so students mark A false; the clash disappears once items are understood as chunks. Note too that Miller wrote of short-term memory; the working memory model came later (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974), and Nelson Cowan (2001) puts the limit nearer 4 chunks.", "traps": {"b": "This would mean R is true but irrelevant; in fact chunking is the very reason a span of about seven items can carry a lot of information, so R does explain A.", "c": "This calls R false, but chunking (George Miller, 1956) is a well established finding, seen in chess masters and in anyone who reads a phone number in groups.", "d": "This calls A false, but because the span is counted in meaningful units rather than in bits, a store of seven to nine items can indeed hold a large amount of information."}}, {"q": "Fluid intelligence tends to _____ with age while crystallized intelligence tends to _____ with age.", "concept": "The answer is c: fluid intelligence tends to decrease with age, while crystallised intelligence tends to increase. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with his student John Horn (1966), split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that do not depend on learned knowledge: seeing patterns, abstract reasoning, working out unfamiliar tasks. It is measured by tests such as John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It relies heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in early adulthood (around the early twenties) and then declines gradually. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills gained through education and experience, measured by vocabulary and general information tests. It keeps rising into late middle age and holds up well into the sixties. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability is 'invested' in learning, and what is learned crystallises as Gc. Longitudinal work such as K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) broadly supports the pattern, though most declines come later and are smaller than early cross-sectional studies suggested. For example, Meera's 68-year-old grandfather beats her easily at crosswords and knows far more history, but she solves a new puzzle app or a matrix-reasoning test much faster. Option c is right because Gf falls from early adulthood while Gc continues to grow with experience. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse; remember 'fluid flows away, crystals build up'. Option d is tempting because Gc does level off and finally dips in very old age, but across most of adulthood it rises.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the pattern: fluid reasoning falls after early adulthood, and crystallised knowledge accumulates rather than declines.", "b": "Fluid intelligence does not increase across adulthood; it peaks early and then declines.", "d": "The first half is right, but crystallised intelligence keeps rising into late middle age rather than staying flat."}}, {"q": "The concept of primary mental abilities was given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the concept of primary mental abilities was given by Louis Leon Thurstone (1938). Earlier, Charles Spearman (1904) had found that scores on different mental tests all correlate positively, and explained this by one general factor, g, plus specific factors, s. Thurstone, at the University of Chicago, gave a large battery of tests to students and analysed the scores with his own method of multiple factor analysis. Instead of one dominant g, he found about seven fairly separate group factors, which he called primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning (induction). He argued that a person is better described by a profile across these abilities than by a single IQ. Example: Kabir may be strong in number and space but weak in word fluency, while his sister Meera shows the opposite profile, though both earn the same overall IQ. Later work found that the seven abilities themselves correlate, so g returned at the top of hierarchical models. Option b is right because the term primary mental abilities comes from Thurstone's 1938 monograph of that name. Exam trap: Spearman also used factor analysis, so a student who remembers only 'factor theory' may pick him; Spearman's answer was g and s, Thurstone's was several primary abilities.", "traps": {"a": "Charles Spearman (1904) proposed the two-factor theory: one general factor (g) common to all tests plus specific factors (s); he did not split intelligence into primary abilities.", "c": "J. P. Guilford's Structure of Intellect model (The Nature of Human Intelligence, 1967) crosses operations, contents and products into 120 (later 150 and 180) abilities; it is a different and much larger model.", "d": "Jean Piaget (1936) described stages of cognitive development, from sensorimotor to formal operational; he did not offer a factor theory of intelligence."}}, {"q": "ERG theory of motivation was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: ERG theory of motivation was given by Clayton Alderfer (1969). Clayton Alderfer, an American organisational psychologist, reworked Abraham Maslow's (1943) five-level hierarchy and tested it on employees in organisations. He reduced the five needs to three. Existence needs are material and physical (food, water, pay, safe working conditions), matching Abraham Maslow's physiological and safety needs. Relatedness needs are for meaningful relationships with family, friends, colleagues and supervisors, matching belongingness and the social part of esteem. Growth needs are for personal development and full use of one's abilities, matching self-esteem and self-actualisation. ERG theory differs from Abraham Maslow's in three ways. First, the needs are not a rigid ladder, and several can be active at once. Second, a lower need does not have to be satisfied before a higher one can motivate. Third, it adds frustration-regression: when a higher need is blocked, the person falls back and seeks more of a lower need, whereas Abraham Maslow described only satisfaction-progression, moving up once a need is met. Example: Arjun is passed over for promotion and for the chance to lead a project (growth frustrated); instead of pushing for development, he starts demanding a pay rise and spending more time chatting with colleagues (existence and relatedness), which is frustration-regression. Option a is right because Clayton Alderfer proposed the existence, relatedness and growth model. Exam trap: all four names are need theorists of motivation, and David McClelland (1961) also had three needs (achievement, affiliation and power), so 'three needs' alone can pull students away from Clayton Alderfer; Frederick Herzberg (1959) had two factors and Abraham Maslow five levels.", "traps": {"b": "Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed the five-level hierarchy (physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, self-actualisation) with satisfaction-progression; ERG is Clayton Alderfer's three-level revision of it.", "c": "Frederick Herzberg (1959) gave the two-factor, or motivation-hygiene, theory of job satisfaction, splitting motivators from hygiene factors; it does not name existence, relatedness and growth needs.", "d": "David McClelland (1961) proposed learned needs for achievement, affiliation and power, measured with the Thematic Apperception Test; it also has three needs, but not existence, relatedness and growth."}}, {"q": "Among the following schedules of reinforcement, which typically produces the highest rate of response and is most resistant to extinction?", "concept": "The answer is variable ratio, because among the schedules of reinforcement the variable ratio schedule produces the highest rate of responding and the greatest resistance to extinction. Understand why this schedule is so powerful within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning framework. A variable ratio schedule reinforces a response after an unpredictable number of responses that varies around some average; for instance, on a VR-10 schedule reinforcement comes on average every tenth response, but the actual number required fluctuates unpredictably from one reinforcement to the next. Two properties combine to make this schedule uniquely potent. First, because it is a ratio schedule, reinforcement depends directly on how many responses the organism makes, so the faster the organism responds, the more reinforcement it earns, which drives a very high, sustained rate of responding. Second, because the ratio is variable and unpredictable, the organism can never know which particular response will be the one that pays off, so the very next response might be rewarded, and this uncertainty keeps the organism responding vigorously and continuously without the post-reinforcement pauses seen in fixed schedules. This same unpredictability makes variable ratio schedules extraordinarily resistant to extinction: when reinforcement stops, the organism cannot easily tell the difference between an unusually long unrewarded run (which was normal during training) and actual extinction, so it keeps responding for a long time. The prototypical real-world example is gambling on a slot machine, which pays off on a variable ratio schedule and hooks players into persistent, rapid play. So for the highest and most extinction-resistant responding, the answer is variable ratio.", "traps": {"a": "A fixed ratio schedule produces high response rates but includes a characteristic post-reinforcement pause after each reinforcement, and it is less resistant to extinction than a variable ratio schedule.", "c": "A fixed interval schedule produces a low, scalloped pattern of responding with slow rates early in the interval, so it yields neither the highest response rate nor the greatest extinction resistance.", "d": "A variable interval schedule produces steady but moderate response rates, lower than the high-output variable ratio schedule, and less resistant to extinction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the notion of the confirmatory bias?", "concept": "The correct answer is all of the above. Confirmatory bias (confirmation bias) is the pervasive tendency to seek, interpret, favour, and recall information in a way that confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, hypotheses, or expectations, while giving disproportionately little attention to information that contradicts them. The concept was demonstrated experimentally by Peter Wason, whose selection task and 2-4-6 rule-discovery task showed that people tend to test hypotheses by looking for confirming rather than disconfirming evidence. In clinical assessment and diagnosis, confirmation bias is a serious source of error: a clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may then ignore or discount symptoms and information that do not fit that hypothesis (option A), may disregard evidence that contradicts a stereotype about a patient group (option B), and may interpret genuinely ambiguous or neutral information as though it supports the favoured hypothesis (option C). All three describe real manifestations of the same underlying bias, so the inclusive answer is correct. This bias contributes to premature closure and diagnostic overshadowing, which is why clinicians are trained to actively consider alternative hypotheses and seek disconfirming evidence, and why structured assessment reduces error. This item tests recognition that confirmation bias operates through several complementary mechanisms in clinical judgment.", "traps": {"a": "Ignoring disconfirming information is one manifestation of confirmation bias, but selecting it alone omits the other equally valid manifestations listed.", "b": "Ignoring information that contradicts stereotypes is a form of confirmation bias, but it is only one aspect of the broader tendency.", "c": "Interpreting ambiguous information as supporting one's hypothesis is a genuine feature of confirmation bias, but it too is only one part of the whole."}}, {"q": "What is test-retest reliability", "concept": "The correct answer is that the test will produce consistent results. Reliability refers to the consistency, stability, or repeatability of measurement, and test-retest reliability is one specific form of it. Test-retest reliability is assessed by administering the same test to the same group of people on two separate occasions and then correlating the two sets of scores. If the test is reliable in this sense, individuals should obtain similar scores across the two administrations, yielding a high correlation (the test-retest reliability coefficient, or coefficient of stability). In other words, a reliable test produces consistent, reproducible results over time. This is especially important for measures of stable traits such as intelligence or personality, which should not fluctuate substantially over short intervals. A key complication is the practice or carryover effect: if respondents remember items or improve simply through repeated exposure, the second scores may rise, artificially inflating or distorting the reliability estimate, so an appropriate time interval must be chosen. Note that option A actually defines validity (measuring what it claims to measure), and option C describes a practice effect, which is a threat to, not a definition of, test-retest reliability. The broader concept of reliability is central to classical test theory. This item tests the definition of test-retest reliability as consistency of scores across time.", "traps": {"a": "Measuring what it claims to measure is the definition of validity, not reliability, so this describes a different psychometric property.", "c": "Improved performance on a second administration reflects a practice effect, which is actually a threat to test-retest reliability, not its definition.", "d": "Since only option B correctly defines test-retest reliability and the others are wrong, the inclusive option cannot be correct."}}, {"q": "Projective tests claim to reveal information about:", "concept": "The correct answer is unconscious processes. Projective tests are personality assessment tools built on the projective hypothesis: the idea that when a person is presented with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they will project their own inner needs, feelings, conflicts, motives, and unconscious processes onto it, so their interpretations reveal aspects of their hidden psychological makeup. This approach is rooted in psychoanalytic theory, tracing to Sigmund Freud's emphasis on the unconscious. Classic examples include the Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by Hermann Rorschach, in which respondents describe what they see in inkblots, and the Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, in which respondents tell stories about ambiguous pictures. Because the stimuli are ambiguous, there are no right or wrong answers, and the assumption is that responses spring from unconscious material the person cannot or would not consciously report. The distractors point to other assessment domains: intellectual attainment (option a) is measured by intelligence and achievement tests; parenting style (option c) by specific scales or observation; and career aptitude (option d) by vocational interest and aptitude inventories. Understanding what projective tests claim to reveal matters because it clarifies their theoretical purpose, to access unconscious dynamics, while also inviting critical evaluation, since projective tests have been criticised for low reliability and validity compared with objective measures.", "traps": {"a": "Intellectual attainment is measured by intelligence and achievement tests, not projective techniques.", "c": "Parenting style (Diana Baumrind) is assessed through specific questionnaires or observation, not projective tests of personality.", "d": "Career aptitude is measured by vocational interest and aptitude inventories, not projective tests."}}, {"q": "The MMPI measures which of the following?", "concept": "The correct answer is all of the above. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is one of the most widely used and researched objective personality and psychopathology assessment instruments. It was developed in the late 1930s and published in 1943 by Starke Hathaway, a psychologist, and J. Charnley McKinley, a psychiatrist, at the University of Minnesota. It is a broad-band, empirically keyed self-report inventory consisting of hundreds of true/false items. The original MMPI contains ten clinical scales that assess a wide range of psychological domains, and these scales span exactly the areas listed: mood and emotional disturbance (for example the Depression and Hypomania scales), physical or somatic concerns (the Hypochondriasis and Hysteria scales, which tap bodily preoccupation), and social attitudes and interpersonal orientation (the Social Introversion scale, plus scales tapping social deviance). It also includes validity scales (such as the L, F, and K scales) to detect defensiveness, exaggeration, or careless responding. Because the MMPI casts a wide net across mood, somatic, and social-attitudinal content, all three options are genuinely assessed, making the inclusive answer correct. The instrument was later revised as the MMPI-2 (1989) and MMPI-2-RF, and the current version is the MMPI-3 (2020). This item tests broad awareness of the MMPI's comprehensive, multi-domain coverage of personality and psychopathology.", "traps": {"a": "Mood-related pathology (for example the Depression scale) is assessed by the MMPI, but selecting it alone ignores the many other domains it covers.", "b": "Physical or bodily concerns (the Hypochondriasis scale) are measured, but this is only one of the several clinical scales.", "c": "Social attitudes and introversion (the Social Introversion scale) are assessed, but again this is only one facet of the broad inventory."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to Inter-rater reliability?", "concept": "The correct answer is the degree to which two clinicians will agree on the interpretation or scoring of a test. Reliability refers to the consistency or repeatability of measurement, and it comes in several forms. Inter-rater reliability (also called inter-observer or inter-scorer reliability) specifically concerns the degree of agreement between two or more independent raters, observers, or clinicians when they score, rate, or interpret the same responses, behaviour, or test. High inter-rater reliability means different clinicians reach the same judgments, indicating that the scoring is objective and not idiosyncratic to a particular rater. It is especially important for subjectively scored instruments such as projective tests, behavioural observations, and structured clinical interviews, where scorer judgment plays a large role. It is commonly quantified using statistics such as Cohen's kappa, developed by Jacob Cohen, which corrects for chance agreement, or the intraclass correlation coefficient. The distractors describe other psychometric properties: option A refers to construct or convergent validity, option B to predictive (criterion) validity, and option D to internal consistency reliability, which is measured by Cronbach's alpha and concerns how well the items hang together. This item tests the ability to distinguish inter-rater reliability from other reliability and validity concepts.", "traps": {"a": "The degree to which two tests measure the same construct concerns convergent or construct validity, not reliability between raters.", "b": "Predicting future behaviour is predictive validity, a form of criterion validity, not inter-rater reliability.", "d": "The degree to which items relate to each other is internal consistency reliability, measured by Cronbach's alpha, not agreement between raters."}}, {"q": "What is Rorschach's projective test designed to measure?", "concept": "The correct answer is unconscious intentions. The Rorschach Inkblot Test was created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921. It is the archetypal projective test, a category of personality assessment based on the projective hypothesis, which holds that when a person is presented with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they will project their own inner needs, conflicts, feelings, and unconscious processes onto it in the way they interpret it. The Rorschach consists of ten symmetrical inkblot cards; the examinee is asked what each blot might be, and responses are scored on dimensions such as location (which part of the blot was used), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), and content, using systems such as John Exner's Comprehensive System. The underlying assumption, rooted in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory of the unconscious, is that because there is no correct answer, the person's interpretations reveal hidden aspects of personality, unconscious motives, and emotional functioning that they could not or would not report directly on a self-report questionnaire. The distractors are wrong because dreams belong to psychodynamic dream analysis, conscious desires contradict the projective aim of accessing unconscious material, and brain size is a physical measure. Although the Rorschach's reliability and validity are debated, it remains standard exam content. This item tests the projective purpose of the Rorschach in accessing unconscious material.", "traps": {"b": "Dreams are the focus of psychodynamic dream analysis, not the inkblot test, which uses ambiguous visual stimuli rather than dream reports.", "c": "Conscious desires contradicts the whole premise of a projective test, which is designed to tap unconscious rather than consciously reported material.", "d": "Brain size is a physical, neuroanatomical measure with no connection to a projective personality test."}}, {"q": "_____ is a sub process of _____:", "concept": "The answer is c: interviewing is a sub-process of psychological assessment. Psychological assessment is the broad process of collecting and combining information from many sources to answer a referral question about a person: Is this a learning disability? How severe is the depression? Which treatment suits? Its sources include the clinical interview, behavioural observation, psychological tests, the case history, school or medical records and reports from family. Psychological testing is narrower. Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour; it yields a score. Assessment, as Gary Groth-Marnat stresses in his Handbook of Psychological Assessment, is the clinician's work of integrating test scores with everything else into a reasoned conclusion and recommendations. So testing, interviewing and observation are all sub-processes, or tools, within assessment; none of them contains the others. Example: Kabir, 9, is referred for falling marks. The psychologist interviews his parents and teacher, talks with and observes Kabir, gives an intelligence test and a reading test, reads his school reports, and then writes a report that ties it all together. The whole sequence is assessment; the interview is one step in it. Option c is right because it is the only option that puts a component (interviewing) inside the umbrella process (psychological assessment). Exam trap: testing and assessment are often used loosely as synonyms, so options that put testing above interviewing or observation look plausible; assessment is always the umbrella, and the other three are its parts. Key note: JEPAS(PG) 2020 asks the same item with the same options. Neither paper has an official key, and the JEPAS 2020 mock on this site is keyed a, after Lee Cronbach's view of a test as a systematic way of observing behaviour. c is the reading modern texts back (Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik, Gary Groth-Marnat: interviewing, observation and testing are all tools inside assessment), so it is used here.", "traps": {"a": "Psychological testing and observation are both sub-processes of assessment that sit side by side; testing is not a part of observation, although a tester does observe behaviour during a test.", "b": "Testing and interviewing are parallel tools within assessment: testing gives standardised scores, interviewing gives history and context, and neither is a sub-process of the other.", "d": "This reverses the relation: observation is one of the methods used within psychological assessment, not the larger process that contains it."}}, {"q": "A salesperson knocks on your door and requests you to buy the latest technology refrigerator of his \"internationally famed\" organization. When you turn him down, he requests you to buy a portable ice box. You find yourself obliged to comply. He has used, to near success:", "concept": "The answer is b: asking first for an expensive refrigerator, which you refuse, and then for a cheap ice box, which you feel obliged to buy, is the door-in-the-face technique. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1975) demonstrated it with students. Asked to counsel juvenile delinquents two hours a week for two years, every student refused. Asked next to take the delinquents on a single two-hour trip to the zoo, about 50% agreed, against under 17% of students asked only the small favour. The large request is meant to be refused; it makes the second, real request look like a concession. Because of the reciprocity norm (we repay favours and concessions), the target feels pressure to concede in turn, and by contrast the second request also seems smaller than it is. Guilt about the first refusal may add to the pull. The technique works best when the same person makes both requests, one soon after the other, and the first is not absurdly large. In the stem, the salesman's real goal was always the ice box. By retreating from the refrigerator he seemed to give something up, and you felt you owed him a yes. Option b is right because the order is a large refused request followed by the smaller target request. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door uses the reverse order (small then large), so check which request came first. Key note: the door-in-the-face works through the reciprocity norm (a concession is returned with a concession), so d names the mechanism, not the technique; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Foot-in-the-door (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966) puts the small request first and the large one second; here the large request came first and was refused.", "c": "Low-ball (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) gets agreement to an attractive deal and then worsens its terms; the salesman never changed the terms of a deal you had accepted.", "d": "Reciprocity is Cialdini's principle (we repay favours and concessions) that explains why the door-in-the-face works; it names the mechanism, not the technique the salesman used."}}, {"q": "______ technique involves presenting target people with small requests and then following up with larger requests, the one that is desired all along.", "concept": "The answer is b: making a small request first and then following it with the larger request wanted all along is the foot-in-the-door technique. (JEMAS(PG) 2022 garbles this option as 'Put in the door'; it means foot in the door.) Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966) showed it with Californian homeowners. Asked cold to put a large, ugly 'Drive Carefully' sign on their lawns, about 17% agreed. But about 76% agreed if, two weeks earlier, they had accepted a small 'Be a safe driver' sign. Two explanations are offered: consistency (having said yes once, refusing now feels inconsistent) and self-perception (watching yourself agree, you infer that you are the kind of person who supports such causes). The first request must be small enough that nearly everyone accepts it. Its relatives work differently. Door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) reverses the order. Low-ball (Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins agreement to a good deal and then worsens the terms. That's-not-all (Jerry Burger, 1986) sweetens the offer before the person has answered. Example: a volunteer asks Riya to sign a petition for a cleaner lake; a week later he asks her to give up a Sunday to clear rubbish from it, and she agrees. Option b is right because small-then-large is the defining sequence of the foot-in-the-door. Exam trap: door-in-the-face is the mirror image (large, refused, then small), and examiners swap the two; fix the order of the requests before you answer.", "traps": {"a": "Low-ball (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins a commitment to an attractive deal and then raises its cost; there is no small-then-large pair of requests.", "c": "Door-in-the-face (Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) uses the reverse order: a huge request that is refused, then the smaller real one, working through reciprocal concessions.", "d": "That's-not-all (Jerry Burger, 1986) sweetens a single offer with extras or a discount before the person replies; it has one request, not an escalation."}}, {"q": "Which term refers to the tendency to attribute one's successes to internal factors and failures to external factors?", "concept": "The answer is b: self-serving bias. The self-serving bias is the tendency to take credit for our successes by attributing them to internal causes (ability, effort) while blaming our failures on external causes (bad luck, an unfair examiner, a noisy hall). A student who tops a test says \"I worked hard\", but after failing says \"the paper was out of syllabus\". The usual explanation is motivational: the bias protects and enhances self-esteem, and it also helps us present ourselves well to others. It is one of several attributional biases studied in social psychology, along with the fundamental attribution error and the actor-observer bias. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly the success-internal, failure-external pattern that defines the self-serving bias. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (named by Lee Ross in 1977) is about how we explain other people's behaviour, overestimating their dispositions and underestimating the situation; the self-serving bias is about explaining our own outcomes and depends on whether the outcome was good or bad. If the stem mentions success versus failure, think self-serving bias; if it mentions judging others' behaviour as personality-driven, think fundamental attribution error.", "traps": {"a": "The fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977) is the tendency to over-explain other people's behaviour by their dispositions while ignoring situational forces; it does not hinge on success or failure.", "c": "The actor-observer bias (Jones and Nisbett) contrasts explaining our own behaviour situationally with explaining others' dispositionally; it is not about successes versus failures.", "d": "Attributional bias is the umbrella term for all systematic errors in attribution; the specific success-failure pattern has its own name, self-serving bias."}}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Adler, b. Bandura, c. Jung, d. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "concept": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (a-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (b-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (c-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (d-1). Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), founder of individual psychology, borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is the imagined final goal, never fully real, that pulls a person's behaviour forward, such as \"I must be the best\". Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021) described reciprocal determinism (1978), later called triadic reciprocal causation (1986): person factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) treated libido as general psychic energy governed by principles borrowed from physics. By the principle of equivalence, energy withdrawn from one interest reappears in another of equal value; by the principle of entropy, energy flows toward a balance that is never reached. Karen Horney (The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, 1937) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives the strategies of moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Kabir retires from cricket and pours the same passion into coaching, Jung would call it the principle of equivalence. Option c is right because it is the only set in which all four pairs are correct. Exam trap: option d gets Adler and Horney right but swaps Bandura and Jung; \"reciprocal\" and \"equivalence\" both sound like a balance of forces, so students trade them. Bandura is the social-cognitive theorist of three interacting factors; Jung is the energy theorist.", "traps": {"a": "a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1 gives Adler triadic reciprocal causation (Bandura's), Bandura the principle of equivalence (Jung's) and Jung fictional finalism (Adler's); only Horney with basic anxiety is right.", "b": "a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4 has every pair wrong: basic anxiety is Horney's, fictional finalism is Adler's, triadic reciprocal causation is Bandura's and the principle of equivalence is Jung's.", "d": "a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1 is right for Adler and Horney but swaps the middle two: the principle of equivalence is Jung's law of psychic energy, and triadic reciprocal causation is Bandura's."}}, {"q": "\"Moving towards people, moving away from people, moving against people\" are constituents of whose classification of pattern of needs:", "concept": "The answer is c: moving towards, against and away from people are Karen Horney's three neurotic trends. Karen Horney (1937), in The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, argued that personality is shaped mainly by social relationships, not sexual instincts. A child whose parents show indifference (no genuine warmth, broken promises, favouritism, ridicule) feels basic hostility, represses it out of helplessness or fear, and is left with basic anxiety: a pervasive feeling of being lonely and helpless in a hostile world. To cope, the child adopts strategies that harden into ten neurotic needs, such as needs for affection, a dominant partner, power, prestige, self-sufficiency and perfection. In Our Inner Conflicts (1945) she grouped these needs into three trends. The compliant type moves towards people, seeking love and approval; the aggressive type moves against people, seeking power and admiration; the detached type moves away from people, seeking independence and distance. A healthy person uses all three flexibly; a neurotic person is locked into one, and the clash among them is the core of neurosis. For example, Arjun agrees with everyone and panics if a friend seems annoyed (towards), while his brother Kabir turns every conversation into a contest (against). Option c is right because the stem quotes Horney's own triad. Exam trap: Erich Fromm also listed social strategies (mechanisms of escape in 1941, character orientations in 1947), and both were neo-Freudians of the cultural school, so d is the near miss; but the towards, against and away wording is Horney's alone.", "traps": {"a": "John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950, Personality and Psychotherapy) translated Freud into learning theory (drive, cue, response, reward, approach-avoidance conflict); they did not classify needs as movements towards, against or away from people.", "b": "Melanie Klein's object relations theory describes the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions, splitting and projective identification; it concerns the infant's inner objects, not adult neurotic trends.", "d": "Erich Fromm described mechanisms of escape (1941: authoritarianism, destructiveness, automaton conformity) and character orientations (1947: receptive, exploitative, hoarding, marketing, productive), close cousins of Horney's trends but with different names."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a mature defense mechanism?", "concept": "Defence mechanisms are unconscious ego strategies that reduce anxiety arising from conflict between impulses, conscience and reality. Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea, and Anna Freud (1936) catalogued the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. George E. Vaillant (1977), drawing on the Grant Study, a decades-long follow-up of Harvard men reported in Adaptation to Life, ranked defences in four levels of maturity. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Level II, immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out. Level III, neurotic: intellectualisation, repression, displacement, reaction formation, dissociation. Level IV, mature: altruism, sublimation, suppression, anticipation and humour. Men who relied on mature defences fared better in later health, work and relationships. Mature defences keep the feeling in awareness and turn it to adaptive use. Altruism is gaining real satisfaction from constructive service to others: Meera, whose brother died by suicide, volunteers at a crisis helpline. Separate it from reaction formation, where a hidden hostile wish is replaced by exaggerated, rigid kindness. Two pairs drive variant questions: suppression (a conscious choice to postpone a worry, mature) versus repression (unconscious exclusion from awareness, neurotic), and sublimation (an impulse redirected into a valued outlet, such as aggression into competitive boxing, mature) versus displacement (the feeling shifted onto a safer target, neurotic). Rationalisation sits with the neurotic defences in most textbook tables; the DSM-IV Defensive Functioning Scale placed it at the lower disavowal level. Option a is right because altruism is one of Vaillant's five mature defences, while intellectualisation, repression and rationalisation all sit lower.", "traps": {"b": "Intellectualisation is the excessive use of abstract reasoning, facts and technical language to avoid feeling the emotion attached to a conflict, for example a patient calmly quoting survival statistics about his own cancer. George E. Vaillant (1977) placed it, together with isolation of affect, among the neurotic defences. It tempts because it looks calm, rational and grown-up, but the feeling is kept out of awareness instead of being used adaptively, which is what makes a defence mature.", "c": "Repression is the unconscious exclusion of distressing thoughts, wishes or memories from awareness; Sigmund Freud treated it as the cornerstone of psychoanalytic theory. George E. Vaillant (1977) classed it as a neurotic defence. It tempts because it is the most famous defence and is easily confused with suppression, its mature cousin. The difference is awareness: suppression is a conscious decision to set a worry aside for later, whereas repression happens without the person knowing.", "d": "Rationalisation is offering plausible, socially acceptable reasons for behaviour whose real motives are unacceptable, as in Aesop's sour grapes, where the fox decides the grapes he cannot reach were sour anyway. Most textbook tables list it as a neurotic defence, and the DSM-IV Defensive Functioning Scale put it at the disavowal level. It tempts because it sounds logical and adult, but it distorts the truth to protect self-esteem instead of meeting the conflict adaptively."}}, {"q": "The ___ complex is to girls as the ___ complex is to boys.", "concept": "The correct answer is electra, oedipus. In Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development, during the phallic stage (roughly ages 3 to 6) the child experiences unconscious sexual desire for the opposite-sex parent and rivalry with the same-sex parent. For boys, Freud called this the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek myth of Oedipus, who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother; the boy desires his mother, views his father as a rival, and fears castration, eventually resolving the conflict by identifying with the father. For girls, the parallel is the Electra complex, a term actually coined by Carl Jung (though the concept is Freud's), named after the Greek figure Electra who sought revenge against her mother; the girl is said to desire her father and experience penis envy, resolving the conflict by identifying with the mother. Thus the Electra complex is to girls as the Oedipus complex is to boys, making option a correct; option b reverses them. Options c and d list psychosexual stages (oral, phallic) rather than the named complexes. Although these ideas are heavily criticised and no longer scientifically accepted, they remain staple exam content because they are central to Freud's account of how gender identity, morality (the superego), and adult relationships supposedly develop through the resolution of these childhood conflicts.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the pairing; the Oedipus complex applies to boys and the Electra complex to girls, not the other way around.", "c": "Oral and phallic are psychosexual stages, not the complexes that describe attachment to the opposite-sex parent.", "d": "Phallic and oral are stages of development, not the named complexes for girls and boys."}}, {"q": "According to Eysenck, extraverts seek to _____ their arousal while introverts seek to _____ their arousal.", "concept": "The answer is c: according to Hans Eysenck, extraverts seek to increase their arousal, while introverts seek to decrease it. Hans Eysenck (1967), in The Biological Basis of Personality, linked his extraversion-introversion dimension to the brain. He proposed that the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) in the brainstem sets each person's baseline level of cortical arousal, and that everyone feels and performs best at a moderate, optimal level, an idea close to the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908). Introverts, Eysenck argued, have a higher resting arousal, so they already sit near or above their optimum; extra stimulation such as noise, crowds or novelty overloads them, and they choose quiet settings to bring arousal down. Extraverts have a lower resting arousal, so they are under-aroused and seek parties, risk, variety and loud music to push it up. The evidence fits: in the lemon juice test, introverts salivate more than extraverts when drops of lemon juice are placed on the tongue, showing stronger physiological reactivity, and introverts are also more sensitive to pain and faint stimuli and condition more easily. Jeffrey Gray (1970) later revised the biology in his reinforcement sensitivity theory. For example, after a long week Arjun, an extravert, recharges at a crowded cricket match, while his introverted friend Kabir recharges by reading alone in a quiet room. Option c is right because extraverts are chronically under-aroused and seek stimulation to raise arousal, while introverts are over-aroused and avoid it. Exam trap: it seems natural to assume extraverts are 'more aroused' because they look lively. In Eysenck's theory the opposite is true: their lively behaviour makes up for low cortical arousal.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses Eysenck's theory: introverts already have high cortical arousal and avoid more, while extraverts are under-aroused and seek more.", "b": "'Hide' and 'reveal' describe self-disclosure, not arousal; Eysenck's theory concerns the level of cortical arousal set by the reticular activating system.", "d": "'Reveal' and 'hide' again confuse social openness with arousal; Eysenck spoke of raising or lowering arousal towards an optimal level."}}, {"q": "Which brain structure is primarily involved in regulating emotions such as fear and aggression?", "concept": "The amygdala, option c, is the brain structure primarily involved in regulating emotions such as fear and aggression. The amygdala is an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei located deep within the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. It is the brain's threat-detection and emotional-significance hub, rapidly evaluating sensory input for danger and triggering the physiological and behavioural components of fear, including the fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) response via its connections to the hypothalamus and brainstem. Joseph LeDoux's research mapped a fast, low road from the thalamus directly to the amygdala that allows a near-instant fear response before the cortex has fully processed the stimulus, and a slower high road through the cortex that permits more deliberate appraisal. The amygdala is central to fear conditioning, the learning of associations between neutral cues and aversive events, and damage to it produces blunted fear and reduced aggression, as in the classic studies of the patient who could not recognise fearful facial expressions. It is implicated in anxiety disorders, PTSD, and aggression. The tight link between the amygdala and fear, threat, and aggression is the key exam fact, making option c correct.", "traps": {"a": "The hippocampus is essential for forming new declarative memories and spatial navigation, not the primary regulator of fear and aggression.", "b": "The hypothalamus coordinates the body's stress and autonomic responses and homeostasis, acting downstream of the amygdala rather than as the primary emotion-appraisal centre.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning, not emotional regulation."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Surgery is one of the methods to understand brain behaviour relations in humans. Reason (R): Lesion method destroys part of the brain.", "concept": "The answer is d: LBS's official key for this item, in both the 2019 and the 2021 paper, marks (A) false and (R) true. Reason: the lesion method destroys part of the brain. True. A lesion is damage to or removal of tissue, and the lesion (ablation) method infers a region's function from what is lost, as in the ablation studies of Pierre Flourens (1820s) and the cortical lesions Karl Lashley made in rats (Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence, 1929). Assertion: surgery is one of the methods to understand brain-behaviour relations in humans. On the key's reading, false: as a research method, surgery means experimental lesioning, deliberately destroying or removing tissue to see what changes, and that is done only in animals. No one may operate on a human brain to find out what it does. In humans, brain-behaviour relations are studied through naturally occurring damage (strokes, tumours, head injuries), neuropsychological testing, electrical recording (EEG, event-related potentials), neuroimaging (CT, MRI, fMRI, PET) and non-invasive stimulation (transcranial magnetic stimulation). Patients who need surgery for epilepsy or a tumour can be tested before and after, and some of the best known findings came that way: Henry Molaison (H.M.), whose medial temporal lobes William Scoville removed in 1953 and whom Brenda Milner tested, and the split-brain patients of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga (1960s). But there the surgery is treatment, and the research method is the clinical study of a lesion. Example: a researcher who wants to know what the hippocampus does can lesion it in rats, but in people has to find patients whose hippocampus was damaged by illness or by a necessary operation. Option d is right on the key's reading because R correctly defines the lesion method, while A wrongly lists surgery as a method that can be used on humans. Exam trap: H.M. and the split-brain cases make A look obviously true, so most students pick a. Key note: this mock used to mark a, because therapeutic surgery such as H.M.'s has taught a great deal about the human brain and is a lesion in all but name. LBS's official key marks d for this question in both 2019 and 2021, on the reading that surgery is never a research method in humans. Both can be argued, so the mock now follows LBS.", "traps": {"a": "a treats surgery as a human research method that the lesion method explains. On the key's reading, deliberate brain surgery for research is never done in people; what we learn from cases like H.M. comes from operations done as treatment, so A is false.", "b": "b also needs A to be true. R is true, but on the key's reading A is false, because surgery as a research method is used only in animals.", "c": "c has it the wrong way round: R is true, since destroying or removing tissue and watching what is lost is the definition of the lesion method; it is A that the key treats as false."}}, {"q": "Put the parts of the brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: 1. Metencephalon 2. Mesencephalon 3. Diancephalon 4. Telencephalon 5. Myelencephalon", "concept": "The answer is c: from higher to lower the order is telencephalon (4), diencephalon (3), mesencephalon (2), metencephalon (1) and myelencephalon (5). The names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube forms three swellings: the forebrain (prosencephalon), the midbrain (mesencephalon) and the hindbrain (rhombencephalon). Before birth the forebrain divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: cortex, basal ganglia, limbic structures and corpus callosum. The diencephalon ('between-brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with its tectum (the superior and inferior colliculi) and tegmentum. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow-brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. Example: Arjun remembers the order with a simple rule: telencephalon on top, then the other four in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: metencephalon and mesencephalon differ by one letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it. Option d keeps the middle of the order but drops the telencephalon to the bottom.", "traps": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5 simply follows the list as printed: it starts with the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and puts the telencephalon fourth, which is not a top-to-bottom order.", "b": "2, 4, 1, 3, 5 starts with the midbrain, places the cerebral hemispheres second and puts the diencephalon below the pons, scrambling the order.", "d": "3, 2, 1, 5, 4 has the middle stretch right (diencephalon, midbrain, pons and cerebellum, medulla) but puts the telencephalon, the highest division, at the bottom."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a neurotransmitter?", "concept": "The answer is d: insulin is not a neurotransmitter; it is a hormone made by the pancreas. A neurotransmitter is a chemical that a neuron stores in vesicles at its axon terminal and releases into the synaptic cleft, where it acts within milliseconds on receptors of the next cell, only a fraction of a micrometre away. A hormone is released by an endocrine (ductless) gland into the blood and travels to distant target cells, acting over minutes to hours. Insulin is a protein hormone released by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas when blood glucose rises after a meal. It lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose, so blood sugar falls; glucagon from the alpha cells does the opposite. Insulin also reaches the brain from the blood and tells the hypothalamus that the body is fed, but it is not released by neurons at synapses, so it is not counted as a neurotransmitter. Serotonin (from the raphe nuclei: mood, sleep, appetite) and dopamine (from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area: movement and reward) are classic monoamine transmitters. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the tricky one: it is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but a few brainstem neurons also release it as a transmitter, so it is both. Example: when Riya eats a plate of jalebis, her pancreas pours insulin into her blood, while inside her brain dopamine is released at synapses of the reward circuit. Option d is right because insulin is the only substance listed that works purely as a blood-borne hormone. Exam trap: epinephrine is the near miss. When a list offers only dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline and adrenaline, adrenaline is marked as the odd one out because it acts mainly as a hormone; once a pure hormone such as insulin is offered, adrenaline counts as a neurotransmitter and insulin is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is a monoamine neurotransmitter released by neurons of the raphe nuclei; it regulates mood, sleep and appetite and is the target of SSRIs, so it is a true neurotransmitter.", "b": "Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter of the nigrostriatal (movement) and mesolimbic (reward) pathways, linked to Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but some brainstem neurons release it at synapses, so it is both a hormone and a neurotransmitter; that dual role is why it tempts, yet insulin is the purer exception."}}, {"q": "Procedural memory can be affected by damage to the:", "concept": "The answer is b: procedural memory can be affected by damage to the cerebellum. Procedural memory is memory for how to do things: motor skills such as cycling, typing or playing an instrument, and habits learned by repetition. It is a kind of implicit (nondeclarative) memory, shown in better performance rather than in conscious recall, and it is separate from declarative memory for facts and events, which depends on the hippocampus. The best evidence comes from patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed his medial temporal lobes in 1953 he could form no new conscious memories, yet Brenda Milner (1962) found that he improved day by day at mirror drawing, tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror, though each time he denied having done the task before. The operation spared his cerebellum and basal ganglia, though years of the anti-epileptic drug phenytoin later left his cerebellum markedly atrophied (Suzanne Corkin, 2002). The cerebellum compares intended with actual movement and corrects the error, so it is vital for learning and timing motor skills; Richard Thompson (1980s) showed that eyeblink conditioning depends on it. Damage causes ataxia and poor motor learning. Example: after a cerebellar stroke, Meera, a tabla player, can still describe every rhythm she knows, but her strokes become uneven and she struggles to relearn even simple patterns. Option b is right because the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie most closely to procedural skill learning. Exam trap: the limbic system (hippocampus) is the memory answer for facts and events, not for skills; H.M. is the proof. Key note: procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (striatum) and the cerebellum, with the motor cortex involved; the basal ganglia are not offered, and the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie to motor skill learning (H.M. still learned mirror drawing), so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebrum is the whole of both cerebral hemispheres, including the cortex, hippocampus and basal ganglia; it is far too broad to name the structure tied to procedural memory.", "c": "The motor cortex sends commands for voluntary movement and does change as skills are practised, so it is involved; but it executes movement rather than being the classic site of skill memory, which is the cerebellum.", "d": "The limbic system, especially the hippocampus and amygdala, supports declarative and emotional memory; H.M. lost about the front half of each hippocampus yet still learned mirror drawing, which shows that skills do not depend on it."}}, {"q": "The principal function of myelin is to:", "concept": "The answer is c: the principal function of myelin is to increase the speed at which an action potential travels along the axon. Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped in many layers around axons. In the central nervous system it is made by oligodendrocytes, each of which can wrap segments of several axons; in the peripheral nervous system it is made by Schwann cells, each wrapping one segment of one axon. Between the segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1835 to 1922), where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated. Because ions cannot leak across the myelinated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes. The impulse seems to jump from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap). Thin unmyelinated axons conduct at about 1 metre per second; large myelinated axons at up to about 100 metres per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions must be pumped back after each impulse. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, and the prefrontal cortex is among the last regions to finish, in the twenties. Example: Meera, 29, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling in her legs. An MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord: multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks CNS myelin, so conduction slows or fails. Option c is right because myelin's job is faster propagation of the action potential. Exam trap: myelin changes the speed, not the size, of the impulse; by the all-or-none law, every action potential in a given neuron has the same amplitude.", "traps": {"a": "The amount of transmitter released depends on calcium entry and firing rate at the axon terminal, not on myelin; myelin covers the axon, not the synapse.", "b": "The synaptic cleft is a fixed gap of about 20 to 40 nanometres between neurons, and myelin neither reaches nor changes it.", "d": "Action potentials are all-or-none: their size is fixed for a given neuron, so myelin speeds conduction without making the spike any larger."}}, {"q": "An elderly acquaintance of yours has suffered from partial blindness since she had a stroke. Apparently the stroke damaged her:", "concept": "The answer is a: partial blindness after a stroke points to damage in the occipital lobe. The occipital lobe, at the back of the cerebral cortex, holds the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) around the calcarine fissure. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve, partly cross at the optic chiasm, relay in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and arrive in V1. Because of this crossing, each occipital lobe sees the opposite half of the visual field from both eyes. A stroke in the posterior cerebral artery, which feeds the occipital lobe, therefore often leaves a person blind in the same half of the field in both eyes (contralateral homonymous hemianopia), or with smaller blind islands (scotomas), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Damage to both occipital lobes can produce cortical blindness. Beyond V1, visual information flows along two streams: a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe (Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin, 1982). Suppose Meera's grandmother can read the right half of a newspaper line but keeps missing the words on the left; a right occipital stroke would explain it. Option a is right because partial loss of sight with intact eyes means the visual cortex is damaged, and the visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: parietal damage can make a person ignore the left side of space (hemispatial neglect), which looks like blindness but is a failure of attention, not of seeing.", "traps": {"b": "The parietal lobe handles touch and body sense (somatosensory cortex) and spatial attention; its damage usually causes sensory loss or hemispatial neglect; a deep parietal stroke can cut the optic radiation and blind a lower quarter of the field, but the classic site for stroke blindness is the occipital lobe.", "c": "The temporal lobes handle hearing, language comprehension (Wernicke's area) and memory; a deep temporal lesion can cut a small upper quarter of vision through the optic radiation, but the classic site for stroke blindness is the occipital lobe.", "d": "The reticular formation is a brainstem network that controls arousal, sleep and alertness; damage there causes drowsiness or coma, not loss of part of the visual field."}}, {"q": "Which of the following hormone is being secreted by adrenal gland?", "concept": "The adrenal glands secrete both cortisol and epinephrine, so option c, both A and B, is correct. The adrenal glands sit atop each kidney and have two functionally distinct parts. The outer adrenal cortex produces steroid hormones, including glucocorticoids such as cortisol, mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone, and small amounts of sex steroids. Cortisol is the primary stress hormone of the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; it mobilises glucose, suppresses inflammation, and helps the body sustain a prolonged stress response, with secretion following a circadian rhythm peaking in the morning. The inner adrenal medulla, functionally an extension of the sympathetic nervous system, produces the catecholamines epinephrine, also called adrenaline, and norepinephrine. These are the hormones of the fast fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) response, raising heart rate, blood pressure, and blood sugar within seconds of a threat. Because both cortisol from the cortex and epinephrine from the medulla originate in the adrenal gland, both listed hormones are correct, which is exactly why the combined answer is the right one. Melatonin, by contrast, is made elsewhere, so option c is the complete and accurate choice.", "traps": {"a": "Cortisol is indeed an adrenal hormone, but choosing it alone is incomplete because epinephrine is also adrenal, so the combined option is required.", "b": "Epinephrine is correctly adrenal, but selecting it alone ignores cortisol, which the adrenal cortex also secretes.", "d": "Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland to regulate sleep and circadian rhythm, not by the adrenal gland."}}, {"q": "The thyroid gland controls:", "concept": "The answer is c: the thyroid gland controls metabolism. The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the front of the neck, below the larynx. It releases thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), hormones made with iodine, which set the body's metabolic rate: how fast cells burn fuel and produce heat and energy. The thyroid is controlled by a feedback loop: the hypothalamus releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), which makes the anterior pituitary release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which drives the thyroid; high thyroid hormone levels then switch the loop down. Too much thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) speeds everything up: weight loss, a racing heart, heat intolerance, restlessness and anxiety. Too little (hypothyroidism) slows everything down: weight gain, tiredness, feeling cold, slowed thinking and low mood, which is why doctors check thyroid function in depression. In infancy, untreated thyroid deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism (once called cretinism), with stunted growth and intellectual disability; iodine deficiency is a major preventable cause, which is why salt is iodised in India. Example: Riya feels low, has gained weight and always feels cold; a blood test shows low thyroxine. Option c is right because setting the metabolic rate is the thyroid's main job. Exam trap: thyroid problems change mood, which tempts students towards \"emotions\", but emotion is organised by the limbic system and the adrenal stress hormones; and blood glucose is regulated by insulin and glucagon from the pancreas, not by the thyroid.", "traps": {"a": "Glucose is regulated by the pancreas: insulin from the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans lowers blood glucose and glucagon from the alpha cells raises it; the thyroid sets how fast energy is used, not glucose absorption.", "b": "Emotions are organised by the limbic system (amygdala, hypothalamus) and the autonomic nervous system with adrenal hormones; thyroid disease can alter mood, but emotion is not the gland's function.", "d": "Sexuality and reproduction are governed by the gonads (testosterone, oestrogen, progesterone) under the pituitary's FSH and LH; the thyroid is not a sex gland."}}, {"q": "The link between Nervous System and Endocrine System is possible through:", "concept": "The answer is b: the link between the nervous system and the endocrine system is the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is a small structure, about the size of an almond, at the base of the brain just below the thalamus. It controls hunger, thirst, body temperature, sexual behaviour and the stress response, and it does much of this through hormones. It sits directly above the pituitary gland, often called the master gland, and is joined to it by a stalk. The link works in two ways. First, some hypothalamic neurons make oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) and send them down their axons into the posterior pituitary, which releases them into the blood. Second, the hypothalamus secretes releasing and inhibiting hormones into a tiny network of blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system, which carries them to the anterior pituitary; there they switch on or off the hormones that control the thyroid, adrenal glands, gonads and growth. Geoffrey Harris (1955) established this neural control of the anterior pituitary. A clear example is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Before her board exam, Riya's hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone, her pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and her adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which raises her blood sugar and keeps her alert: a nervous signal (worry) has become a hormonal one. Option b is right because the hypothalamus is the brain structure that turns neural signals into hormonal ones through its control of the pituitary. Exam trap: the pituitary is the gland that gives orders to the other glands, but it is itself ruled by the hypothalamus, so the bridge between the two systems is the hypothalamus.", "traps": {"a": "The corpus callosum is the large band of fibres joining the left and right cerebral hemispheres; it links the two halves of the brain, not the brain and the glands.", "c": "The reticular formation in the core of the brainstem controls arousal, alertness and the sleep-wake cycle (Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949); it has no direct role in hormone release.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates balance, posture and smooth, well-timed movement and supports motor learning; it is not part of the neuroendocrine link."}}, {"q": "What is the resolution point of Erik Erikson's fourth stage of psychosocial development?", "concept": "The answer is b: the fourth stage of Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994), industry versus inferiority, resolves in the virtue of competence. In Childhood and Society (1950) Erikson gave each of his eight psychosocial stages a crisis between two poles, and in Insight and Responsibility (1964) he named the virtue, or basic strength, that a good resolution leaves. Stage 4 covers the school years, about 6 to 11 or puberty, matching the latency period of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). School brings tools, tasks and classmates to compare oneself with. A child who finishes tasks, learns skills and earns recognition develops industry, a sense of being able to make things work; repeated failure, harsh comparison or being made to feel behind brings inferiority. Competence is the free use of skill and intelligence in completing tasks, unimpaired by a sense of inferiority. Teachers and peers become key relationships as the child's world widens beyond the family. The virtues to memorise, in order: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care, wisdom. Example: nine-year-old Kabir struggles with long division, but his teacher breaks it into steps and praises each success; by the end of term he volunteers to solve problems at the board, the sense of competence Erikson described. Option b is right because competence is the virtue of the fourth stage. Exam trap: the other three options are also Erikson virtues, from stage 2 (will), stage 3 (purpose) and stage 5 (fidelity), so count the stages carefully.", "traps": {"a": "Fidelity is the virtue of Erikson's fifth stage, identity versus role confusion (adolescence): loyalty to freely chosen values and people.", "c": "Will is the virtue of Erikson's second stage, autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3 years).", "d": "Purpose is the virtue of Erikson's third stage, initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6 years)."}}, {"q": "Grief refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: grief is the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend or relative. Three words are often mixed up. Bereavement is the objective state of having lost someone close through death: it describes your situation. Grief is the emotional response to that loss: sorrow, yearning, anger, guilt, anxiety and loneliness, often with bodily signs such as poor sleep and loss of appetite. Mourning is the period of adjustment and the culturally shaped rituals through which people express grief and adapt, such as a funeral, a set time of wearing white, or the thirteen days of rites many Hindu families observe. John Bowlby (1980) saw grief as the response to a broken attachment and described phases: numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, and reorganisation. Colin Murray Parkes (1972), studying London widows, described a similar course. Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) later replaced fixed phases with a dual process model, in which the bereaved move back and forth between facing the loss and rebuilding daily life. Example: when Arjun's father dies, Arjun is bereaved (his state); the heavy sadness and longing he feels is grief; the cremation, the prayers and the year of family observances make up mourning. Option b is right because it names the emotion itself, an intense emotional state, rather than a period, a phase or a general mood. Exam trap: option a sounds almost identical but describes a period of adjustment, which is mourning, not grief; option c describes only the first numb phase within grief.", "traps": {"a": "This is mourning: the period of emotional adjustment, shaped by cultural customs and rituals, that follows a death. It describes a process over time, not the emotional state itself.", "c": "This is the shock or numbness phase that opens grief in John Bowlby's and Colin Murray Parkes's accounts; it is one early phase, not grief as a whole.", "d": "Demoralisation is a general state of discouragement and helplessness (Jerome Frank's term for what brings people to therapy) that can occur without any loss, so it is not specific to death."}}, {"q": "An example of a cognitive therapy is rational-emotive therapy, which was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is c: rational-emotive therapy was developed by Albert Ellis. Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007), an American psychologist first trained in psychoanalysis, grew frustrated with its slowness and introduced rational therapy in 1955. It was later called rational-emotive therapy (RET) and, from 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). He set it out in Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy (1962), and it is usually counted as the first of the modern cognitive therapies. Its centre is the ABC model. A is an activating event, B is the belief about it, C is the emotional and behavioural consequence. People assume A causes C, but Ellis argued that B does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('I must succeed', 'others must treat me fairly'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it') and global self-rating. The therapist then actively Disputes the belief (D), which leads to an Effective new philosophy (E). Example: Arjun fails one test (A) and feels worthless (C). Ellis would expose the belief 'I must never fail, or I am a total failure' (B) and dispute it until Arjun holds a preference instead: 'I would like to pass, but failing once does not make me worthless.' Option c is right because Ellis created RET, the classic cognitive therapy named in the stem. Exam trap: Aaron T. Beck (1967) developed the other main cognitive therapy (cognitive triad, automatic thoughts), and exam setters often swap the two. Joseph Wolpe (1958) belongs to behaviour therapy and Carl Rogers (1951) to person-centred therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Rogers (1951) developed client-centred, later person-centred, therapy, a humanistic approach that avoids the direct disputing Ellis used.", "b": "Joseph Wolpe (1958) developed systematic desensitisation based on reciprocal inhibition, a behaviour therapy rather than a cognitive one.", "d": "Allen Bergin is known for psychotherapy outcome research (the Handbook of Psychotherapy and Behavior Change, edited with Sol Garfield from 1971) and for work on religion and values in therapy, not for creating RET."}}, {"q": "An analogue observation enables:", "concept": "The answer is a: analogue observation enables surreptitious observation of the client. In behavioural assessment the clinician watches behaviour directly rather than relying only on what the client reports. Naturalistic observation happens in the real setting (home, classroom, ward). Analogue observation happens in a contrived, controlled setting built to resemble the real one: a clinic playroom where a parent and child carry out a set task, or a role-played social situation in which a client must refuse a request or start a conversation. Because the setting is arranged in advance, the clinician can watch from behind a one-way mirror or through a video link, unseen and unobtrusive, which is what 'surreptitious' means. This reduces reactivity (people behaving differently because they know they are being watched) and lets behaviour be coded in a standard way. The cost is generalisation: behaviour in a clinic room may not match behaviour at home. Example: Meera's parents say she refuses every instruction, so the psychologist asks her mother to have Meera tidy toys in a playroom and records Meera's compliance through a one-way mirror. Option a is right because the controlled analogue setting is what makes hidden, unobtrusive observation of the client possible. Exam trap: the word 'analogue' also appears in analogue research, where non-clinical people stand in for patients; that is a research strategy, not an observation method. Key note: IMHANS 2019 prints 'Superstitious', plainly a slip for 'surreptitious' (unseen, unobtrusive), and the option is shown corrected; b describes analogue research, a different use of the word, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Using non-clinical people as stand-ins for a clinical group (snake-fearful students for phobic patients) is analogue research, a research design, not what analogue observation of a client enables.", "c": "Watching several clients at once belongs to group settings such as group therapy or classroom observation; it is not the defining advantage of an analogue setting.", "d": "Analogue observation needs only one observer behind a mirror or a video record; requiring three or more clinicians is not part of the method."}}, {"q": "\"Dereflection\" is a component of:", "concept": "The answer is c: dereflection is a technique of logotherapy, the meaning-centred therapy of Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997). Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi concentration camps, described his approach in Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German). Logotherapy (from logos, meaning) holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning, and that a frustrated search for meaning produces an existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis. Frankl gave it two signature techniques. Dereflection counters hyper-reflection, excessive attention to oneself or one's symptom, which often makes the symptom worse, as in insomnia or sexual dysfunction. The client is helped to turn attention away from the self and towards a meaningful task or other people, so the problem fades for lack of feeding. Paradoxical intention, the second technique, asks the client to wish for or do the very thing he fears, which breaks anticipatory anxiety. Socratic dialogue then helps the client find meaning in work, love or suffering. Example: Arjun lies awake checking whether sleep is coming. His therapist uses dereflection, asking him to spend that time planning the music class he teaches, and sleep arrives once he stops watching for it. Option c is right because dereflection is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: logotherapy is one of the existential therapies, so a is the near miss; but when the specific school is offered, a named technique goes to its own school, not to the broad family.", "traps": {"a": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, 1909 to 1994; Irvin Yalom, 1980) is the broad family that works with death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; logotherapy belongs to it, but dereflection is Frankl's own technique, so the specific school is the better answer.", "b": "Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) relies on the core conditions of empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, with no directive techniques such as dereflection.", "d": "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls) heightens present awareness through the empty chair, dialogue and exaggeration; it turns attention towards experience, not away from it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following defines Beck's cognitive triad?", "concept": "The answer is a: the cognitive triad of Aaron Beck (1967) is a set of negative beliefs about the self, the world and the future. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' dreams and thoughts were full of automatic negative themes about loss and failure rather than hidden anger turned inwards. In his 1967 book on depression and in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues), he proposed that depression rests on three linked negative views. The self: 'I am worthless, unlovable, a failure.' The world, or current experience: 'Everything is against me; no one helps.' The future: 'It will never get better.' These views flow from dysfunctional schemas laid down by early experience and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. Hopelessness, the negative view of the future, is the corner most closely tied to suicide, which Beck measured with his Hopelessness Scale (1974). Cognitive therapy targets the triad through collaborative empiricism: client and therapist treat each belief as a hypothesis, test it with thought records and behavioural experiments, and build more balanced alternatives. Example: after failing one exam, Arjun thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'my teachers are unfair and nobody cares' (world) and 'I will never get a job' (future). Option a is right because it lists all three corners correctly: self, world and future. Exam trap: examiners swap one corner for a near miss, putting 'others' in place of the world or the future, or substituting a time sequence (past, present, future); the mnemonic is self, world, future.", "traps": {"b": "Self and world are right, but 'others' replaces the future; Aaron Beck's third corner is the future, and hopelessness about it is the corner most tied to suicide risk.", "c": "Self and future are right, but 'others' replaces the world (current experience); interpersonal themes do feature in depression, but 'others' is not one of Aaron Beck's three corners.", "d": "Past, present and future is a time sequence, not Aaron Beck's triad; the triad is about what the person believes about themselves, their world and what lies ahead."}}, {"q": "The technique used to extinguish fear by forcing clients to remain in anxiety arousing situation is called –", "concept": "The answer is b: flooding is the technique that extinguishes fear by keeping the client in the anxiety-arousing situation until the fear fades. Flooding is a form of exposure therapy. The client is exposed, usually in real life (in vivo), to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, all at once, and is prevented from escaping. It rests on classical extinction: if the conditioned stimulus (the feared object) appears again and again without the feared outcome, the conditioned fear response weakens. It also blocks avoidance. In the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947), fears are learnt by classical conditioning and kept alive by operant avoidance, because each escape is rewarded by relief, and that relief stops the person from ever learning that the danger is not real. When escape is prevented, anxiety rises to a peak and then falls through habituation, and the client learns that nothing terrible happens. Sessions are long, often hours, because ending while anxiety is still high could strengthen the fear. A related method, implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl, 1967), floods the client in imagination with exaggerated scenes. Flooding is quicker than graded exposure but more distressing, so it needs full informed consent. Example: Rohan is terrified of lifts. With his therapist he rides a lift up and down a tall building for two hours. His panic peaks in the first 20 minutes, then slowly subsides until he feels bored. Option b is right because keeping the client in the feared situation with no escape is the definition of flooding. Exam trap: students confuse flooding with systematic desensitisation; both are exposure-based, but desensitisation is gradual and paired with relaxation, while flooding starts at the top of the hierarchy with no relaxation.", "traps": {"a": "Time-out (time out from positive reinforcement) is an operant technique, a form of negative punishment, that removes a child from a rewarding setting to reduce misbehaviour; it does not involve fear.", "c": "Inhibition here points to Joseph Wolpe's (1958) reciprocal inhibition, the principle behind systematic desensitisation, in which relaxation blocks anxiety; it does not keep the client in a highly anxious situation.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because flooding is the exact name of the technique described."}}, {"q": "The behavioural technique for reducing anxiety in which patients practise relaxation while visualising anxiety-provoking situations of increasing intensity is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the technique is systematic desensitisation, developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition. It rests on reciprocal inhibition: relaxation and anxiety cannot occur at the same time, so if deep relaxation is paired with a feared stimulus, the relaxation inhibits the anxiety. This is a form of counterconditioning, replacing a fear response with a calm one. Therapy has three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive muscle relaxation, tensing and releasing one muscle group after another. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated on Joseph Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when the current step no longer causes anxiety. Example: Riya fears flying; she first imagines looking at a photograph of a plane, then booking a ticket, then waiting at the gate and finally take-off, relaxing at every stage. Option c is right because the stem names all three parts: relaxation, visualisation and situations of increasing intensity. Exam trap: graded exposure while relaxed is desensitisation, but exposure to the most feared situation at full intensity from the start is flooding (implosion when the scene is imagined); and desensitisation works by reciprocal inhibition, not by operant reinforcement, extinction alone or catharsis.", "traps": {"a": "Symptom substitution is the psychoanalytic prediction that removing a symptom without resolving its underlying conflict will make a new symptom appear; it is a criticism aimed at behaviour therapy, not a technique.", "b": "Time-out (from positive reinforcement) is an operant technique that briefly removes a child from a rewarding setting to reduce unwanted behaviour; it uses no relaxation or visualisation.", "d": "A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) is an operant system in which tokens earned for target behaviours are exchanged for rewards, used in wards and classrooms; it builds behaviour rather than reducing anxiety."}}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:", "concept": "The answer is b: analysis of resistance is where the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895), in Studies on Hysteria, noticed that patients struggled against remembering painful material even while asking to be cured. Freud called this opposition resistance: anything in the patient that blocks the work of treatment and access to the unconscious. It is the defence of repression showing itself in the consulting room. Resistance can look like arriving late, missing sessions, forgetting to pay, long silences, changing the subject just as something painful comes up, intellectualising, or suddenly feeling cured (a flight into health). Freud came to see resistance not as a nuisance but as information: what the patient avoids points to what matters most. So the analyst gently points out the obstruction and explores with the patient what it is protecting against, rather than pushing past it. Kabir, three months into therapy, arrives fifteen minutes late every time his father is due to come up; the therapist notes the pattern and wonders aloud what it might be like to talk about his father, opening the topic he has been avoiding. Option b is right because it describes both halves of the technique: the client's obstruction and the therapist's exploration of why it happens. Exam trap: the other three options are also psychoanalytic techniques, so they all sound right; match each to its name. Saying whatever comes to mind is free association, interpreting dreams is dream analysis, and redirecting feelings about significant others onto the therapist is transference.", "traps": {"a": "This is free association, Sigmund Freud's fundamental rule that the client says whatever comes to mind without censoring it; it is the basic method, not the analysis of resistance.", "c": "This is dream analysis, which Sigmund Freud (1900) called the royal road to the unconscious; the analyst interprets the hidden (latent) content behind the remembered (manifest) dream.", "d": "This is transference, which Sigmund Freud recognised in the Dora case (1905): the client redirects feelings about parents or other significant figures onto the therapist."}}, {"q": "The first step in the counselling process is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first step in the counselling process is building rapport, a warm, trusting and accepting relationship between counsellor and client. The counsellor builds it by greeting the client warmly, giving full attention (eye contact, an open posture, nodding), listening without judging, reflecting feelings, explaining what counselling involves and promising confidentiality within its limits. Carl Rogers (1957) argued that the counsellor's empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness are the conditions that make change possible, and Gerard Egan (1975), in The Skilled Helper, likewise opens the helping process with attending to the client and helping him explore his concerns. Indian guidance texts give the process as building rapport, exploring the problem, assessment (including tests where needed), setting goals, intervention, and termination with follow-up. Without trust, clients hide or soften the real problem, so later steps rest on half the facts. Example: Farah, a Class 9 student, is sent to the school counsellor for missing classes. Asked at once 'Why are you bunking?', Farah says nothing. If the counsellor first chats about Farah's favourite subject, listens patiently and promises confidentiality, Farah soon admits that she is being teased on the school bus. Option c is right because exploring, testing and problem solving all depend on the trust built at the start. Exam trap: discussing the problem feels like the obvious opening, since the client came with a problem, but real discussion begins only once rapport exists; and solving the problem is not a step the counsellor performs for the client.", "traps": {"a": "Administering tools such as aptitude or personality tests belongs to the assessment stage, which comes after rapport; tests given to a tense, distrustful client yield guarded, unreliable answers.", "b": "Discussing and exploring the problem is the second stage; a client will not open up about painful matters until rapport and trust have been built.", "d": "Solving the client's problem is not a step the counsellor carries out; counselling helps the client reach his own solution, and any resolution comes near the end of the process, not at the start."}}, {"q": "Which option is not commonly diagnosed in criminals?", "concept": "The answer is a: depersonalization disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals. Depersonalization/derealization disorder (DSM-5-TR) involves persistent or recurrent experiences of feeling detached from one's own thoughts, feelings, body or actions, as if watching oneself from outside (depersonalization), or of the surroundings seeming dreamlike or unreal (derealization). Reality testing stays intact: the person says \"it is as if I am in a dream\", knows it is not so, and is usually frightened by the feeling. It is a dissociative disorder with no special link to offending. By contrast, forensic research shows antisocial personality disorder is the most common diagnosis among offenders; a large review of prison surveys by Seena Fazel and John Danesh (2002) found that nearly half of male prisoners met its criteria. Narcissistic traits are over-represented among violent offenders, and psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, though present in only a minority of prisoners, occur at several times the general-population rate. Clinical example: a college student who, under exam stress, feels her hands belong to someone else and her room looks like a film set has depersonalization and derealization, not a criminal tendency. Option a is right because depersonalization is an inward, distressing dissociative experience with no established link to crime. Exam trap: do not pick schizophrenia just because most people with schizophrenia are not violent; the question asks about rates among criminals, where it is elevated.", "traps": {"b": "Schizophrenia is found in a minority of offenders, yet psychotic illness is several times more common in prisons than in the general population, so it is a recognised forensic diagnosis.", "c": "Narcissistic personality disorder, marked by grandiosity, entitlement and low empathy, is over-represented among offenders, and narcissists are prone to aggression when their self-image is threatened.", "d": "Antisocial personality disorder, a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights, is the single most common diagnosis among prisoners."}}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "concept": "The answer is a: affective flattening is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to or taken away from normal experience. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to an acute Type I illness that responds to antipsychotics, and mainly negative symptoms to a chronic Type II illness with a poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen turned the split into two rating scales, the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The SAPS covers hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and positive formal thought disorder, and the last includes derailment, tangentiality, incoherence and circumstantiality. Affective flattening, the first domain of the SANS, is reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture. Example: Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for ten years, speaks in a flat monotone, rarely smiles or makes eye contact, and shows no change of expression when told of his sister's wedding. Option a is right because flattening is the absence of something normal, which makes it negative. Exam trap: circumstantiality looks like the odd one out, since it is mild and is also seen in obsessional personalities and in epilepsy; but Andreasen rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, because it adds needless detail rather than taking something away.", "traps": {"b": "Bizarre behaviour (odd clothing, agitated or aggressive acts, strange repetitive rituals) is one of the main domains of Nancy Andreasen's SAPS (1984); it is a positive symptom, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Circumstantiality is speech that wanders through needless detail before finally reaching its point (tangential speech never gets there); Andreasen's SAPS rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, so it belongs in the list.", "d": "Delusions are fixed false beliefs held despite clear contrary evidence, such as ideas of persecution or reference; they are the most typical positive symptom of all."}}, {"q": "Which of these is not an information provided in DSM-IV-TR?", "concept": "The answer is d: DSM-IV-TR does not give information on treatment options. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Since DSM-III, directed by Robert Spitzer (1980), the manual has been descriptive and atheoretical: it says what a disorder looks like and how to diagnose it reliably, stays neutral about causes and is silent on treatment. DSM-IV-TR (2000) is a text revision of DSM-IV (1994): the criteria stayed almost the same, but the descriptive text was updated. For each disorder the text covers diagnostic features (the essential features), subtypes and specifiers, recording procedures, associated features and disorders (including laboratory findings), specific culture, age and gender features, prevalence, course, familial pattern and differential diagnosis, followed by the diagnostic criteria, the list of symptoms that must be present and for how long. DSM-IV-TR also used five axes: clinical disorders; personality disorders and mental retardation; general medical conditions; psychosocial and environmental problems; and the Global Assessment of Functioning. Treatment guidance lives elsewhere, in the American Psychiatric Association's separate practice guidelines, in national guidelines and in textbooks. Example: a clinical psychologist, Kabir, uses DSM-IV-TR to confirm that his client meets the criteria for a major depressive episode and to rule out bipolar disorder, but turns to a practice guideline to choose between CBT and an antidepressant. Option d is right because the manual is a diagnostic and descriptive tool, not a treatment manual. Exam trap: the size of the book and the word 'Statistical' tempt students to think it covers everything about a disorder; it covers diagnosis and description, never therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Essential (diagnostic) features are described for every disorder in the DSM-IV-TR text, so this is information the manual does provide.", "b": "Associated features and disorders, such as typical laboratory findings and common comorbid conditions, form a standard subsection of each DSM-IV-TR entry.", "c": "The list of symptoms that must be present, the diagnostic criteria, has been the heart of the manual since Robert Spitzer's DSM-III (1980)."}}, {"q": "Lifetime prevalence rate for OCD is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the lifetime prevalence of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is around 2.5%. OCD is defined by obsessions, recurrent intrusive and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety (fear of germs, doubts about locking the door, taboo images), and/or compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, praying) done to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. It must be time-consuming (more than an hour a day) or cause marked distress or impairment. The classic figure comes from the Epidemiologic Catchment Area (ECA) study of the 1980s, which interviewed more than 18,000 adults at five US sites with a structured diagnostic interview. Marvin Karno and colleagues (1988) reported a lifetime OCD prevalence of about 2.5%, far higher than clinicians had assumed, since many sufferers hide their rituals. Lifetime prevalence is the proportion of people who have had the disorder at any point in their life; 12-month prevalence counts only recent cases and is therefore lower. OCD usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood, earlier in males. Example: Arjun, 20, checks the gas knob 30 times before leaving home and is late for college every day; he is one of the 2 to 3 people in every 100 who will have OCD at some point. Option a is right because about 2.5% is the standard lifetime figure. Exam trap: 'around 1%' (option d) is close to the 12-month figure, so it catches students who mix up lifetime and 12-month prevalence. Key note: the later National Comorbidity Survey Replication (Ayelet Meron Ruscio and colleagues, 2010) gives about 2.3%, and DSM-5 gives a 12-month figure of 1.2% (1.1% to 1.8% internationally), so a '1%' option is a 12-month trap; around 2.5% stands.", "traps": {"b": "1.5% matches neither the lifetime figure (about 2.5% in the ECA, 2.3% in the NCS-R) nor the DSM-5 12-month figure (1.2%); it is a distractor that sits between the two.", "c": "4.5% roughly doubles the survey figures; no major epidemiological study puts lifetime OCD that high, so it overstates the disorder.", "d": "About 1% matches the DSM-5 12-month prevalence (1.2% in the United States), which counts only recent cases; it is a trap for students who mix up 12-month and lifetime prevalence."}}, {"q": "Which aspect of the definition of abnormality includes unhappiness?", "concept": "The answer is a: the part of the definition of abnormality that includes unhappiness is distress. Distress refers to unpleasant, upsetting feelings: sadness, unhappiness, anxiety, fear, guilt or emotional pain. It is one of the four Ds that Ronald Comer (1992) uses to sum up abnormality, along with deviance, dysfunction and danger. The personal distress criterion treats behaviour as abnormal when it causes the person great suffering. It matters because many disorders, such as depression and the anxiety disorders, are felt mainly as suffering, and DSM-5 (2013) builds 'clinically significant distress or impairment' into its definition of a mental disorder. But distress is neither necessary nor sufficient on its own. Some people with a disorder feel little distress: a person in a manic episode may feel wonderful, and someone with antisocial personality disorder may feel no guilt. And distress can be normal: grief after a parent's death, or nerves before a viva, is not a disorder. That is why clinicians weigh all four Ds together and ask whether the distress is out of proportion to its cause and lasting. Example: Riya has felt deeply unhappy and hopeless for two months since moving to a new city, cries most evenings and has lost interest in everything. Her unhappiness is the distress feature of abnormality. Option a is right because unhappiness is a form of personal suffering, which is exactly what the distress criterion describes. Exam trap: dysfunction can sound close, because unhappy people often function worse, but dysfunction refers to impaired daily functioning (work, study, relationships, self-care), not to the feeling itself.", "traps": {"b": "Deviance is behaviour that is different, extreme or that violates social norms; it concerns how the behaviour looks to others, not how the person feels.", "c": "Dysfunction means behaviour that interferes with daily life, such as being unable to study, work or care for oneself; it is about impaired functioning, not unhappiness.", "d": "Danger to self or others covers suicidal or violent risk. It is the least common of the four Ds and has nothing specific to do with unhappiness."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "concept": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the leading evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn fears of things that threatened our ancestors, such as snakes, spiders, heights, deep water and angry faces, far more easily than fears of modern dangers such as electric sockets or cars. Prepared fears can form after a single bad experience, or even from watching someone else, and they resist extinction. This explains why phobias cluster around a short list of ancestral objects. Strong evidence comes from rhesus monkeys: Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) showed laboratory-reared monkeys videos of other monkeys reacting with fear; they learned to fear toy snakes and toy crocodiles, but not flowers or toy rabbits edited into the same footage. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) later described an evolved \"fear module\" in the brain that such cues trigger selectively. Example: Riya has never been bitten, yet one scream from her cousin at a garden snake leaves her with a lasting snake phobia, while a far more dangerous shock from a faulty switch leaves no phobia at all. Option c is right because preparedness is the evolutionary explanation that dominates textbook accounts of phobias. Exam trap: a learned fear response (option b) sounds scientific but is plain conditioning with no evolutionary claim, while the non-associative account (option a) is also evolutionary, so students who spot only that word may pick it. Key note: Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies (2002) proposed non-associative fear acquisition, in which some fears (of heights, water, strangers) are innate and appear without any learning; it is evolutionary too, but a minority view, and preparedness remains the predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition is the view of Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies (2002) that some fears are innate and need no learning at all. It is evolutionary, but a minority position, so it is not the predominant theory the stem asks for.", "b": "A learned fear response is classical conditioning, as when John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned Little Albert to fear a white rat. It is a learning explanation and makes no claim about evolution.", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition is not the name of any theory; specific phobia is a DSM-5 diagnosis. Stanley Rachman's (1977) three pathways (conditioning, vicarious learning, information) explain how phobias are acquired, but they are not an evolutionary theory either."}}, {"q": "‘Reinforcement will have positive effect on performance,’ which type of hypothesis is this?", "concept": "The correct answer is alternate and directional. In hypothesis testing, researchers distinguish between the null hypothesis and the alternate (alternative) hypothesis, and between directional and non-directional forms. The null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or no relationship, for example that reinforcement has no effect on performance. The alternate hypothesis (H1) states that there is an effect or relationship. The statement 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' asserts that an effect exists, so it is an alternate hypothesis, not a null one. Furthermore, it specifies the direction of that effect: not merely that reinforcement affects performance, but that it improves it (a positive effect). When a hypothesis specifies the direction of the expected effect (such as 'greater than,' 'less than,' 'improves,' or 'reduces'), it is a directional hypothesis, tested with a one-tailed test. When it merely predicts a difference or relationship without specifying which way it will go (such as 'reinforcement will affect performance'), it is non-directional, tested with a two-tailed test. Because this statement both claims an effect (alternate) and specifies that the effect is positive (directional), it is an alternate and directional hypothesis. This is a standard research-methodology classification.", "traps": {"a": "Null and directional is wrong because the statement predicts an effect (so it is alternate, not null), even though it is directional.", "c": "Null and non-directional is wrong on both counts: it predicts an effect (alternate) and specifies a positive direction (directional).", "d": "Alternate and non-directional is half right (alternate) but wrong on direction, since the statement does specify a positive direction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following variable(s) can be labelled as organismic variable(s)? 1. Task complexity 2. Intelligence 3. Errors made while learning task 4. Heart rate", "concept": "The answer is b: intelligence and heart rate are organismic variables, so 2 and 4 only. Robert Woodworth (1869 to 1962), whose Dynamic Psychology (1918) put the organism and its drives at the centre, expanded the behaviourist stimulus-response (S-R) formula into S-O-R, placing the organism between stimulus and response, and experimental psychology still sorts variables this way. Stimulus or task variables are features of the situation or task the experimenter presents: task complexity, list length, noise, instructions. Organismic (O, or subject) variables are characteristics or states that the participant brings to the study: age, sex, intelligence, personality, motivation, fatigue, and physiological states such as heart rate or blood sugar. Response variables are the behaviour measured as the outcome, the dependent variable: errors, reaction time, trials to criterion. Organismic variables usually cannot be manipulated, so they are measured, selected or controlled (by matching or random assignment); left uncontrolled, they act as extraneous variables. Example: Arjun learns a finger maze. The number of turns in the maze is a task variable, his IQ and resting heart rate are organismic variables, and the errors he makes are the response variable. Option b is right because only intelligence (a trait) and heart rate (a physiological state) belong to the organism. Exam trap: errors made while learning come from the person, so they feel organismic, but they are the response being measured (the DV); task complexity is a property of the task. Key note: heart rate is recorded as a response measure in some experiments, for example in psychophysiology, but as a state of the organism it is an O variable here; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "\"4 only\" leaves out intelligence, which is the textbook example of an organismic (subject) variable, a stable characteristic the participant brings to the study.", "c": "c adds task complexity, which is a stimulus or task variable set by the experimenter, not a characteristic of the organism.", "d": "d adds errors made while learning, which is the response variable (the dependent measure), not an organismic variable."}}, {"q": "How many subjects would be required in a 3 × 3×2 factorial design with ten subjects per cell?", "concept": "The answer is d: 180 subjects. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at once, crossing every level of each factor with every level of the others. The notation tells you the structure: each number is one factor, and its value is that factor's number of levels. So 3 × 3 × 2 means three factors, two with three levels each and one with two levels. The number of cells (conditions) is the product of the levels: 3 × 3 × 2 = 18. With ten subjects in each cell, the total is 18 × 10 = 180. Example: a researcher studies anxiety reduction by therapy type (CBT, mindfulness, waiting list), session frequency (weekly, twice weekly, daily) and gender (male, female); that gives 18 combinations, and with ten participants in each, 180 people are needed. Factorial designs, analysed with factorial analysis of variance (ANOVA), are efficient because one study tests every main effect and every interaction. Option d is right because you multiply the levels to get the cells and then multiply by the subjects per cell. Exam trap: students add the levels (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) or stop at the number of cells (18, option c). The calculation assumes a between-subjects design, the standard reading, in which each participant serves in only one cell.", "traps": {"a": "10 is the number of subjects in one cell; it would be the total only if every participant served in all 18 conditions (a fully within-subjects design), which the question does not suggest.", "b": "90 multiplies only part of the design (3 × 3 × 10), ignoring the third factor with two levels.", "c": "18 is the number of cells (3 × 3 × 2), not the number of subjects; it still has to be multiplied by ten subjects per cell."}}, {"q": "Type II error occurs :", "concept": "The correct answer is that a Type II error occurs whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted. In the null-hypothesis significance-testing framework formalized by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, two kinds of decision errors are possible. A Type I error (alpha error) occurs when a true null hypothesis is rejected, that is, the researcher concludes there is an effect when in fact there is none, a false positive; its probability is set by the significance level alpha. A Type II error (beta error) occurs when a false null hypothesis is accepted (not rejected), that is, the researcher fails to detect a real effect that actually exists, a false negative; its probability is denoted beta. The complement of a Type II error, 1 minus beta, is the statistical power of a test, the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis and detecting a true effect. Power increases with larger sample size, larger effect size, lower measurement error, and a higher alpha. Correctly accepting a true null and correctly rejecting a false null are the two right decisions, not errors, which rules out options a and b. Because a Type II error is specifically the failure to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, option c is correct.", "traps": {"a": "Accepting a true null hypothesis is a correct decision, not any kind of error.", "b": "Rejecting a false null hypothesis is also a correct decision (correctly detecting an effect), not an error.", "d": "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (a false positive), not a Type II error."}}, {"q": "After the recent heavy catastrophic floods in a given state, a psychologist interviewed 120 participants to understand the psychological consequences of the event. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is a: interviewing flood survivors after the disaster is ex post facto field research. 'Ex post facto' is Latin for 'after the fact'. Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, described it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred or cannot be manipulated, so the researcher works backward from the observed effects to their presumed cause. Here the independent variable is the flood. No psychologist can create a flood, or decide at random who is hit and who is spared, so there is no manipulation and no random assignment. What the psychologist can do is go to the affected area afterwards and measure outcomes such as anxiety, sleep problems, grief or post-traumatic stress, perhaps comparing badly hit and lightly hit villages. Because the data are gathered in people's real surroundings rather than a laboratory, it is a field study. The design has known weaknesses: exposed and unexposed groups differ in many ways besides the flood, so causal conclusions are weak, and it is tempting to assume that whatever came first caused whatever followed. Example: Kabir, a clinical psychologist, interviews 120 survivors in Kerala three months after the floods to record their distress. Option a is right because the event came first, the researcher had no control over it, and the work took place in the community. Exam trap: 'field' appears in two options, and what separates them is manipulation. A field experiment still has the researcher deliberately change something, such as assigning some villages to a counselling programme; here nothing was changed.", "traps": {"b": "A field experiment manipulates an independent variable in a real-life setting (for example, giving some flood-hit villages a counselling programme and not others); here the psychologist manipulated nothing.", "c": "A controlled (laboratory) experiment manipulates the independent variable under tight control with random assignment; a flood can be neither created nor assigned.", "d": "Psychometric research is about constructing, standardising and validating tests; this psychologist studies the effects of an event, not the properties of a measure."}}, {"q": "In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is :", "concept": "The correct answer is left of the median. Skewness describes the asymmetry of a distribution. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution, the long tail points toward the low (left) end of the scale. Extreme low scores in that tail pull the arithmetic mean downward more than they move the median, because the mean is sensitive to every value while the median depends only on rank position. The result is the ordering mean less than median less than mode. So the mean sits to the left of the median. The mirror image holds for a positively (right) skewed distribution, where the ordering is mode less than median less than mean, placing the mean to the right. This relationship among the three measures of central tendency was popularized through the empirical work of Karl Pearson, who also defined coefficients of skewness using the gap between mean and mode or mean and median. Understanding it lets you infer the shape of a distribution from summary statistics alone, which is why it is a staple of psychology statistics papers.", "traps": {"b": "Right of the median describes a positively skewed distribution, where the tail extends toward high values.", "c": "Mean equals median only in a symmetric distribution, not a skewed one.", "d": "In negative skew the mode is the highest of the three, so equating mean with mode is incorrect."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a measure of central tendency?", "concept": "Mean deviation is not a measure of central tendency, so c is the correct answer. Central tendency refers to a single value that represents the centre or typical score of a distribution, summarising where the bulk of the data cluster. The three classic measures of central tendency are the mean, the arithmetic average obtained by summing all scores and dividing by their number; the median, the middle score when all values are arranged in order, which divides the distribution into two equal halves and is robust to extreme scores; and the mode, the most frequently occurring score, which is the only measure usable with nominal data. Mean deviation, by contrast, belongs to an entirely different family of statistics called measures of dispersion or variability, which describe how spread out the scores are around the centre rather than where the centre lies. Mean deviation is calculated as the average of the absolute differences of each score from the mean (or median). Other measures of dispersion include the range, the variance, the standard deviation, and the quartile deviation. The conceptual point the question tests is the distinction between location and spread: central tendency answers where is the centre, while dispersion answers how scattered are the data. Because mean deviation quantifies scatter, it cannot be a measure of central tendency, making it the correct exception.", "traps": {"a": "The mean is the arithmetic average and is the most commonly used measure of central tendency, so it does not fit a question asking what is NOT one.", "b": "The median is the middle value of an ordered distribution and is a core measure of central tendency, especially valued when data are skewed.", "d": "The mode is the most frequent value and is a legitimate measure of central tendency, the only one applicable to categorical data."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a goodness of fit test?", "concept": "The answer is a: the chi-square test is the classic goodness of fit test. A goodness of fit test asks how well observed data match the distribution that a hypothesis predicts. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced chi-square for exactly this purpose. You sort cases into categories, count the observed frequency (O) in each, work out the expected frequency (E) if the hypothesis were true, and add up (O minus E) squared divided by E across the categories. A small chi-square means the data fit the expected pattern; a large one, read against the chi-square table with (number of categories minus 1) degrees of freedom, means they do not. Because it works on counts in categories, it needs no normal distribution and is classed as non-parametric. Example: Meera asks 120 students which of four counselling slots they prefer. If there is no preference, each slot should get 30. She observes 50, 30, 25 and 15, and chi-square tells her whether that spread departs from the even split more than chance allows. Applied to a two-way table (are gender and choice of stream related?), the same statistic becomes the chi-square test of independence, a different use. Option a is right because chi-square compares observed counts with the counts a model expects, which is what fit means. Exam trap: the runs test is also non-parametric and also works on counts, so it looks similar, but it tests whether a sequence is random, not whether data fit a distribution. The Kolmogorov-Smirnov one-sample test is the other well-known goodness of fit test, but it is not offered here.", "traps": {"b": "The t-test of William Sealy Gosset, who published as 'Student' (1908), compares two means, or one mean with a known value, on interval data. It asks whether means differ, not whether frequencies fit a predicted distribution.", "c": "Analysis of variance, developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, compares three or more group means by splitting variance into between-group and within-group parts. It tests mean differences, not goodness of fit.", "d": "The runs test (Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz, 1940) counts runs, unbroken streaks of the same symbol, to judge whether a sequence is random or whether two samples come from one population. It is a test of randomness, not of fit to an expected distribution."}}], "repeats-3": [{"q": "Rejecting the concept of S-R connectionism, the S-O-R concept was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: Robert S. Woodworth (1869 to 1962) of Columbia University rejected the plain stimulus-response (S-R) formula and proposed S-O-R, stimulus-organism-response. Edward Thorndike's (1898) connectionism, and later John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism, explained behaviour as learned bonds between a stimulus (S) and a response (R). Woodworth argued that the same stimulus produces different responses depending on what is going on inside the organism (O): its drives, needs, past learning, expectations, attention and bodily state. In Dynamic Psychology (1918) he stressed drive and motivation, and in his textbook Psychology (1929) he set out the S-O-R formula. This mediational view, in which inner states stand between stimulus and response, was later taken up by neobehaviourists and by cognitive psychology. Woodworth was a functionalist and is remembered for making psychology 'dynamic', concerned with why organisms act as well as how. Example: a plate of hot samosas (S) makes hungry Kabir reach for one (R), but after a big lunch the same plate gets no response; the difference lies in the organism (O), his state of hunger. Option a is right because the S-O-R concept is Woodworth's, introduced to correct the S-R connectionist view. Exam trap: Edward B. Titchener (1867 to 1927) (c) tempts because he is another early founder working in America, but he was a structuralist who analysed conscious experience into elements by introspection, not a critic of S-R bonds; and Thorndike is the connectionist the stem rejects, not the reformer.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Jung founded analytical psychology (collective unconscious, archetypes, introversion and extraversion); he worked on the psyche, not on reforming the S-R formula of learning.", "c": "Edward B. Titchener founded structuralism at Cornell, breaking consciousness into sensations, images and feelings by trained introspection; he had nothing to do with S-O-R.", "d": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis (unconscious conflict, id, ego and superego); his model of behaviour is driven by instincts, not framed as stimulus, organism and response."}}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II: a. Free recall and recognition, b. Sentence verification task, c. Priming, d. Scanning task. 1. Semantic memory, 2. Sensory memory, 3. Episodic memory, 4. Implicit memory", "concept": "The answer is d: free recall and recognition test episodic memory (3), sentence verification tests semantic memory (1), priming tests implicit memory (4) and the scanning task, by elimination, sensory memory (2). Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory into episodic memory, personal events tied to a time and place, and semantic memory, general knowledge of facts and concepts. Free recall (write down every word from the list you just heard) and recognition (which of these words were on the list?) ask about one specific study episode, so they are episodic tasks. In a sentence verification task the person judges sentences such as 'A canary is a bird' as true or false as fast as possible; Allan Collins and Ross Quillian (1969) used the reaction times to map the network of stored facts, so the task taps semantic memory. Priming is an influence of earlier exposure without conscious recollection: having seen BUTTER, a person completes BUT___ as BUTTER more often, even without remembering the word; Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) called this implicit memory. Scanning a briefly flashed display, as in George Sperling's (1960) partial report, taps the brief iconic store of sensory memory. Example: Riya recognises yesterday's list words (episodic), confirms that a sparrow is a bird (semantic) and fills BUT___ with BUTTER without knowing why (implicit). Option d is right because it is the only option with all four pairings correct. Exam trap: recall and recognition feel like knowledge tests, so students send them to semantic memory; they test memory for an episode. Key note: Saul Sternberg's (1966) memory scanning task is really a short-term memory task, while scanning a briefly flashed display, as in Sperling's partial report, taps sensory (iconic) memory; with the other three pairs fixed, d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Every pairing is wrong: a-4 sends recall and recognition to implicit memory though both need conscious remembering, b-2 makes sentence verification sensory, c-1 makes priming semantic and d-3 makes scanning episodic.", "b": "b-1 (sentence verification as semantic) is right, but a-2 calls recall and recognition sensory, c-3 makes priming episodic though it works without recollection, and d-4 makes scanning implicit.", "c": "Every pairing is wrong: recall and recognition are episodic (not semantic), sentence verification is semantic (not implicit), priming is implicit (not sensory) and scanning is sensory (not episodic)."}}, {"q": "In signal-detection research, when no stimulus is presented and the subject reports seeing one, it is called a:", "concept": "The answer is c: in signal-detection research, reporting a stimulus when none was presented is called a false alarm. Classical psychophysics assumed a fixed threshold, but wartime radar operators showed that whether a person says 'yes' depends both on what they can sense and on how willing they are to say yes. Signal detection theory, set out by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates the two. On every trial a signal is either present or absent, and the observer says either 'yes' or 'no', which gives four outcomes. Signal present and 'yes' is a hit. Signal present and 'no' is a miss. Signal absent and 'yes' is a false alarm. Signal absent and 'no' is a correct rejection. From the hit and false alarm rates the researcher works out sensitivity (d prime, how well the person tells signal from noise) and the criterion (beta, how ready they are to say yes). A liberal observer, like a touchy smoke detector, gives many hits but also many false alarms; a conservative one gives few false alarms but more misses. Example: Kabir, an airport security screener, flags a bag as holding a knife when it holds only a steel tiffin box; no target was present but he said yes, so that is a false alarm. Option c is right because 'false alarm' is the signal-detection name for a yes response on a trial with no signal. Exam trap: students swap the two errors. A miss is saying no when the signal was there; a false alarm is saying yes when it was not. Key note: 'false positive' names the same event in diagnostic-testing language, so d is not wrong in plain terms; in signal-detection research the term is false alarm, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A hit is a correct 'yes' when the signal really is present, the opposite cell of the table from a false alarm, which happens on a trial with no signal.", "b": "A miss is saying 'no' when a signal was present, a failure to detect; the stem describes the reverse error, a 'yes' when nothing was there.", "d": "False positive is the medical and statistical name (as in a Type I error) for the same kind of event, so it is a near synonym; but the stem asks for the signal-detection term, which is false alarm."}}, {"q": "Hermann Ebbinghaus, had done the pioneering experiments on:", "concept": "The answer is d: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1850 to 1909) did the pioneering experiments on memory, reported in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory, 1885). Ebbinghaus showed that a higher mental process could be measured experimentally, something Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) had doubted. Acting as his own sole participant, he invented the nonsense syllable (consonant-vowel-consonant strings such as DAX or BUP) to avoid the meanings and associations of real words. He learned lists until he could recite them twice without error and then relearned them after delays. Relearning was quicker even when nothing could be recalled, and he measured this with the savings method: original trials minus relearning trials, divided by original trials, times 100. His forgetting curve drops steeply, losing more than half within an hour and about two thirds within a day, then levels off without reaching zero. He also found the spacing effect (spread-out practice beats massed practice) and noticed that items at the start and end of a list are learned more easily. Example: Meera learns a list of nonsense syllables in 20 readings; a week later she recalls none, but relearns the list in 12 readings, a 40% saving. Option d is right because nonsense syllables, savings and the forgetting curve are all memory research, and the serial position finding is one result within it. Exam trap: the serial position effect is a real Ebbinghaus observation, so it baits the half-informed; but it is one part of his memory work, and memory is the full answer. Key note: WBJEEB dropped this question (as JEMAS(PG) 2024 Q59) from its final answer key for JEMAS(PG) 2024 (Nov 2024), so it was not scored in the real exam. c can be argued too, since Ebbinghaus did first describe the serial position effect; d stays the answer here.", "traps": {"a": "Perception was the territory of Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, 1912) and others; the Ebbinghaus illusion of size contrast carries his name, but his pioneering experimental programme was on memory.", "b": "Psychophysics was founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860); Ebbinghaus borrowed Fechner's quantitative spirit for memory but did not pioneer psychophysics.", "c": "The serial position effect (better recall of first and last items) was noticed by Ebbinghaus but studied systematically later (for example by Bennet Murdock, 1962); it is one finding inside his memory work, not the field he pioneered."}}, {"q": "Among the following pioneering psychologists who is not associated with Behaviourism?", "concept": "The answer is a: Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021) is not associated with behaviourism; he founded cognitive therapy. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), held that psychology should study only observable behaviour and its environmental causes, leaving out mind and consciousness. The neobehaviourists who followed refined it. Edwin Ray Guthrie (1886 to 1959) explained learning by contiguity alone: a stimulus that accompanied a movement will tend to be followed by that movement again, at full strength after one trial. Clark Leonard Hull (1884 to 1952) built a hypothetico-deductive system of drive reduction in which reaction potential equals habit strength times drive (Principles of Behavior, 1943). Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist, studied operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement. Beck, a psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, moved in a different direction. In the 1960s he argued that depression grows from negative automatic thoughts and schemas, summed up in the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), and he built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). His therapy targets thoughts, which strict behaviourists refused to treat as causes. Example: Riya fails a test and thinks 'I am useless and always will be'. A Beck therapist tests that thought against the evidence, while a Skinnerian would look at how studying is reinforced. Option a is right because Beck's theory is cognitive, from the school that rose against behaviourism. Exam trap: cognitive behaviour therapy uses behavioural techniques, so Beck's name sits near behaviour therapy in textbooks, but he is a cognitive pioneer, not a behaviourist.", "traps": {"b": "Edwin Ray Guthrie was a neobehaviourist known for contiguity theory and one-trial learning (The Psychology of Learning, 1935), so he belongs to behaviourism.", "c": "Burrhus Frederic Skinner was the leading radical behaviourist: operant conditioning, the Skinner box and schedules of reinforcement.", "d": "Clark Leonard Hull was a neobehaviourist whose drive-reduction theory (Principles of Behavior, 1943) defined reaction potential as habit strength times drive."}}, {"q": "A physical stimulus is converted into a neural impulse by a process known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the conversion of a physical stimulus into a neural impulse is called transduction. Every sense organ contains receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy. Their job is to translate that energy into the only language the nervous system understands: changes in membrane voltage that lead to nerve impulses. In the eye, light is absorbed by photopigments such as rhodopsin in the rods and cones; this changes the receptor's membrane potential, and the signal passes through bipolar cells to ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. In the ear, sound waves bend the stereocilia of hair cells in the cochlea, opening ion channels. In the skin, pressure deforms mechanoreceptors; in the nose and tongue, chemicals bind to receptor proteins. In each case the first change is a graded receptor potential whose size follows the strength of the stimulus; this then triggers all-or-none impulses whose rate codes intensity. Because all impulses look alike, Johannes Peter Muller (1801 to 1858) proposed the doctrine of specific nerve energies: the quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, which is why pressing on a closed eye makes you see flashes of light. Sensation is detection and transduction; perception is the later interpretation by the brain. Example: Riya steps into sunlight; within milliseconds her rods and cones turn light into electrical signals, and only then can her brain recognise her friend's face across the road. Option c is right because transduction is the technical term for converting stimulus energy into neural signals at the receptor. Exam trap: all four options begin with \"trans-\"; only transduction is the sensory term, while transposition and transaction belong to other areas of psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Transposition is a Gestalt idea: a melody keeps its identity when every note is shifted (Christian von Ehrenfels, 1890), and the animals of Wolfgang Kohler (1918) learned relations such as \"the lighter one\" rather than absolute stimuli. It is not sensory coding.", "b": "Transaction appears in the transactional analysis of Eric Berne (1961) and the transactional model of stress of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984); it means an exchange between people, or between person and environment, not energy conversion.", "d": "Transition is an everyday word for any change from one state to another, as in life transitions; it is not the name for how receptors code a stimulus."}}, {"q": "______ is the study of the meaning of words and language.", "concept": "The answer is c: semantics is the study of the meaning of words and language. Language can be analysed at several levels. Phonology studies the sound system (phonemes); morphology studies how words are built from morphemes, the smallest units of meaning; syntax studies the rules that combine words into sentences; semantics studies meaning, of words, phrases and whole sentences; and pragmatics studies how language is used in social context, such as knowing that 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request, not a question about ability. Semantics covers word meanings, synonyms and opposites, ambiguity, and how the meaning of a sentence is built from its parts. Noam Chomsky (1957) showed that syntax and meaning can come apart with his sentence 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously', which is grammatically perfect but meaningless. Charles Hockett (1960) listed semanticity, the link between symbols and the things they stand for, among the design features of human language. The same root appears in memory research: Endel Tulving's (1972) semantic memory is our store of facts and word meanings. Example: Riya reads 'the boat drifted to the bank' and 'she deposited cash at the bank' and picks the right sense of 'bank' each time; that skill is semantic. Option c is right because the stem asks specifically about meaning, and meaning is what semantics studies. Exam trap: linguistics (option a) is the broad scientific study of all aspects of language, of which semantics is only one branch, so it is too wide; syntax (option d) is grammar, the rules of word order, not meaning.", "traps": {"a": "Linguistics is the whole scientific study of language (sounds, structure, meaning and use); semantics is just one of its branches, so it is too broad for a stem about meaning alone.", "b": "Encoding is a memory term: turning information into a form that can be stored (acoustic, visual or semantic encoding); it is a process, not the field that studies meaning.", "d": "Syntax is the set of rules for combining words into grammatical sentences (word order, Noam Chomsky's transformational grammar); a sentence can be syntactically correct yet meaningless."}}, {"q": "Individual differences in intelligence are due to", "concept": "The answer is d: individual differences in intelligence arise from many factors, both genetic and environmental, acting together. Behaviour genetics estimates the genetic share with twin and adoption studies. Identical twins, who share all their genes, have more similar IQ scores than fraternal twins, who share about half, and adopted children come to resemble their biological parents more than their adoptive parents as they grow. Heritability, the proportion of differences within a population linked to genetic differences, rises with age: a combined analysis of about 11,000 twin pairs led by Claire Haworth and Robert Plomin (2010) found about 0.4 at age 9 rising to about 0.66 by age 17. But heritability is a population statistic, not a fixed limit for any one person. The environment matters too: prenatal health, nutrition, schooling, a stimulating home, family income and freedom from toxins such as lead. James Flynn (1984, 1987) showed that average IQ scores rose by about three points per decade across many nations, far too fast for genes to explain. Genes and environment also work together: a curious child seeks out books, and the books then sharpen her abilities. Example: identical twins Riya and Meera are adopted into different homes; their scores stay close, yet the twin given better schooling scores a few points higher. Option d is right because it is the only option that includes both genetic and environmental causes without narrowing them. Exam trap: option c sounds balanced, but economic status is only one environmental factor; 'many factors including genetic and environmental' is the full interactionist answer.", "traps": {"a": "'Only genetic factors' is the extreme hereditarian position; twin studies show substantial heritability, but well below 1, and the Flynn effect shows that environment shifts scores too.", "b": "'Only environmental factors' is the extreme environmentalist position; it ignores twin and adoption evidence that genetic relatedness predicts similarity in IQ.", "c": "Genetic and economic factors leaves out most of the environment, such as nutrition, schooling and home stimulation; economic status is only one environmental influence."}}, {"q": "The \"law of effect,\" formulated by Edward Thorndike, states that:", "concept": "The answer is that behaviors followed by pleasurable consequences are more likely to be repeated, because that is the essence of Edward Thorndike's law of effect. Understand the law and its place in the history of learning theory. Edward Thorndike, working around 1900, conducted classic experiments placing hungry cats in puzzle boxes from which they could escape to reach food by performing some action, such as pulling a loop or pressing a lever. At first the cat clawed and struggled randomly, but eventually it happened upon the correct response by accident and escaped. Over repeated trials, the time to escape gradually decreased, and Thorndike plotted these learning curves. Crucially, the improvement was gradual and incremental, not sudden, which led him to conclude that the cat was not reasoning or gaining insight but rather learning by trial and error, with successful responses being gradually stamped in. From this he formulated the law of effect: responses that produce a satisfying or pleasurable consequence in a given situation become more strongly connected to that situation and are more likely to recur, whereas responses followed by discomfort become weakened and less likely to recur. This idea, that consequences shape the future probability of behavior, was the direct forerunner of B. F. Skinner's principle of reinforcement in operant conditioning. Thorndike also proposed the law of exercise, that connections strengthen with practice. Contrast his consequence-driven, incremental account with Bandura's observational learning, Kohler's insight learning, and Freud's unconscious motivation. The statement that satisfying consequences make behavior more likely to repeat is the law of effect, Thorndike's signature contribution.", "traps": {"b": "Learning by observing and imitating models is Albert Bandura's social learning theory, a different mechanism from Thorndike's stamping-in of responses by their consequences.", "c": "Insight through the sudden restructuring of a problem's elements is the Gestalt view of learning associated with Wolfgang Kohler's work with chimpanzees, not Thorndike's incremental trial-and-error law of effect.", "d": "The claim that all behavior is motivated by unconscious drives is a psychoanalytic proposition from Sigmund Freud, entirely unrelated to Thorndike's behavioral law of effect."}}, {"q": "Thorndike's Multifactor theory of intelligence is at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of:", "concept": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike's (1927) multifactor theory sits at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of mental organisation, that is, how the abilities that make up intelligence are structured. Theorists who correlated many tests asked whether the abilities behind them form one general power, a few broad group factors or a host of separate bits. Thorndike gave the most splintered answer. For him intelligence is made up of a multitude of separate, minute elements of ability, each a set of stimulus-response connections. Any mental act uses a number of these elements together, two tasks correlate only to the extent that they share elements, and there is no general intelligence: a brighter person simply has more connections. The theory is therefore called atomistic. Even so, Thorndike grouped tasks into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence. At the other extreme stands Charles Spearman's (1904) two-factor theory, in which all intellectual activity depends mainly on one general factor, g, plus a specific factor, s, for each task; group-factor theories such as that of Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) sit in between. Example: Kabir repairs engines brilliantly but is average at abstract puzzles; Thorndike would call these largely separate abilities. Option c is right because the range from multifactor to two-factor theories is a range of views on how the mind's abilities are organised, which is exactly the phrase the textbook uses. Exam trap: the opposite pole is Spearman's g, not Thurstone's primary mental abilities, which are a middle position; and do not mix up Thorndike (multifactor) with Thurstone (primary mental abilities). Key note: the stem copies a sentence from Frank S. Freeman's Theory and Practice of Psychological Testing (third edition, 1962), which reads 'the nature of mental organization', so c is the answer both Jammu papers intend. Option b, intellectual organisation, means much the same thing here; if you picked it you understood the idea, but on these papers c is the safe mark, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Motor organisation concerns the control and coordination of movement (motor development and the motor system), not the structure of intelligence.", "b": "'Intellectual organisation' means much the same as mental organisation here, but the sentence this stem is copied from (Frank S. Freeman, 1962) says 'mental organization', so c is the intended answer (see the Key note).", "d": "Reasoning is one specific ability inside the structure (one of Louis Leon Thurstone's primary mental abilities), not the broad question of how intelligence as a whole is organised."}}, {"q": "The unconscious perception of stimuli that are too weak to exceed the absolute threshold for detection is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: subliminal perception is the unconscious perception of stimuli too weak or brief to exceed the absolute threshold for detection. 'Limen' is Latin for threshold, so subliminal means below the threshold. The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus a person detects 50 per cent of the time (Gustav Fechner, 1860). A subliminal stimulus is not consciously reported, yet it can still be processed. The best evidence comes from masked priming: Anthony Marcel (1983) flashed words so briefly, followed by a pattern mask, that people could not say what they had seen, yet an unseen word still speeded their responses to a related word. Such effects are real but small and short-lived. The famous claim of James Vicary (1957) that flashing 'Eat popcorn' and 'Drink Coca-Cola' during a film raised cinema sales was a hoax that he admitted in 1962, and there is no good evidence that subliminal advertising changes what people buy. Example: Riya sees a smiling face for a few milliseconds, instantly masked; she reports seeing nothing, but then rates an unfamiliar Chinese character a little more favourably. Option c is right because the stem defines subliminal perception exactly. Exam trap: a and b are one and the same thing, the just noticeable difference or difference threshold (Ernst Weber, 1834), which concerns noticing a change between two stimuli, not perceiving a stimulus below the absolute threshold.", "traps": {"a": "The just noticeable difference is the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect half the time (Ernst Weber's law); it concerns differences between stimuli, not stimuli below the absolute threshold.", "b": "The difference threshold is simply another name for the just noticeable difference, so it fails for the same reason: it is about detecting a change, not unconscious perception of very weak stimuli.", "d": "Response bias, from signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966), is a person's tendency to say 'yes' or 'no' whatever the signal, shaped by expectations and motives; it is not unconscious detection of weak stimuli."}}, {"q": "Who has emphasized the linguistic relativity hypothesis?", "concept": "The answer is d: the linguistic relativity hypothesis is tied to Benjamin Lee Whorf (1956, Language, Thought, and Reality, published after his death), who built on the ideas of his teacher Edward Sapir (1929), so it is also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. It claims that the language you speak shapes how you think about and perceive the world. It comes in two strengths. The strong form, linguistic determinism, says language fixes thought, so speakers of very different languages live in different mental worlds. The weak form, linguistic relativity proper, says language only nudges what we notice, remember and find easy to think about. Whorf argued, for example, that the Hopi language treats time differently from English, so Hopi speakers think about time differently. Later research challenged the strong form. Eleanor Rosch (1972, 1973) studied the Dani of New Guinea, whose language has only two basic colour terms, and found they still learned and remembered the focal colours more easily than other shades, much as English speakers do. Weaker effects of language on memory and attention do find support. Example: Kabir speaks a language with separate words for a mother's brother (mama) and a father's brother (chacha), so he may notice that family distinction more readily than an English speaker who says only \"uncle\". Option d is right because Whorf is the name most closely tied to the claim that language shapes thought. Exam trap: Noam Chomsky and Steven Pinker are famous language theorists, but both argue for a universal, inborn capacity for language that does not lock thought into a particular language, the reverse of Whorf.", "traps": {"a": "Noam Chomsky (1957, Syntactic Structures; the language acquisition device) stressed an inborn universal grammar shared by all humans, not the idea that each language moulds thought differently.", "b": "Jean Piaget held that thought develops first and that language reflects the child's cognitive stage, the reverse direction to linguistic relativity.", "c": "Steven Pinker (1994, The Language Instinct) treats language as an evolved instinct and is a well known critic of the Whorf hypothesis, arguing that people think in a language-independent 'mentalese'."}}, {"q": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for:", "concept": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) can be used to help assess eligibility for disability allowance, because establishing intellectual functioning is part of documenting a qualifying disability such as intellectual disability or cognitive impairment. The WAIS, originally developed by David Wechsler (the first version, the Wechsler-Bellevue, appeared in 1939, with the WAIS proper in 1955 and now in its fourth and fifth editions), is the most widely used individually administered adult intelligence test. It yields a Full Scale IQ along with index scores for verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed. Because a diagnosis of intellectual disability requires demonstrating significantly subaverage intellectual functioning (roughly an IQ below 70) alongside deficits in adaptive behavior, a standardized IQ measure like the WAIS provides exactly the objective intellectual data that disability determinations require. The question is testing whether the student understands the practical, applied use of intelligence testing in clinical and medico-legal contexts: an IQ score can substantiate a claim for a disability benefit by quantifying cognitive limitation. The other options describe contexts where an adult intelligence test is either irrelevant or the wrong instrument entirely.", "traps": {"a": "Housing benefit is determined by income and financial need, not by intellectual functioning, so an IQ test is irrelevant to that decision.", "c": "Suicide risk is assessed through clinical interview and risk-screening instruments measuring mood, hopelessness, and intent, not through an intelligence test.", "d": "Special educational needs concern children and are assessed with child-focused tools; the WAIS is for adults (the WISC is the child version), so it does not fit this context."}}, {"q": "The first attempt for standardization of mental tests in India was made by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first attempt to standardise a mental test in India was made by C. Herbert Rice (1922). Standardisation means giving a test to a large, representative sample under uniform conditions, so that norms can be built and any one score can be read against them. Intelligence testing began with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) in Paris, and Binet-type scales soon spread across the world. In India, C. Herbert Rice, working in Lahore, began adapting the Binet scale for Indian children in 1922, work that produced the Hindustani Binet Performance Point Scale. As its name says, the scale put Binet-type items into Hindustani, added performance tasks and scored the whole by points, and it was tried out on Indian school children to give local norms. That makes it the first Indian adaptation and standardisation of a Binet-type test. Later Indian work built on it. V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test), and S. M. Mohsin and Sohan Lal developed Hindi group tests of general mental ability. Example: if Meera's teacher wants to know whether Meera's score is high, she needs norms from Indian children like Meera, and Rice's scale was the first to offer them. Option c is right because Rice's work, begun in 1922, came before the work of Kamat, Mohsin and Sohan Lal. Exam trap: the Binet-Kamat test is the best known Indian Binet, so it tempts as 'the first'; it was a later revision, not the first attempt.", "traps": {"a": "S. M. Mohsin built a Hindi group test of general intelligence; it is an important Indian test but came well after Rice's 1922 scale.", "b": "V. V. Kamat revised the Binet scale for Marathi and Kannada speakers (the Binet-Kamat test); it is the best known Indian Binet revision, but it followed Rice's work.", "d": "Sohan Lal developed a Hindi group test of general mental ability later; it is a standard Indian group test, not the first attempt at standardisation."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are not Factor Analytically derived personality inventories? 1. 16 PF 2. Comrey Personality Scales 3. Jenkins Activity Survey 4. Edwards Personal Preference Schedule", "concept": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey (3) and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (4) are not factor-analytically derived. Personality inventories are built in four main ways. The logical-content strategy writes items whose content obviously fits the trait; the theoretical strategy writes items for the constructs of a theory; the empirical criterion-group strategy keeps whatever items separate a criterion group from controls; and the factor-analytic strategy correlates many items, keeps those that load on each factor and names the factors afterwards. Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (1949) came from factor analyses of trait terms, and Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970; 180 items, eight factors such as trust and orderliness) were built the same way, so 1 and 2 are factor-analytic. The Jenkins Activity Survey of C. David Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Ray Rosenman measures the Type A behaviour pattern; its Type A scale items were chosen because they agreed with Structured Interview classifications, a criterion approach. Allen Edwards's Edwards Personal Preference Schedule (revised edition, 1959) is theory-based: it measures 15 needs from Henry Murray's (1938) list, using forced-choice pairs matched for social desirability. Example: Arjun gives 300 new items to 1,000 students and keeps only the clusters that emerge from factor analysis; that is Cattell's and Comrey's route, not Edwards's. Option b is right because 3 and 4 are the only two not built by factor analysis. Exam trap: the EPPS controls social desirability so carefully that it looks statistical, but its items were written for Murray's need theory. Key note: the Jenkins Activity Survey also reports subscales (speed and impatience, job involvement, hard-driving competitiveness) found by factor analysis, but its main Type A scale was built to match interview classifications, so it is not classed as a factor-analytic inventory; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 and 2 are exactly the two inventories that are factor-analytic (Cattell's 16PF and Comrey's scales), so this option names the opposite of what the stem asks.", "c": "1 and 2 are factor-analytic, so they cannot be on a 'not factor-analytic' list; only 3 in this set is correct, and 4 is left out.", "d": "2, the Comrey Personality Scales (Andrew Comrey, 1970), was derived by factor analysis, so it does not belong; 3 and 4 are correct."}}, {"q": "The Minnesota Multiphasic personality inventory yields score on how many clinical scales?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) yields scores on 10 clinical scales. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI at the University of Minnesota by empirical criterion keying: an item went onto a scale if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. The original test had 550 true-false items; the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567. The 10 clinical scales are known by number: 1 Hypochondriasis, 2 Depression, 3 Hysteria, 4 Psychopathic Deviate, 5 Masculinity-Femininity, 6 Paranoia, 7 Psychasthenia, 8 Schizophrenia, 9 Hypomania and 0 Social Introversion. Eight were keyed against psychiatric groups; scales 5 and 0 were built later from other criterion groups. Scales 1, 2 and 3 form the 'neurotic triad', and 6, 7, 8 and 9 the 'psychotic tetrad'. Scores are T scores with a mean of 50 and an SD of 10; on the MMPI-2 a T of 65 or more is clinically significant, and clinicians read the profile as a pattern, often by two-point code types. Separate validity scales, ? (cannot say), L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (correction), check for faking or careless answering. Example: Rakesh's profile peaks on scales 2 and 7, a 2-7 code type typical of anxious depression. Option c is right because the standard MMPI and MMPI-2 profile has exactly ten clinical scales. Exam trap: 13 is the number of 'basic scales', the 10 clinical scales plus the 3 validity scales L, F and K, and the MMPI-2-RF (Yossef Ben-Porath and Auke Tellegen, 2008) has 9 Restructured Clinical scales. None of 12, 15 or 11 is the clinical-scale count.", "traps": {"a": "Twelve is not a recognised count of MMPI clinical scales. The standard profile shows 10 clinical scales, with the validity scales (?, L, F, K) plotted separately.", "b": "Fifteen is the number of MMPI-2 content scales (James Butcher and colleagues, 1990), which group items by obvious content. They are a different family from the 10 clinical scales.", "d": "Eleven is a near-miss: the clinical scales are numbered 1 to 9 and then 0, which makes ten, not eleven. Validity scales such as L, F and K are counted separately."}}, {"q": "Projective tests originated from:", "concept": "The answer is a: projective tests originated from the idea of Sigmund Freud (1896) that behaviour is driven by unconscious motives. In his early papers Freud described projection as a defence in which a person attributes his or her own unacceptable feelings or wishes to others. More broadly, psychoanalysis held that unconscious motives shape everything we do, including how we see ambiguous things. Lawrence Frank (1939) turned this into the projective hypothesis and coined the term 'projective methods': when people meet an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus with minimal instructions, they impose their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing on it, revealing what they could not or would not report directly. Hence the tests: Hermann Rorschach's (1921) ten inkblots, Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray's (1935) Thematic Apperception Test of ambiguous pictures, sentence completion and drawing tests. Rorschach did not stumble on his blots; he deliberately designed and selected them, perhaps inspired by klecksography, a popular game of making pictures from blots. Projective tests are prized for clinical richness but criticised for weak reliability and validity; John Exner's (1974) Comprehensive System was a major attempt to standardise Rorschach scoring. Example: shown a picture of a boy staring at a violin, Riya tells a story about a child forced to practise by a harsh father; the clinician wonders whether she is projecting her own feelings about pressure at home. Option a is right because the logic of projection and hidden motives comes from Freud's psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung is a tempting near miss because his word association test (from 1904) was an early projective-style method, but option b names his theory of psychological types, which led to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, an objective self-report inventory.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Jung's theory of psychological types (1921: introversion, extraversion and four functions) gave rise to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, a self-report inventory; his word association test was projective in spirit, but the types theory was not the origin of projective tests.", "c": "Tests that avoid language are non-verbal or culture-fair tests, such as the Army Beta (1917) and John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938); they are ability tests, not projective personality tests.", "d": "This is a myth: Hermann Rorschach deliberately created and selected his inkblots (published 1921), possibly inspired by klecksography, the game of making pictures from blots, not by an accidental spill on a test booklet."}}, {"q": "In administering the Rorschach, the inquiry refers to that part of the examination in which:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach, the inquiry is the part of the examination in which the cards are shown to the test taker for the second time. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published ten inkblots: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) administration has two main phases. In the response phase (free association) the examiner hands over the cards one at a time in a fixed order, asks 'What might this be?', and records every response word for word without suggesting answers. In the inquiry phase, after all ten cards, the examiner goes back through each card and each response to learn where on the blot it was seen (location), what made it look that way, such as form, colour, shading or movement (determinants), and what it was (content), using non-leading questions such as 'What made it look like that?'. An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow, in which the examiner asks directly about popular responses or determinants the person never used. Option c is right because the inquiry depends on re-exposing the same cards so that each response can be scored. For example, if Riya said 'a bat' to Card I, in the inquiry the examiner hands her Card I again and asks her to show where the bat is and what made it look like one. Exam trap: option b, the first exposure, is the response phase; the inquiry always comes after it and never uses leading questions.", "traps": {"a": "Demographic and background information is collected in the interview before testing begins; it is not part of the Rorschach inquiry.", "b": "The first exposure of the cards is the response (free association) phase, in which the test taker says what each blot might be; the inquiry follows it.", "d": "The examiner answers the test taker's questions only briefly and non-directively (for instance 'It's up to you'); the inquiry is the examiner's structured questioning about location and determinants, not an open question period."}}, {"q": "Which is not a projective test", "concept": "The intended answer is the word association test, though this is a somewhat debatable item and deserves care. Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli onto which a person projects unconscious thoughts and feelings. The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach), the Thematic Apperception Test or TAT (developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, in which the person tells stories about ambiguous pictures), and the sentence completion test (such as the Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank by Julian Rotter, in which the person finishes stems like 'I feel...') are all classic projective techniques. The word association test, in which a person responds to a stimulus word with the first word that comes to mind, was developed by Francis Galton and used clinically by Carl Gustav Jung to detect emotionally charged complexes, and it is often itself classified as a projective technique. However, in many entrance-exam answer keys the word association test is treated as the odd one out, being regarded as more of a controlled association or diagnostic-of-complexes method rather than a full storytelling or interpretive projective task, so it is the intended answer here. (The item is contested: word association is arguably projective too.) This item tests classification of projective techniques, and the examiners intend word association as the exception.", "traps": {"b": "The Rorschach inkblot test is the classic projective test using ambiguous inkblots, so it is clearly projective and not the exception.", "c": "The Thematic Apperception Test asks people to tell stories about ambiguous pictures, a hallmark projective technique, so it is not the odd one out.", "d": "The sentence completion test presents incomplete sentences for the person to finish, projecting inner attitudes, so it is a projective technique."}}, {"q": "The term social loafing refers to:", "concept": "The answer is c: social loafing means people work harder individually than in groups. Social loafing is the drop in effort that occurs when people's contributions are pooled in a group, compared with when each works alone and can be identified. The first evidence came from the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913): when men pulled on a rope, the group's total pull was less than the sum of the members pulling separately, and each person's share fell as the group grew. Part of this could be poor coordination, but Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) showed that blindfolded people who merely believed others were pulling with them also pulled less, so motivation drops too. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: blindfolded students wearing headphones clapped and shouted less loudly per person in supposed groups than alone. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard when they believe their effort matters and will be noticed. Loafing shrinks when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful or the group is close-knit. Example: in Riya's four-member college project, two members contribute little because the teacher grades only the final report; when the teacher asks each student to submit a named section, everyone works harder. Option c is right because it captures the essence: less effort in the group than alone. Exam trap: option a uses the everyday meaning of 'loafing' (idling), and option d describes social facilitation, a different group effect.", "traps": {"a": "Playing when expected to be working is the everyday sense of 'loafing' (idling or slacking); the social psychology term is specifically about reduced effort when efforts are pooled in a group.", "b": "Working harder in groups than alone is the reverse of social loafing; it resembles social compensation (Kipling Williams and Steven Karau, 1991), where people work harder to make up for weak teammates.", "d": "The opposite of social inhibition is social facilitation, better performance on simple tasks in the presence of others (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965), not loafing."}}, {"q": "People are likely to invest less effort in a task when they are working with others. What is this phenomenon called?", "concept": "The answer is b: investing less effort when working with others on a shared task is social loafing. Social loafing is the tendency of individuals to exert less effort when their contributions are pooled into a group product than when they work alone. Its forerunner was the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913), who found that each person pulled a rope less hard as the team grew. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: students asked to clap or shout as loudly as they could made less noise per person in groups, even when they only believed that others were shouting with them, which showed that motivation was lost, not just coordination. Loafing happens because individual effort cannot be identified or evaluated, responsibility is spread across members, and people feel their own effort makes little difference. It shrinks when each person's output is identifiable, the task matters to them, the group is cohesive or each member has a distinct role. Example: in a group project, Kabir does very little because he knows the teacher will mark only the final report, not each member's part. Option b is right because the stem describes reduced individual effort in a group, the definition of social loafing. Exam trap: social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965) is the look-alike, but it runs the other way: the presence of others improves performance on simple, well-learned tasks, especially when each person's effort is visible.", "traps": {"a": "Social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965) is better performance on simple or well-learned tasks, and worse on complex ones, when others are present; it is about arousal from an audience or co-workers, not reduced effort on a pooled task.", "c": "Deindividuation (Leon Festinger and colleagues, 1952; Philip Zimbardo, 1969) is the loss of self-awareness and restraint in an anonymous crowd, which can release impulsive or aggressive acts; it is about losing identity, not slacking on a group task.", "d": "The sleeper effect (Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss, 1951) is the delayed rise in persuasion from a low-credibility source as the source is forgotten; it is an attitude change effect, not a group performance effect."}}, {"q": "Cognitive dissonance theory was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive dissonance theory was developed by Leon Festinger. Festinger (1919 to 1989), an American social psychologist and a student of Kurt Lewin, proposed it in A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance (1957). Dissonance is the uncomfortable tension of holding two inconsistent cognitions, especially when behaviour contradicts an attitude. Because the tension is unpleasant, people try to reduce it: by changing the attitude, changing the behaviour, adding consonant cognitions or trivialising the inconsistency. Dissonance grows with the importance of the cognitions and the ratio of dissonant to consonant ones. The classic test is Festinger and James Carlsmith's (1959) study: students did a boring task, then were paid either $1 or $20 to tell the next participant it was fun. The $1 group later rated the task as more enjoyable, because $1 was too little to justify lying, so they changed their attitude to fit their behaviour; the $20 group had an external justification. Other paradigms include effort justification (Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills, 1959) and post-decision dissonance (Jack Brehm, 1956). Example: a smoker who knows smoking causes cancer tells himself 'my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety'. Option a is right because Festinger named and formulated cognitive dissonance theory. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's self-perception theory (1967) explains the same $1 and $20 result without any tension, so if a question asks for the rival explanation of dissonance findings, the answer is Bem, not Festinger.", "traps": {"b": "Albert Bandura developed social learning theory (renamed social cognitive theory in 1986), with observational learning and self-efficacy (1977); he did not propose dissonance theory.", "c": "Albert Ellis developed rational emotive behaviour therapy (begun in 1955) and the ABC model of irrational beliefs, a clinical theory rather than a social psychology theory of attitude change.", "d": "Jean Piaget's disequilibrium, the conflict between a scheme and experience, sounds like dissonance, but it belongs to his theory of cognitive development, not to attitude change."}}, {"q": "According to psychodynamic theory, individuals are sometimes motivated to respond to the world through the perspectives they have learnt from other important people in their developmental past. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: this is object relations theory. In psychodynamic language an 'object' is not a thing but a person, or part of a person, towards whom feelings and wishes are directed, usually the mother or another early caregiver. Object relations theory holds that the infant takes in (internalises) mental pictures of the self and of important others from early relationships, along with the feelings that link them. These internal objects then act as templates: later in life the person sees and responds to partners, bosses and therapists through the perspectives learnt from those early figures. The school grew in Britain after Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960) described splitting of the world into good and bad objects and the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions; W. R. D. Fairbairn (1952) argued that the infant is object-seeking rather than pleasure-seeking; Donald Winnicott (1953) described the transitional object and the good-enough mother; Otto Kernberg (1975) linked split internal object relations to borderline personality organisation. Example: Kabir grew up with a harsh, critical father, and as an adult he hears criticism in every neutral remark from his manager, reacting to the internal father image rather than to the manager. Option c is right because the stem describes exactly this internalised, relationship-based template learnt from important others. Exam trap: 'object permanence' shares the word 'object' but belongs to Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) sensorimotor stage, where the infant learns that things go on existing when out of sight.", "traps": {"a": "Object permanence is Jean Piaget's sensorimotor achievement (knowing that things exist when unseen); it is a cognitive milestone, not a psychodynamic theory of relating to others.", "b": "'Distorted perspective theory' is not a recognised psychodynamic theory; it is a made-up distractor built from the stem's word 'perspectives'.", "d": "Ego defence refers to defence mechanisms such as repression and projection, set out by Anna Freud (1936); they protect the ego from anxiety rather than carry learnt templates of important others."}}, {"q": "Replacing the fragmented self with the cohesive self is the primary developmental and therapeutic goal of:", "concept": "The answer is b: replacing a fragmented self with a cohesive self is the central developmental and therapeutic goal of Heinz Kohut's self psychology. Heinz Kohut (1971), in The Analysis of the Self and later The Restoration of the Self (1977), argued that narcissism has its own healthy line of development, not only a pathological one. The child's self grows through selfobjects: people experienced as part of the self because they perform functions the child cannot yet perform alone. Three selfobject needs must be met: mirroring (the parent's delighted response that confirms the child's 'Look at me!'), idealising (merging with a calm, strong parent) and twinship (feeling essentially alike with others). When parents respond well but imperfectly, each bearable failure lets the child take over the soothing function, which Kohut called transmuting internalisation, and this builds a cohesive self. When mirroring and idealising fail chronically, the self stays fragmented and enfeebled, and narcissistic problems follow. In therapy the analyst offers empathic attunement; the client forms selfobject transferences (mirror, idealising, twinship), and the therapist's small, understood empathic failures repeat the healthy process, so the self becomes cohesive. Imagine Kabir, who feels he falls apart whenever his boss ignores him; a Kohutian therapist would read this as a fragile, fragmenting self and work through empathy to strengthen its cohesion. Option b is right because 'fragmented self' and 'cohesive self' are Kohut's signature terms. Exam trap: Otto Kernberg (1975) also wrote on narcissism and borderline states, so a is the near miss; but Kernberg's goal is integrating split good and bad images of self and others, not building self cohesion through selfobjects.", "traps": {"a": "Otto Kernberg (1975) described borderline personality organisation and pathological narcissism built on splitting; his transference-focused psychotherapy aims to integrate split good and bad images through confrontation and interpretation, not Kohut's empathic building of a cohesive self.", "c": "David Malan, at the Tavistock Clinic, developed brief dynamic psychotherapy around the triangle of conflict (defence, anxiety, hidden feeling) and the triangle of person (others, therapist, parents); his focus is one circumscribed conflict, not self cohesion.", "d": "Mardi Horowitz described stress response syndromes (alternating intrusion and avoidance after trauma) and co-developed the Impact of Event Scale (1979); his work concerns processing traumatic events, not the fragmented self."}}, {"q": "The concept of 'borderline personality organization' was given by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the concept of borderline personality organisation was given by Otto Kernberg (1967, 1975). Otto Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, combined Melanie Klein's (1946) object relations theory with ego psychology. He described three levels of personality organisation, judged on identity, level of defences and reality testing. Neurotic organisation has an integrated identity, higher-level defences such as repression, and intact reality testing. Borderline organisation has identity diffusion (an unstable, contradictory sense of self and others), primitive defences centred on splitting (with projective identification, idealisation, devaluation, denial and omnipotence), and reality testing that is mostly intact but can slip under stress. Psychotic organisation has identity diffusion, primitive defences and a loss of reality testing. Borderline organisation is wider than the DSM diagnosis of borderline personality disorder: it also takes in narcissistic, antisocial, paranoid and schizoid personalities. Otto Kernberg traced it to intense early aggression that keeps 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others apart, and later developed transference-focused psychotherapy to treat it. Example: Meera praises her new manager as the best person she has ever worked with, then, after one critical remark, calls her cruel and worthless, yet she stays fully in touch with reality; this is splitting with intact reality testing, the borderline level. Option d is right because borderline personality organisation is Otto Kernberg's structural model. Exam trap: Heinz Kohut (1971) and Otto Kernberg both wrote on narcissism in the 1970s and famously disagreed, and Margaret Mahler's (1975) rapprochement crisis is often linked to the origins of borderline problems, so all three names look plausible; the structural term itself is Otto Kernberg's alone.", "traps": {"a": "Heinz Kohut (1971) founded self psychology, which explains narcissism as a deficit left by failures of parental mirroring and idealisation (selfobjects); he wrote on narcissistic personality, not borderline organisation.", "b": "Margaret Mahler (1975) described separation-individuation, the infant's 'psychological birth' through subphases such as rapprochement; her work is used to explain borderline problems, but the term borderline personality organisation is not hers.", "c": "Carl Jung (1921) founded analytical psychology, with archetypes and psychological types; he did not describe levels of personality organisation."}}, {"q": "In the early work of Hartshorne and May, designed to assess children's moral behavior, it was found that:", "concept": "The answer is b: Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928) found that children's honesty depended heavily on the situation, so the same child could cheat in one setting and stay honest in another that tested the same moral principle. In the Character Education Inquiry at Teachers College, Columbia University, reported in Studies in Deceit, they asked a simple question: is honesty a general trait that a child carries everywhere? They gave thousands of American schoolchildren disguised chances to lie, cheat and steal in class tests, party games, athletic contests and home tasks: for instance, marking their own test papers when they could secretly change answers, or keeping coins from a box. Almost every child cheated somewhere, and few cheated everywhere. Honesty in one situation predicted honesty in another only weakly, and knowing the moral rule, or attending character education or Sunday school, barely predicted conduct. What mattered was the situation: the risk of being caught, the effort needed and what classmates were doing. They called this the doctrine of specificity. Walter Mischel (1968) later built on it in his critique of trait consistency, which started the person-situation debate; Seymour Epstein (1979) replied that behaviour averaged over many occasions is far more stable. Example: Riya peeks at a classmate's answer sheet when the teacher steps out, yet on the way home returns the extra change a shopkeeper gave her by mistake. Option b is right because it states the inquiry's central finding of situation-specific (situational) morality. Exam trap: the absolute words \"always\" and \"never\" in a and c describe exactly the consistent moral character that the study failed to find.", "traps": {"a": "This is the opposite of the finding: no group of \"moral\" children kept to recognised standards in every situation, and honesty in one test predicted honesty in the next only weakly.", "c": "Nearly every child cheated somewhere when the risk of being caught was low, so a group of children who never cheat did not exist; the doctrine of specificity says conduct shifts with the situation.", "d": "The inquiry's conclusion was about situations, not sex differences; the claim that one sex has a weaker conscience (superego) echoes Sigmund Freud (1925), not Hartshorne and May."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five Traits?", "concept": "The correct answer is sense of humour, which is NOT one of the Big Five traits. The Big Five, or five-factor model, developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa, Robert McCrae, and Lewis Goldberg, describes personality in terms of five broad dimensions summarised by the acronym OCEAN: Openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), Extraversion (outgoing and energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (compassionate and cooperative versus antagonistic), and Neuroticism (emotionally reactive and anxious versus stable). Among the options, openness to experience (b), conscientiousness (c), and extraversion (d) are all genuine factors within this model. Sense of humour (a), while an important and appealing personal quality, is not one of the five factors; it is a specific characteristic that may relate loosely to traits like extraversion or openness but does not constitute a distinct dimension in the model. The trap lies in the fact that sense of humour sounds like a plausible personality descriptor, but the Big Five is a specific empirical taxonomy, and only the five OCEAN dimensions qualify. Knowing the exact five factors matters because the Big Five is the most widely researched and cross-culturally validated framework in personality psychology, and students must be able to distinguish the genuine factors from everyday trait words that do not belong to the model.", "traps": {"b": "Openness to experience is a genuine Big Five trait, so it is not the answer.", "c": "Conscientiousness is one of the five factors, so it is not the answer.", "d": "Extraversion is a core Big Five dimension, so it is not the answer."}}, {"q": "The 16PF test is based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: the 16PF is based on Raymond Cattell's theory. Raymond Cattell, a British-born psychologist who worked in the United States, built his trait theory by factor analysis, a statistical method that finds the few underlying factors that explain the correlations among many measures. He started from the trait words Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had collected from the dictionary, merged synonyms, and factor-analysed three kinds of data: L-data (life records and observer ratings), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (behaviour on objective tests). He separated surface traits, clusters of behaviour that go together, from source traits, the deeper underlying causes. He found 16 source traits, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire, the 16PF (1949), such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance and liveliness, each scored as a bipolar dimension. Factor-analysing the 16 again gives broader second-order (global) factors such as extraversion and anxiety. Example: Riya's 16PF profile shows high warmth and liveliness but low emotional stability, which helps her counsellor see a sociable young woman who is easily upset under pressure. Option b is right because Cattell designed the 16PF to measure the source traits of his own factor-analytic theory. Exam trap: Hans Eysenck (a) also used factor analysis, but he arrived at only three broad dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism), measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975); Gordon Allport (c) preferred the idiographic study of the individual and did not build the 16PF.", "traps": {"a": "Hans Eysenck's theory has three broad, biologically based dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism), measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975); it is a rival factor model, not the basis of the 16PF.", "c": "Gordon Allport (1937) described cardinal, central and secondary traits and favoured the idiographic study of the unique individual; he did not use factor analysis to derive 16 source traits.", "d": "The 16PF has a clear theoretical source, Cattell's factor-analytic trait theory, so 'none of the above' does not fit."}}, {"q": "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are ______", "concept": "The answer is a: the two parts of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. The nervous system splits into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system. The peripheral system has a somatic branch, which carries sensation and voluntary movement of skeletal muscles, and an autonomic branch, which runs the heart, glands, blood vessels and the smooth muscle of the gut largely without conscious control. The British physiologist John Newport Langley named the autonomic nervous system in 1898. Its sympathetic division, arising from the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, prepares the body for action in what Walter B. Cannon called the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils dilate, digestion slows and glucose is released. The parasympathetic division, arising from the brainstem and sacral cord, does the rest-and-digest work: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and restarts digestion. Picture a near-miss on a scooter: the pounding heart is sympathetic, and the slow calming afterwards is parasympathetic. Option a is right because these two divisions are the standard textbook parts of the autonomic system. Exam trap: voluntary versus involuntary is the split between somatic and autonomic, not a division within the autonomic system; some modern texts add a third, enteric division in the gut wall, but exam keys expect sympathetic and parasympathetic.", "traps": {"b": "Voluntary versus involuntary describes the difference between the somatic nervous system (voluntary skeletal muscle) and the autonomic nervous system as a whole, not the parts within the autonomic system.", "c": "The thalamus and hypothalamus are the two main parts of the diencephalon; the hypothalamus controls the autonomic system, but both belong to the central nervous system.", "d": "Cerebrospinal and cortical are not autonomic divisions at all: cerebrospinal refers to the brain and spinal cord (the central nervous system) and cortical to the cerebral cortex."}}, {"q": "Find out the correct answers from the codes given below: Topographical organization: 1. Applies to sensory cortical areas 2. High degree of topographical organisation shows in the auditory pathway 3. Fibres in this pathway originate from the ganglion cells of the retina 4. Fibres then synapse at the lateral geniculate body of the hypothalamus", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 1 and 3 are correct. Topographical (topographic) organisation means that neighbouring points on a sensory surface are represented by neighbouring points in the brain, so each sensory cortex carries an orderly map: retinotopic in vision (the primary visual cortex maps the retina, with the small fovea given a disproportionately large share), tonotopic in hearing (each sound frequency has its own place in auditory cortex) and somatotopic in touch (the body map, or homunculus, of the postcentral gyrus). So statement 1, that it applies to sensory cortical areas, is true. Statement 3 describes the visual pathway: the fibres of the optic nerve are the axons of the retina's ganglion cells, which carry the signal out of the eye, so it is true. Statement 4 is false: optic tract fibres synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (body) of the thalamus, not of the hypothalamus, before the optic radiations carry the signal to the striate cortex (area 17). The hypothalamus receives only a small retinal input, to its suprachiasmatic nucleus, for setting circadian rhythms. Example: a small stroke in one spot of Arjun's left primary visual cortex blinds one matching patch of his right visual field, because the map keeps retinal neighbours together. Option c is right because 1 and 3 are true and 4 is false. Exam trap: the lateral geniculate body sounds hypothalamic because both lie in the diencephalon, but the geniculate bodies are thalamic relays (lateral for vision, medial for hearing). Key note: statement 2 names the auditory pathway, which is tonotopic in reality, but statement 3 then says 'this pathway' starts in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot both describe the same pathway; the paired statements describe the visual pathway and c (1 and 3) is the only fully correct set; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 and 3 are right, but 4 is false: the lateral geniculate body belongs to the thalamus, not the hypothalamus.", "b": "2 places the pathway in hearing, yet 3 says 'this pathway' begins in the retina, so 2 and 3 cannot describe the same pathway (hearing relays through the medial geniculate nucleus); this option also leaves out the true statement 1.", "d": "1 is true, but 4 is false because the lateral geniculate body is a thalamic relay nucleus, not part of the hypothalamus."}}, {"q": "Which area is known as the satiety centre?", "concept": "Twentieth-century hunger research produced the dual-centre model, which entrance exams still test. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that bilateral lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) in rats caused hyperphagia (overeating) and marked obesity; later studies showed that stimulating the VMH stopped eating. Bal Anand and John Brobeck (1951) found the reverse for the lateral hypothalamus (LH): lesions produced aphagia (refusal to eat), sometimes fatal without tube feeding; later work added adipsia and showed that LH stimulation made sated animals eat. Eliot Stellar (1954) combined these into the theory that the LH is the hunger or feeding centre and the VMH is the satiety centre. VMH obesity has a dynamic phase of rapid weight gain, then a static phase in which the animal defends its new, higher weight; lesioned rats also become finicky eaters. John P. J. Pinel's Biopsychology warns that the model is too simple: VMH lesions raise insulin and fat storage, part of their effect comes from damage to nearby structures such as the paraventricular nucleus, and LH lesions cause wider motor problems. Current models stress the arcuate nucleus, where leptin from fat cells and stomach ghrelin (a hunger signal) act, while gut cholecystokinin signals satiety mainly via the vagus nerve. Memory aid: lesion the Lateral and the animal Lacks appetite; lesion the Ventromedial and it Very Much overeats. Exam neighbours: the suprachiasmatic nucleus is the circadian clock, and the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make vasopressin and oxytocin. Option b is right because the ventromedial hypothalamus is the structure classically named the satiety centre.", "traps": {"a": "The lateral hypothalamus is the classic hunger or feeding centre. Bal Anand and John Brobeck (1951) showed that lesioning it caused aphagia, and later studies showed that stimulating it made sated rats start eating. It tempts because it sits right beside the ventromedial hypothalamus and the two are always taught as a pair, so students swap them. Remember that lateral lesions mean lack of eating, the opposite of what a satiety centre lesion does.", "c": "The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain: the two cerebral hemispheres with their cortex, white matter and deep nuclei, responsible for perception, voluntary movement, language and thought. Cortical areas such as the insula and orbitofrontal cortex process taste and food reward, but no cerebral region is called the satiety centre. It tempts only as the most familiar structure in the list; the classical feeding centres are subcortical, in the hypothalamus.", "d": "The basal ganglia (caudate nucleus, putamen and globus pallidus, working in loops with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra) control the initiation and smoothness of movement and habit learning. Loss of their dopamine input causes Parkinson's disease, and degeneration of the striatum causes Huntington's disease. The ventral striatum handles food reward, which may tempt, but reward and motivation are not the satiety signal that ends a meal; that belongs to the hypothalamus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following chemical substances is NOT classified as an opioid?", "concept": "The answer is a: cocaine is not an opioid; it is a stimulant. Opioids are substances that act on the brain's opioid receptors, chiefly the mu receptor, the same receptors used by the body's own endorphins and enkephalins. They relieve pain, suppress coughing and cause euphoria and drowsiness, and in overdose they stop breathing; naloxone reverses an overdose by blocking the receptors. Opioids come in three groups. Natural opiates are taken from the sap of the opium poppy: morphine and codeine, a weaker relative used for cough and mild pain. Semi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically changing a natural one: heroin is diacetylmorphine, morphine with two acetyl groups added so that it enters the brain faster. Fully synthetic opioids, such as methadone and fentanyl, are built in the laboratory. Cocaine comes from a different plant, the coca leaf of South America. It blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (and serotonin), so these transmitters pile up in the synapse, giving alertness, confidence and euphoria, followed by a crash; heavy use can cause paranoia and cocaine psychosis. It is also a local anaesthetic, first used in eye surgery by Carl Koller (1884). Example: Arjun, who misuses codeine cough syrup, becomes drowsy with pinpoint pupils; his friend Kabir, who snorts cocaine at a party, becomes restless and talkative with dilated pupils. Option a is right because cocaine is a stimulant that works on monoamine reuptake, not on opioid receptors. Exam trap: in law, cocaine has long been grouped with heroin and morphine as a 'narcotic' drug, and 'cocaine' sounds like 'codeine', so students lump it with the opioids; pharmacologically the two classes have opposite effects.", "traps": {"b": "Codeine is a natural opiate alkaloid of the opium poppy, a weaker relative of morphine used in cough syrups and painkillers; the liver converts part of it to morphine, so it is a true opioid.", "c": "Heroin (diacetylmorphine) is a semi-synthetic opioid made by adding two acetyl groups to morphine; it acts at mu opioid receptors and is the classic opioid of dependence.", "d": "Morphine is the main natural alkaloid of opium and the reference opioid against which other painkillers are measured, so it cannot be the exception."}}, {"q": "Most current Alzheimer treatments work by altering levels of which neurotransmitter?", "concept": "The answer is d: most current Alzheimer treatments work by raising levels of acetylcholine. The cholinergic hypothesis, set out by Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982), grew from the finding that Alzheimer brains lose cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, at the base of the forebrain, which send acetylcholine to the cortex and hippocampus. Acetylcholine is vital for attention, learning and memory, so its loss fits the memory decline. Normally the enzyme acetylcholinesterase breaks acetylcholine down in the synapse soon after release. Cholinesterase inhibitors block that enzyme, so acetylcholine lingers longer and signals more strongly. Three are in wide use: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. They modestly improve or steady symptoms for a time in mild to moderate disease, but they do not stop the underlying plaques and tangles. The exception is memantine, used in moderate to severe disease, which blocks NMDA glutamate receptors and so protects neurons from excess glutamate stimulation. Newer antibody drugs that clear amyloid act on the disease process itself rather than on any transmitter. Example: Kabir's grandmother is diagnosed with mild Alzheimer's disease and started on donepezil; her neurologist explains that the tablet keeps more acetylcholine available at her synapses. Option d is right because the main drug class for Alzheimer's disease targets acetylcholine. Exam trap: dopamine (option a) is the transmitter targeted in Parkinson's disease (L-dopa) and schizophrenia, so students who mix up the neurodegenerative diseases pick it; GABA loss marks Huntington's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Dopamine is the target in Parkinson's disease (L-dopa replaces lost nigrostriatal dopamine) and in schizophrenia (antipsychotics block D2 receptors), not in standard Alzheimer treatment.", "b": "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory transmitter, boosted by benzodiazepines for anxiety; loss of GABA neurons in the striatum marks Huntington's disease, not Alzheimer's disease.", "c": "Noradrenaline, from the locus coeruleus, is linked to arousal and mood and is targeted by antidepressants such as SNRIs; Alzheimer drugs do not work mainly on it."}}, {"q": "What hormone secreted by the pineal gland increases when periods of darkness begin and promotes a feeling of drowsiness?", "concept": "The answer is b: melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, rises when darkness begins and promotes drowsiness. The pineal gland is a small, pine-cone-shaped gland near the centre of the brain; Rene Descartes (1649) thought it was the seat of the soul. Aaron Lerner (1958) and colleagues isolated its hormone, melatonin, from cattle pineal glands. Melatonin is made from serotonin and is released mainly at night. Its timing is set by the body's master clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus. Light falling on special melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract; in daylight the SCN keeps the pineal quiet, and as darkness falls the brake comes off, so melatonin climbs through the evening, peaks in the middle of the night and falls by morning. Rising melatonin lowers body temperature and alertness and signals 'night' to the body, so it works as a darkness hormone that opens the gate to sleep rather than as a strong sedative. Bright screens at night can suppress it and delay sleep, and low-dose melatonin is used to shift the clock in jet lag and delayed sleep phase. Example: Aditya scrolls on his phone until 2 a.m.; the bright light holds back his melatonin, so he lies awake even though he is tired. Option b is right because melatonin is the pineal hormone tied to darkness and drowsiness. Exam trap: cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, so it tempts; but cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, peaks in the early morning and helps wake us, the opposite timing.", "traps": {"a": "Testosterone is an androgen made mainly by the testes (with small amounts from the adrenal glands and ovaries); it governs male sexual development and libido, not sleep onset.", "c": "Cortisol, from the adrenal cortex under the HPA axis, peaks soon after waking (the cortisol awakening response) and promotes alertness and coping with stress.", "d": "Epinephrine (adrenaline), from the adrenal medulla, drives the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915), raising heart rate and arousal, the opposite of drowsiness."}}, {"q": "White matter is primarily composed of", "concept": "White matter gets its name from the pale, glossy appearance of myelin, the lipid-rich sheath wrapped around axons by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system (and by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system; Pio del Rio Hortega named oligodendroglia in 1921 and in 1922 likened them to Schwann cells as the myelin-forming cells of the CNS). Myelin acts as electrical insulation. Because current cannot leak across the insulated segments, the action potential effectively jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next, a process Ichiji Tasaki described as saltatory conduction. This dramatically speeds signalling and is why long-distance tracts, such as the corpus callosum or spinal columns, are white. Grey matter, by contrast, is where the computation happens: it is packed with neuronal cell bodies, dendrites, and synapses, and its lack of heavy myelination gives it a duller, greyish-brown tone. So the organising principle is simple: grey matter is where neurons integrate information; white matter is the myelinated wiring that carries the results elsewhere. When you read that a lesion is in white matter (as in multiple sclerosis, an autoimmune demyelinating disease), you should picture damaged insulation on axonal cables, producing slowed or blocked conduction rather than direct loss of cell bodies. For the exam, anchor on the phrase myelinated axons whenever white matter is described.", "traps": {"b": "Dendrites are generally not myelinated, and white matter is defined by axonal tracts rather than dendritic fields.", "c": "Cell bodies (somata) cluster in grey matter, not white matter, which is precisely the tissue that lacks the pale appearance of myelin.", "d": "Unmyelinated axons appear greyish and are found in grey matter; it is the fatty myelin sheath that gives white matter its colour."}}, {"q": "Choose the odd one for pattern of sympathetic activation during emotions:", "concept": "The answer is b: pupil constriction is the odd one out, because it is a parasympathetic response, while the other three are signs of sympathetic activation. The autonomic nervous system has two branches that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for action, the fight or flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). During strong emotions such as fear or anger it raises heart rate and blood pressure, speeds breathing and widens the airways, dilates the pupils (mydriasis) to let in more light, releases glucose for energy, slows digestion and increases sweating. Sweat is salty water that conducts electricity, so sweating lowers skin resistance (raises skin conductance); this galvanic skin response is one of the channels a polygraph records. The parasympathetic branch is the rest and digest system: it slows the heart, stimulates digestion and constricts the pupils (miosis) through fibres in the oculomotor nerve. Example: when a dog suddenly lunges at Meera on the street, her heart pounds, she breathes fast, her palms sweat and her pupils widen; once she is safe at home, parasympathetic activity slows her heart and her pupils return to normal. Option b is right because constriction of the pupil is the parasympathetic opposite of the sympathetic dilation seen in emotion. Exam trap: \"lowered skin resistance\" sounds like a decrease and so like calming, but it reflects more sweating and is a classic sign of sympathetic arousal.", "traps": {"a": "Increased heart rate is a core sympathetic effect that pumps more blood to the muscles for fight or flight, so it belongs in the pattern.", "c": "Lowered skin resistance comes from sympathetic activation of the sweat glands; it is the galvanic skin response used in lie detection, so it belongs.", "d": "Faster breathing, with widened airways, brings in more oxygen during sympathetic arousal, so it belongs in the pattern."}}, {"q": "A part of the brain that sends signals \"alert\" to higher centres of the brain in response to incoming message is:", "concept": "The answer is d: the reticular formation alerts the higher centres of the brain to incoming messages. The reticular formation ('reticulum' means little net) is a net-like column of neurons running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain. Sensory pathways heading for the cortex send side branches (collaterals) into it, so it samples nearly all incoming information. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), projects through the thalamus to the whole cortex and keeps it awake and alert. The classic evidence came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrically stimulating the brainstem reticular formation in a drowsy or anaesthetised cat changed the slow, synchronised EEG of sleep into the fast, desynchronised EEG of alert waking. Earlier, Frederic Bremer (1935) had shown that cutting the brainstem at the midbrain (cerveau isolé) left the forebrain in permanent sleep-like EEG, while cutting lower, between medulla and spinal cord (encéphale isolé), left normal sleep and waking. Damage to the RAS causes coma. The system also filters input, passing on novel or important signals and damping repetitive ones, and its descending fibres adjust muscle tone and reflexes. Example: Meera dozes through the drone of the fan in a lecture, but jolts awake the moment the teacher calls her name; her reticular formation passed on the important signal and roused her cortex. Option d is right because sending an 'alert' signal to the cortex in response to incoming input is the defining job of the reticular activating system. Exam trap: the limbic system and amygdala also respond to incoming signals, but they add emotional meaning; general arousal and wakefulness belong to the reticular formation.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala, an almond-shaped nucleus in the medial temporal lobe, attaches fear and emotional significance to stimuli (Joseph LeDoux's low road, 1996); it does not set the general arousal level of the cortex.", "b": "The hippocampus, in the medial temporal lobe, consolidates new declarative memories and spatial maps; Henry Molaison (H.M.), studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957), could not form new memories after its removal.", "c": "The limbic system, built on James Papez's circuit (1937) and named by Paul MacLean (1952), is a ring of structures for emotion and memory, not the brainstem network that alerts the cortex."}}, {"q": "According to Erik Erikson’s psychosocial theory, the key challenge in adolescence is :", "concept": "According to Erikson's psychosocial theory, the key challenge in adolescence is identity versus role confusion, option c. This is the fifth stage, occurring roughly between ages twelve and eighteen. The central task is for the adolescent to develop a coherent and stable sense of self, an identity, by integrating their various roles, values, beliefs, talents, and goals into a unified whole and answering the question of who they are and what they want to become. Adolescents experiment with different roles, ideologies, and relationships in a period Erikson called a psychosocial moratorium, a sanctioned time of exploration before committing to adult roles. Successful resolution yields a firm sense of identity and the ego strength of fidelity, the ability to commit to values and others. Failure produces role confusion, an unclear, fragmented, or unstable sense of self, uncertainty about one's place in the world. James Marcia later extended this into four identity statuses based on the presence of exploration and commitment: identity achievement, moratorium, foreclosure, and diffusion. The exam mapping is adolescence equals identity versus role confusion, making option c correct over the infancy, school-age, and toddler stages listed.", "traps": {"a": "Trust versus mistrust is the first stage, in infancy, concerning the dependability of caregivers, not the adolescent identity task.", "b": "Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage, in school-age children, concerning competence and mastery of skills, not adolescence.", "d": "Autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage, in toddlerhood, concerning self-control and independence, not adolescence."}}, {"q": "Elizabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of:", "concept": "The answer is d: Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969) is known for her research on death and dying. She was a Swiss-born psychiatrist who interviewed terminally ill patients at a Chicago hospital. In her book On Death and Dying she proposed five stages that people often pass through after learning that their illness is terminal: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live to see my son's wedding'), depression (grieving the losses to come) and acceptance (a calm, often quiet readiness). The mnemonic is DABDA. Her work changed medical care: it encouraged doctors to talk honestly with dying patients and supported the hospice movement, which Cicely Saunders (1967) had begun with St Christopher's Hospice in London. Later research found that people do not move through the stages in a fixed order; they skip, repeat or mix them, so the stages are best read as common reactions rather than a timetable. The model was also stretched, with little evidence, to the grief of the bereaved. Example: when Meera, 58, is told her cancer has spread, she first insists the scans are wrong (denial), later snaps at the nurses (anger), and weeks later promises to give up sweets if she can see her granddaughter born (bargaining). Option d is right because her classic book and five-stage model are about how people face their own death. Exam trap: coping with stress (option c) is the near miss, because dying is a stressor; but stress and coping research belongs to Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), not Kubler-Ross.", "traps": {"a": "Eating disorder research is linked with names such as Hilde Bruch (anorexia nervosa) and Gerald Russell (who named bulimia nervosa in 1979), not Elisabeth Kubler-Ross.", "b": "Adolescent behaviour research is linked with G. Stanley Hall (storm and stress, 1904) and Erik Erikson (identity), not Elisabeth Kubler-Ross.", "c": "Coping with stress is Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman's transactional model (appraisal, problem-focused and emotion-focused coping); facing death is a stressor, but Elisabeth Kubler-Ross studied the specific reactions of the dying."}}, {"q": "Which of the following expresses a correct sequence in cognitive development?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, mental representation appears first, at the end of infancy, then symbolic thought, then intuitive thought. Jean Piaget (1952) described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards), passed through in an invariant order. The sensorimotor stage has six substages. In the last one, at about 18 to 24 months, the child can for the first time represent objects and actions in the mind instead of only acting on them; Piaget called this the beginning of thought, and it makes deferred imitation and full object permanence possible. The preoperational stage is then divided into two substages. In the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4 years) the child lets one thing stand for another: words, pretend play and drawings. In the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7 years) the child asks endless \"why\" questions and reasons by intuition rather than logic, still unable to conserve or to explain answers. Example: at 20 months Arjun copies his father's shaving the next day (mental representation); at 3 he uses a banana as a phone (symbolic thought); at 5 he insists the taller glass holds more juice but cannot say why (intuitive thought). Option c is right because it is the only option that lists achievements in the order in which Piaget's child reaches them. Exam trap: each distractor mixes real Piagetian terms in the wrong positions. Voluntary movement and object permanence belong to infancy, so object permanence cannot follow intuitive thought; conservation is a concrete operational gain that cannot precede object permanence; and information processing is a separate theory, not a Piagetian stage.", "traps": {"a": "Voluntary (intentional) movement and object permanence are sensorimotor achievements of the first two years, while intuitive thought belongs to ages 4 to 7, so putting object permanence last reverses the order.", "b": "Concrete operations (7 to 11) come long after mental representation (about 18 to 24 months), and information processing is a separate approach modelled on the computer, not a step in Piaget's sequence.", "d": "Conservation is the hallmark of the concrete operational stage (7 to 11), so it cannot come before object permanence (infancy) or intuitive thought (4 to 7)."}}, {"q": "The term Clinical Psychology was first proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: Lightner Witmer first proposed the term clinical psychology. Lightner Witmer (1867 to 1956) studied with James McKeen Cattell at the University of Pennsylvania, took his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt (founder of the Leipzig laboratory, 1879) in 1892, and then returned to Pennsylvania. In March 1896 he opened the first psychological clinic there, after a schoolteacher brought him a boy who could not learn to spell. Later that year he described his 'clinical method' to the American Psychological Association, and in 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic, whose first issue carried his article 'Clinical Psychology', the first printed use of the name. His clinic worked mainly with children who had learning and behaviour problems, combining assessment with remedial teaching, so early clinical psychology was closer to what we now call school psychology and special education than to adult psychotherapy. For founding both the name and the first clinic, Witmer is called the father of clinical psychology. Option a is right because Witmer coined the term and set up the first clinic in which it was practised. Exam trap: Alfred Binet and James McKeen Cattell are famous for mental testing, which clinical psychologists later relied on heavily, so they look like founders of the field; but neither of them named it. Ernst Heinrich Weber is a psychophysicist with no link to clinics. Remember the pair: 1896, the first clinic; 1907, the name in print.", "traps": {"b": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, built the first practical intelligence test (1905) to identify Paris schoolchildren who needed special teaching; he founded modern intelligence testing, not the named field of clinical psychology.", "c": "James McKeen Cattell coined the term 'mental test' (1890) and measured reaction time and sensory abilities; he pioneered the study of individual differences and was Witmer's teacher, but he did not name clinical psychology.", "d": "Ernst Heinrich Weber was a German physiologist known for Weber's law (1834), that the just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the original stimulus; he belongs to psychophysics, not clinical psychology."}}, {"q": "Self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation involving which of the following?", "concept": "The correct answer is clients observing and recording their own behaviour. Self-monitoring is an assessment and intervention technique, central to behavioural and cognitive-behavioural approaches, in which clients systematically observe, track, and record their own behaviours, thoughts, feelings, and the surrounding circumstances (antecedents and consequences) as they occur in everyday life. For example, a client might keep a diary of every cigarette smoked, each panic attack and its triggers, food intake, or automatic negative thoughts. Self-monitoring serves two purposes. As assessment, it yields ecologically valid, real-time data about the frequency, intensity, and context of the target behaviour that would be hard to capture in the clinic and less subject to retrospective memory bias. As intervention, the very act of monitoring often produces reactivity, meaning the behaviour changes simply because it is being observed (undesirable behaviours tend to decrease and desirable ones increase), so it is therapeutic in itself. It fits within the functional analysis tradition of behaviour therapy associated with B.F. Skinner and is a staple of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy through thought records. The distractors describe physiological self-measurement (galvanic skin response, heart rate), which belongs to biofeedback, or dream recording, which belongs to psychodynamic work, none of which is behavioural self-observation. This item tests the definition of self-monitoring.", "traps": {"a": "Measuring one's own galvanic skin response is a physiological biofeedback measurement, not the behavioural self-observation that defines self-monitoring.", "b": "Measuring one's own heart rate is again a physiological measurement, not the systematic recording of behaviour, thoughts, or feelings that self-monitoring entails.", "d": "Recording dreams is a psychodynamic technique for accessing unconscious material, not the behavioural self-observation method of self-monitoring."}}, {"q": "Which therapy technique is based on the concept of \"unconditional positive regard\"?", "concept": "The answer is b: client-centred therapy is built on the concept of unconditional positive regard. It was developed by the American psychologist Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), first as non-directive counselling in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and then as client-centred therapy in his 1951 book of that name; it is now usually called person-centred therapy. Rogers held that every person has an actualising tendency, a drive to grow. Problems arise from conditions of worth: a child who is loved only when she meets others' standards learns to deny feelings that do not fit, creating incongruence between self-concept and experience. In a 1957 paper Rogers named the 'necessary and sufficient' conditions for change, three of which are the therapist's core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance of the client as a person, without judgement or conditions) and accurate empathic understanding. When the client feels accepted, the need to defend the self falls and he can own denied feelings. Example: a client admits he resents his ill mother. The therapist neither praises nor criticises but accepts him and reflects, 'You feel angry, and guilty about feeling angry', so he can explore the feeling rather than hide it. Option b is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions and the defining attitude of client-centred therapy. Exam trap: unconditional positive regard is an attitude, not a technique, and it does not mean approving of every behaviour; it means valuing the person, which separates it from praise or reinforcement in behaviour therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis, Freud's method, relies on free association, interpretation of resistance and transference, and a neutral 'blank screen' analyst, not the warm acceptance of unconditional positive regard.", "c": "Behavioural therapy applies conditioning principles (reinforcement, exposure, modelling) to change specific behaviours; reinforcement there is contingent on behaviour, the opposite of unconditional regard.", "d": "Gestalt therapy, from Fritz Perls, is an active, often confrontational here-and-now therapy using the empty chair and awareness exercises; it is humanistic but not built around unconditional positive regard."}}, {"q": "Token Economy is an influential intervention based upon:", "concept": "The answer is a: the token economy is based on operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938) in The Behavior of Organisms, holds that behaviour is shaped by its consequences: a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more frequent. A token economy applies this to a whole group, such as a hospital ward, a classroom or a hostel. Staff define clear target behaviours (making one's bed, attending a group, finishing homework), and each time one occurs the person receives a token: a point, a star or a plastic chip. Tokens have no value in themselves; they are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers that are later exchanged for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. Because they can buy many things they act as generalised reinforcers, like money, and they bridge the delay between a behaviour and the final reward. Tokens can also be taken back for problem behaviour, which is response cost. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) ran the classic programme at Anna State Hospital, Illinois, where long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs. When tokens were handed out regardless of work, the work almost stopped; when the contingency returned, so did the work. Example: in a children's home, Kabir earns a star each morning he is dressed and ready for school on time, and ten stars buy an extra hour of cricket on Sunday. Option a is right because the method works through contingent reinforcement, the heart of operant conditioning. Exam trap: tokens gain their value by being paired with backup rewards, which sounds like classical pairing, but the programme as a whole changes voluntary behaviour through its consequences, so it is operant; and Skinner supplied the principles, while Ayllon and Azrin built the token economy.", "traps": {"b": "Implicit learning, studied by Arthur Reber (1967) with artificial grammars, is picking up complex patterns without awareness; it is a cognitive phenomenon, not a system of planned reinforcement.", "c": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) links a neutral stimulus to one that already triggers a reflex and underlies systematic desensitisation and aversion therapy; the token economy changes voluntary behaviour through its consequences instead.", "d": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) offers empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard in a non-directive relationship; rewards given only for target behaviours are the opposite of unconditional regard."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT an example of primary prevention of mental illness?", "concept": "The answer is c: Narcotics Anonymous is not primary prevention; it serves people who already have an addiction, so it is tertiary prevention. Gerald Caplan (1964), in Principles of Preventive Psychiatry, adapted the public health levels of prevention to mental illness. Primary prevention aims to stop a disorder from arising at all, lowering its incidence (new cases) through education, stress reduction, prenatal care, nutrition and genetic counselling. Secondary prevention aims at early detection and prompt treatment of cases that have already begun, shortening them and lowering prevalence, as in screening schoolchildren for depression. Tertiary prevention aims to reduce the disability and relapse of people who are already ill, through rehabilitation, aftercare and self-help groups. Narcotics Anonymous, founded in 1953 on the twelve-step model of Alcoholics Anonymous, is a self-help fellowship for people already dependent on drugs, helping them stay abstinent and rebuild their lives: relapse prevention and rehabilitation. The other three options act before any disorder exists. DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education), begun in Los Angeles schools in 1983, teaches children to resist drugs before they start using them. Genetic screening and counselling identify carriers or risks so that a disorder can be avoided. Prenatal care protects the foetus from infection, malnutrition, alcohol and birth complications that can cause intellectual disability. Arjun, recovering from heroin dependence and attending weekly NA meetings, is receiving tertiary prevention; his younger sister, in a DARE class at school, is receiving primary prevention. Option c is right because NA begins only after addiction exists. Exam trap: DARE tempts as the 'not primary' answer because evaluations found it largely ineffective, but effectiveness does not change its level: it still targets children who are not yet using.", "traps": {"a": "DARE (1983) is a school programme that teaches children to refuse drugs before they begin using them. That is primary prevention by design, whatever its later evaluations showed.", "b": "Genetic screening, with counselling, identifies carriers and at-risk pregnancies so that a disorder can be avoided before it begins, which is primary prevention in Caplan's scheme.", "d": "Prenatal health care (nutrition, infection control, avoiding alcohol and other teratogens) stops conditions such as intellectual disability from arising, the classic example of primary prevention."}}, {"q": "Behaviour therapies apply learning principles to:", "concept": "The answer is c: behaviour therapies apply the principles of learning to eliminate unwanted behaviour (and to build desirable behaviour in its place). Behaviour therapy treats problem behaviour as learned, so it can be unlearned or replaced. It grew in the 1950s from three roots: the reciprocal inhibition and systematic desensitisation of Joseph Wolpe (1958) in South Africa, the group of Hans Eysenck (1952) at the Maudsley Hospital in London, and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953) in the United States. Behaviour therapists focus on the symptom itself, here and now, instead of hunting for hidden causes; Eysenck's motto was that if you get rid of the symptom you have eliminated the neurosis. A functional analysis asks what triggers the behaviour and what consequences keep it going. Classical conditioning methods include systematic desensitisation, exposure, flooding and aversion therapy; operant methods include token economies, shaping, extinction and time out; modelling, from Albert Bandura (1969), adds learning by observation. Example: Kabir is terrified of dogs. His therapist builds an anxiety hierarchy from a picture of a puppy up to stroking a real dog, and pairs each step with relaxation until the fear response is gone. Option c is right because the stated goal of behaviour therapy is to change, and usually remove, the unwanted behaviour through relearning. Exam trap: option a, treating the underlying causes of dysfunction, is the aim of psychoanalysis. Behaviourists argued that the symptoms are the disorder, and research has found little evidence that removing a symptom simply produces a new one (symptom substitution).", "traps": {"a": "Identifying and treating the underlying causes of dysfunction is the aim of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and other insight therapies. Behaviour therapy targets the behaviour itself.", "b": "Improving learning and insight sounds close, but insight is the goal of psychodynamic therapy. Behaviour therapy uses learning principles as a tool for change, not to produce self-understanding.", "d": "Improving communication and social sensitivity describes sensitivity training and encounter groups (Kurt Lewin's T-groups, 1946) and interpersonal approaches. Social skills training exists in behaviour therapy, but it is one technique, not the general aim."}}, {"q": "Illnesses that seem to result from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called:", "concept": "The answer is c: illnesses that come from an interaction of physical and psychological factors are called psychosomatic. 'Psychosomatic' joins psyche (mind) and soma (body); the term is credited to the German physician Johann Christian Heinroth (1818). A psychosomatic disorder is a real physical disease, with measurable tissue damage or physiological change, whose onset or course is worsened by psychological factors such as stress, conflict or emotion. Franz Alexander (1950), in Psychosomatic Medicine, proposed that a specific unconscious conflict acting on a vulnerable organ produced each of seven classic psychosomatic disorders, the 'holy seven': peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, bronchial asthma, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. The specificity idea failed (most ulcers proved to be caused by Helicobacter pylori bacteria), but the broader point survived: chronic stress raises cortisol, suppresses immunity and worsens many diseases. George Engel (1977) widened this into the biopsychosocial model. DSM-5 now calls the category 'psychological factors affecting other medical conditions', and ICD-10 codes it F54. Example: Kabir has genuine asthma, confirmed by lung function tests; his attacks become much more frequent in the weeks before his board exams and after quarrels at home. The disease is real, and stress is making it worse. Option c is right because the stem stresses an interaction of physical and psychological factors, which defines psychosomatic illness. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary or 'all in the mind'; there is real bodily disease. In conversion disorder, by contrast, the symptom (paralysis, blindness, seizures) has no physical disease behind it, which is why examiners pair the two.", "traps": {"a": "'Somatic' simply means bodily; DSM-5 somatic symptom disorder is distressing bodily symptoms with excessive thoughts, feelings and behaviours about them, whether or not a medical disease is present.", "b": "'Hysterical' is the old label, from Hippocrates (about 400 BCE) to Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's Studies on Hysteria (1895), for conversion and dissociative symptoms without organic disease; DSM-III (1980) dropped it.", "d": "Conversion disorder (DSM-5 functional neurological symptom disorder) shows neurological symptoms such as paralysis or seizures that do not fit any recognised neurological disease, so there is no physical illness for psychological factors to interact with."}}, {"q": "Which of these has been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence?", "concept": "The answer is d: criminal behaviour leading to prison, psychosis and antisocial personality disorder have all been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence. Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive, persistent pattern of violating others' basic rights or major age-appropriate rules: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations such as truancy and running away. DSM requires 3 of 15 criteria in the past 12 months and separates a childhood-onset type (a symptom before 10) from an adolescent-onset type. The links run in three directions. First, antisocial personality disorder (ASPD): DSM requires evidence of conduct disorder before age 15 for an adult ASPD diagnosis, and Lee Robins (1966), in Deviant Children Grown Up, found that adult antisocial personality almost never appeared without childhood conduct problems. Second, crime: Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated a rare life-course-persistent pattern, starting in early childhood and continuing into adult offending and imprisonment, from a common adolescence-limited pattern that fades in early adulthood. Third, psychosis: birth-cohort studies such as the Dunedin study in New Zealand found that children with conduct problems had higher rates of adult psychotic and schizophreniform disorders, so CD is a broad risk marker, not only a route to crime. Example: Kabir, 14, has been setting fires, stealing and bullying since age 9; his risk of adult offending, of ASPD and even of later psychosis is raised. Option d is right because each of the three outcomes has research support. Exam trap: students know the conduct disorder to ASPD link and pick c, overlooking the evidence for imprisonment and psychosis.", "traps": {"a": "Adolescent conduct disorder does predict adult offending and imprisonment, especially in Terrie Moffitt's life-course-persistent group, but choosing this alone ignores the equally supported links to psychosis and ASPD.", "b": "Cohort studies such as Dunedin found more adult psychotic and schizophreniform disorder after childhood conduct problems, so this is a real association, but only one of three.", "c": "Antisocial personality disorder requires conduct disorder before age 15, the best-known link; it is true but incomplete, because crime and psychosis are associated too."}}, {"q": "\"Hallucinations\" are examples of disorders of:", "concept": "The answer is b: hallucinations are disorders of perception. Descriptive psychopathology, laid out by Karl Jaspers (1913) in General Psychopathology, sorts symptoms by the mental function they disturb: consciousness, perception, thought (form and content), mood, memory and so on. A hallucination is a perception without an object: the person sees, hears, smells, tastes or feels something when no matching external stimulus is present, yet it is as vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside. Jean-Etienne Esquirol (1838) gave this modern definition and separated it from an illusion, which is a misperception of a real stimulus (a coat on a hook seen as a man). Auditory hallucinations, such as voices commenting on the person, are the commonest in schizophrenia; visual ones point more towards delirium or other organic causes. Example: Riya hears a man's voice calling her name in an empty room every night. Nothing in the room produced the sound, so it is a hallucination, a fault in perception. Option b is right because a hallucination is by definition a false perception, so in a mental status examination it is recorded under perception. Exam trap: examiners offer 'thinking' because hallucinations and delusions travel together in psychosis. A delusion is a false, fixed belief, a disorder of thought content; a hallucination is a false perception. Judgement (deciding how to act) and intelligence are separate headings altogether.", "traps": {"a": "Judgement is the ability to weigh a situation and act sensibly, tested by asking what the person would do on finding a stamped, addressed letter in the street; a hallucinating person may judge well or badly, but the symptom itself is perceptual.", "c": "Intelligence is general reasoning ability measured by tests such as the Wechsler scales; hallucinations occur in people of any IQ and are not a fault of intellect.", "d": "Disorders of thinking are delusions (thought content) and formal thought disorder such as loosening of associations (thought form); hallucinations often accompany them in psychosis but are classed under perception."}}, {"q": "\"First rank symptoms\" of schizophrenia given by Schneider include all except", "concept": "Kurt Schneider, a German psychiatrist, set out first-rank symptoms (FRS) in Clinical Psychopathology (English edition 1959) as experiences that, in the absence of organic brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia. There are eleven, in four groups. Auditory hallucinations of three kinds: audible thoughts (hearing one's own thoughts spoken aloud), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity (made) experiences: made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts, and somatic passivity, where bodily sensations are felt as imposed from outside. Finally, delusional perception: a normal perception given a sudden delusional meaning, such as Raju seeing a red car and instantly knowing he has been chosen to save the nation. Schneider called symptoms such as other hallucinations, perplexity, depressive or euphoric mood change and emotional impoverishment second-rank. FRS are not pathognomonic: they also occur in mania and other psychoses. DSM-IV let one bizarre delusion or one running-commentary voice satisfy Criterion A, but DSM-5 and DSM-5-TR removed this special weighting, so two Criterion A symptoms are needed, at least one being delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. ICD-11 keeps experiences of influence, passivity and control among its core symptoms. Depersonalisation is a nonspecific self-experience found in anxiety, depression, fatigue and depersonalization/derealization disorder, and the person knows the change is only 'as if', so it is not an FRS. Option a is right because depersonalisation is the only option absent from Schneider's list of first-rank symptoms.", "traps": {"b": "A hallucinatory voice giving a running commentary on the patient's actions ('now he is opening the door, now he is sitting down') is one of Schneider's three auditory first-rank symptoms, alongside audible thoughts and voices arguing about the patient in the third person. It tempts because many students remember FRS only as passivity and thought alienation, and assume any hallucination is merely second-rank. These three voice types count as first-rank.", "c": "Delusional perception is a two-stage first-rank symptom: a real, normal perception (for example, a dog lifting its paw) followed at once by a delusional meaning that has no understandable link to it ('the world is ending and I am the saviour'). It tempts because the word 'perception' suggests a perceptual disorder like a hallucination, but the perception itself is normal; the abnormality lies in the sudden, unrelated meaning attached to it.", "d": "Thought broadcasting is the experience that one's thoughts are diffusing out of the mind so that other people can know or share them. It belongs with thought insertion and thought withdrawal in Schneider's thought alienation group of first-rank symptoms. It tempts because the option is misprinted as 'Broad casting' and may look unfamiliar, and because students confuse it with the belief that others can read one's mind, which is not itself first-rank."}}, {"q": "Which one of these statements is TRUE?", "concept": "The answer is c: siblings of people diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to have mental health problems in their lifetime. Schizophrenia runs in families because risk is partly genetic and polygenic, carried by many common gene variants of small effect. Irving Gottesman (1991) pooled about 40 family and twin studies: lifetime risk is about 1% in the general population, about 9% for a sibling, about 13% for a child of one affected parent and about 48% for an identical co-twin. These genes overlap with those for bipolar disorder, depression and autism, and siblings also share family stress, so first-degree relatives show raised rates of several disorders, not only schizophrenia. The other statements are popular myths. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent and are far more often victims of violence than perpetrators. Hearing voices or holding odd beliefs does not by itself mean schizophrenia: Marius Romme and Sandra Escher (1989) showed that many voice-hearers in the general population cope well and never seek help, and hallucinations also occur in bereavement, post-traumatic stress disorder, bipolar disorder, severe depression, drug use and on falling asleep. Example: Arjun's elder brother has schizophrenia; Arjun's own lifetime risk of schizophrenia is several times the average, and his risk of depression is also higher, yet most siblings never develop schizophrenia. Option c is right because family and twin studies consistently show raised risk in relatives. Exam trap: options a and d play on media stereotypes, and b equates a symptom with a diagnosis. Key note: c is the clearly supported statement, though d is not baseless: meta-analyses such as Seena Fazel and colleagues (2009) find a raised relative risk of violent crime, including homicide, in schizophrenia, mostly explained by co-occurring substance misuse. The absolute risk is tiny and textbooks treat the blanket claim as a myth, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is the media stereotype: most people with schizophrenia are never violent, and any added risk is concentrated in those with untreated illness and substance misuse, so the illness does not 'make people violent'.", "b": "Voices and unusual beliefs occur in many people without schizophrenia (Marius Romme and Sandra Escher's voice-hearers, bereavement, PTSD, bipolar disorder), and diagnosis needs a cluster of symptoms with duration and impairment, not one experience.", "d": "Studies do find a raised relative risk of homicide, concentrated in people with substance misuse, but the absolute risk is very small and most people with schizophrenia never harm anyone; textbooks treat this blanket claim as a myth."}}, {"q": "The violation of a society's explicit and implicit norms can be viewed as not normal in the sense that the violation is:", "concept": "The answer is a: behaviour that violates a society's explicit and implicit norms is called deviant. There is no single test for abnormality, so textbooks such as Abnormal Psychology by Ronald Comer (1992) sum up the usual features as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Deviance means behaviour that is different, extreme, unusual or bizarre. It can be judged in two ways. The statistical criterion calls abnormal whatever is rare, far from the average; its flaw is that rare talent, such as an IQ of 150, would also count. The social-norm criterion calls abnormal whatever breaks a society's norms, both the explicit rules (laws) and the implicit ones (unspoken customs). Its flaw is cultural relativity: norms differ across cultures and eras. A possession trance at a temple festival may be accepted custom in one setting and a symptom in another, and homosexuality was listed as a disorder in DSM-II (1968) until 1973. So deviance alone never proves a disorder. Example: Kabir walks to college in winter wearing only shorts and talks loudly to himself on the bus. People stare because he is violating norms; his behaviour is deviant, but a clinician would still ask whether he is distressed, dysfunctional or dangerous. Option a is right because 'deviant' is the term for breaking a society's norms. Exam trap: the other three Ds are also features of abnormality, so all four options look correct. The test is which D the stem defines: norm violation is deviance, suffering is distress, inability to cope with daily life is dysfunction, and risk of harm is danger.", "traps": {"b": "Distress is the D that covers unpleasant, upsetting feelings such as unhappiness and anxiety, the person's own suffering. Breaking norms can happen without any distress.", "c": "Dysfunction is behaviour that interferes with daily functioning: work, study, relationships and self-care. A norm-breaker may function perfectly well.", "d": "Danger to self or others covers suicidal or violent risk. Most norm violations, such as odd dress, carry no danger at all."}}, {"q": "The term “dementia praecox” was coined by", "concept": "The answer is a: Emil Kraepelin. The German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926) is the name attached to \"dementia praecox\", Latin for early (precocious) dementia. He used the term in the fourth edition of his textbook in 1893, and in the sixth edition of 1899 he drew his famous dividing line: dementia praecox, which begins in youth and runs a deteriorating course, versus manic-depressive insanity, which comes in episodes with good recovery between them. He united pictures described by others, hebephrenia (Ewald Hecker), catatonia (Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum) and paranoid states, as forms of one illness defined above all by its course. Think of a 19-year-old student who withdraws, hears voices and over the years loses his drive and social skills: to Kraepelin, the downhill course itself was diagnostic. In 1911 Eugen Bleuler renamed the group schizophrenia because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. Option a is right because Kraepelin made the Latin term famous and built the concept around it. Exam trap: the French physician Benedict Morel used the French phrase \"démence précoce\" earlier, in the 1850s, for young patients who deteriorated, and Arnold Pick used the Latin term in 1891; neither is offered here. Key note: strictly, Kraepelin popularised rather than coined the term, but among these options he is the standard answer.", "traps": {"b": "Eugen Bleuler (spelled \"Eugene\" on the CIP paper) did not coin dementia praecox; he replaced it in 1911 with the new name schizophrenia and described the four A's.", "c": "Karl Jaspers wrote General Psychopathology (1913), founding the phenomenological method and the idea of primary delusions and understandability, but he did not coin dementia praecox.", "d": "Adolf Meyer, the Swiss-American founder of psychobiology, saw schizophrenia as a \"reaction type\" to life stresses and introduced the life chart; he did not coin the term."}}, {"q": "Which one is NOT a compulsive behaviour in obsessive-compulsive disorder?", "concept": "The answer is hoarding because, in this classic framing, hoarding is not classified as a compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder; the others (tapping, washing, checking) are textbook compulsions. To understand this, you must grasp the structure of OCD. It has two components. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images, or urges that cause marked anxiety (for example, fears of contamination, doubts about safety, or a need for symmetry). Compulsions are repetitive behaviors or mental acts that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession, in order to reduce the anxiety or prevent a feared event. The most common compulsions are precisely washing and cleaning (in response to contamination fears), checking (in response to doubt, like checking locks or the stove), ordering and arranging, counting, and repeating actions such as tapping. So tapping, washing, and checking are all standard compulsive rituals. Hoarding, the difficulty discarding possessions and the distress at parting with them, was historically considered a symptom or subtype of OCD, but in the DSM-5 it was reclassified as its OWN distinct condition, Hoarding Disorder, within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter but separate from OCD itself. That is why hoarding is the odd one out: it is not a compulsive ritual performed to neutralize an obsession in the way washing or checking are; it now stands as a separate diagnosis. The conceptual point to retain is that classic compulsions neutralize obsessions, while hoarding is a distinct disorder of acquisition and difficulty discarding. Key note: RINPAS 2024 asks this same item with the same four options, and its printed key gives a (tapping), counting hoarding as a compulsion the older way. DSM-5 (2013) made hoarding disorder a diagnosis of its own, which is why hoarding is used here.", "traps": {"a": "Tapping is a genuine repetitive compulsive ritual in OCD, often performed to neutralize anxiety or a feared outcome, so it is not the odd one out.", "c": "Washing (in response to contamination obsessions) is the most classic OCD compulsion of all, so it clearly belongs.", "d": "Checking (locks, appliances, safety) is a core OCD compulsion driven by pathological doubt, so it belongs among the compulsions."}}, {"q": "What assumption does ANCOVA have that ANOVA does not?", "concept": "The correct answer is homogeneity of regression slopes. Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA), an extension of the analysis of variance developed by Ronald Fisher, combines ANOVA and regression: it compares group means on a dependent variable while statistically controlling for one or more continuous covariates that are related to the dependent variable, thereby reducing error variance and removing confounding. ANCOVA shares ANOVA's basic assumptions of normality, independence of observations, and homogeneity of variance (homoscedasticity). But it adds a crucial extra assumption not required by ordinary ANOVA: homogeneity of regression slopes. This means the relationship (the regression slope) between the covariate and the dependent variable must be the same across all the treatment groups. In practice this is tested by checking that there is no significant interaction between the independent variable and the covariate. The assumption matters because ANCOVA adjusts each group's mean using a common (pooled) slope; if the slopes differ across groups, that single adjustment is invalid and the covariate's effect depends on group membership, making the adjusted means misleading. Because homogeneity of regression slopes is the assumption unique to ANCOVA and absent from ANOVA, option d is correct.", "traps": {"a": "Homogeneity of variance is an assumption shared by ANOVA as well, so it does not distinguish ANCOVA.", "b": "Homoscedasticity is essentially the same equal-variance assumption and is common to both, not unique to ANCOVA.", "c": "Homogeneity of sample size is a desirable design feature, not a formal statistical assumption unique to ANCOVA."}}, {"q": "Which non-parametric test is used for comparing medians of three or more independent groups?", "concept": "The correct answer is the Kruskal-Wallis test. The Kruskal-Wallis test, named after William Kruskal and Wilson Allen Wallis, is the non-parametric alternative to the one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA). It is used to compare three or more independent groups when the data are ordinal or when the assumptions of ANOVA (such as normality and homogeneity of variance) are not met. By ranking all observations together and comparing the mean ranks across groups, it tests whether the samples come from the same distribution, effectively comparing the medians of three or more independent groups. Because the question specifies three or more independent groups and a non-parametric procedure, option B is correct. The Mann-Whitney U test (option A) is limited to comparing exactly two independent groups, so it cannot handle three or more. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C), from Frank Wilcoxon, compares two related or paired samples and so applies to dependent rather than independent data. The Friedman test (option D) is also non-parametric and handles three or more conditions, but it is designed for related or repeated-measures data (the same subjects measured under multiple conditions), not independent groups. The key mapping is that independent groups with three or more levels call for Kruskal-Wallis, the rank-based analogue of one-way ANOVA.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test compares only two independent groups, not three or more.", "c": "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test compares two related samples, not several independent groups.", "d": "The Friedman test compares three or more related (repeated-measures) conditions, not independent groups."}}, {"q": "Parameter is characteristic of:", "concept": "The correct answer is the population. In statistics, a clear distinction is drawn between a parameter and a statistic. A parameter is a numerical value that describes a characteristic of an entire population, the complete set of all individuals or observations of interest, for example the true mean height of all adults in a country or the true proportion of voters favouring a candidate. A statistic, by contrast, is a numerical value computed from a sample, a subset drawn from the population, for example the mean height of 500 surveyed adults. The reason this distinction matters is that we usually cannot measure an entire population directly (it is too large, costly, or inaccessible), so we draw a sample, calculate sample statistics, and use them to estimate the unknown population parameters, a process called statistical inference. Parameters are typically denoted by Greek letters (mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation), while sample statistics use Roman letters (x-bar for the sample mean, s for the sample standard deviation). The accuracy of estimating a parameter from a statistic depends on sampling methods and sample size. Because a parameter by definition describes the population, the population is the correct answer.", "traps": {"a": "A characteristic of the sample is a statistic, not a parameter; this is the precise opposite of the correct answer.", "c": "An individual is a single case, not a population characteristic; parameters summarise whole populations.", "d": "A test is a measurement instrument, not the group whose characteristic a parameter describes."}}, {"q": "'Ethics' can be defined as the formulation of principles to:", "concept": "The answer is d: ethics is the formulation of principles that guide behaviour, telling people what they ought and ought not to do. The word comes from the Greek ethos, meaning character or custom. Ethics is wider than any one tool for changing behaviour: it is a set of standards of right conduct that people use to decide how to act, especially when interests conflict. Professional ethics turns these standards into codes. In research, modern codes grew out of abuse: the Nuremberg Code (1947) after Nazi experiments, the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), which named three principles: respect for persons (applied through informed consent), beneficence (do no harm and maximise benefit) and justice (fair selection of participants). Psychologists follow the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016), and Indian researchers follow the Indian Council of Medical Research's National Ethical Guidelines (2017). These codes do not mainly punish or reward; they guide decisions in advance. Example: Meera plans to study anxiety in college students. Ethical principles guide her to obtain informed consent, tell students they may withdraw at any time, keep their answers confidential, and offer debriefing and help if a questionnaire upsets anyone. Option d is right because guiding behaviour is the purpose of ethical principles; reducing bad behaviour or rewarding good behaviour are at most side effects. Exam trap: the distractors borrow the language of operant conditioning (reinforce, punish, reduce behaviour), which makes them sound psychological and precise; but ethics is a guide to conduct, not a reinforcement schedule.", "traps": {"a": "Reducing inappropriate behaviour is the goal of behaviour-modification techniques such as extinction or response cost; ethical principles may have that effect, but their purpose is to guide all conduct.", "b": "Punishing inappropriate behaviour is the job of law or of disciplinary bodies that enforce a code after a breach; ethics itself sets out the standards before anyone acts.", "c": "Reinforcing appropriate behaviour is the operant language of B. F. Skinner (1938); ethics states what appropriate behaviour is but does not work by rewarding it."}}, {"q": "When the assumption of normal distribution is met and you want to compare the means of two independent groups, which statistical test is most appropriate?", "concept": "The answer is the independent-samples t-test because it is the standard parametric test for comparing the means of exactly two separate, unrelated groups when the parametric assumptions, especially normality, are satisfied. The t-test was developed by William Sealy Gosset, who published under the pseudonym 'Student' in 1908 while working at the Guinness brewery, which is why it is often called Student's t-test. It works by calculating a t-statistic, essentially the difference between the two group means divided by an estimate of the variability (standard error) of that difference, and then judging whether that difference is large enough, relative to the noise, to be unlikely under the null hypothesis of no true difference. Because it uses the actual means and standard deviations rather than just ranks, it is more powerful than its non-parametric counterpart when its assumptions hold. Choosing the right test depends on three questions: how many groups, whether they are independent or related, and whether parametric assumptions are met. Two independent groups with normality gives the independent-samples t-test; two related or paired groups gives the paired-samples t-test; and if normality fails you drop to the non-parametric versions, Mann-Whitney U for independent groups and Wilcoxon signed-rank for paired ones. Since the question specifies normality met, two independent groups, and comparison of means, the independent-samples t-test is correct.", "traps": {"a": "The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric alternative used when normality is violated, not when the data are normally distributed as stated here.", "c": "The chi-square test analyses categorical frequency data and associations between categories, not the comparison of two group means.", "d": "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is a non-parametric test for two related or paired samples, not for two independent normally distributed groups."}}, {"q": "_____ is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even though they are measured in different units.", "concept": "The answer is c: the coefficient of variation (CV) is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even when they are measured in different units. Karl Pearson (1896) defined it as the standard deviation expressed as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Because the SD and the mean are in the same units, the units cancel and the CV is a pure percentage. That allows comparisons the SD cannot make: heights in centimetres against weights in kilograms, or the spread of marks in a class whose mean is 30 against one whose mean is 80. The set with the lower CV is the more consistent (more uniform, less variable). Example: in Riya's class, height has a mean of 160 cm and an SD of 8 cm (CV = 5 per cent), while weight has a mean of 50 kg and an SD of 5 kg (CV = 10 per cent); weight varies relatively twice as much, even though 8 looks larger than 5. Because it divides by the mean, the CV strictly needs ratio data with a true zero. Option c is right because it is the standard relative, unit-free measure of dispersion. Exam trap: the standard deviation is the best-known measure of spread, so it attracts guesses; but it is stated in the original units, so comparing an SD in centimetres with one in kilograms is meaningless.", "traps": {"a": "Range (highest minus lowest score) is an absolute measure in the original units and depends on just two extreme scores, so it cannot compare data measured in different units.", "b": "The standard deviation is the most used measure of spread, but it is in the original units (cm, kg), so SDs of differently measured variables cannot be compared directly; dividing it by the mean gives the CV.", "d": "Mean deviation, the average absolute distance of scores from the mean, is also in the original units; its unit-free form is the coefficient of mean deviation, not mean deviation itself."}}, {"q": "Which of the following can reduce experimenter bias?", "concept": "The answer is d: double blinding reduces experimenter bias. Experimenter bias, or the experimenter expectancy effect, is the way a researcher's expectations can unintentionally shape results: through subtle cues to participants, through how ambiguous responses are scored, or through how data are recorded. Robert Rosenthal (1966) made the effect famous. In Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode's (1963) study, students were told their rats were 'maze-bright' or 'maze-dull', though the rats had been assigned at random; the 'bright' rats learned faster, apparently because the students handled them differently. In a single-blind study the participants do not know which condition they are in, which controls their own expectations, such as placebo effects. In a double-blind study neither the participants nor the researchers who deal with them and record the data know who is in which condition. Because the experimenter does not know, their expectations cannot leak into the participants' behaviour or the scoring. Double-blind designs are standard in drug trials, where an independent person holds the code that links each participant to drug or placebo. Example: Dr Anil tests a new antidepressant. A pharmacist labels identical capsules with codes, and neither Dr Anil, who rates the patients' mood, nor the patients know which capsules hold the drug. The code is broken only after rating ends. Option d is right because double blinding removes the experimenter's knowledge of the conditions, which is the very source of experimenter bias. Exam trap: control groups are vital, but they control extraneous variables and placebo effects, not the experimenter's own expectations; an experimenter who knows which group is which can still bias the results. Demand characteristics and the Barnum effect are sources of bias, not cures for it.", "traps": {"a": "A control group gives a baseline for comparison and controls extraneous factors such as maturation or placebo response. It does not stop an experimenter who knows the groups from biasing results, so it is not the best answer.", "b": "Demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne (1962), are cues in a study that tell participants what the researcher expects, so they change their behaviour. They are a source of bias, not a way to reduce it.", "c": "The Barnum effect, shown by Bertram Forer (1949) and named by Paul Meehl (1956), is the tendency to accept vague, general personality descriptions as accurate for oneself. It is an error in judging feedback, not a method of control."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of the mean?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'it is best used with ordinal data' is not a characteristic of the mean. The arithmetic mean (M = ΣX ÷ N) is the sum of the scores divided by their number. It has three classic properties. First, it is the balance point of the distribution: deviations above and below it cancel, so the sum of deviations about the mean is always zero. Second, the sum of squared deviations about the mean is smaller than about any other value, the least-squares property that makes the mean the basis of variance, SD and regression. Third, because it uses every score, it is affected by extreme scores: the marks 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8, but adding one score of 40 lifts it to about 13.3. The mean assumes equal distances between scores, so it needs interval or ratio data. Ordinal data, such as class ranks or finishing positions in a race, show only order; the gap between first and second need not equal the gap between second and third, so the median is the proper average, and for nominal data the mode. Example: if Arjun's teacher records only class ranks, the median rank is a fair summary, but a mean rank assumes that the gap between 1st and 2nd equals the gap between 9th and 10th, which ranks cannot tell us. Option d is right because ordinal data suit the median, not the mean. Exam trap: the item asks for what is NOT true; options a, b and c are all genuine properties, so a hurried reader picks the first true statement.", "traps": {"a": "Being pulled by extreme scores is a true characteristic of the mean, and its main weakness; that is why the median is preferred for skewed data.", "b": "Minimising the sum of squared deviations (the least-squares property) is a true characteristic of the mean.", "c": "The sum of deviations about the mean is always zero; this is a true, defining property of the mean as the balance point of the distribution."}}]});
